{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Chardonnay is especially associated with the Côte des Blancs; Pinot Noir with the Montagne de Reims and Côte des Bar; Meunier with the western Marne Valley. The eastern valley around Aÿ has substantial Pinot Noir.","f":"Champagne's three main grapes, and the district each leads"},{"b":"Brut Nature under 3, with no sugar added after secondary fermentation; Extra Brut 0 to 6; Brut under 12; Extra Dry 12 to 17; Sec 17 to 32; Demi-Sec 32 to 50; Doux over 50 grams per litre.","f":"Dosage ladder, driest to sweetest, in grams per litre"},{"b":"Non-vintage: 15 months from tirage, 12 of them on the lees. Vintage: 36 months from tirage.","f":"Minimum ageing for non-vintage and vintage Champagne"},{"b":"Base wine, assemblage, tirage, second fermentation in bottle, lees ageing, riddling (remuage), disgorgement (dégorgement), dosage (liqueur d'expédition), cork and cage.","f":"The traditional method in order"},{"b":"Whole villages, not vineyards: 17 Grand Cru and 42 Premier Cru communes.","f":"Grand Cru and Premier Cru in Champagne: what is ranked, and how many"},{"b":"Blanc de Blancs: white grapes, usually Chardonnay but not necessarily Chardonnay alone. Blanc de Noirs: black grapes, Pinot Noir, Meunier or both, pressed with limited extraction from their skins.","f":"Blanc de Blancs versus Blanc de Noirs"},{"b":"NM négociant-manipulant, a house buying grapes; RM récoltant-manipulant, a grower using its own fruit; CM coopérative de manipulation.","f":"Label codes NM, RM, CM"},{"b":"Dom Pérignon (Moët & Chandon), Cristal (Louis Roederer), Comtes de Champagne (Taittinger), Sir Winston Churchill (Pol Roger), Grande Cuvée (Krug), Belle Epoque (Perrier-Jouët).","f":"Prestige cuvées and their houses"},{"b":"Magnum 1.5 L, Jeroboam 3 L, Rehoboam 4.5 L, Methuselah 6 L, Salmanazar 9 L, Balthazar 12 L, Nebuchadnezzar 15 L.","f":"Champagne bottle sizes, 1.5 litres and up"},{"b":"2,550 litres in all: the cuvée is the first 2,050 litres, the taille the last 500.","f":"The pressing fractions from 4,000 kilograms"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"lead"},{"name":"Meunier","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Blanc","role":"minor"}],"index":0,"name":"Champagne","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Champagne","exp":"Meunier is particularly important in the western Marne Valley, and later budburst can help reduce frost exposure. The eastern valley around Aÿ has substantial Pinot Noir. The other district-to-grape associations are broad tendencies, not exclusive planting rules.","id":"r-ur7pr5ch","opts":["Vallée de la Marne","Côte des Bar","Montagne de Reims","Côte des Blancs"],"q":"Which Champagne district is planted mainly to Meunier?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Champagne","exp":"Extra Dry is 12 to 17 grams per litre, the step above Brut's ceiling of 12, so it is sweeter than Brut despite its name. Sec (17 to 32) and Demi-Sec (32 to 50) are sweeter still; Extra Brut (0 to 6) is drier.","id":"r-joo4ny67","opts":["Sec","Extra Dry","Demi-Sec","Extra Brut"],"q":"Which Champagne sweetness term is the next step sweeter than Brut?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Champagne","exp":"Fifteen months in all, of which twelve must be on the lees. Thirty-six months is the vintage minimum, and most houses exceed both.","id":"r-h4bd98sm","opts":["12 months","24 months","36 months","15 months"],"q":"What is the minimum ageing for a non-vintage Champagne, counted from tirage?"},{"accept":["A village","village","villages","a commune","commune","communes","the village","whole villages"],"ans":"A village","cat":"Champagne","exp":"Champagne ranks entire communes on the old échelle des crus; seventeen hold Grand Cru. Burgundy ranks vineyards and Bordeaux ranks estates.","id":"r-fjsjjvm6","q":"Grand Cru on a Champagne label attaches to what kind of place?","sa":1},{"accept":["Côte des Bar","Cote des Bar","the Côte des Bar","the Cote des Bar","Côtes des Bar","Cotes des Bar"],"ans":"Côte des Bar","cat":"Champagne","exp":"The Côte des Bar sits a hundred kilometres south-east of Épernay on Kimmeridgian marl; it was excluded in 1908 and restored to the appellation in 1927.","id":"r-pv1g68wp","q":"Which Champagne district lies in the Aube, nearer to Chablis than to Reims?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Champagne","exp":"Cristal is Roederer's prestige cuvée, first made in 1876 for the Russian court and bottled in clear glass. Moët makes Dom Pérignon, Taittinger Comtes de Champagne and Pol Roger Sir Winston Churchill.","id":"r-q8dr9pgt","opts":["Taittinger","Moët & Chandon","Louis Roederer","Pol Roger"],"q":"Which house makes the prestige cuvée Cristal?"},{"accept":["Disgorgement","dégorgement","degorgement","disgorging"],"ans":"Disgorgement","cat":"Champagne","exp":"After riddling has gathered the lees in the neck, the neck is frozen and the cap removed so that pressure ejects the plug; dosage and corking follow.","id":"r-q9d1kato","q":"Name the step in which the frozen plug of sediment is expelled from the bottle.","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Champagne","exp":"Seventeen communes are Grand Cru and forty-two are Premier Cru. Seven is the count of Chablis Grand Cru climats and fifty-one the count of Alsace Grand Crus.","id":"r-uxq9k1yo","opts":["17","42","51","7"],"q":"How many villages hold Grand Cru rank in Champagne?"}],"sheet":"france","slug":"champagne","summary":"Champagne is the benchmark sparkling wine, grown at the cold northern edge of French viticulture around Reims and Épernay. Three grapes carry nearly the whole vineyard: Chardonnay, Pinot Noir and Meunier. The wine is made by the traditional method, a second fermentation in the bottle it is sold in, followed by months or years on the lees. Chalk beneath the slopes stores water and gives the cellars. Cru rank attaches to whole villages, seventeen of them Grand Cru. A server needs the method in order, the sweetness ladder with Extra Dry sweeter than Brut, the four districts and their grapes, and the ageing minimums of fifteen months for non-vintage and three years for vintage."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Riesling, Gewurztraminer, Pinot Gris and Muscat: the only grapes of Grand Cru, Vendanges Tardives and Sélection de Grains Nobles, with a few site exceptions.","f":"The four noble grapes of Alsace"},{"b":"51 named sites; the tier dates from 1975 (Schlossberg first), Kaefferkopf the last in 2007, each a separate appellation since 2011.","f":"Alsace Grand Cru: how many, and since when"},{"b":"VT: late harvest at a set minimum sugar, may be dry or sweet. SGN: botrytised berries at a higher minimum, always sweet. Both noble grapes only, no chaptalisation.","f":"Vendanges Tardives versus Sélection de Grains Nobles"},{"b":"Pinot Blanc leads, with Auxerrois, Pinot Gris, Pinot Noir, Riesling and Chardonnay; rosé must be Pinot Noir alone. Traditional method.","f":"Crémant d'Alsace: grapes and the rosé rule"},{"b":"Riesling: citrus, acidity. Gewurztraminer: lychee, rose, lower acidity. Pinot Gris: smoky pear, weight. Muscat: grapey aromatics. Check sweetness on the label; aroma and grape alone do not establish residual sugar.","f":"Tells of Riesling, Gewurztraminer, Pinot Gris and Muscat in Alsace"},{"b":"Sylvaner at Zotzenberg; blends at Altenberg de Bergheim and Kaefferkopf; Pinot Noir at Hengst and Kirchberg de Barr (2022 vintage) and Vorbourg (2024).","f":"Grand Cru exceptions to the noble-grape rule"},{"b":"AOC Alsace whites outside VT and SGN must indicate sec, demi-sec, moelleux or doux, either directly or on a clearly marked scale. Do not infer sweetness from the grape alone.","f":"Alsace sweetness labelling rule since 2021"},{"b":"Edelzwicker: any blend of permitted whites, vintage optional. Gentil: at least 50 per cent noble grapes, each vinified separately.","f":"Edelzwicker versus Gentil"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Riesling","role":"lead"},{"name":"Gewürztraminer","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Gris / Grigio","role":"lead"},{"name":"Muscat","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Blanc","role":"blending"},{"name":"Auxerrois","role":"blending"},{"name":"Silvaner","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"rising"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"}],"index":1,"name":"Alsace","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Alsace","exp":"The noble four are Riesling, Gewurztraminer, Pinot Gris and Muscat. Pinot Blanc, Sylvaner and Auxerrois make good everyday wine but, apart from Sylvaner at Zotzenberg, stay out of Grand Cru.","id":"r-i3yb0ecp","opts":["Muscat","Sylvaner","Pinot Blanc","Auxerrois"],"q":"Which grape is one of Alsace's four noble varieties?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Alsace","exp":"Fifty-one named lieux-dits, each a separate appellation since 2011. Seventeen and forty-two are Champagne's Grand Cru and Premier Cru village counts; seven is the number of Chablis Grand Cru climats.","id":"r-qw0wgwnd","opts":["17","51","7","42"],"q":"How many Grand Cru sites does Alsace recognise?"},{"accept":["100 per cent","100%","one hundred percent","all of it","100 percent","the whole wine"],"ans":"100 per cent","cat":"Alsace","exp":"An AOC Alsace wine labelled Riesling must be entirely Riesling. Do not extend that answer to every grape name: Alsace Pinot Blanc may include Auxerrois, while Muscat can represent permitted Muscat varieties.","id":"r-xv7j6svw","q":"What proportion of Riesling must an AOC Alsace wine labelled Riesling contain?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Alsace","exp":"Sélection de Grains Nobles is picked berry by berry from botrytised bunches at the highest minimum sugar. Vendanges Tardives may finish dry; Klevener is a grape mention and Crémant the sparkling wine.","id":"r-n93fcdby","opts":["Crémant d'Alsace","Klevener de Heiligenstein","Sélection de Grains Nobles","Vendanges Tardives"],"q":"Which Alsace label term names a botrytised wine that is always sweet?"},{"accept":["Pinot Noir","pinot noir","Pinot Nero"],"ans":"Pinot Noir","cat":"Alsace","exp":"The rosé must be Pinot Noir alone, the region's one black grape; the white Crémant leans on Pinot Blanc and Auxerrois, with Chardonnay allowed only in this appellation.","id":"r-un902c4x","q":"Which grape makes Crémant d'Alsace rosé?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Alsace","exp":"A 2005 amendment let Zotzenberg at Mittelbergheim write Sylvaner into its rules. Schlossberg (granite, the first Grand Cru), Rangen (volcanic, at Thann) and Brand (granite, Turckheim) keep to the noble four.","id":"r-vax730rw","opts":["Rangen","Brand","Schlossberg","Zotzenberg"],"q":"Which Alsace Grand Cru admits Sylvaner under its own rules?"},{"accept":["2021","the 2021 vintage","2021 vintage"],"ans":"2021","cat":"Alsace","exp":"The 2021 rule applies to AOC Alsace white wines, except Vendanges Tardives and Sélection de Grains Nobles. It requires sec, demi-sec, moelleux or doux, directly or on a clearly marked scale.","id":"r-ouejvn7b","q":"From which vintage must a still Alsace white outside the late-harvest categories declare its sweetness on the label?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Alsace","exp":"Pinot Noir is the one black grape of the appellation, making light reds, rosé and Crémant rosé, and since the 2022 vintage Grand Cru wine at Hengst and Kirchberg de Barr.","id":"r-libbrrj0","opts":["Pinot Noir","Syrah","Cabernet Franc","Gamay"],"q":"Which is the one black grape permitted in AOC Alsace?"}],"sheet":"france","slug":"alsace","summary":"Alsace runs along the eastern foothills of the Vosges, from Strasbourg towards Colmar and Thann. Its labels commonly name grapes, though this is not unique in France. Riesling, Gewurztraminer, Pinot Gris and Muscat are the four noble varieties, central to fifty-one Grand Cru sites with written exceptions. White styles range from dry to sweet: check the label rather than guessing. Since the 2021 vintage, AOC Alsace whites outside Vendanges Tardives and Sélection de Grains Nobles must indicate sweetness. Pinot Blanc may include Auxerrois. Crémant d'Alsace is traditional-method sparkling wine; Pinot Noir makes red and rosé. Vineyard soils, slopes and producer choices give the region considerable stylistic variety."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Pays Nantais (Melon de Bourgogne), Anjou-Saumur (Chenin Blanc, Cabernet Franc), Touraine (Chenin, Cabernet Franc, Sauvignon), Centre (Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Noir).","f":"Loire's four districts, mouth to source, with their lead grapes"},{"b":"The wine stays on its fine lees through the winter and is bottled straight off them from the March after harvest: a yeasty roundness and a faint prickle. Muscadet's signature.","f":"Sur lie: what it means"},{"b":"Coteaux du Layon (with Chaume as Premier Cru), Quarts de Chaume (Grand Cru, 2011), Bonnezeaux, Coteaux de l'Aubance: botrytised or shrivelled Chenin kept fresh by acid.","f":"Sweet Chenin appellations of Anjou"},{"b":"Chinon, Bourgueil, Saint-Nicolas-de-Bourgueil in Touraine; Saumur-Champigny in Anjou-Saumur. Light to medium, raspberry and pencil lead, served cool.","f":"Cabernet Franc appellations of the Loire"},{"b":"Both Sauvignon Blanc for white. Sancerre also makes red and rosé from Pinot Noir; Pouilly-Fumé is white only. Sancerre on the left bank, Pouilly on the right.","f":"Sancerre and Pouilly-Fumé: grapes and colours"},{"b":"Pouilly-Fumé: Loire Sauvignon Blanc. Pouilly-sur-Loire: Chasselas on the same ground. Pouilly-Fuissé: Mâconnais Chardonnay in Burgundy.","f":"The three Pouillys"},{"b":"All Chenin Blanc: sec, demi-sec, moelleux (sweet), and sparkling mousseux and pétillant by the traditional method. Montlouis across the river mirrors it.","f":"Vouvray's styles of Chenin"},{"b":"Tuffeau: soft Cretaceous limestone of Touraine and Saumur. Silex: flint, Pouilly-Fumé's smoke. Caillottes: hard pebbly limestone at Sancerre. Terres blanches: Kimmeridgian marl.","f":"Soil words of the Loire"},{"b":"Rosé d'Anjou: off-dry, Grolleau. Cabernet d'Anjou: off-dry to sweet, Cabernet Franc and Cabernet Sauvignon. Rosé de Loire: dry.","f":"Rosé d'Anjou, Cabernet d'Anjou, Rosé de Loire"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Melon de Bourgogne","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chenin Blanc","role":"lead"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Franc","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"blending"},{"name":"Gamay","role":"minor"},{"name":"Grolleau","role":"minor"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"blending"},{"name":"Malbec","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pineau d'Aunis","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chasselas","role":"minor"},{"name":"Romorantin","role":"minor"}],"index":2,"name":"Loire Valley","questions":[{"a":3,"cat":"Loire Valley","exp":"Muscadet is Melon de Bourgogne alone, a neutral, acid grape brought from Burgundy. Folle Blanche makes the neighbouring Gros Plant; Chenin and Sauvignon belong further upstream.","id":"r-hksiq6zd","opts":["Folle Blanche","Sauvignon Blanc","Chenin Blanc","Melon de Bourgogne"],"q":"Which grape makes the wine called Muscadet?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Loire Valley","exp":"Sancerre's red and rosé are Pinot Noir. Chinon, Bourgueil and Saumur-Champigny are Cabernet Franc.","id":"r-ppcqtkhr","opts":["Saumur-Champigny","Sancerre","Bourgueil","Chinon"],"q":"Which Loire appellation makes its red wine from Pinot Noir?"},{"accept":["Quarts de Chaume","Quarts-de-Chaume","Quarts de Chaume Grand Cru","quarts de chaume"],"ans":"Quarts de Chaume","cat":"Loire Valley","exp":"Quarts de Chaume, a sheltered slope above the Layon, was raised to Grand Cru in 2011; Chaume beside it is the valley's one Premier Cru. Both are sweet Chenin Blanc.","id":"r-hpus2zme","q":"Name the Loire's only Grand Cru appellation.","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Loire Valley","exp":"Pouilly-Fumé is Sauvignon from the right bank opposite Sancerre. Pouilly-Fuissé, Pouilly-Loché and Pouilly-Vinzelles are Chardonnay appellations of the Mâconnais.","id":"r-t0bk4fd8","opts":["Pouilly-Fumé","Pouilly-Vinzelles","Pouilly-Fuissé","Pouilly-Loché"],"q":"Which Pouilly appellation is made from Sauvignon Blanc?"},{"accept":["Bottled straight off its lees","aged on its lees","on the lees","kept on its lees through the winter","on lees","lees ageing","aged on the lees","bottled off the lees"],"ans":"Bottled straight off its lees","cat":"Loire Valley","exp":"The wine rests on its fine lees through the winter and is bottled directly off them from the following March, gaining texture and a slight prickle.","id":"r-50zdxf65","q":"What does sur lie mean on a Muscadet label?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Loire Valley","exp":"Savennières is Chenin Blanc on schist on the Loire's north bank near Angers, dry and long-lived; Coulée de Serrant and Roche aux Moines are its named crus.","id":"r-9pd562ja","opts":["Sauvignon Blanc","Melon de Bourgogne","Chenin Blanc","Chardonnay"],"q":"Which grape makes the white wine Savennières?"},{"accept":["Cabernet Franc","Breton","cabernet franc"],"ans":"Cabernet Franc","cat":"Loire Valley","exp":"Cabernet Franc, called Breton in Touraine, with a small share of Cabernet Sauvignon allowed: raspberry, pencil shavings and leaf, served slightly cool.","id":"r-vkepdsgx","q":"Which grape makes the red wines of Chinon and Bourgueil?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Loire Valley","exp":"The Pays Nantais, around Nantes at the river mouth, is the maritime district of Muscadet; Anjou-Saumur, Touraine and the Centre follow upstream in that order.","id":"r-2tk7ecba","opts":["Pays Nantais","Centre","Touraine","Anjou-Saumur"],"q":"Which of the Loire's four districts lies nearest the Atlantic?"}],"sheet":"france","slug":"loire","summary":"The Loire is France's longest river, and its wine region is a 400-kilometre corridor read from the Atlantic mouth upstream in four districts. The Pays Nantais makes Muscadet from Melon de Bourgogne; Anjou-Saumur is Chenin Blanc country, dry at Savennières and sweet on the Layon, with Cabernet Franc at Saumur-Champigny; Touraine holds Vouvray and Montlouis for Chenin in every style and Chinon and Bourgueil for Cabernet Franc; the Centre makes Sauvignon Blanc at Sancerre and Pouilly-Fumé. Acidity runs through all of it, and the exam's first ask is which grape belongs to which name. Crémant de Loire is the traditional-method sparkling wine, and Pouilly-Fumé is never Pouilly-Fuissé."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Regional (Bourgogne), village (Meursault), premier cru (village plus climat), grand cru (the climat alone on the label: Chambertin).","f":"The four tiers of Burgundy, bottom to top"},{"b":"Seven climats in one appellation: Blanchot, Bougros, Les Clos, Grenouilles, Preuses, Valmur, Vaudésir.","f":"Grand crus of Chablis: how many, and name them"},{"b":"Chambertin, Chambertin-Clos de Bèze, Chapelle, Charmes, Griotte, Latricières, Mazis, Mazoyères and Ruchottes, all suffixed Chambertin.","f":"The nine grand crus of Gevrey-Chambertin"},{"b":"Romanée-Conti, La Tâche, La Romanée, Richebourg, Romanée-Saint-Vivant, La Grande Rue; Échezeaux and Grands Échezeaux lie next door in Flagey.","f":"The grand crus of Vosne-Romanée"},{"b":"Corton, on the hill shared by Aloxe-Corton, Ladoix-Serrigny and Pernand-Vergelesses; Corton-Charlemagne is its white.","f":"The only red grand cru of the Côte de Beaune"},{"b":"Nuits-Saint-Georges, Beaune, Pommard, Volnay and Meursault: premier cru is their ceiling.","f":"Famous villages with no grand cru"},{"b":"Fuissé is Mâconnais Chardonnay under the rock of Solutré; Fumé is Loire Sauvignon Blanc on flint.","f":"Pouilly-Fuissé against Pouilly-Fumé"},{"b":"A regional red blending Gamay with Pinot Noir: Pinot Noir must be at least 30 per cent of the blend and Gamay at least 15 per cent.","f":"Bourgogne Passe-Tout-Grains: the blend rule"},{"b":"Kimmeridgian limestone marl with fossil oysters under the crus; Portlandian limestone on the higher Petit Chablis plateau.","f":"The soils of Chablis"},{"b":"Thirty-three: twenty-four on the Côte de Nuits, eight on the Côte de Beaune, one in Chablis.","f":"The count of grand crus in Burgundy"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"lead"},{"name":"Aligoté","role":"minor"},{"name":"Gamay","role":"minor"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"minor"}],"index":3,"name":"Burgundy","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Burgundy","exp":"La Tâche is one of the six grand crus of Vosne-Romanée and a monopole of the Domaine de la Romanée-Conti. Gevrey has the Chambertins, Chambolle has Musigny and Morey the four clos.","id":"r-xvjs1lqo","opts":["Vosne-Romanée","Chambolle-Musigny","Gevrey-Chambertin","Morey-Saint-Denis"],"q":"Which village holds the grand cru La Tâche?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Burgundy","exp":"Volnay, like Pommard, Beaune and Meursault, tops out at premier cru. Aloxe-Corton holds Corton and Corton-Charlemagne; Puligny and Chassagne share Montrachet and Bâtard-Montrachet and each holds grand crus of its own.","id":"r-6r9tvr4z","opts":["Chassagne-Montrachet","Volnay","Puligny-Montrachet","Aloxe-Corton"],"q":"Which of these Côte de Beaune villages has no grand cru vineyard?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Burgundy","exp":"The crus sit on Kimmeridgian limestone marl rich in fossil oysters. Portlandian limestone caps the plateau and carries Petit Chablis; the other two are Jurassic stages found elsewhere.","id":"r-9383ljra","opts":["Portlandian","Bajocian","Oxfordian","Kimmeridgian"],"q":"On which geological formation do the premier and grand cru vineyards of Chablis lie?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Burgundy","exp":"Blanchot, Bougros, Les Clos, Grenouilles, Preuses, Valmur and Vaudésir make seven, all on one slope on the right bank of the Serein.","id":"r-seswlq5z","opts":["Seven","Twelve","Five","Nine"],"q":"How many grand cru climats share the single Chablis Grand Cru appellation?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Burgundy","exp":"Musigny alone among the Côte de Nuits grand crus permits a white, a tiny Chardonnay bottling. The others are red only.","id":"r-hbz6bbeh","opts":["Clos de Vougeot","Richebourg","Musigny","Chambertin"],"q":"Which grand cru of the Côte de Nuits may also be made as a white wine?"},{"accept":["Bouzeron","bouzeron","Bourgogne Bouzeron"],"ans":"Bouzeron","cat":"Burgundy","exp":"Bouzeron, in the Côte Chalonnaise, became a village appellation for Aligoté in 1997; everywhere else the grape is sold as Bourgogne Aligoté.","id":"r-rkgv0zwg","q":"Aligoté has one village appellation of its own in Burgundy. Name it.","sa":1},{"accept":["Bourgogne Passe-Tout-Grains","Passe-Tout-Grains","Passetoutgrains","Bourgogne Passetoutgrains","passe tout grains"],"ans":"Bourgogne Passe-Tout-Grains","cat":"Burgundy","exp":"Passe-Tout-Grains is the blend with fixed floors: at least 30 per cent Pinot Noir and 15 per cent Gamay. Coteaux Bourguignons may blend the two in any share.","id":"r-wyoys7so","q":"Which regional appellation must blend at least 30 per cent Pinot Noir with at least 15 per cent Gamay?","sa":1},{"accept":["Pouilly-Fuissé","Pouilly Fuissé","Pouilly-Fuisse","Pouilly Fuisse"],"ans":"Pouilly-Fuissé","cat":"Burgundy","exp":"The INAO recognised twenty-two premier cru climats for Pouilly-Fuissé on 3 September 2020, a mention added to the appellation rather than a separate one.","id":"r-qcnpx0yb","q":"Which Mâconnais appellation became the first in its sub-region to carry premier cru climats, from the 2020 vintage?","sa":1}],"sheet":"france","slug":"burgundy","summary":"Burgundy is one grape per colour read through place: Pinot Noir for red, Chardonnay for white. The Côte d'Or, a limestone slope from Dijon to Maranges, splits into the Côte de Nuits for red and the Côte de Beaune for both; Chablis sits alone to the north; the Côte Chalonnaise and the Mâconnais run south. The law ranks ground in four tiers, regional, village, premier cru and grand cru, and a grand cru stands on the label under its climat name alone. Holdings are small and split among heirs, so the grower's name matters as much as the vineyard's."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Saint-Amour, Juliénas, Chénas, Moulin-à-Vent, Fleurie, Chiroubles, Morgon, Régnié, Côte de Brouilly, Brouilly.","f":"The ten crus of Beaujolais, north to south"},{"b":"Gamay on granite and schist in the north; clay and limestone in the south for plain Beaujolais.","f":"The grape of Beaujolais and its rock"},{"b":"The third Thursday of November; carbonic or semi-carbonic maceration gives banana and bubblegum scents.","f":"Beaujolais Nouveau: release day and method"},{"b":"Beaujolais, Beaujolais-Villages, then the ten crus, which need not print the word Beaujolais.","f":"The three tiers of Beaujolais"},{"b":"Moulin-à-Vent and Morgon; Morgon's Côte du Py is the famous schist hill.","f":"The two crus to cellar"},{"b":"Chiroubles, the highest, and Fleurie; Saint-Amour and Régnié are light too.","f":"The lightest, most perfumed crus"},{"b":"Chardonnay, mostly from the northern fringe by the Mâconnais.","f":"The grape of Beaujolais Blanc"},{"b":"Lightly chilled, around 13 to 14 °C; Morgon and Moulin-à-Vent with a few years take a brief decant.","f":"Serving cru Beaujolais"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Gamay","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"minor"}],"index":4,"name":"Beaujolais","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Beaujolais","exp":"Brouilly, ringing Mont Brouilly at the southern end of the cru zone, is the biggest; Morgon is the other large one, Fleurie mid-sized and Chiroubles among the smallest.","id":"r-n3kqmt2y","opts":["Brouilly","Morgon","Chiroubles","Fleurie"],"q":"Which cru is the largest of the ten by vineyard area?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Beaujolais","exp":"Régnié was promoted from Beaujolais-Villages in 1988, making the crus ten. The other three had held cru status since the 1930s and 1940s.","id":"r-9gajwnvv","opts":["Saint-Amour","Chénas","Régnié","Côte de Brouilly"],"q":"Which cru was the last to be added to the list, in 1988?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Beaujolais","exp":"Nouveau is released at one minute past midnight on the third Thursday of November, weeks after harvest, as Beaujolais or Beaujolais-Villages.","id":"r-jdtz5r5y","opts":["The first Thursday of November","The third Thursday of November","The last Thursday of November","The third Thursday of October"],"q":"On which day is Beaujolais Nouveau released each year?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Beaujolais","exp":"Côte de Brouilly is the hill itself, Mont Brouilly, whose hard blue-green stone gives riper, more concentrated wine; Brouilly is the larger, flatter cru around its foot. The two are separate appellations.","id":"r-lo3exmzj","opts":["Moulin-à-Vent","Brouilly","Saint-Amour","Côte de Brouilly"],"q":"Which cru takes in the steep slopes of the blue-stone hill at the southern end of the cru zone, with a larger cru ringing its foot?"},{"accept":["Côte du Py","Cote du Py","the Py","Py"],"ans":"Côte du Py","cat":"Beaujolais","exp":"The Côte du Py is a hill of decomposed schist that gives Morgon's deepest, most ageworthy wine and appears on labels as a climat.","id":"r-e7kw2hgr","q":"Name the hill of decomposed schist above Villié-Morgon that is Morgon's most famous site.","sa":1},{"accept":["Carbonic maceration","Macération carbonique","maceration carbonique","carbonic","whole-bunch carbonic fermentation","semi-carbonic maceration"],"ans":"Carbonic maceration","cat":"Beaujolais","exp":"Carbonic maceration ferments intact berries from within, extracting colour and fruit esters but little tannin; most Beaujolais is made by the semi-carbonic variant.","id":"r-ymy0j5iq","q":"What is the whole-bunch fermentation in a sealed, carbon-dioxide-filled vat that gives Nouveau its banana and bubblegum scent?","sa":1},{"accept":["Chardonnay","chardonnay"],"ans":"Chardonnay","cat":"Beaujolais","exp":"Beaujolais Blanc is Chardonnay, grown mostly on the limestone fringe near the Mâconnais; Gamay is the region's red grape only.","id":"r-pvopnxq7","q":"Which grape makes the white wine labelled Beaujolais Blanc?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Beaujolais","exp":"Moulin-à-Vent's manganese-streaked granite gives the firmest, most tannic Gamay, a wine that can age ten years and taste like Burgundy; Chiroubles, Saint-Amour and Régnié are light crus for early drinking.","id":"r-inxdxtv3","opts":["Moulin-à-Vent","Chiroubles","Régnié","Saint-Amour"],"q":"Which cru is held, with Morgon, to be the most structured and longest-lived of the ten?"}],"sheet":"france","slug":"beaujolais","summary":"Beaujolais is Gamay on granite, a run of hills between the Mâconnais and Lyon that belongs to greater Burgundy in law and tastes nothing like it. The ten crus occupy the granite and schist of the north, from Saint-Amour down to Brouilly, and none of them need print the word Beaujolais on the label; Beaujolais-Villages covers the hamlets around them and plain Beaujolais the clay and limestone of the south. Whole bunches fermented under carbon dioxide give the light, fruity style; the crus, made more like red Burgundy, can age. Serve it lightly chilled, and remember Nouveau arrives on the third Thursday of November."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Côte-Rôtie, Condrieu (with Château-Grillet inside), Saint-Joseph, Crozes-Hermitage, Hermitage, Cornas, Saint-Péray.","f":"The northern Rhône crus, north to south"},{"b":"Côte-Rôtie up to 20 per cent Viognier; Hermitage and Crozes up to 15 per cent Marsanne and Roussanne; Saint-Joseph 10 per cent; Cornas none. All co-fermented.","f":"White grapes allowed in northern Rhône reds"},{"b":"Thirteen named varieties, eighteen with colour variants: Grenache leads, with Syrah, Mourvèdre, Cinsault, Counoise, and whites such as Clairette, Bourboulenc and Roussanne.","f":"The grapes of Châteauneuf-du-Pape"},{"b":"Côtes du Rhône, Côtes du Rhône Villages, Villages with a named commune, then the crus, which drop the regional name.","f":"The southern Rhône ladder"},{"b":"Both on the west bank. Tavel is rosé only; Lirac makes red, white and rosé.","f":"Tavel against Lirac"},{"b":"Muscat de Beaumes-de-Venise (Muscat) and Rasteau (Grenache): fermentation stopped with grape spirit, a step called mutage.","f":"The Rhône's vins doux naturels"},{"b":"Vinsobres 2006, Rasteau dry wine 2010, Cairanne from 2015, Gigondas white from 2023, Laudun from 2024.","f":"The crus and changes of the south since 2006"},{"b":"La Mouline on the Côte Blonde, La Landonne and La Turque on the Côte Brune, all Côte-Rôtie: the La Las.","f":"Guigal's three single vineyards"},{"b":"A cold, dry northerly wind down the valley: it dries canopies, cuts rot, stresses vines and forces low bush training or staking.","f":"The mistral wind, defined"},{"b":"Hermitage is one steep hill above Tain, small and long-lived; Crozes is the big appellation on the flatter land around it.","f":"Hermitage against Crozes-Hermitage"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"lead"},{"name":"Grenache","role":"lead"},{"name":"Mourvèdre","role":"blending"},{"name":"Viognier","role":"lead"},{"name":"Marsanne","role":"lead"},{"name":"Roussanne","role":"blending"},{"name":"Cinsault","role":"blending"},{"name":"Carignan","role":"minor"},{"name":"Muscat Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Counoise","role":"minor"},{"name":"Clairette","role":"minor"}],"index":5,"name":"Rhône Valley","questions":[{"a":2,"cat":"Rhône Valley","exp":"Cornas is red only and Syrah only. Côte-Rôtie may co-ferment Viognier, Hermitage and Saint-Joseph a share of Marsanne and Roussanne.","id":"r-u848219e","opts":["Hermitage","Saint-Joseph","Cornas","Côte-Rôtie"],"q":"Which northern Rhône appellation permits Syrah alone, with no white grape in the vat?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Rhône Valley","exp":"Côte-Rôtie allows up to 20 per cent Viognier, fermented with the Syrah; 15 per cent is the Hermitage and Crozes figure for Marsanne and Roussanne, 10 per cent the Saint-Joseph one.","id":"r-ayar6ch3","opts":["20 per cent","5 per cent","15 per cent","10 per cent"],"q":"Up to what share of Viognier may be co-fermented into Côte-Rôtie?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Rhône Valley","exp":"Tavel's entire appellation is dry rosé. Lirac next door makes all three colours; Gigondas and Vacqueyras are red-led crus under the Dentelles.","id":"r-uetux3db","opts":["Vacqueyras","Gigondas","Lirac","Tavel"],"q":"Which southern Rhône cru makes rosé and nothing else?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Rhône Valley","exp":"Muscat de Beaumes-de-Venise is the valley's Muscat vin doux naturel; Rasteau makes the Grenache one. The other three are dry crus.","id":"r-oeusjd18","opts":["Beaumes-de-Venise","Châteauneuf-du-Pape","Vacqueyras","Vinsobres"],"q":"Which appellation produces a fortified sweet Muscat, a vin doux naturel, in the southern Rhône?"},{"accept":["Galets roulés","galets roules","galets","pudding stones","galet"],"ans":"Galets roulés","cat":"Rhône Valley","exp":"The galets roulés are quartzite stones rounded by the ancient Rhône; they soak up the day's heat and release it at night, and lie thickest on the plateau of La Crau.","id":"r-fps89wpa","q":"What are the heat-holding rounded stones that cover parts of Châteauneuf-du-Pape called?","sa":1},{"accept":["Viognier","viognier"],"ans":"Viognier","cat":"Rhône Valley","exp":"Condrieu is Viognier only, dry and perfumed with apricot; the same grape is the sole variety of Château-Grillet and the minority partner in Côte-Rôtie.","id":"r-kgpmgbbh","q":"Which grape alone makes the white wine of Condrieu?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Rhône Valley","exp":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape is red and white, with no rosé permitted; Grenache leads the red, and the white draws on Clairette, Grenache Blanc, Roussanne and Bourboulenc. Tavel, across the river, is the rosé cru.","id":"r-rcp05153","opts":["Red and rosé","Red and white","Red only","Red, white and rosé"],"q":"Which colours of wine may Châteauneuf-du-Pape produce?"},{"accept":["Laudun","laudun"],"ans":"Laudun","cat":"Rhône Valley","exp":"The INAO approved Laudun, on the west bank in the Gard, as a cru on 11 September 2024 from the 2024 vintage; the cru takes no rosé.","id":"r-w1ylwmhs","q":"Which Rhône village was approved as a cru by the INAO in September 2024, for red and white wine only?","sa":1}],"sheet":"france","slug":"rhone","summary":"The Rhône is two regions on one river. North of a fifty kilometre gap below Valence the climate is continental, the slopes steep granite, and Syrah is the only black grape, with Viognier at Condrieu and Marsanne and Roussanne for the other whites; the crus run from Côte-Rôtie to Cornas and Saint-Péray. South of the gap the valley widens into Mediterranean country where bush-trained Grenache leads blends with Syrah and Mourvèdre, crowned by Châteauneuf-du-Pape and its thirteen named grapes. Labels climb from Côtes du Rhône through Villages to the crus, which drop the regional name; Tavel is rosé only and two villages fortify sweet wine."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Mourvèdre at least 50 per cent, usually far more, with Grenache and Cinsault; at least eighteen months in wood before sale.","f":"Bandol red: what the law requires"},{"b":"Grenache, Cinsault, Syrah, Mourvèdre and Tibouren, with Rolle (Vermentino) as a white lift; always a blend.","f":"The grapes of Provence rosé"},{"b":"Côtes de Provence (the largest), Coteaux d'Aix-en-Provence (west), Coteaux Varois en Provence (centre).","f":"The three broad appellations of Provence"},{"b":"Sainte-Victoire and Fréjus (2005), La Londe (2008), Pierrefeu (2013), Notre-Dame des Anges (2019).","f":"The five sub-zones of Côtes de Provence"},{"b":"A port near Marseille making mostly dry white from Marsanne, Clairette and Ugni Blanc; among the first appellations, 1936. Not the liqueur.","f":"Cassis, the appellation"},{"b":"A 1955 classification of twenty-three Côtes de Provence estates; it stays with the land and cannot be won today.","f":"Cru Classé in Provence"},{"b":"Bellet, in the hills behind Nice (Braquet, Folle Noire, Rolle), and Palette, just east of Aix, led by Château Simone (oak-aged, all three colours).","f":"The two tiny appellations of Provence: where each lies"},{"b":"Direct press or a few hours of skin contact, cold settling, cool steel fermentation, malolactic blocked, early bottling.","f":"How pale rosé is made"},{"b":"West: limestone, mistral, Aix, Bandol, Cassis. East: schist, granite and porphyry of the Maures and Esterel.","f":"West Provence against east Provence"},{"b":"The Provençal name for Vermentino, the region's main white grape.","f":"Rolle, the grape defined"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Grenache","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cinsault","role":"lead"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"blending"},{"name":"Mourvèdre","role":"lead"},{"name":"Tibouren","role":"minor"},{"name":"Vermentino","role":"lead"},{"name":"Clairette","role":"minor"},{"name":"Marsanne","role":"minor"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"minor"},{"name":"Carignan","role":"minor"},{"name":"Braquet","role":"minor"}],"index":6,"name":"Provence","questions":[{"a":2,"cat":"Provence","exp":"Bandol red is built on Mourvèdre, at least 50 per cent by law and usually more, with Grenache and Cinsault as partners; Syrah is allowed only as a small accessory grape.","id":"r-w6ouftrq","opts":["Syrah","Grenache","Mourvèdre","Cinsault"],"q":"Which grape must make up at least half of a red Bandol?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Provence","exp":"Cassis, a port near Marseille, is the region's white-wine appellation, from Marsanne, Clairette and Ugni Blanc. Bandol is Mourvèdre red, the other two are red and rosé country.","id":"r-7iw9hxi7","opts":["Bandol","Cassis","Coteaux Varois en Provence","Les Baux-de-Provence"],"q":"Which Provence appellation is known above all for its dry white wine?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Provence","exp":"Twenty-three estates were classified in 1955. 1855 is the Médoc, 1936 the first appellations including Cassis, and 1977 the creation of the Côtes de Provence appellation itself.","id":"r-brk3scap","opts":["1955","1855","1977","1936"],"q":"In which year were the Côtes de Provence crus classés ranked?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Provence","exp":"Tibouren is the local variety, delicate and herbal, at home around Saint-Tropez and Fréjus, and the same vine as Liguria's Rossese. The other three are grown across the whole of southern France.","id":"r-gmkenqww","opts":["Cinsault","Grenache","Mourvèdre","Tibouren"],"q":"Which grape is Provence's own rosé speciality, scarcely grown anywhere else in France?"},{"accept":["Rolle","rolle"],"ans":"Rolle","cat":"Provence","exp":"Vermentino goes by Rolle in Provence and the Languedoc, and the name appears on labels; it is the region's main white grape and gives many rosés a citrus lift.","id":"r-rxf31t00","q":"Under what name is Vermentino grown and labelled in Provence?","sa":1},{"accept":["Bellet","bellet","Vin de Bellet"],"ans":"Bellet","cat":"Provence","exp":"Bellet is the tiny appellation of the Nice hills, with Braquet and Folle Noire for its rosé and red and Rolle for its white.","id":"r-753m0yc4","q":"Which appellation in the hills above Nice grows Braquet and Folle Noire?","sa":1},{"accept":["18 months","eighteen months","18","eighteen","a year and a half"],"ans":"18 months","cat":"Provence","exp":"Bandol's rules require at least eighteen months in wood, traditionally large old foudres, which is part of why the reds taste reductive and tannic on release.","id":"r-m147lwcw","q":"How long must a red Bandol spend in wood before it can be sold?","sa":1},{"accept":["Palette","palette","AOC Palette"],"ans":"Palette","cat":"Provence","exp":"Palette, an appellation since 1948 on a few hillsides at Meyreuil, is led by Château Simone, which ages its red, white and rosé for years in old wood.","id":"r-avh9l46r","q":"Which small appellation just east of Aix-en-Provence is the home of Château Simone?","sa":1}],"sheet":"france","slug":"provence","summary":"Provence is the rosé region of France: about nine bottles in ten are pale, dry and pink, blended from Grenache, Cinsault and Syrah with Mourvèdre and the local Tibouren. Côtes de Provence is the large appellation, Coteaux d'Aix-en-Provence and Coteaux Varois en Provence the two other broad ones, and a ring of small historic appellations holds the character: Bandol for Mourvèdre reds that age, Cassis for white, Bellet above Nice, Palette near Aix and Les Baux-de-Provence in the Alpilles. The climate is Mediterranean, the mistral dries the western half and limestone gives way to schist and granite in the east. A server holds Bandol equals Mourvèdre, the rosé grapes and the 1955 crus classés."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Lafite Rothschild, Latour and Mouton Rothschild (Pauillac), Margaux (Margaux), Haut-Brion (Pessac-Léognan, Graves). Mouton promoted in 1973.","f":"The 1855 First Growths and their communes"},{"b":"Left: gravel, Cabernet Sauvignon, Médoc and Graves. Right: clay and limestone, Merlot with Cabernet Franc, Saint-Émilion and Pomerol.","f":"Left Bank against Right Bank"},{"b":"Saint-Estèphe, Pauillac, Saint-Julien, Margaux, with Listrac and Moulis inland; all inside Haut-Médoc.","f":"The Médoc communes, north to south"},{"b":"1855 (Médoc and Sauternes; changed twice: Cantemerle added 1856, Mouton promoted 1973); Graves 1953 and 1959 (one tier); Saint-Émilion (1955, revised about every ten years, last 2022); Cru Bourgeois (every five years); Pomerol none.","f":"The classifications of Bordeaux"},{"b":"The cold Ciron meets the warmer Garonne, autumn mists bring botrytis to thin-skinned Sémillon; picked in several tries; at least 45 grams of sugar.","f":"Sauternes: how the sweetness happens"},{"b":"Merlot (most planted), Cabernet Sauvignon, Cabernet Franc, Petit Verdot, Malbec, Carmenère.","f":"The red grapes of Bordeaux"},{"b":"Sauvignon Blanc, Sémillon, Muscadelle, with Sauvignon Gris and a few others; dry at Pessac-Léognan and Entre-Deux-Mers, sweet at Sauternes and Barsac.","f":"The white grapes of Bordeaux"},{"b":"Premier Grand Cru Classé A: Figeac and Pavie. Ausone, Cheval Blanc and Angélus withdrew before the revision.","f":"Saint-Émilion 2022: the top rank"},{"b":"Three tiers since 2020: Cru Bourgeois, Cru Bourgeois Supérieur and Cru Bourgeois Exceptionnel. The list is revised every five years, most recently in February 2025, so an estate can join or leave it.","f":"Cru Bourgeois du Médoc: the tiers and how often it changes"},{"b":"Allowed by the INAO in 2021 for Bordeaux and Bordeaux Supérieur: Arinarnoa, Castets, Marselan, Touriga Nacional, Alvarinho, Liliorila; five per cent of the vineyard, ten per cent of a blend.","f":"Bordeaux's six trial grapes"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Merlot","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Franc","role":"blending"},{"name":"Petit Verdot","role":"minor"},{"name":"Malbec","role":"minor"},{"name":"Carmenère","role":"minor"},{"name":"Sémillon","role":"lead"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"lead"},{"name":"Muscadelle","role":"minor"}],"index":7,"name":"Bordeaux","questions":[{"a":2,"cat":"Bordeaux","exp":"Haut-Brion in Pessac-Léognan was the only non-Médoc red classified in 1855. Latour and Lafite are Pauillac, Margaux is Margaux.","id":"r-1yep51p7","opts":["Lafite Rothschild","Margaux","Haut-Brion","Latour"],"q":"Which 1855 First Growth lies outside the Médoc, in the Graves?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Bordeaux","exp":"Mouton Rothschild rose from Second to First Growth in 1973, the only promotion the 1855 list has known. Léoville Las Cases remains a Second, Lynch-Bages a Fifth and Palmer a Third.","id":"r-wiw8ypia","opts":["Château Mouton Rothschild","Château Lynch-Bages","Château Léoville Las Cases","Château Palmer"],"q":"Which château was promoted to First Growth in 1973?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Bordeaux","exp":"Pomerol never classified, so Pétrus and Le Pin hold no rank. Saint-Émilion has its own revisable list, and Pauillac and Sauternes estates were ranked in 1855.","id":"r-3woyqvf6","opts":["Pauillac","Pomerol","Saint-Émilion","Sauternes"],"q":"Which Bordeaux appellation has no official classification of its estates?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Bordeaux","exp":"Merlot covers more of Bordeaux than any other grape, leading the Right Bank and the regional appellations; Cabernet Sauvignon leads only on the Left Bank gravel.","id":"r-ah322j2l","opts":["Merlot","Cabernet Franc","Cabernet Sauvignon","Petit Verdot"],"q":"Which grape is the most widely planted in the whole Bordeaux region?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Bordeaux","exp":"Entre-deux-Mers gained red-wine recognition for the 2023 vintage. Its established dry whites remain important, but teaching the appellation as white-only is now outdated. Distinguish the appellation from the wider geographical area.","id":"r-67o947d9","opts":["Graves","Pessac-Léognan","Bordeaux Supérieur","Entre-Deux-Mers"],"q":"Which Bordeaux appellation added red wine to its formerly white-only rules in 2023?"},{"accept":["Ciron","the Ciron","ciron","Le Ciron"],"ans":"Ciron","cat":"Bordeaux","exp":"The spring-fed Ciron runs cold under the forest into the warmer Garonne; the autumn mists where they meet bring botrytis to the Sémillon of Sauternes and Barsac.","id":"r-qc6jj56f","q":"Which cold tributary meets the Garonne in the Sauternes district and brings the mists that breed noble rot?","sa":1},{"accept":["Château d'Yquem","Chateau d'Yquem","Yquem","d'Yquem","Chateau dYquem","Château Yquem"],"ans":"Château d'Yquem","cat":"Bordeaux","exp":"Yquem was placed alone above the Premiers Crus of Sauternes and Barsac in 1855 and has never shared the rank.","id":"r-0jcyrd8j","q":"Which estate alone holds the rank of Premier Cru Supérieur in the 1855 classification of Sauternes?","sa":1},{"accept":["Figeac and Pavie","Pavie and Figeac","Château Figeac and Château Pavie","Chateau Figeac and Chateau Pavie","Figeac, Pavie","Pavie, Figeac","Figeac Pavie","Pavie Figeac"],"ans":"Figeac and Pavie","cat":"Bordeaux","exp":"The 2022 revision named Figeac, promoted, and Pavie as the two A estates after Ausone, Cheval Blanc and Angélus withdrew from the process.","id":"r-vdz3lwda","q":"Which two châteaux hold Premier Grand Cru Classé A in the 2022 Saint-Émilion classification?","sa":1}],"sheet":"france","slug":"bordeaux","summary":"Bordeaux is the maritime blending region of south-western France, split by the Gironde estuary into a Left Bank of gravel where Cabernet Sauvignon leads and a Right Bank of clay and limestone where Merlot leads with Cabernet Franc. The Médoc communes run north from Margaux through Saint-Julien and Pauillac to Saint-Estèphe; Pessac-Léognan holds the gravel south of the city; Saint-Émilion and Pomerol face them across the water. Whites blend Sauvignon Blanc and Sémillon, dry at Pessac-Léognan and Entre-Deux-Mers, sweet at Sauternes, where the Ciron's mists bring noble rot. The 1855 classification froze the Médoc's five growths, Saint-Émilion reclassifies about every decade and Pomerol has no list at all."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"A sweet wine whose fermentation is stopped by adding neutral grape spirit (mutage), keeping the grape's own sugar; 15 to 18 per cent alcohol; Grenache or Muscat.","f":"Vin doux naturel, defined"},{"b":"Banyuls and Banyuls Grand Cru (sea terraces), Maury (upper Agly schist), Rivesaltes (the broad one), Muscat de Rivesaltes.","f":"The Roussillon vins doux naturels"},{"b":"The same terraced vineyard at the Spanish border: fortified and sweet as Banyuls, dry red, rosé or white as Collioure.","f":"Banyuls against Collioure"},{"b":"Traditional method sparkling from at least 90 per cent Mauzac in the upper Aude; the abbey of Saint-Hilaire claims 1531. Crémant de Limoux leads with Chardonnay and Chenin.","f":"Blanquette de Limoux"},{"b":"Piquepoul alone from the Bassin de Thau lagoon: lemon, salt, high acid; the oyster wine of Bouzigues; an appellation since 2013.","f":"Picpoul de Pinet"},{"b":"The oldest red appellation of the Languedoc, 1948, Carignan-based, in two zones: the coast at Fitou and Leucate, and the high Corbières.","f":"Fitou, the oldest red"},{"b":"Cooler hills north of Montpellier; its own appellation since 2017; red and rosé only; Syrah at least half.","f":"Pic Saint-Loup, the rule"},{"b":"Caramany, Latour-de-France, Lesquerde, Tautavel and Les Aspres (2017); the Villages appellation is red only.","f":"The named communes of Côtes du Roussillon Villages"},{"b":"Pic Saint-Loup, Terrasses du Larzac, La Clape, Picpoul de Pinet, Faugères, Saint-Chinian, Minervois, Corbières, Fitou, Cabardès, Malepère, Limoux, Montpeyroux (approved 2025).","f":"The Languedoc crus with their own appellation"},{"b":"La Livinière and Boutenac stand under their own names, the EU approving the changes in March 2024; Montpeyroux approved by the INAO in June 2025, red only, from the 2026 vintage.","f":"Recent changes in the Languedoc ladder"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Grenache","role":"lead"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"lead"},{"name":"Mourvèdre","role":"blending"},{"name":"Carignan","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cinsault","role":"blending"},{"name":"Lledoner Pelut","role":"minor"},{"name":"Mauzac","role":"minor"},{"name":"Piquepoul","role":"minor"},{"name":"Grenache Blanc","role":"blending"},{"name":"Muscat Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"rising"},{"name":"Chenin Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"rising"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"minor"}],"index":8,"name":"Languedoc-Roussillon","questions":[{"a":3,"cat":"Languedoc-Roussillon","exp":"Picpoul de Pinet, on the shore of the Bassin de Thau, is Piquepoul Blanc alone, the high-acid lip-stinger. Bourboulenc leads La Clape white, Clairette has its own Languedoc appellation and Mauzac is Limoux's grape.","id":"r-mi85b8iy","opts":["Clairette","Bourboulenc","Mauzac","Piquepoul"],"q":"Which white grape alone makes the appellation wine grown on the shore of the Bassin de Thau?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Languedoc-Roussillon","exp":"Fitou was granted its appellation in 1948, decades before Faugères (1982) and Corbières and Minervois (1985), and lies in two zones within Corbières country.","id":"r-8rm2td10","opts":["Corbières","Fitou","Faugères","Minervois"],"q":"Which is the oldest red wine appellation of the Languedoc, dating from 1948?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Languedoc-Roussillon","exp":"Blanquette de Limoux is built on Mauzac, at least nine tenths; Chardonnay and Chenin Blanc lead the Crémant de Limoux instead, and Clairette has no part in either.","id":"r-1ii11zsy","opts":["Clairette","Chenin Blanc","Mauzac","Chardonnay"],"q":"Which grape must make up at least 90 per cent of Blanquette de Limoux?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Languedoc-Roussillon","exp":"Mutage is the addition of neutral spirit to the fermenting must, which kills the yeast and keeps the sugar. Saignée draws off rosé juice, remuage riddles sparkling wine and débourbage settles juice.","id":"r-rfzd26u0","opts":["Mutage","Saignée","Débourbage","Remuage"],"q":"What is the French term for stopping the fermentation of a vin doux naturel by adding grape spirit?"},{"accept":["Banyuls","banyuls","AOC Banyuls"],"ans":"Banyuls","cat":"Languedoc-Roussillon","exp":"Banyuls is the Grenache vin doux naturel of the schist terraces at the border; the same vines, vinified dry, are sold as Collioure.","id":"r-75p12ktw","q":"Which Roussillon vin doux naturel comes from terraces falling to the sea at the Spanish border, with Collioure as its dry counterpart?","sa":1},{"accept":["La Livinière","La Liviniere","Liviniere","Minervois-La Livinière","Minervois La Liviniere","Livinière"],"ans":"La Livinière","cat":"Languedoc-Roussillon","exp":"Minervois-La Livinière was the first village-level cru of the Languedoc in 1999, and in March 2024 the European Union approved its change of name to La Livinière alone.","id":"r-1zqxr3db","q":"Which village inside Minervois became the first cru of the Languedoc in 1999?","sa":1},{"accept":["Pays d'Oc","Pays d Oc","IGP Pays d'Oc","Pays dOc","Vin de Pays d'Oc"],"ans":"Pays d'Oc","cat":"Languedoc-Roussillon","exp":"IGP Pays d'Oc, the former Vin de Pays d'Oc of 1987, is the largest IGP in France and the label on most of the region's varietal Merlot, Cabernet, Syrah, Chardonnay and Sauvignon.","id":"r-p9ix0dbm","q":"Under which regional IGP is most of the varietal wine of the Languedoc-Roussillon sold?","sa":1},{"accept":["Terrasses du Larzac","terrasses du larzac","Larzac","Les Terrasses du Larzac"],"ans":"Terrasses du Larzac","cat":"Languedoc-Roussillon","exp":"Terrasses du Larzac gained its own appellation in 2014 for red wine alone, Grenache, Syrah, Mourvèdre and Carignan from the cool foothills south of the Cévennes.","id":"r-xfwomchy","q":"Which red-only Languedoc appellation was created in 2014 on the limestone foothills below the causse?","sa":1}],"sheet":"france","slug":"languedoc-roussillon","summary":"The Languedoc-Roussillon is the Mediterranean arc from the Rhône delta to the Spanish border, the largest vineyard in France and for a century its bulk-wine tank. It now runs on two tracks: IGP Pays d'Oc varietal wines from the plain, and a rising ladder of appellations in the hills where Syrah, Grenache, Mourvèdre and old Carignan grow over garrigue, led by Pic Saint-Loup, Terrasses du Larzac, Faugères, Minervois, Corbières and Fitou. Limoux makes sparkling wine from Mauzac, Picpoul de Pinet the oyster white. Roussillon, under the Pyrenees, is Catalan in spirit and the home of the vins doux naturels: Banyuls, Maury and Rivesaltes, Grenache or Muscat stopped with spirit."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"All Nebbiolo; 38 months from 1 November after harvest, 18 in wood; Riserva 62 months. Minimum 13% alcohol.","f":"Barolo: grape, minimum ageing, Riserva"},{"b":"All Nebbiolo; 26 months, 9 in wood; Riserva 50 months. 12.5% minimum. North-east of Alba, lower and earlier than Barolo.","f":"Barbaresco: grape, minimum ageing, Riserva"},{"b":"Tortonian marl (La Morra, Barolo village): softer, perfumed, earlier. Serravallian sandstone and compact marl (Serralunga, Monforte, Castiglione Falletto): denser, more tannic, longer-lived.","f":"Tortonian versus Serravallian in Barolo"},{"b":"Spanna in Alto Piemonte (Gattinara, Ghemme), Chiavennasca in Valtellina, Picotendro in Carema and the Aosta valley.","f":"Nebbiolo's names outside the Langhe"},{"b":"Gavi: Cortese. Roero: Arneis. Asti and Moscato d'Asti: Moscato Bianco. Nizza: Barbera. Dogliani: Dolcetto. Brachetto d'Acqui: Brachetto.","f":"Grape behind Gavi, Roero's white, Asti, Nizza, Dogliani, Acqui"},{"b":"Both Moscato Bianco, both tank method. Moscato d'Asti is frizzante, sweet, near 5 to 5.5% alcohol; Asti is fully sparkling, usually sweet and stronger, with a dry Asti Secco allowed since 2017.","f":"Moscato d'Asti versus Asti"},{"b":"Menzioni Geografiche Aggiuntive, the official vineyard names a label may carry: Barbaresco from 2007 (66), Barolo from 2010 (181, including the eleven communes).","f":"MGA: meaning and dates"},{"b":"Eleven in all; the five that matter: La Morra, Barolo, Castiglione Falletto, Serralunga d'Alba, Monforte d'Alba. Also Novello, Verduno, Grinzane Cavour, Diano d'Alba, Roddi, Cherasco.","f":"Barolo's communes: how many, which matter"},{"b":"Piedmont's traditional method sparkling: Pinot Nero and Chardonnay, vintage only, at least 30 months on the lees, from hills above 250 metres.","f":"Alta Langa DOCG"},{"b":"None. Piedmont, with the Valle d'Aosta next door, is one of the two Italian regions with no IGT; its wines are DOCG, DOC or plain vino.","f":"IGT in Piedmont"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Nebbiolo","role":"lead"},{"name":"Barbera","role":"lead"},{"name":"Dolcetto","role":"lead"},{"name":"Muscat Blanc","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cortese","role":"minor"},{"name":"Arneis","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"minor"},{"name":"Brachetto","role":"minor"},{"name":"Grignolino","role":"minor"},{"name":"Freisa","role":"minor"},{"name":"Ruchè","role":"minor"},{"name":"Erbaluce","role":"minor"},{"name":"Timorasso","role":"rising"}],"index":0,"name":"Piedmont","questions":[{"a":3,"cat":"Piedmont","exp":"Barolo ages at least 38 months from 1 November after harvest, 18 in wood. Barbaresco takes 26, Barbaresco Riserva 50 and Barolo Riserva 62.","id":"r-er74kq74","opts":["62 months","50 months","26 months","38 months"],"q":"What is the minimum total ageing for Barolo before release?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Piedmont","exp":"Serralunga's eastern ridge is Serravallian sandstone and compact marl. La Morra is Tortonian and perfumed; Castiglione Falletto mixes the two; Grinzane Cavour is a minor commune.","id":"r-oyjvx60h","opts":["Serralunga d'Alba","Grinzane Cavour","Castiglione Falletto","La Morra"],"q":"Which Barolo commune sits on the harder Serravallian soils and makes the most tannic, longest-lived wines?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Piedmont","exp":"The Tanaro runs past Alba with the Langhe on its right bank and the sandy Roero on its left. The Po drains Piedmont eastward; the Adige and Ticino are elsewhere.","id":"r-zfkw4jxv","opts":["Ticino","Adige","Tanaro","Po"],"q":"Which river divides the Langhe from the Roero?"},{"accept":["Cortese","cortese","Cortese di Gavi","cortese di gavi"],"ans":"Cortese","cat":"Piedmont","exp":"Gavi is a town in south-east Piedmont; its dry white is all Cortese, DOCG since 1998 under the names Gavi or Cortese di Gavi.","id":"r-82r21zuw","q":"Name the grape of Gavi.","sa":1},{"accept":["Spanna","spanna"],"ans":"Spanna","cat":"Piedmont","exp":"In Alto Piemonte the grape is Spanna; in Valtellina it is Chiavennasca and in Carema Picotendro. Same vine, alpine editions.","id":"r-uo11gkul","q":"What is Nebbiolo called in Gattinara and Ghemme?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Piedmont","exp":"Alta Langa is bottle fermented, vintage only, with at least 30 months on the lees. Asti and Brachetto d'Acqui are sweet tank wines; Erbaluce di Caluso is a white DOCG that includes a sparkling style.","id":"r-jgsizxwr","opts":["Alta Langa","Brachetto d'Acqui","Asti","Erbaluce di Caluso"],"q":"Which Piedmont DOCG is a traditional method sparkling wine from Pinot Nero and Chardonnay?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Piedmont","exp":"Barolo mapped its Menzioni Geografiche Aggiuntive in 2010; Barbaresco did so in 2007. Roero became DOCG in 2004 and Nizza in 2014.","id":"r-7a090n11","opts":["2007","2010","2014","2004"],"q":"In which year did Barolo adopt its official list of vineyard names, the MGA?"},{"accept":["Arneis","arneis","Roero Arneis","roero arneis"],"ans":"Arneis","cat":"Piedmont","exp":"Roero DOCG covers both a Nebbiolo red and the white Roero Arneis, a grape all but lost by the 1970s and replanted on the sandy left bank of the Tanaro.","id":"r-37wvbjta","q":"Which white grape shares the Roero DOCG with Nebbiolo?","sa":1}],"sheet":"italy","slug":"piedmont","summary":"Piedmont sits in Italy's north-west corner, hemmed in by the Alps and the Apennines, with no coast and no IGT. Nebbiolo is the grape the exam cares about: it makes Barolo and Barbaresco in the Langhe hills around Alba, pale garnet wines with tar, rose, high acid and heavy tannin, each DOCG defined by its ageing minimums. Barbera is the everyday red, Dolcetto the early one, Moscato Bianco the sweet fizz of Asti, Cortese the white of Gavi, Arneis that of Roero. Autumn fog, the nebbia, names the grape; the Tanaro divides Langhe from Roero; the age of the marl divides perfume from power."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Chardonnay, Pinot Nero, Pinot Bianco (max 50%), Erbamat up to 10% since 2017. Traditional method, second fermentation in bottle. DOCG 1995, the first Italian DOCG reserved for bottle-fermented sparkling wine.","f":"Franciacorta: grapes and method"},{"b":"Non vintage 18 months; Satèn and Rosé 24; Millesimato 30; Riserva 60 (vintage dated).","f":"Franciacorta lees minimums by style"},{"b":"Franciacorta from white grapes only (Chardonnay, Pinot Bianco), bottled under 5 atmospheres for a softer mousse, always Brut, 24 months on lees.","f":"What is Satèn?"},{"b":"Nebbiolo (Chiavennasca) 90% minimum, 24 months' ageing with 12 in wood. West to east: Maroggia, Sassella, Grumello, Inferno, Valgella.","f":"Valtellina Superiore: grape and subzones"},{"b":"Dried-grape Nebbiolo fermented dry, 14% minimum, DOCG 2003; also spelt Sfursat. Lombardy's answer to Amarone.","f":"Sforzato di Valtellina"},{"b":"Turbiana (once Trebbiano di Lugana, genetically Verdicchio), on white clay at the south end of Lake Garda, mostly Lombardy, partly Veneto.","f":"Lugana: grape and place"},{"b":"Hills south of the Po near Pavia; Italy's largest Pinot Nero planting; Oltrepò Pavese Metodo Classico DOCG (70% Pinot Nero minimum); Bonarda here means Croatina.","f":"Oltrepò Pavese: what to know"},{"b":"Moscato di Scanzo, a sweet red passito from a hamlet near Bergamo, Lombardy, DOCG since 2009.","f":"Italy's smallest DOCG"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Blanc","role":"blending"},{"name":"Nebbiolo","role":"lead"},{"name":"Turbiana","role":"lead"},{"name":"Croatina","role":"minor"},{"name":"Groppello","role":"minor"},{"name":"Erbamat","role":"rising"},{"name":"Moscato di Scanzo","role":"minor"},{"name":"Lambrusco","role":"minor"},{"name":"Riesling","role":"minor"}],"index":1,"name":"Lombardy","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Lombardy","exp":"Franciacorta sits on the moraine left by the Iseo glacier, south of Lake Iseo near Brescia. Garda warms Lugana, Como sits below Valtellina, Maggiore is on the Piedmont border.","id":"r-xzjdl4td","opts":["Iseo","Garda","Maggiore","Como"],"q":"Which lake lies immediately north of the Franciacorta hills?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Lombardy","exp":"The basic Franciacorta needs 18 months on the lees; Satèn and Rosé need 24, Millesimato 30 and Riserva 60.","id":"r-37y6wmnu","opts":["12 months","18 months","24 months","30 months"],"q":"What is the minimum time on the lees for a non-vintage Franciacorta?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Lombardy","exp":"Chiavennasca is Valtellina's name for Nebbiolo. Spanna is the Alto Piemonte name, Picotendro the Carema and Aosta name, Prunent the Ossola valley's.","id":"r-zrxd10vq","opts":["Picotendro","Prunent","Spanna","Chiavennasca"],"q":"Under what name does Nebbiolo grow in Valtellina?"},{"accept":["Turbiana","turbiana","Trebbiano di Lugana","trebbiano di lugana","Verdicchio","verdicchio"],"ans":"Turbiana","cat":"Lombardy","exp":"Lugana is Turbiana, long sold as Trebbiano di Lugana and shown by DNA to be the same vine as Verdicchio, grown on white clay at Garda's south end.","id":"r-uf2jj6ug","q":"Which grape variety makes the white wine Lugana?","sa":1},{"accept":["Satèn","Saten","saten","Franciacorta Satèn","Franciacorta Saten"],"ans":"Satèn","cat":"Lombardy","exp":"Satèn is Chardonnay and Pinot Bianco bottled under five atmospheres, always Brut, with 24 months on the lees; the name is the consortium's own.","id":"r-sonah49e","q":"Name the Franciacorta style made only from white grapes at lower pressure.","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Lombardy","exp":"Sforzato, or Sfursat, dries Chiavennasca after picking and ferments it dry to at least 14%. Valtellina Superiore and Rosso di Valtellina are fresh-grape wines; Sassella is a Superiore subzone.","id":"r-ovmfqnj7","opts":["Valtellina Superiore Sassella","Valtellina Superiore","Sforzato di Valtellina","Rosso di Valtellina"],"q":"Which Valtellina wine is made from grapes dried after the harvest and fermented dry?"},{"accept":["Croatina","croatina"],"ans":"Croatina","cat":"Lombardy","exp":"In Oltrepò Pavese the wine called Bonarda is made from Croatina, soft, dark and often frizzante; the name Bonarda means other grapes in Piedmont and Argentina.","id":"r-av69pprr","q":"What grape is sold as Bonarda in the Oltrepò Pavese?","sa":1}],"sheet":"italy","slug":"lombardy","summary":"Lombardy is Milan's hinterland, and its wine is sorted by altitude and water rather than by one great grape. Franciacorta, on glacial moraine south of Lake Iseo, is the country's benchmark traditional method sparkling wine, Chardonnay with Pinot Nero and Pinot Bianco. Valtellina terraces Nebbiolo, called Chiavennasca here, on the sunny north bank of the Adda in an alpine valley, and dries it for Sforzato. Lugana makes a fresh white from Turbiana on the south shore of Garda, and Oltrepò Pavese, across the Po, holds the largest planting of Pinot Nero in Italy. Lakes, not sea, do the moderating."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Corvina 45 to 95% (Corvinone may replace up to half of it), Rondinella 5 to 30%, balance from other local reds such as Molinara and Oseleta.","f":"Valpolicella blend rule"},{"b":"Valpolicella: fresh. Ripasso: re-fermented on Amarone skins. Amarone: dried grapes fermented dry, 14% minimum (usually 15 plus). Recioto: dried grapes, fermentation stopped, sweet.","f":"Four wines, one blend, four methods"},{"b":"DOCG 2010; appassimento; pressed no earlier than 1 December; 2 years' ageing (Riserva 4); Classico from Negrar, Fumane, Marano, Sant'Ambrogio, San Pietro in Cariano.","f":"Amarone della Valpolicella: law in brief"},{"b":"Prosecco DOC (9 provinces, Veneto and Friuli); Conegliano Valdobbiadene Prosecco Superiore DOCG and Asolo Prosecco DOCG; Rive (43, vintage, hand picked); Superiore di Cartizze (one hill, 107 ha).","f":"The Prosecco pyramid"},{"b":"Glera, 85% minimum; Martinotti tank method; Brut Nature, Extra Brut, Brut (under 12 g/l), Extra Dry (12 to 17), Dry (17 to 32), Demi-Sec (32 to 50).","f":"Prosecco: grape, method, sweetness"},{"b":"Garganega 70% minimum, Trebbiano di Soave and Chardonnay allowed. Soave DOC; Classico (Soave and Monteforte hills); Colli Scaligeri; Soave Superiore DOCG; Recioto di Soave DOCG (1998, Veneto's first).","f":"Soave: grape and zones"},{"b":"Created 2020: Glera with 10 to 15% Pinot Nero, vintage dated, at least 60 days' second fermentation in tank, Brut Nature to Extra Dry only.","f":"Prosecco DOC Rosé"},{"b":"Bardolino (Corvina-led light red, Bardolino Superiore DOCG), Chiaretto di Bardolino rosé, Custoza white blend; Lugana is mostly Lombardy.","f":"Garda's Veneto shore"},{"b":"A sweet wine from dried grapes; from recia, Veronese dialect for ear, the ripest outer wings of the bunch once chosen for drying.","f":"Recioto: the word"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Glera","role":"lead"},{"name":"Garganega","role":"lead"},{"name":"Corvina","role":"lead"},{"name":"Rondinella","role":"blending"},{"name":"Molinara","role":"minor"},{"name":"Oseleta","role":"minor"},{"name":"Trebbiano di Soave","role":"blending"},{"name":"Pinot Gris / Grigio","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"blending"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"blending"},{"name":"Durella","role":"minor"},{"name":"Raboso","role":"minor"},{"name":"Moscato Giallo","role":"minor"},{"name":"Tai","role":"minor"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"minor"}],"index":2,"name":"Veneto","questions":[{"a":3,"cat":"Veneto","exp":"Corvina is 45 to 95% of Valpolicella, Rondinella 5 to 30%; Molinara is now optional and Oseleta a minor addition for colour and tannin.","id":"r-zqxodjvi","opts":["Oseleta","Molinara","Rondinella","Corvina"],"q":"Which grape leads the Valpolicella blend?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Veneto","exp":"Ripasso passes finished Valpolicella over Amarone or Recioto pomace. Superiore is a year-aged still wine, Recioto the sweet dried-grape wine, Classico the historic zone.","id":"r-mxf4g0nn","opts":["Valpolicella Ripasso","Recioto della Valpolicella","Valpolicella Superiore","Valpolicella Classico"],"q":"Which wine is Valpolicella re-fermented on the skins left from Amarone?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Veneto","exp":"Extra Dry is 12 to 17 g/l, sweeter than Brut (under 12) and Brut Nature (under 3); Dry runs 17 to 32 and Demi-Sec 32 to 50. The scale reads the opposite way from what the words suggest.","id":"r-hcpqy2kf","opts":["Extra Dry","Demi-Sec","Dry","Brut Nature"],"q":"Which Prosecco sweetness level holds 12 to 17 grams of sugar per litre?"},{"accept":["Garganega","garganega"],"ans":"Garganega","cat":"Veneto","exp":"Soave is at least 70% Garganega, with Trebbiano di Soave and Chardonnay permitted for the balance; Classico comes from the volcanic and limestone hills of Soave and Monteforte.","id":"r-kagl4ybg","q":"Name the grape of Soave.","sa":1},{"accept":["Cartizze","cartizze","Superiore di Cartizze","Valdobbiadene Superiore di Cartizze"],"ans":"Cartizze","cat":"Veneto","exp":"Superiore di Cartizze is one hill of 107 hectares between San Pietro di Barbozza, Santo Stefano and Saccol, the most prized site of Conegliano Valdobbiadene.","id":"r-zuwhsrua","q":"What is the name of the single hill at the top of the Prosecco pyramid in Valdobbiadene?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Veneto","exp":"Prosecco must be at least 85% Glera; the balance may come from Verdiso, Bianchetta, Perera, Glera Lunga, Chardonnay, Pinot Bianco, Pinot Grigio or Pinot Nero. Soave sets a 70% floor for Garganega, a figure often confused with it.","id":"r-cs0wd91v","opts":["70%","85%","75%","90%"],"q":"What is the minimum share of Glera in a wine labelled Prosecco?"},{"accept":["Recioto della Valpolicella","Recioto","recioto","recioto della valpolicella"],"ans":"Recioto della Valpolicella","cat":"Veneto","exp":"Recioto is the sweet ancestor of Amarone: the same appassimento grapes, fermentation halted with sugar left. Amarone is the same must taken to dryness.","id":"r-02cm81ao","q":"Which Valpolicella wine is sweet, made from dried grapes with the fermentation stopped?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Veneto","exp":"Bardolino lies on Garda's eastern shore and Custoza on the moraine just south of it. Iseo warms Franciacorta and Como lies below Valtellina, both in Lombardy.","id":"r-3hibjpqd","opts":["Maggiore","Iseo","Garda","Como"],"q":"Which lake moderates the Bardolino and Custoza vineyards?"}],"sheet":"italy","slug":"veneto","summary":"Veneto, from the Alps and Lake Garda to Venice, is usually Italy's largest wine region by volume and the home of three wines every server pours: Prosecco, Soave and Valpolicella. Prosecco is Glera made sparkling in tank, with the Conegliano Valdobbiadene and Asolo hills as DOCG above a wide DOC. Soave is Garganega from volcanic and limestone hills east of Verona. Valpolicella is Corvina-led red north of Verona, which dried grapes turn into Amarone (dry) and Recioto (sweet), with Ripasso in between. Garda cools the west, the Adriatic the east, and the plain in the middle grows the volume."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Tocai Friulano until 2007, when the EU reserved Tokaj for Hungary; the vine is Sauvignonasse (Sauvignon Vert); Tai in Veneto (Lison DOCG).","f":"Friulano: old name, the vine, the Veneto name"},{"b":"Collio: province of Gorizia, DOC 1968, whites and Collio Bianco. Friuli Colli Orientali: province of Udine, DOC 1970, whites plus native reds and sweet wines. Both on ponca.","f":"Collio versus Friuli Colli Orientali"},{"b":"Friuli's flysch: alternating marl and sandstone from an Eocene sea bed, crumbly and free draining; opoka across the Slovenian border. The soil of Collio and the Colli Orientali.","f":"Ponca: what it is"},{"b":"Ramandolo (sweet Verduzzo, 2001); Colli Orientali del Friuli Picolit (2006); Rosazzo (Friulano-led white blend, 2011); Lison (Tai, shared with Veneto).","f":"Friuli's four DOCGs"},{"b":"Oslavia, a hamlet of Collio above Gorizia; Ribolla Gialla fermented on its skins for weeks or months, often in buried Georgian amphorae, from the late 1990s.","f":"Orange wine's Friulian home"},{"b":"Refosco dal Peduncolo Rosso (red stem, high acid); Schioppettino (Prepotto, peppery, also Ribolla Nera); Pignolo (Rosazzo, tannic); Tazzelenghe (tongue-cutter, acid); Terrano (Carso).","f":"Native reds of Friuli"},{"b":"Friuli Grave (largest, gravel of the Tagliamento), Friuli Isonzo (gravel terraces, fuller whites), Aquileia, Latisana, Annia (lagoon side); Friuli DOC (2016) covers the region.","f":"The plain DOCs of Friuli"},{"b":"Limestone karst above Trieste, red earth, the Bora wind; Vitovska and Malvasia whites, Terrano red; the village of Prosecco lies on its edge.","f":"Carso: where and what"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Friulano","role":"lead"},{"name":"Ribolla Gialla","role":"rising"},{"name":"Pinot Gris / Grigio","role":"lead"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Blanc","role":"blending"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"blending"},{"name":"Malvasia Istriana","role":"minor"},{"name":"Verduzzo","role":"minor"},{"name":"Picolit","role":"minor"},{"name":"Refosco dal Peduncolo Rosso","role":"minor"},{"name":"Schioppettino","role":"rising"},{"name":"Pignolo","role":"minor"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Franc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Vitovska","role":"minor"},{"name":"Terrano","role":"minor"},{"name":"Glera","role":"minor"}],"index":3,"name":"Friuli","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Friuli","exp":"Veneto adopted Tai when the EU withdrew Tocai from 2007; Lison DOCG is its showcase. Turbiana is Lugana's grape, Trebbiano di Soave is Verdicchio and Garganega is Soave's.","id":"r-ls1a2rd8","opts":["Tai","Trebbiano di Soave","Turbiana","Garganega"],"q":"Under what name does Veneto label the grape Friuli calls Friulano?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Friuli","exp":"Collio, in full Collio Goriziano, is the hill country of Gorizia province on the Slovenian border. The Colli Orientali are in Udine, Grave largely in Pordenone, Carso in Trieste.","id":"r-101yioif","opts":["Trieste","Udine","Pordenone","Gorizia"],"q":"In which province do the Collio hills lie?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Friuli","exp":"Ramandolo, DOCG since 2001, is late-picked and partly dried Verduzzo from the foothills near Nimis. Rosazzo is a dry white blend, Lison is Tai, Picolit's DOCG is its own grape.","id":"r-zwbc1qza","opts":["Lison","Ramandolo","Colli Orientali del Friuli Picolit","Rosazzo"],"q":"Which Friulian DOCG is a sweet wine from Verduzzo?"},{"accept":["Ponca","ponca","opoka"],"ans":"Ponca","cat":"Friuli","exp":"Ponca is the Friulian word for the Eocene flysch of alternating marl and sandstone that crumbles under the hill vineyards; Slovenian growers in Brda call it opoka.","id":"r-u25l4q74","q":"What is the local name of the marl and sandstone soil of Collio and the Colli Orientali?","sa":1},{"accept":["Oslavia","oslavia","Oslavje"],"ans":"Oslavia","cat":"Friuli","exp":"Oslavia's growers took Ribolla Gialla back to long skin maceration and buried amphorae from the late 1990s, and the term orange wine was coined for the result.","id":"r-jpxcg1qr","q":"Name the Collio hamlet above Gorizia where skin-fermented Ribolla Gialla became orange wine.","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Friuli","exp":"Friuli Grave, named for its gravel like Bordeaux's Graves, makes the largest share of the region's wine. Isonzo is the gravel terrace south of Gorizia; Aquileia and Latisana lie towards the lagoons.","id":"r-ted2i45n","opts":["Friuli Isonzo","Friuli Aquileia","Friuli Grave","Friuli Latisana"],"q":"Which Friuli DOC makes the largest share of the region's wine?"},{"accept":["Sauvignonasse","sauvignonasse","Sauvignon Vert","sauvignon vert"],"ans":"Sauvignonasse","cat":"Friuli","exp":"Friulano is Sauvignonasse, also called Sauvignon Vert, the vine long confused with Sauvignon Blanc in Chile; it has no relation to Hungary's Furmint.","id":"r-05cxppmt","q":"Which grape is Friulano by DNA?","sa":1}],"sheet":"italy","slug":"friuli","summary":"Friuli Venezia Giulia is the far north-eastern corner: Alps behind, Adriatic in front, Slovenia along the hills. It is Italy's white wine conscience, varietal, precise, dry whites from Friulano, Ribolla Gialla, Pinot Grigio, Sauvignon, Pinot Bianco and Malvasia Istriana, made in the cold-fermented, reductive style the region pioneered in Italy in the 1960s. The two hill DOCs, Collio and Friuli Colli Orientali, on the marl and sandstone called ponca, carry the reputation; the gravel plain of Grave and Isonzo carries the volume. The same hills started the modern orange wine movement, and the Colli Orientali keep Friuli's reds, Schioppettino, Refosco and Pignolo, and its sweet Picolit and Ramandolo."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Created 1993, the first Italian DOC for the traditional method only. Chardonnay, Pinot Nero, Pinot Bianco, Pinot Meunier. Lees: 15 months non-vintage, 24 vintage, 36 riserva. Sold as Trentodoc.","f":"Trento DOC: year, method and lees minimums"},{"b":"Schiava (Vernatsch): Santa Maddalena and Lago di Caldaro. Lagrein: Bolzano's Gries district. Teroldego: the Campo Rotaliano in Trentino.","f":"Trentino-Alto Adige's three native reds and their homes"},{"b":"Tramin, Termeno in Italian, in Alto Adige's Bassa Atesina south of Bolzano; Cantina Tramin and J. Hofstätter are its houses.","f":"Gewürztraminer: the village it is named for"},{"b":"Vernatsch is Schiava; Weissburgunder is Pinot Bianco; Grauburgunder or Ruländer is Pinot Grigio; Blauburgunder is Pinot Nero; Goldmuskateller is Moscato Giallo.","f":"German label words for Alto Adige grapes"},{"b":"Two faces of Lagrein: Kretzer is the rosé from a short maceration, Dunkel the dark, fully macerated red.","f":"Kretzer and Dunkel: what each means"},{"b":"A flat gravel and sand plain where the Noce meets the Adige north of Trento, between Mezzolombardo and Mezzocorona; Teroldego Rotaliano DOC.","f":"Campo Rotaliano: what and where"},{"b":"Valle Isarco, Val Venosta, Terlano, Santa Maddalena, Colli di Bolzano, Meranese; Lago di Caldaro is its own DOC, shared with Trentino.","f":"Alto Adige DOC: name the sub-zones"},{"b":"Quartz porphyry, a reddish volcanic rock, credited with the long life of Terlano's Pinot Bianco.","f":"The soil of Terlano and Bolzano"},{"b":"Nosiola from the Valle dei Laghi, dried on racks until Holy Week, fermented slowly, aged for years in small casks. Not the Tuscan Vin Santo.","f":"Vino Santo Trentino: grape, place, method"},{"b":"In 2024 the ministry recognised 86 named sites (Unità Geografiche Aggiuntive) with yields cut by a quarter and up to five grapes each, all grown inside the site, from the 2024 vintage.","f":"Alto Adige UGAs: what changed in 2024"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Pinot Gris / Grigio","role":"lead"},{"name":"Gewürztraminer","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Blanc","role":"blending"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"blending"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"blending"},{"name":"Schiava","role":"lead"},{"name":"Lagrein","role":"minor"},{"name":"Teroldego","role":"minor"},{"name":"Müller-Thurgau","role":"minor"},{"name":"Kerner","role":"minor"},{"name":"Marzemino","role":"minor"},{"name":"Nosiola","role":"minor"}],"index":4,"name":"Trentino-Alto Adige","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Trentino-Alto Adige","exp":"Vernatsch is the German name for Schiava, the pale red of Santa Maddalena and Lago di Caldaro. Lagrein keeps one name in both languages; Teroldego and Marzemino are Trentino's grapes.","id":"r-h8tsccks","opts":["Schiava","Lagrein","Marzemino","Teroldego"],"q":"Which grape appears as Vernatsch on a German-language label from Alto Adige?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Trentino-Alto Adige","exp":"Trento DOC was created in 1993 for bottle-fermented wine only, before Franciacorta reached DOCG in 1995. Prosecco is tank method; Alta Langa is Piedmont's later traditional method DOCG.","id":"r-1rzmmalj","opts":["Prosecco DOC","Franciacorta DOCG","Trento DOC","Alta Langa DOCG"],"q":"Which sparkling appellation was the first in Italy written solely for the traditional method?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Trentino-Alto Adige","exp":"Teroldego Rotaliano comes from the flat gravel plain where the Noce meets the Adige north of Trento. The Valle Isarco grows Kerner and Sylvaner, the Val Venosta Riesling and the Valle dei Laghi Nosiola.","id":"r-u15xe4zu","opts":["The Valle dei Laghi","The Campo Rotaliano","The Valle Isarco","The Val Venosta"],"q":"In which zone does Teroldego make its best wine?"},{"accept":["15 months","fifteen months","15","fifteen"],"ans":"15 months","cat":"Trentino-Alto Adige","exp":"Trento DOC asks fifteen months on the lees for a non-vintage wine, twenty-four for a vintage wine and thirty-six for a riserva, which is why it reads richer than most tank wines.","id":"r-tm9u7o2y","q":"What is the minimum lees ageing for a non-vintage Trento DOC?","sa":1},{"accept":["Tramin","Termeno","Tramin (Termeno)","Termeno (Tramin)","Tramin an der Weinstrasse","Termeno sulla Strada del Vino"],"ans":"Tramin","cat":"Trentino-Alto Adige","exp":"Tramin, Termeno in Italian, sits in the Bassa Atesina south of Bolzano; the grape's name reads as Traminer from Tramin, and the village co-operative is one of its best producers.","id":"r-p84hs9ym","q":"Which village in Alto Adige gives Gewürztraminer its name?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Trentino-Alto Adige","exp":"Vino Santo Trentino dries Nosiola, the province's native white, in the Valle dei Laghi. Trebbiano and Malvasia make Tuscany's Vin Santo; Garganega makes Recioto di Soave.","id":"r-a5hj2ogs","opts":["Nosiola","Garganega","Malvasia","Trebbiano"],"q":"Which grape is dried for Vino Santo Trentino?"},{"accept":["Porphyry","Quartz porphyry","porphyry","porfido"],"ans":"Porphyry","cat":"Trentino-Alto Adige","exp":"Quartz porphyry, a reddish volcanic rock, underlies the Bolzano basin and Terlano; growers credit it with the minerality and long life of the Terlano whites.","id":"r-fv0bnlzm","q":"Which rock underlies the vineyards of Terlano and Bolzano?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Trentino-Alto Adige","exp":"Kretzer is Alto Adige's word for a rosé, drawn off Lagrein after a short maceration; the fully macerated red is called Dunkel, the German word for dark.","id":"r-g6qodysj","opts":["A dark red","A dried-grape sweet wine","A sparkling wine","A rosé"],"q":"What kind of wine is a Lagrein Kretzer?"}],"sheet":"italy","slug":"trentino-alto-adige","summary":"Trentino-Alto Adige is the Adige valley from the hinterland of Lake Garda to the Brenner pass, two provinces under one regional name. Alto Adige, the German-speaking north around Bolzano, makes Italy's most precise varietal whites from Pinot Grigio, Pinot Bianco, Gewürztraminer and Sauvignon, with Schiava and Lagrein as its native reds. Trentino, the Italian-speaking south around Trento, is the home of Trento DOC, the first Italian appellation written for traditional method sparkling wine alone, and of Teroldego on the gravel of the Campo Rotaliano. Altitude, a wide day to night swing and co-operative cellars shape both. An Alto Adige label may carry each grape and village in Italian, in German or in both."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Sangiovese 80 to 100 per cent, no white grapes. Annata; Riserva 24 months; Gran Selezione 30 months, estate fruit, 90 per cent Sangiovese from the 2027 vintage. Black rooster seal; its own DOCG since 1996.","f":"Chianti Classico: grape rule and tiers"},{"b":"100 per cent Sangiovese. At least two years in oak and four months in bottle, released from 1 January of the fifth year after harvest; Riserva the sixth year. DOC 1966, DOCG 1980.","f":"Brunello di Montalcino: grape, oak and release"},{"b":"Vino Nobile di Montepulciano: Sangiovese (Prugnolo Gentile, 70 per cent minimum) from a Tuscan town. Montepulciano d'Abruzzo: a grape grown in Abruzzo. No link between them.","f":"The two Montepulcianos"},{"b":"Cabernet planted at Bolgheri in 1944; first commercial vintage 1968. Bolgheri Sassicaia became a single estate DOC in 2013; at least 80 per cent Cabernet Sauvignon.","f":"Sassicaia: dates and status"},{"b":"Alberese is limestone; galestro is friable, layered material rich in clay and lime. Chianti Classico also has other formations. Neither term proves a fixed flavour or the best vineyard.","f":"Galestro and alberese: which is which"},{"b":"Rufina, Colli Senesi, Colli Fiorentini, Colli Aretini, Colline Pisane, Montalbano, Montespertoli, Terre di Vinci. June 2026 rules add Terre di Vinci; the Sangiovese minimum becomes 60 per cent from the 2026/27 harvest campaign.","f":"Chianti DOCG sub-zones: name the eight"},{"b":"Brunello at Montalcino, Prugnolo Gentile at Montepulciano, Morellino at Scansano, Sangioveto in old Chianti usage. One grape, four names.","f":"Sangiovese's Tuscan aliases"},{"b":"Grapes are dried, then fermented and matured in small wooden caratelli. White versions often use Trebbiano and Malvasia; Occhio di Pernice uses red grapes. Production rules and sweetness vary by appellation and producer.","f":"Vin Santo: how it is made"},{"b":"Sassicaia and Solaia: Cabernet-led. Tignanello: Sangiovese with Cabernet. Ornellaia: a Bolgheri blend. Masseto: Merlot-led, with Cabernet Franc in some vintages. Le Pergole Torte, Cepparello and Flaccianello: Sangiovese.","f":"The Super Tuscans and their grapes"},{"b":"Vernaccia di San Gimignano: DOC in 1966 among Italy's first, a DOCG since 1993, Vernaccia at least 85 per cent. Dry, nutty, faintly bitter.","f":"Tuscany's white DOCG"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Sangiovese","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"lead"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"blending"},{"name":"Cabernet Franc","role":"blending"},{"name":"Canaiolo","role":"blending"},{"name":"Colorino","role":"minor"},{"name":"Vernaccia","role":"minor"},{"name":"Trebbiano","role":"minor"},{"name":"Malvasia","role":"minor"},{"name":"Vermentino","role":"rising"},{"name":"Muscat Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"minor"}],"index":5,"name":"Tuscany","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Tuscany","exp":"Vino Nobile is Sangiovese, locally Prugnolo Gentile, at 70 per cent minimum from the Tuscan town of Montepulciano. The Montepulciano grape grows in Abruzzo; Canaiolo is a blending partner; Aglianico belongs to Campania.","id":"r-4hgq9k9o","opts":["Sangiovese","Montepulciano","Aglianico","Canaiolo"],"q":"Which grape makes Vino Nobile di Montepulciano?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Tuscany","exp":"Ordinary Chianti Classico requires at least 80 per cent Sangiovese; Gran Selezione rises to 90 from the 2027 vintage. Separate Chianti DOCG moves to 60 from 2026/27. Brunello is 100; Morellino's ordinary and Superiore minimum remains 85 under the 2026 rules.","id":"r-llhw0yl0","opts":["100 per cent","80 per cent","70 per cent","85 per cent"],"q":"What is the minimum share of Sangiovese in Chianti Classico?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Tuscany","exp":"Brunello leaves the cellar from 1 January of the fifth year after harvest, after at least two years in oak and four months in bottle; the Riserva waits for the sixth.","id":"r-4ypl41j4","opts":["The fourth year","The sixth year","The fifth year","The third year"],"q":"In which year after harvest may Brunello di Montalcino first be released?"},{"accept":["Tenuta San Guido","San Guido","Sassicaia","Tenuta San Guido (Sassicaia)","Bolgheri Sassicaia"],"ans":"Tenuta San Guido","cat":"Tuscany","exp":"Tenuta San Guido planted Cabernet at Bolgheri in 1944 and sold Sassicaia from the 1968 vintage; in 2013 Bolgheri Sassicaia, until then a sub-zone of Bolgheri DOC, became a DOC for that one estate.","id":"r-3ghmevrx","q":"Which Bolgheri estate was given a DOC of its own in 2013?","sa":1},{"accept":["Vernaccia di San Gimignano","Vernaccia","San Gimignano","Vernaccia San Gimignano"],"ans":"Vernaccia di San Gimignano","cat":"Tuscany","exp":"Vernaccia di San Gimignano, a DOC in 1966 among Italy's first, became Tuscany's only white DOCG in 1993; the region's other ten DOCGs are red, with Elba Aleatico Passito a sweet red among them.","id":"r-hszl6h7c","q":"Which Tuscan white wine has held DOCG status since 1993?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Tuscany","exp":"Alberese is the limestone; galestro is the crumbly schistous marl beside it; macigno is the sandstone of Rufina and tufo the volcanic rock around Pitigliano.","id":"r-pvlwdixf","opts":["Galestro","Tufo","Macigno","Alberese"],"q":"Which soil word names Chianti Classico's hard, pale limestone?"},{"accept":["Chianti Classico","Chianti Classico DOCG"],"ans":"Chianti Classico","cat":"Tuscany","exp":"The Gallo Nero is the seal of the Chianti Classico consortium, founded in 1924, and it marks a Chianti Classico DOCG wine, never a plain Chianti.","id":"r-cz8pnaea","q":"Which wine does the black rooster on a neck label mark?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Tuscany","exp":"Merlot leads Masseto. Some vintages include Cabernet Franc, so the wine must not always be described as pure Merlot. Consult the estate's vintage sheet. Its informal Super Tuscan reputation does not replace its Toscana IGT classification.","id":"r-7l28s0ma","opts":["Merlot","Sangiovese","Cabernet Sauvignon","Cabernet Franc"],"q":"Which grape variety leads Masseto?"}],"sheet":"italy","slug":"tuscany","summary":"Tuscany connects inland Sangiovese hills with a varied Tyrrhenian coast. Chianti Classico lies between Florence and Siena, Brunello comes from Montalcino, Vino Nobile from Montepulciano and Morellino from Scansano. Elevation, exposure, soils and vintage conditions vary within each area. Bolgheri is particularly associated with Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot and Cabernet Franc; San Gimignano with Vernaccia. Super Tuscan is an informal description, not a legal category. Learn the exact appellation and vintage before applying grape or ageing rules, especially after the Chianti and Morellino changes of 2026."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"The dry wine: 37 months in all before release, at least 12 in wood and 4 in bottle. It is 100 per cent Sagrantino, DOC 1979 and DOCG 1992.","f":"Montefalco Sagrantino: the ageing rule"},{"b":"Montefalco Sagrantino (1992) and Torgiano Rosso Riserva (1990). Both red; Orvieto, the famous white, is a DOC.","f":"Umbria's two DOCGs"},{"b":"Grechetto and Procanico, the local name for Trebbiano Toscano, at least 60 per cent together, with other permitted whites for the rest. Grechetto gives the nut and bitter twist, Procanico the crisp volume.","f":"Orvieto: which grapes, and in what share?"},{"b":"A Sangiovese led DOCG (1990) near Perugia with at least three years of ageing, built by Lungarotti; Rubesco Riserva Vigna Monticchio is the reference bottle.","f":"Torgiano Rosso Riserva: what it is"},{"b":"Rosso: DOC, Sangiovese led with a compulsory Sagrantino share, 18 months of ageing. Sagrantino: DOCG, pure Sagrantino, 37 months.","f":"How does Montefalco Rosso differ from Montefalco Sagrantino?"},{"b":"It carries the highest tannin and polyphenol content measured in an Italian variety; thick skins, small bunches, late ripening, and a history as a sweet passito for the church.","f":"Why is Sagrantino famous among grapes?"},{"b":"A distinct Trebbiano from Spoleto and Montefalco, late ripening and high in acid, once trained up trees; Spoleto DOC since 2011, citrus and salt, sometimes skin fermented.","f":"What is Trebbiano Spoletino?"},{"b":"Landlocked central Italy with no foreign border; Tuscany to the west, the Apennines and Marche to the east, Lazio to the south. The Tiber runs through it; Lake Trasimeno lies in the west.","f":"Umbria on the map"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Sagrantino","role":"lead"},{"name":"Sangiovese","role":"lead"},{"name":"Grechetto","role":"lead"},{"name":"Trebbiano Toscano","role":"blending"},{"name":"Trebbiano Spoletino","role":"rising"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"blending"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"}],"index":6,"name":"Umbria","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Umbria","exp":"Montefalco Rosso is Sangiovese led with a compulsory Sagrantino share of at least 10 per cent; Sagrantino alone makes the DOCG. Merlot is a permitted minor partner and Montepulciano is Abruzzo's grape.","id":"r-mxn65oeq","opts":["Sangiovese","Montepulciano","Merlot","Sagrantino"],"q":"Which grape leads the blend of Montefalco Rosso?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Umbria","exp":"The rules ask 37 months in all, with at least 12 in wood and 4 in bottle. Eighteen months is Montefalco Rosso, 30 its Riserva, and 24 is Chianti Classico Riserva.","id":"r-oqhbaemp","opts":["30 months","18 months","37 months","24 months"],"q":"What is the minimum total ageing for Montefalco Sagrantino before release?"},{"accept":["Grechetto and Trebbiano","Grechetto and Procanico","Grechetto and Trebbiano Toscano","Trebbiano and Grechetto","Procanico and Grechetto","Grechetto, Trebbiano"],"ans":"Grechetto and Trebbiano","cat":"Umbria","exp":"Orvieto must be at least 60 per cent Grechetto and Procanico together, Procanico being the local name for Trebbiano Toscano; other local whites fill the rest.","id":"r-s8qrgj00","q":"Which two grapes make up the required majority of an Orvieto blend?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Umbria","exp":"Torgiano Rosso Riserva, a DOCG since 1990, is Sangiovese led and aged at least three years. Montefalco and Bevagna lie in the Sagrantino DOCG, and Todi is a Grechetto town with a DOC of its own.","id":"r-ihludty2","opts":["Todi","Torgiano","Bevagna","Montefalco"],"q":"Umbria's Sangiovese led Rosso Riserva DOCG is named for which town near Perugia?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Umbria","exp":"Muffa nobile, noble rot, names the botrytised Orvieto from the foggy valleys of the Paglia and Lake Corbara. Abboccato and amabile are the lighter off dry and medium sweet grades; Classico names the hills around the town.","id":"r-7lk68s0b","opts":["Classico","Abboccato","Amabile","Muffa Nobile"],"q":"Which term on an Orvieto label marks a sweet wine made from botrytised grapes?"},{"accept":["Lazio","Latium"],"ans":"Lazio","cat":"Umbria","exp":"The Orvieto zone runs south from the town across the border into the province of Viterbo in Lazio, so the DOC belongs to both regions.","id":"r-tp9mnjgm","q":"Which region shares the Orvieto DOC with Umbria?","sa":1},{"accept":["Passito","Montefalco Sagrantino Passito","Sagrantino Passito","Sweet passito"],"ans":"Passito","cat":"Umbria","exp":"The DOCG also covers the passito, a sweet, tannic red from bunches dried for weeks. It is the older form, made for the church calendar before dry Sagrantino arrived in the 1970s.","id":"r-ger35dfq","q":"Besides the dry secco, in which style may Montefalco Sagrantino be made?","sa":1}],"sheet":"italy","slug":"umbria","summary":"Umbria is the green heart of Italy, the one region with neither a coastline nor a foreign border, hill country between Tuscany and the Adriatic side of the Apennines. Two wines carry its name on an exam: Montefalco Sagrantino, a DOCG made entirely from the most tannic grape in Italy, dry or as a sweet passito; and Orvieto, a white of Grechetto and Trebbiano from a volcanic plateau on the Lazio border. Torgiano Rosso Riserva, a Sangiovese led DOCG built by one family near Perugia, is the third. Sangiovese fills most of the red vineyard; Grechetto and Trebbiano Spoletino are the whites to know."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"At least 85 per cent Montepulciano, other local reds to 15. From the 2023 harvest the Superiore and Riserva come from a named provincial sub-zone and ask 90 per cent.","f":"Montepulciano d'Abruzzo DOC: the grape rule"},{"b":"Colline Teramane Montepulciano d'Abruzzo (Teramo, 2003), Terre Tollesi or Tullum (Tollo, 2019) and Casauria (Pescara province, 2025). Casauria requires at least 90 per cent Montepulciano, with 18 months ageing or 24 for Riserva.","f":"Abruzzo's three DOCGs"},{"b":"A deep cherry rosato of Montepulciano, at least 85 per cent, from a short skin maceration or a bleed; its own DOC since 2010. Dry, with real tannin; cerasa means cherry.","f":"What is Cerasuolo d'Abruzzo?"},{"b":"Montepulciano d'Abruzzo: the grape, grown in Abruzzo. Vino Nobile di Montepulciano: Sangiovese from the Tuscan town. No shared grape, no shared place.","f":"Montepulciano d'Abruzzo or Vino Nobile di Montepulciano: which names the grape?"},{"b":"The DOC allows Trebbiano Abruzzese and Trebbiano Toscano, 85 per cent together. The Abruzzese is a distinct vine, long confused with Bombino Bianco, and makes the rare long lived whites.","f":"Trebbiano d'Abruzzo: which Trebbiano?"},{"b":"A mountain white of Abruzzo and Marche named for grazing sheep; early ripening, high acid and alcohol, herbal and stony. Sold as Abruzzo DOC, or as Offida DOCG in Marche.","f":"What is Pecorino, the grape?"},{"b":"Abruzzo's traditional overhead pergola, planted for volume; most new vines are Guyot or cordon, but the region's two most famous estates keep the tendone for shade and acidity.","f":"What is the tendone?"},{"b":"The regional DOCs gained provincial sub-zones (Terre di Chieti, Colline Pescaresi, Terre de L'Aquila, and Colline Teramane for Trebbiano), and Superiore and Riserva wines must come from one and print it.","f":"The 2023 Abruzzo reform in one line"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Montepulciano","role":"lead"},{"name":"Trebbiano Abruzzese","role":"lead"},{"name":"Trebbiano Toscano","role":"blending"},{"name":"Pecorino","role":"rising"},{"name":"Passerina","role":"minor"},{"name":"Cococciola","role":"minor"},{"name":"Sangiovese","role":"blending"}],"index":7,"name":"Abruzzo","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Abruzzo","exp":"Cerasuolo d'Abruzzo is at least 85 per cent Montepulciano, kept on its skins for hours rather than days. Sangiovese leads Tuscany, Aglianico Campania, and Nero d'Avola goes into Sicily's Cerasuolo di Vittoria, a different wine.","id":"r-zjy8q5ey","opts":["Montepulciano","Sangiovese","Nero d'Avola","Aglianico"],"q":"Which red grape makes Cerasuolo d'Abruzzo?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Abruzzo","exp":"Cerasuolo d'Abruzzo is a deep cherry rosato of Montepulciano, a DOC of its own since 2010. The red is Montepulciano d'Abruzzo and the white is Trebbiano d'Abruzzo.","id":"r-cm19b1ak","opts":["Amber","Red","White","Rosato"],"q":"What colour is Cerasuolo d'Abruzzo?"},{"accept":["Colline Teramane","Montepulciano d'Abruzzo Colline Teramane","Colline Teramane DOCG","Teramo hills","Teramo"],"ans":"Colline Teramane","cat":"Abruzzo","exp":"Colline Teramane in Teramo province became a DOCG in 2003. Casauria, another former Montepulciano d'Abruzzo sub-zone, became a separate DOCG in 2025, so the date distinguishes them. Tullum was recognised in 2019.","id":"r-o566n8yj","q":"Which former Montepulciano d'Abruzzo sub-zone became a DOCG in 2003?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Abruzzo","exp":"Pecorino takes its name from pecora, sheep, which grazed on its vines in the mountains; it was revived in the 1980s. Passerina, Cococciola and Montonico are other local whites.","id":"r-93hbmkya","opts":["Passerina","Montonico","Pecorino","Cococciola"],"q":"Which white grape of Abruzzo is named after sheep?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Abruzzo","exp":"The DOC asks 85 per cent Montepulciano with other local reds allowed to 15; the Colline Teramane DOCG and the new sub-zone wines raise the floor to 90 per cent.","id":"r-pvjac9m1","opts":["90 per cent","85 per cent","100 per cent","70 per cent"],"q":"What is the minimum share of Montepulciano in Montepulciano d'Abruzzo DOC?"},{"accept":["Tullum","Terre Tollesi","Tullum DOCG","Terre Tollesi DOCG","Tullum (Terre Tollesi)"],"ans":"Tullum","cat":"Abruzzo","exp":"Tullum, also written Terre Tollesi, was raised from DOC to DOCG in 2019 for Montepulciano reds and Pecorino and Passerina whites from the Chieti hills around Tollo.","id":"r-m1jtxbxp","q":"Which Abruzzo DOCG is centred on the commune of Tollo in Chieti province?","sa":1},{"accept":["Tendone","Pergola abruzzese","Tendone system"],"ans":"Tendone","cat":"Abruzzo","exp":"The tendone spreads the canopy overhead at head height; planted for volume, it is kept by the region's most famous estates for the shade and acidity it gives Montepulciano and Trebbiano.","id":"r-ojxpvhop","q":"What name does Abruzzo give its traditional overhead vine canopy, spread at head height?","sa":1}],"sheet":"italy","slug":"abruzzo","summary":"Abruzzo is the mountainous region east of Rome whose vineyards run down from the Apennines to the Adriatic. One grape rules it: Montepulciano, dark, soft and generous, the grape and not the Tuscan town, which makes Montepulciano d'Abruzzo and, pressed off its skins after a few hours, the cherry coloured rosato Cerasuolo d'Abruzzo. Trebbiano d'Abruzzo makes whites in varied styles. Pecorino is the rising white. The region now has three DOCGs: Colline Teramane, Tullum and Casauria, recognised in 2025; and since the 2023 harvest its regional DOCs reserve Superiore and Riserva for named provincial sub-zones such as Terre di Chieti."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Three years with at least one in wood; the Riserva four years with eighteen months in wood. The wine is at least 85 per cent Aglianico, a DOCG since 1993, the first in the south.","f":"Taurasi: the ageing rule"},{"b":"Taurasi (1993), Fiano di Avellino (2003) and Greco di Tufo (2003), all in Irpinia; Aglianico del Taburno (2011) in the Sannio.","f":"Campania's four DOCGs"},{"b":"Both Irpinia, both 85 per cent, both DOCG 2003. Fiano: waxy, hazelnut, honeysuckle, weight, long ageing, Apianum on the label. Greco: taut, flinty, higher acid, from the sulphur ground at Tufo, also spumante.","f":"How do Fiano di Avellino and Greco di Tufo differ?"},{"b":"Same grape. Taurasi: Campania, Irpinia, clay and limestone with ash, DOCG 1993. Vulture: Basilicata, an extinct volcano, DOC 1971, Superiore DOCG 2010.","f":"How does Taurasi differ from Aglianico del Vulture?"},{"b":"The Vesuvio DOC's traditional name: Rosso led by Piedirosso, Bianco by Coda di Volpe or Caprettone, grown on volcanic ash where vines still stand on their own roots.","f":"What is Lacryma Christi del Vesuvio?"},{"b":"Per' e Palummo, dove's foot, for its red stems: a light, peppery red of Vesuvius, the Campi Flegrei and Ischia, soft and best young.","f":"What is Piedirosso?"},{"b":"Campania's everyday white, crisp and unoaked; Falanghina del Sannio DOC (2011) and the Campi Flegrei; two distinct varieties, Beneventana and Flegrea, under one name.","f":"What is Falanghina?"},{"b":"Altitude of 400 to 700 metres, cold winters, a wide day to night swing and clay over volcanic ash: Fiano and Greco keep acid and structure that the coast cannot.","f":"Why does Irpinia make whites that age?"},{"b":"Mastroberardino of Atripalda kept Aglianico, Fiano and Greco in cultivation after phylloxera and the war, and its Taurasi vintages of the 1960s proved the wine's ageing.","f":"Which house kept Irpinia's grapes alive after phylloxera and the war?"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Aglianico","role":"lead"},{"name":"Fiano","role":"lead"},{"name":"Greco","role":"lead"},{"name":"Falanghina","role":"lead"},{"name":"Piedirosso","role":"blending"},{"name":"Coda di Volpe","role":"blending"},{"name":"Biancolella","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pallagrello","role":"minor"}],"index":8,"name":"Campania","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Campania","exp":"Taurasi is at least 85 per cent Aglianico from the hills of Irpinia. Piedirosso makes the light reds of Vesuvius, Montepulciano belongs to Abruzzo and Nero d'Avola to Sicily.","id":"r-n7m5v03m","opts":["Aglianico","Nero d'Avola","Montepulciano","Piedirosso"],"q":"Which grape variety makes Taurasi?"},{"accept":["Taurasi","Taurasi DOCG","Taurasi (Aglianico)"],"ans":"Taurasi","cat":"Campania","exp":"Taurasi, a DOC since 1970, became a DOCG in 1993, the first in the south of Italy; Fiano di Avellino and Greco di Tufo followed in 2003.","id":"r-sbo86dco","q":"Which was the first DOCG granted in southern Italy, in 1993?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Campania","exp":"Taurasi asks three years with at least one in wood; the Riserva takes four years with eighteen months in wood. Five years and two in wood is Brunello's rule.","id":"r-yso2awwo","opts":["Five years, two in wood","Four years, eighteen months in wood","Two years, one in wood","Three years, one in wood"],"q":"What is the minimum ageing for Taurasi before release?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Campania","exp":"The DOCG is Greco di Tufo: at least 85 per cent Greco from eight communes around Tufo, with Coda di Volpe allowed for the rest. Fiano leads the Avellino DOCG and Falanghina the Sannio's whites.","id":"r-xv3qj63b","opts":["Coda di Volpe","Greco","Falanghina","Fiano"],"q":"Which grape must make at least 85 per cent of the DOCG white named for the village of Tufo?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Campania","exp":"Aglianico del Vulture grows on the extinct Monte Vulture volcano in Basilicata, east of Campania. It is the same grape as Taurasi but not a Campanian wine; Puglia lies further east and Calabria to the south.","id":"r-acv9auks","opts":["Basilicata","Campania","Puglia","Calabria"],"q":"In which region is Aglianico del Vulture made?"},{"accept":["Piedirosso","Per' e Palummo","Per e Palummo","Palombina","Piedirosso (Per' e Palummo)"],"ans":"Piedirosso","cat":"Campania","exp":"The Vesuvio DOC's red is led by Piedirosso, the dove's foot grape of the bay, with Sciascinoso and Aglianico as partners; the Bianco is Coda di Volpe or Caprettone.","id":"r-or9ojkb6","q":"Which grape leads the red Lacryma Christi del Vesuvio?","sa":1},{"accept":["Falanghina","Falanghina Beneventana","Falanghina Flegrea","Falanghina del Sannio"],"ans":"Falanghina","cat":"Campania","exp":"Falanghina, two distinct varieties under one name, Beneventana and Flegrea, makes the citrus white of Falanghina del Sannio DOC and the Campi Flegrei. Fiano and Greco are the Irpinian DOCG whites.","id":"r-v4u8jc1e","q":"Which white grape gives the crisp, unoaked everyday white of the Sannio and the Campi Flegrei?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Campania","exp":"Aglianico del Taburno, a DOCG since 2011, grows on the Taburno massif in the Sannio. Taurasi is Irpinia's DOCG, Aglianico del Vulture is Basilicata's, and Falerno del Massico is a DOC of the Caserta hills.","id":"r-x6f3co9p","opts":["Taurasi","Falerno del Massico","Aglianico del Taburno","Aglianico del Vulture"],"q":"Which Aglianico wine is the DOCG of the Sannio, near Benevento?"}],"sheet":"italy","slug":"campania","summary":"Campania is the region of Naples and Vesuvius, and its serious wine comes from the cold hills of Irpinia inland of the bay, around Avellino. Three DOCGs are the exam's core: Taurasi, the late ripening Aglianico red called the Barolo of the south and the first DOCG granted in the south of Italy, and the two whites, Fiano di Avellino and Greco di Tufo, which age as few southern whites do. A fourth, Aglianico del Taburno, lies in the Sannio. Volcanic ground from Vesuvius and the Campi Flegrei, the Amalfi terraces and Ischia carry the rest. Aglianico del Vulture is the same grape across the border in Basilicata."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Zinfandel in California, the same vine by DNA; Croatia's Crljenak Kaštelanski, now called Tribidrag, is the same vine under its oldest name. Named Primitivo for ripening first.","f":"Primitivo: its other names"},{"b":"All 2011: Castel del Monte Nero di Troia Riserva, Castel del Monte Rosso Riserva, Castel del Monte Bombino Nero (rosato) and Primitivo di Manduria Dolce Naturale (sweet).","f":"Puglia's four DOCGs"},{"b":"At least 85 per cent Primitivo from the Ionian coast of Taranto and Brindisi; DOC 1974; minimum 13.5 per cent, Riserva 14 with two years of ageing and nine months in wood. Dry; the sweet one is the DOCG.","f":"Primitivo di Manduria DOC: the rules"},{"b":"Negroamaro, at least 75 per cent, with Malvasia Nera for perfume and softness; a DOC of 1976 from the Salento north of Lecce, with a Riserva after two years.","f":"Salice Salentino Rosso: which grapes?"},{"b":"Dark plum and dried fig with a bitter, herbal finish and softer tannin than its colour; the Salento's grape, and the base of the region's deep rosato.","f":"Negroamaro: how does it taste?"},{"b":"Uva di Troia, the late ripening, firmer red of northern Puglia around Castel del Monte and Foggia; violets, black fruit, real tannin; two Castel del Monte DOCGs are built on it.","f":"What is Nero di Troia?"},{"b":"Castel del Monte Bombino Nero, from the pale Bombino Nero grape on the Murge; 2011.","f":"Italy's only DOCG for rosato alone"},{"b":"The flattest region in Italy: hot and dry between the Adriatic and the Ionian, terra rossa over limestone, the Murge plateau at 300 to 500 metres and the low Salento peninsula.","f":"What marks Puglia's landscape?"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Primitivo","role":"lead"},{"name":"Zinfandel","role":"lead"},{"name":"Negroamaro","role":"lead"},{"name":"Nero di Troia","role":"lead"},{"name":"Malvasia Nera","role":"blending"},{"name":"Bombino Nero","role":"minor"},{"name":"Bombino Bianco","role":"minor"},{"name":"Verdeca","role":"minor"},{"name":"Susumaniello","role":"rising"},{"name":"Fiano","role":"minor"}],"index":9,"name":"Puglia","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Puglia","exp":"DNA matched Primitivo to California's Zinfandel and both to the Croatian Crljenak Kaštelanski, or Tribidrag. Negroamaro, Nero d'Avola and Montepulciano are unrelated southern reds.","id":"r-rrbj4lbl","opts":["Zinfandel","Negroamaro","Montepulciano","Nero d'Avola"],"q":"Which grape is genetically the same vine as Primitivo?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Puglia","exp":"Salice Salentino is Negroamaro at 75 per cent or more with Malvasia Nera. Primitivo belongs to Manduria and Gioia del Colle, Nero di Troia to Castel del Monte, Aglianico to Campania.","id":"r-wj164s4x","opts":["Primitivo","Nero di Troia","Negroamaro","Aglianico"],"q":"Which grape leads Salice Salentino Rosso?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Puglia","exp":"The DOCG of 2011 is Dolce Naturale, a naturally sweet red from late picked or dried Primitivo; the dry Primitivo di Manduria remains a DOC of 1974.","id":"r-8dkgeim1","opts":["A sparkling red","A rosato","A dry red","A sweet red"],"q":"Which style of wine is the Primitivo di Manduria DOCG?"},{"accept":["Castel del Monte Bombino Nero","Bombino Nero","Castel del Monte Bombino Nero DOCG"],"ans":"Castel del Monte Bombino Nero","cat":"Puglia","exp":"Castel del Monte Bombino Nero, raised in 2011, is the only DOCG in Italy given to a rosato alone, made from at least 90 per cent of the pale Bombino Nero grape on the Murge.","id":"r-lsewqajj","q":"Which Puglian DOCG is a rosato?","sa":1},{"accept":["Nero di Troia","Uva di Troia","Nero di Troia (Uva di Troia)"],"ans":"Nero di Troia","cat":"Puglia","exp":"Nero di Troia, also written Uva di Troia, makes Castel del Monte Nero di Troia Riserva on its own and leads the Castel del Monte Rosso Riserva; it is the structured red of northern Puglia.","id":"r-m8oi7qq5","q":"Which red grape is the base of the Castel del Monte Riserva DOCGs?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Puglia","exp":"Gioia del Colle DOC, of 1987, holds the Primitivo of the Murge at around 300 to 500 metres, fresher than Manduria's. Salice Salentino and Brindisi are Negroamaro DOCs of the Salento; Locorotondo is a Valle d'Itria white.","id":"r-qsfqiplc","opts":["Salice Salentino","Gioia del Colle","Locorotondo","Brindisi"],"q":"Which Puglian DOC grows Primitivo on the limestone Murge plateau?"},{"accept":["Alberello","Alberelli","Alberello pugliese"],"ans":"Alberello","cat":"Puglia","exp":"The alberello, the little tree, is pruned low with no trellis so that it shades its own fruit and survives drought; the oldest Primitivo and Negroamaro still stand this way, some ungrafted in the sand.","id":"r-y7t766pk","q":"What is the Italian name for the free standing bush vine of the old Manduria and Salento vineyards?","sa":1}],"sheet":"italy","slug":"puglia","summary":"Puglia is the heel of Italy, long, flat, hot and dry, with two seas and more vineyard than almost any other region; for a century it sent blending wine north by the tanker, and it still makes more than it bottles. Two red grapes carry the exam: Primitivo, which DNA proved to be Zinfandel, concentrated in Manduria and Gioia del Colle, and Negroamaro, the grape of the Salento and of Salice Salentino. Nero di Troia rules the north around Castel del Monte, which holds three of the region's four DOCGs; the fourth is the sweet Primitivo di Manduria Dolce Naturale. Bush vines, limestone and red clay, and rosato are the region's other signatures."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Rosso: at least 80 per cent Nerello Mascalese, Nerello Cappuccio to 20. Bianco: at least 60 per cent Carricante; Bianco Superiore from Milo alone at 80. DOC 1968, Sicily's first; DOCG pending since the 2023 application.","f":"Etna DOC: the grape rules"},{"b":"Cerasuolo di Vittoria (2005): Nero d'Avola 50 to 70 per cent with Frappato 30 to 50, a red from the south east with a Classico zone.","f":"Sicily's first DOCG"},{"b":"Fine one year, Superiore two, Superiore Riserva four, Vergine (Soleras) five, Vergine Stravecchio or Riserva ten. Vergine is always dry and takes no cooked must or mistella.","f":"Marsala: the age grades"},{"b":"Oro and Ambra come from white grapes (Grillo, Catarratto, Inzolia), Rubino from red (Perricone, Nero d'Avola, Nerello Mascalese). Cooked must, mosto cotto, is allowed only in Ambra.","f":"Marsala: the three colours"},{"b":"Pale ruby, red cherry, dried herbs, smoke and ash, high acid and fine firm tannin; think mountain Pinot Noir or Nebbiolo, not a warm southern red. Named for the plain of Mascali.","f":"Nerello Mascalese: how does it taste?"},{"b":"A named district on Etna's slopes, each on a lava flow of its own age, printed on the label as a cru: Calderara, Feudo di Mezzo, Guardiola, Rampante, Arcurìa among them.","f":"What is a contrada?"},{"b":"Sicilia DOC replaced the island IGT in 2011. From the 2017 vintage the names Grillo and Nero d'Avola belong to the island's DOCs, Sicilia above all, and Terre Siciliane IGT may no longer print them.","f":"Sicilia DOC and the 2017 rule"},{"b":"From Zibibbo, which is Muscat of Alexandria, grown as low bush vines in wind sheltered hollows (alberello pantesco, UNESCO 2014), sun dried for weeks and added to fermenting fresh must.","f":"Passito di Pantelleria: how is it made?"},{"b":"A natural cross of Catarratto and Zibibbo: once the backbone of fine Marsala, now the island's best dry white, and since 2017 named on a label only under a DOC.","f":"What is Grillo?"},{"b":"John Woodhouse shipped the first in 1773, Benjamin Ingham followed in 1812, and Vincenzo Florio founded the Sicilian house in 1833.","f":"Who began the Marsala trade, and when?"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Nero d'Avola","role":"lead"},{"name":"Nerello Mascalese","role":"lead"},{"name":"Nerello Cappuccio","role":"blending"},{"name":"Carricante","role":"minor"},{"name":"Frappato","role":"blending"},{"name":"Grillo","role":"lead"},{"name":"Catarratto","role":"lead"},{"name":"Inzolia","role":"blending"},{"name":"Zibibbo","role":"minor"},{"name":"Malvasia di Lipari","role":"minor"},{"name":"Perricone","role":"minor"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"minor"}],"index":10,"name":"Sicily","questions":[{"a":3,"cat":"Sicily","exp":"Etna Rosso is at least 80 per cent Nerello Mascalese; Nerello Cappuccio is the minor partner allowed to 20. Nero d'Avola is the island's lowland red and Frappato belongs to Vittoria.","id":"r-neqzakg0","opts":["Frappato","Nero d'Avola","Nerello Cappuccio","Nerello Mascalese"],"q":"Which grape is the main variety of Etna Rosso?"},{"accept":["Cerasuolo di Vittoria","Cerasuolo di Vittoria DOCG"],"ans":"Cerasuolo di Vittoria","cat":"Sicily","exp":"Cerasuolo di Vittoria, a Nero d'Avola and Frappato blend from the south east, was promoted in 2005 and was still the island's only DOCG at the time of writing; Etna applied in 2023.","id":"r-se8z55lj","q":"Which was Sicily's first DOCG, promoted in 2005?","sa":1},{"accept":["Nero d'Avola and Frappato","Frappato and Nero d'Avola","Nero d'Avola, Frappato","Nero d Avola and Frappato","Calabrese and Frappato"],"ans":"Nero d'Avola and Frappato","cat":"Sicily","exp":"The DOCG asks 50 to 70 per cent Nero d'Avola and 30 to 50 per cent Frappato; the Frappato gives the perfume and the pale cherry colour the name describes.","id":"r-po4tosc2","q":"Which two grapes make Cerasuolo di Vittoria?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Sicily","exp":"Vergine, or Soleras, is aged at least five years and may not be sweetened or coloured with mosto cotto or mistella, so it is dry by definition; the other grades may be secco, semisecco or dolce.","id":"r-763cbhec","opts":["Vergine","Superiore","Superiore Riserva","Fine"],"q":"Which Marsala grade must always be dry?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Sicily","exp":"Zibibbo is the Sicilian name for Muscat of Alexandria, the grape of Pantelleria's passito; Moscato Bianco makes Moscato di Noto, Malvasia di Lipari the Aeolian sweet wine, and Grillo is a Catarratto and Zibibbo cross.","id":"r-eb2pljhb","opts":["Muscat of Alexandria","Moscato Bianco","Grillo","Malvasia di Lipari"],"q":"Which grape variety is known as Zibibbo?"},{"accept":["Milo","Comune di Milo"],"ans":"Milo","cat":"Sicily","exp":"Etna Bianco Superiore must be at least 80 per cent Carricante grown in the commune of Milo, on the rainy east face of the volcano where the grape is most at home.","id":"r-hhq3ax79","q":"From which single commune may Etna Bianco Superiore come?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Sicily","exp":"Grillo, a cross of Catarratto and Zibibbo, gives fine Marsala its structure, with Catarratto and Inzolia beside it. Carricante is Etna's white, Zibibbo Pantelleria's and Malvasia di Lipari the Aeolian islands'.","id":"r-aow2bymd","opts":["Carricante","Zibibbo","Grillo","Malvasia di Lipari"],"q":"Which white grape is the traditional backbone of fine Marsala?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Sicily","exp":"From 2017 the names Nero d'Avola and Grillo were reserved to the island's DOCs, Sicilia DOC first among them, so Terre Siciliane IGT may no longer print them; Vittoria and Contea di Sclafani are local DOCs that may.","id":"r-ih3gzpr1","opts":["Contea di Sclafani DOC","Terre Siciliane IGT","Sicilia DOC","Vittoria DOC"],"q":"From the 2017 vintage, which denomination may no longer name Nero d'Avola on its label?"}],"sheet":"italy","slug":"sicily","summary":"Sicily is the largest island in the Mediterranean and one of Italy's largest vineyards, long a source of bulk wine and Marsala and now the home of its most talked about mountain. Etna, an active volcano, grows Nerello Mascalese and Carricante at 400 to 1,000 metres on black ash in named contrade, and its DOC of 1968 was the island's first; its DOCG was applied for in 2023 and was still pending at the time of writing. Nero d'Avola is the island's red, Grillo and Catarratto its whites, Cerasuolo di Vittoria its only DOCG, and Marsala, Pantelleria's Zibibbo passito and Malvasia delle Lipari its fortified and sweet wines. Sicilia DOC has covered the island since 2011."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"At least 85 per cent Cannonau, Sardinia's name for Grenache, from anywhere on the island; Riserva after two years; sub-zones Classico, Capo Ferrato, Jerzu and Oliena (Nepente di Oliena). DOC 1972.","f":"Cannonau di Sardegna DOC: the rules"},{"b":"Vermentino di Gallura (DOC 1975, DOCG 1996): at least 95 per cent Vermentino from the granite of the north east; Superiore at higher alcohol.","f":"Sardinia's only DOCG"},{"b":"Sardinia's first DOC (1971), a white of the Tirso plain aged for years under flor in old casks: dry, nutty, sherry like, at least 15 per cent; Superiore three years, Riserva four; a liquoroso exists.","f":"What is Vernaccia di Oristano?"},{"b":"Carignan on old ungrafted bush vines in the coastal sand of the south west; DOC 1977; Superiore and Riserva; Santadi's Terre Brune is the benchmark.","f":"What is Carignano del Sulcis?"},{"b":"Aragonese and Spanish rule from 1324 to 1720 left Cannonau (Grenache), Carignano (Carignan), Monica, Bovale and Torbato.","f":"Which grapes did Spanish rule leave in Sardinia?"},{"b":"Argiolas's Isola dei Nuraghi IGT blend, Cannonau led with Carignano, Bovale and Malvasia Nera, barrique aged, first made in 1988; the island's most famous red.","f":"Turriga: what it is"},{"b":"Vernaccia di Oristano: Sardinia, flor aged, dry and nutty, 15 per cent. Vernaccia di San Gimignano: Tuscany, fresh and dry, a DOCG white. Different grapes, different wines.","f":"How do the two Vernaccias differ?"},{"b":"West of the Italian mainland and south of Corsica across the Strait of Bonifacio; Sicily, not Sardinia, lies off the toe.","f":"Where is Sardinia?"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Grenache / Garnacha","role":"lead"},{"name":"Vermentino","role":"lead"},{"name":"Carignan","role":"lead"},{"name":"Monica","role":"blending"},{"name":"Bovale","role":"blending"},{"name":"Vernaccia di Oristano","role":"minor"},{"name":"Nuragus","role":"minor"},{"name":"Torbato","role":"minor"},{"name":"Nasco","role":"minor"},{"name":"Malvasia di Sardegna","role":"minor"}],"index":11,"name":"Sardinia","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Sardinia","exp":"Cannonau is Grenache, Garnacha in Spain, brought under Aragonese rule; Carignan is the island's Carignano of the Sulcis, and Mourvèdre and Tempranillo are not Sardinian grapes.","id":"r-umqd2vb9","opts":["Grenache","Carignan","Mourvèdre","Tempranillo"],"q":"Which grape variety is known as Cannonau?"},{"accept":["Vermentino di Gallura","Vermentino di Gallura DOCG","Gallura"],"ans":"Vermentino di Gallura","cat":"Sardinia","exp":"Vermentino di Gallura, from the granite north east, became a DOCG in 1996 and remains the island's only one; Cannonau di Sardegna and Carignano del Sulcis are DOCs.","id":"r-utb0zfmy","q":"Which is Sardinia's only DOCG?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Sardinia","exp":"The DOCG asks at least 95 per cent Vermentino; the island wide Vermentino di Sardegna DOC and Cannonau di Sardegna ask 85.","id":"r-fauuoncs","opts":["85 per cent","95 per cent","100 per cent","90 per cent"],"q":"What is the minimum share of Vermentino in Vermentino di Gallura?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Sardinia","exp":"Vernaccia di Oristano is left for years in partly filled chestnut and oak casks under a film of flor yeast, giving a dry, nutty wine of 15 per cent; the other three are fresh dry whites.","id":"r-sv9wfn0e","opts":["Vernaccia di Oristano","Nuragus di Cagliari","Alghero Torbato","Vermentino di Gallura"],"q":"Which Sardinian white is aged under flor in old casks like a sherry?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Sardinia","exp":"The Sulcis is the lowland and island country of the south west around Santadi and Sant'Antioco, where Carignano stands ungrafted on sand; the north east is the Gallura, the west plain Oristano, the mountains Cannonau's home.","id":"r-vlqqyq9s","opts":["The central mountains","The west coast plain","The north east","The south west"],"q":"In which part of Sardinia does Carignano del Sulcis grow?"},{"accept":["Granite","Granitic soil","Decomposed granite","Granite sand","Granitic sand"],"ans":"Granite","cat":"Sardinia","exp":"The Gallura of the north east is granite weathered to coarse sand, which gives its Vermentino the fuller, saline character of the DOCG; limestone and clay cover the Campidano and the south, deep sand the Sulcis.","id":"r-acbp3typ","q":"On which bedrock does Vermentino di Gallura grow?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Sardinia","exp":"Aragon and then Spain held Sardinia from 1324 to 1720 and the grapes of Spain came with them. The French legacy is Corsica's; Austria shaped Alto Adige; the Greeks are credited with Sicily's and Calabria's vines.","id":"r-5wbq7b4a","opts":["Austrian","Greek","Spanish","French"],"q":"Which foreign rule left Cannonau, Carignano and Monica in Sardinia's vineyard?"}],"sheet":"italy","slug":"sardinia","summary":"Sardinia is the second largest island of the Mediterranean, west of the mainland and south of Corsica, and four centuries of Spanish rule wrote its vineyard: Cannonau is Grenache, Carignano is Carignan, and Monica, Bovale and Torbato are thought to have come the same way. Vermentino is the white, with Vermentino di Gallura on the granite of the north east as the island's only DOCG; Cannonau di Sardegna is the signature red DOC, Carignano del Sulcis the old bush vine red of the sandy south west, and Vernaccia di Oristano a flor aged, sherry like white that was the island's first DOC in 1971. Nuragus, Nasco and Malvasia di Bosa are the local names to know."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Albariño; cool, wet Atlantic maritime; granite weathered to sand. High acid, saline, peach and citrus, drunk young with shellfish.","f":"Rías Baixas: grape, climate, soil"},{"b":"Val do Salnés (coast, Cambados, coolest, largest), O Rosal (Miño mouth), Condado do Tea (upstream, warmest), Soutomaior (1996, smallest), Ribeira do Ulla (2000, the last added).","f":"Rías Baixas: the five subzones"},{"b":"The parra lifts the canopy two metres onto granite posts so air dries the bunches after rain and rot is held off; vigour is spread over a wide frame.","f":"Why are Rías Baixas vines on pergolas?"},{"b":"100 per cent. A subzone label needs at least 70 per cent: Albariño alone in Salnés, Albariño with Loureira in Rosal, Albariño with Treixadura in Condado do Tea.","f":"Rías Baixas Albariño on a label: how much Albariño?"},{"b":"Alvarinho, in Portugal's Vinho Verde, with Monção e Melgaço on the Miño its heartland.","f":"Albariño across the border"},{"b":"1988, replacing the 1980 Albariño denomination, which could not stand because a grape name is not an appellation.","f":"Year DO Rías Baixas was granted"},{"b":"The DO's category for a wine aged in wood: casks of no more than 600 litres, with the time in wood stated on the label. The only Rías Baixas label that signals oak.","f":"Rías Baixas Barrica means what?"},{"b":"O Rosal: Albariño with Loureira and Caíño Blanco. Condado do Tea: Albariño with Treixadura.","f":"Rosal and Condado blending partners"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Albariño","role":"lead"},{"name":"Loureira","role":"blending"},{"name":"Treixadura","role":"blending"},{"name":"Caíño Blanco","role":"minor"},{"name":"Godello","role":"minor"},{"name":"Mencía","role":"minor"}],"index":0,"name":"Rías Baixas","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Rías Baixas","exp":"Val do Salnés sits on the flat granite around the Ría de Arousa and holds most of the vineyard and the famous houses. O Rosal and Condado do Tea follow the Miño inland and are warmer; Soutomaior is the smallest subzone.","id":"r-stsdnfjp","opts":["Val do Salnés","O Rosal","Soutomaior","Condado do Tea"],"q":"Which Rías Baixas subzone lies on the coast around Cambados and is the coolest and largest?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Rías Baixas","exp":"Five: Val do Salnés, O Rosal, Condado do Tea, Soutomaior and Ribeira do Ulla. The last two were added in 1996 and 2000 and are the ones candidates forget.","id":"r-ng2pju39","opts":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"q":"How many subzones does the Rías Baixas DO contain?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Rías Baixas","exp":"The parra or emparrado carries the vine two metres up on granite posts so wind dries the bunches after rain. Bush vines are the Mediterranean habit; Guyot and Geneva double curtain are wire systems from elsewhere.","id":"r-j7tr4f7r","opts":["Guyot","Bush vine, the gobelet","Geneva double curtain","Pergola, the parra"],"q":"Which training system is traditional in Rías Baixas for lifting Albariño above the damp ground?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Rías Baixas","exp":"Albariño is Alvarinho across the Miño in Vinho Verde, where Monção e Melgaço is its heartland. Loureiro and Trajadura are the Portuguese names of the Galician Loureira and Treixadura; Arinto is a different Portuguese white.","id":"r-5seoykac","opts":["Arinto","Alvarinho","Loureiro","Trajadura"],"q":"What is the Portuguese name for Albariño?"},{"accept":["Miño","Mino","Minho","the Miño","Rio Miño","Río Miño","the Minho"],"ans":"Miño","cat":"Rías Baixas","exp":"O Rosal lies at the mouth of the Miño, Minho on the Portuguese bank, and Condado do Tea further upstream. Both subzones are warmer than Val do Salnés on the open coast.","id":"r-45rdy3fv","q":"Which river forms the border with Portugal at the O Rosal subzone?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Rías Baixas","exp":"The varietal label is the grape alone. Seventy per cent is the floor for the subzone labels: Albariño alone in Salnés, Albariño with Loureira in Rosal, Albariño with Treixadura in Condado do Tea.","id":"r-5944md5b","opts":["70 per cent","100 per cent","95 per cent","85 per cent"],"q":"What is the minimum share of Albariño in a wine labelled Rías Baixas Albariño?"},{"accept":["1988","in 1988"],"ans":"1988","cat":"Rías Baixas","exp":"The DO was created in 1988. The earlier 1980 denomination had been called Albariño, which European rules would not allow because a grape name cannot serve as an appellation.","id":"r-7hx1h6q7","q":"In which year was the Rías Baixas DO granted?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Rías Baixas","exp":"Condado do Tea, named for the Tea tributary, is the inland Miño subzone, warmest of the five, and blends Albariño with Treixadura. O Rosal is at the river's mouth, Salnés on the coast and Ribeira do Ulla on a different river to the north.","id":"r-edahcay1","opts":["Condado do Tea","Ribeira do Ulla","O Rosal","Val do Salnés"],"q":"Which subzone of Rías Baixas lies furthest upstream on the Miño and is the warmest?"}],"sheet":"spain","slug":"rias-baixas","summary":"Rías Baixas is Spain's Atlantic white: Albariño grown on granite in the drowned river inlets of southern Galicia, where rain falls in metres and the vine is lifted onto stone-posted pergolas to dry in the sea wind. The wine is pale, high in acid, saline and stone-fruited, bottled young and poured with shellfish. The denomination dates from 1988 and holds five separate subzones, of which Val do Salnés around Cambados is the coolest and the biggest, O Rosal and Condado do Tea follow the Miño inland, and Soutomaior and Ribeira do Ulla are the small late additions. A wine labelled Rías Baixas Albariño is the grape alone."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Mencía on slate slopes and clay valley floor, in León, Castilla y León; old bush vines; Godello for whites.","f":"Bierzo: grape, province, soil"},{"b":"A sheltered basin between Atlantic and continental: moderate rain, mild winters, warm short summers, earlier harvest than Ribera del Duero.","f":"Bierzo's climate in one sentence"},{"b":"The Sil, which leaves the basin westward into Galicia, cuts Ribeira Sacra and joins the Miño.","f":"Bierzo: the river and where it goes"},{"b":"Bierzo, Vino de Villa (village), Vino de Paraje (site), Viña Clasificada, Gran Viña Clasificada. Approved in 2017, among the first of its kind in Spain.","f":"Bierzo's 2017 origin ladder, bottom to top"},{"b":"Mencía: lighter, floral, raspberry and graphite, bright acid, used oak. Tempranillo of the Duero: darker, fuller, firmer tannin, more new oak.","f":"Mencía against Tempranillo"},{"b":"1989. Ribera del Duero 1982, Toro 1987, Rioja DOCa 1991.","f":"Year DO Bierzo was granted"},{"b":"Valdeorras in Galicia is its home; in Bierzo it leads the whites, often barrel-fermented or lees-aged for texture.","f":"Godello's home and its Bierzo role"},{"b":"Corullón: steep slate terraces above Villafranca, Descendientes de J. Palacios. Valtuille de Abajo: sandy valley floor, very old part-ungrafted vines.","f":"Corullón and Valtuille"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Mencía","role":"lead"},{"name":"Godello","role":"lead"},{"name":"Doña Blanca","role":"minor"},{"name":"Garnacha Tintorera","role":"minor"},{"name":"Palomino","role":"minor"}],"index":1,"name":"Bierzo","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Bierzo","exp":"Mencía is Bierzo's red, floral and fresh on slate. Monastrell is the grape of Jumilla and Yecla, Bobal of Utiel-Requena, and Garnacha leads in Navarra and Priorat.","id":"r-sywut18w","opts":["Mencía","Monastrell","Bobal","Garnacha"],"q":"Which grape leads the red wines of Bierzo?"},{"accept":["León","Leon","the province of León","provincia de León"],"ans":"León","cat":"Bierzo","exp":"Bierzo occupies the western basin of the province of León, with Ponferrada its capital. It is the last wine region of Castilla y León before the Camino enters Galicia.","id":"r-jasx7g35","q":"In which province of Castilla y León does Bierzo lie?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Bierzo","exp":"The Sil gathers Bierzo's streams and leaves through a gorge to cut Ribeira Sacra before joining the Miño. The Duero drains Ribera, Rueda and Toro; the Ebro is Rioja's river; the Tajo runs through central Spain.","id":"r-65zsl3rn","opts":["Duero","Sil","Tajo","Ebro"],"q":"Which river drains the Bierzo basin westward into Galicia?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Bierzo","exp":"Godello, shared with neighbouring Valdeorras, makes Bierzo's textured whites. Albariño belongs to Rías Baixas, Verdejo to Rueda and Viura to Rioja.","id":"r-mhydr4dt","opts":["Godello","Viura","Verdejo","Albariño"],"q":"Which white grape leads the white wines of Bierzo?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Bierzo","exp":"The slopes around Corullón and Villafranca are slate and quartzite, giving the most mineral Mencía. Sand and clay lie on the valley floor at Valtuille and Cacabelos; granite is Rías Baixas and limestone the Duero plateau.","id":"r-tis3vrb2","opts":["Limestone","Sand","Slate","Granite"],"q":"What soil marks the steep slopes of Corullón in Bierzo?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Bierzo","exp":"Bierzo became a DO in 1989. The distractors are real dates in the same family: Ribera del Duero 1982, Toro 1987 and Rioja's promotion to DOCa in 1991.","id":"r-ky9g46rn","opts":["1991","1982","1987","1989"],"q":"In which year was the Bierzo DO granted?"},{"accept":["Gran Viña Clasificada","Gran Vina Clasificada","gran viña clasificada","Gran Viña"],"ans":"Gran Viña Clasificada","cat":"Bierzo","exp":"The ladder runs Bierzo, Vino de Villa, Vino de Paraje, Viña Clasificada and finally Gran Viña Clasificada, a single vineyard of proven standing. It was approved in 2017, among the first Burgundian ladders in a Spanish denomination.","id":"r-usekh6le","q":"What is the top tier of Bierzo's 2017 classification by origin?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Bierzo","exp":"Valdeorras, just over the border on the Sil, revived Godello and is its reference region. Ribeiro leads with Treixadura, Monterrei is on the Portuguese border further south and Rías Baixas is Albariño's coast.","id":"r-n17dtuqo","opts":["Monterrei","Valdeorras","Rías Baixas","Ribeiro"],"q":"Which Galician denomination downstream on the Sil is the home of Godello?"}],"sheet":"spain","slug":"bierzo","summary":"Bierzo is a sheltered basin in the province of León, the last stop in Castilla y León before the Camino de Santiago climbs into Galicia. It takes Atlantic rain and continental sun in roughly equal measure, and on its slate slopes old bush-vine Mencía makes fragrant, fresh, red-fruited wines that the exam contrasts with the darker Tempranillo of the plateau. Godello leads the whites. The DO dates from 1989, and in 2017 it adopted a Burgundian ladder of village, site and classified vineyard wines, among the first in Spain after Priorat's village wines of 2009. Ponferrada is the capital, Cacabelos the seat of the Consejo, and Corullón and Valtuille the villages a server hears most."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Tempranillo, called Tinto Fino or Tinta del País; at least 75 per cent of a red, with Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot and Malbec allowed in the 95 per cent core.","f":"Ribera del Duero: grape, local names, minimum share"},{"b":"Extreme continental, 720 to about 1,000 metres on the northern meseta; hot days, cold nights, frost risk into May and again in October, around 450 mm of rain.","f":"Ribera del Duero's climate and altitude"},{"b":"1982; Burgos (largest), Valladolid, Soria and Segovia; the Consejo sits at Roa.","f":"Ribera del Duero: DO year, provinces, Consejo seat"},{"b":"Crianza 12 months in barrel within 24; Reserva 12 within 36; Gran Reserva 24 within 60. Stricter than the national floor of six months for Crianza.","f":"Ribera's ageing minimums in oak"},{"b":"Valbuena de Duero; founded 1864 with Bordeaux vines; Único is Tinto Fino with Cabernet Sauvignon aged around ten years, Valbuena 5º the younger wine.","f":"Vega Sicilia in three facts"},{"b":"Albillo Mayor at a minimum of 75 per cent; white Ribera del Duero was admitted under the DO from the 2020 vintage.","f":"White Ribera del Duero: grape and date"},{"b":"Ribera: darker, fuller, firmer tannin, higher alcohol, French oak common. Rioja: brighter red fruit, silkier, American oak traditional, the older houses releasing late.","f":"Ribera against Rioja at the table"},{"b":"The Milla de Oro between Peñafiel and Quintanilla de Onésimo in Valladolid: Vega Sicilia, Pingus, Pesquera, Hacienda Monasterio and others.","f":"The golden mile of Ribera"},{"b":"Tinto Pesquera, founded 1972 at Pesquera de Duero, whose pure Tinto Fino won foreign praise in the 1980s and started the boom.","f":"Ribera's first reputation-maker after Vega Sicilia"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Tempranillo","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"blending"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"minor"},{"name":"Malbec","role":"minor"},{"name":"Grenache / Garnacha","role":"minor"},{"name":"Albillo Mayor","role":"rising"}],"index":2,"name":"Ribera del Duero","questions":[{"a":2,"cat":"Ribera del Duero","exp":"Reds must be at least 75 per cent Tempranillo, and the Tempranillo together with Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot and Malbec must reach 95 per cent, leaving at most 5 per cent for Garnacha and Albillo Mayor.","id":"r-8144ptzn","opts":["100 per cent","85 per cent","75 per cent","50 per cent"],"q":"What is the minimum share of Tempranillo in a red wine of Ribera del Duero?"},{"accept":["Tinto Fino","Tinta del País","Tinta del Pais","tinto fino","Tinto del País"],"ans":"Tinto Fino","cat":"Ribera del Duero","exp":"Ribera's growers call their Tempranillo Tinto Fino or Tinta del País, a small-berried plateau clone. Tinta de Toro is the name downstream in Toro and Cencibel the name in La Mancha.","id":"r-ssoz4y6e","q":"By what local name is Tempranillo known in Ribera del Duero?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Ribera del Duero","exp":"Burgos holds most of the vineyard, with Aranda de Duero and Roa, where the Consejo sits. Valladolid has the famous golden mile around Peñafiel, Soria the high cold eastern end and Segovia a small corner.","id":"r-21okho42","opts":["Segovia","Burgos","Valladolid","Soria"],"q":"Which province holds the largest share of the Ribera del Duero DO?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Ribera del Duero","exp":"Ribera del Duero became a DO in 1982. Rueda was recognised in 1980, Toro in 1987 and Bierzo in 1989, so the Castilla y León dates run in that order.","id":"r-prg033gu","opts":["1982","1989","1980","1987"],"q":"In which year was Ribera del Duero granted its DO?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Ribera del Duero","exp":"White Ribera del Duero, admitted under the DO from the 2020 vintage, is at least 75 per cent Albillo Mayor, the native white once co-planted with Tinto Fino. Verdejo belongs to Rueda, Godello to Bierzo and Galicia, and Garnacha Blanca to Rioja and Catalonia.","id":"r-pr1r1qpa","opts":["Godello","Albillo Mayor","Verdejo","Garnacha Blanca"],"q":"Which grape must make up at least 75 per cent of a white Ribera del Duero?"},{"accept":["Vega Sicilia","Bodegas Vega Sicilia","Vega-Sicilia","vega sicilia"],"ans":"Vega Sicilia","cat":"Ribera del Duero","exp":"Vega Sicilia was planted with Bordeaux varieties in 1864 and was making reds in a Bordeaux idiom for generations before the DO of 1982. Único, Tinto Fino with Cabernet Sauvignon, is released about ten years after the harvest.","id":"r-gxmbibh2","q":"Which Ribera del Duero estate, founded in 1864 at Valbuena de Duero, makes Único?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Ribera del Duero","exp":"Ribera's own rule requires 12 months in barrel within 24 months of ageing, double the national Crianza floor of six months. Rioja asks the same twelve.","id":"r-zl3yw2qh","opts":["Twelve","Eighteen","Nine","Six"],"q":"How many months in oak barrel must a Ribera del Duero Crianza spend at minimum?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Ribera del Duero","exp":"Ribera is extreme continental: cold winters, hot dry summers, a diurnal swing that can reach 20 degrees and frost at both ends of a short season. Maritime describes Rías Baixas; Mediterranean fits Priorat and the Levante.","id":"r-mnv46t4o","opts":["Mediterranean","Maritime","Subtropical","Continental"],"q":"Which climate type describes Ribera del Duero?"}],"sheet":"spain","slug":"ribera-del-duero","summary":"Ribera del Duero is high-plateau Tempranillo: a strip of the upper Duero valley in Castilla y León, mostly in Burgos, between about 720 and 1,000 metres, where hot days and cold nights give the grape, called Tinto Fino or Tinta del País here, a darker colour, thicker skin and firmer tannin than it shows in Rioja. The DO dates from 1982, though Vega Sicilia, planted with Bordeaux vines in 1864, made serious reds long before. Reds must be at least three quarters Tempranillo; French oak and long macerations are the modern habit. White wine from Albillo Mayor was admitted from the 2020 vintage. Peñafiel, Roa, Aranda de Duero, Pesquera, Valbuena and La Horra are the names to place."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Rueda: Verdejo, white, Valladolid, 1980 (first in Castilla y León). Toro: Tinta de Toro (Tempranillo), red, Zamora, 1987.","f":"Rueda and Toro: grape, colour, province, DO year"},{"b":"Rueda Verdejo at least 85 per cent Verdejo; Rueda Sauvignon at least 85 per cent Sauvignon Blanc; plain Rueda at least 50 per cent Verdejo.","f":"Rueda's label percentages"},{"b":"Verdejo oxidises and loses aroma fast in heat; cool fruit picked by machine in the dark reaches the press protected, keeping the fennel and citrus character.","f":"Why Rueda harvests at night"},{"b":"Sandy soils over clay stopped phylloxera spreading, so old bush vines of Tinta de Toro still grow on their own roots.","f":"Toro's ungrafted vines: why they survived"},{"b":"Fennel, bay leaf, grapefruit, bitter almond; oily texture, brisk acid, a Spanish cousin to Sauvignon Blanc.","f":"Verdejo's taste in four words"},{"b":"Dorado: aged oxidatively in oak, golden and nutty. Pálido: aged under flor, pale and Sherry-like. Both are historic styles from Palomino and Verdejo.","f":"Rueda Dorado against Rueda Pálido"},{"b":"Marqués de Riscal of Rioja, arriving in 1972 with a Bordeaux consultant, fermented Verdejo cold and clean; the DO followed in 1980.","f":"The house that made modern Rueda"},{"b":"A quality mark approved in 2019 and launched in 2021: vineyards over 30 years old, a yield cap of 6,500 kilograms per hectare, a tasting panel, first wines from the 2020 vintage.","f":"Gran Vino de Rueda"},{"b":"Reds at least 75 per cent Tinta de Toro; alcohol no more than 15 per cent.","f":"Toro's rules in two numbers"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Verdejo","role":"lead"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"blending"},{"name":"Tempranillo","role":"lead"},{"name":"Grenache / Garnacha","role":"blending"},{"name":"Viura","role":"minor"},{"name":"Palomino","role":"minor"}],"index":3,"name":"Rueda / Toro","questions":[{"a":3,"cat":"Rueda / Toro","exp":"Rueda Verdejo requires at least 85 per cent of the grape, as does Rueda Sauvignon for Sauvignon Blanc. Plain Rueda needs only half Verdejo, the rest from Viura, Palomino and the other permitted whites.","id":"r-99qkjr18","opts":["100 per cent","50 per cent","75 per cent","85 per cent"],"q":"What is the minimum share of Verdejo in a wine labelled Rueda Verdejo?"},{"accept":["Tinta de Toro","tinta de toro","Tinta del Toro"],"ans":"Tinta de Toro","cat":"Rueda / Toro","exp":"Toro's thick-skinned Tempranillo is Tinta de Toro, grown as old bush vines on sand. Ribera del Duero upstream calls the same grape Tinto Fino or Tinta del País.","id":"r-k34vvizg","q":"By what local name is Tempranillo known in Toro?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Rueda / Toro","exp":"Toro is the wine town of Zamora province, west of Rueda on the Duero, with a small spill into Valladolid. Rueda is Valladolid's, Burgos holds most of Ribera, and Salamanca has no major DO.","id":"r-iks2m4s5","opts":["Zamora","Salamanca","Burgos","Valladolid"],"q":"In which province does the Toro DO chiefly lie?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Rueda / Toro","exp":"Marqués de Riscal came to Rueda in 1972 with a Bordeaux consultant and fermented Verdejo cold and clean, the template for the DO of 1980. The other three are Rioja houses that stayed in Rioja.","id":"r-tsfwhwka","opts":["López de Heredia","Marqués de Murrieta","Marqués de Riscal","CVNE"],"q":"Which Rioja house arrived in Rueda in 1972 and launched modern Verdejo?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Rueda / Toro","exp":"Rueda Sauvignon is the varietal label for Sauvignon Blanc, planted on the plateau since the 1970s. Godello is a Galician grape, Garnacha Blanca belongs to Rioja and Catalonia, and Chardonnay has no varietal label in Rueda.","id":"r-zd9qzdui","opts":["Chardonnay","Sauvignon Blanc","Godello","Garnacha Blanca"],"q":"Which white grape other than Verdejo may carry its own varietal label in Rueda at 85 per cent?"},{"accept":["1980","in 1980"],"ans":"1980","cat":"Rueda / Toro","exp":"Rueda's DO came in 1980, ahead of Ribera del Duero in 1982, Toro in 1987 and Bierzo in 1989. It was created as a white wine denomination and admitted reds only in 2008.","id":"r-u6cdbsa5","q":"In which year was Rueda recognised as a DO, the first in Castilla y León?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Rueda / Toro","exp":"Dorado, golden, is the oxidative cask-aged wine that made Rueda's medieval fame. Pálido is aged under flor and stays pale, Espumoso is the sparkling category and Rosado is simply rosé.","id":"r-jw8xsawd","opts":["Dorado","Pálido","Rosado","Espumoso"],"q":"Which historic Rueda style is aged oxidatively in oak to a golden, nutty wine?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Rueda / Toro","exp":"Phylloxera cannot move well through sand, so Toro's sandy soils over clay carried old bush-vine Tinta de Toro through the plague ungrafted. The same is true of Nieva's Verdejo in Rueda.","id":"r-jhvvadtl","opts":["Slate","Sand","Limestone","Clay"],"q":"Which soil allowed many Toro vines to survive phylloxera on their own roots?"}],"sheet":"spain","slug":"rueda-toro","summary":"Two neighbouring denominations on the middle Duero, downstream of Ribera and studied together. Rueda is the white: Verdejo on stony, sandy plateau soils south of Valladolid, picked at night and fermented cold into a herbal, citrus, bitter-almond white that stands beside Sauvignon Blanc, which it also grows. Recognised in 1980, it was the first DO of Castilla y León. Toro, to the west in Zamora, is the red: Tinta de Toro, Tempranillo under its local name, on sand that carried old vines through phylloxera ungrafted, making dense, dark, high-alcohol wines. The DO dates from 1987. Marqués de Riscal's arrival in 1972 made modern Rueda; Numanthia and Pintia announced modern Toro."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Crianza 24 months, 12 barrel. Reserva 36 months, 12 barrel, 6 bottle. Gran Reserva 60 months, 24 barrel, 24 bottle. Casks of 330 litres or less.","f":"Rioja red ageing ladder: total, barrel, bottle"},{"b":"Six months in barrel for every tier: Crianza within 18 months, Reserva within 24, Gran Reserva within 48.","f":"Rioja white and rosado ageing ladder"},{"b":"Alta: west, higher, Atlantic-tinged, classic houses at Haro. Alavesa: north bank in Basque Álava, calcareous clay, Tempranillo, cosechero reds. Oriental: east, hotter, lower, Garnacha; Rioja Baja until 2018.","f":"Rioja's three zones in one line each"},{"b":"Tempranillo, Garnacha Tinta, Graciano, Mazuelo (Cariñena) and Maturana Tinta.","f":"Rioja's five red grapes"},{"b":"DO 1925, the first in Spain; Consejo Regulador 1926; DOCa 1991, the first; 2007 white grapes widened; 2017 origin claims and sparkling; 2018 Rioja Oriental.","f":"Rioja: the dates to hold"},{"b":"Green: young, Garantía de Origen. Red: Crianza. Burgundy: Reserva. Blue: Gran Reserva.","f":"Back label colours in Rioja"},{"b":"Bordeaux merchants brought the 225-litre barrica in the 1850s to 1880s; American oak was cheaper and gives coconut, vanilla and dill, which became the traditional signature.","f":"Why American oak in Rioja?"},{"b":"Vino de Zona (one of three zones), Vino de Municipio (one village), Viñedo Singular (a named single vineyard of minimum age, hand picked, panel approved).","f":"Rioja's 2017 origin claims, bottom to top"},{"b":"The bodegas that gathered beside Haro's railway station from the late 1870s: López de Heredia, CVNE, La Rioja Alta, Muga, Gómez Cruzado, Bodegas Bilbaínas and Roda.","f":"Haro's Barrio de la Estación"},{"b":"Espumoso de Calidad de Rioja, traditional method, from 2017: 15 months on lees for the plain category, Reserva 24, Gran Añada (vintage) 36.","f":"Sparkling Rioja tiers"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Tempranillo","role":"lead"},{"name":"Grenache / Garnacha","role":"blending"},{"name":"Graciano","role":"blending"},{"name":"Mazuelo","role":"blending"},{"name":"Maturana Tinta","role":"minor"},{"name":"Viura","role":"lead"},{"name":"Malvasía","role":"blending"},{"name":"Tempranillo Blanco","role":"rising"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"}],"index":4,"name":"Rioja","questions":[{"a":3,"cat":"Rioja","exp":"Gran Reserva needs at least 24 months in barrel and 24 in bottle within 60 months of ageing. Twelve is the barrel minimum for Crianza and Reserva, and eighteen is the national Gran Reserva floor that Rioja exceeds.","id":"r-s9o4ok9l","opts":["Eighteen","Twelve","Thirty-six","Twenty-four"],"q":"How many months in barrel must a red Rioja Gran Reserva spend at minimum?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Rioja","exp":"Rioja Baja, the hot eastern zone, became Rioja Oriental in 2018 to shed the sense of lowness in the old name. Alta and Alavesa kept their names; the Sonsierra is a north-bank district of Rioja Alta, not a zone.","id":"r-mpqizvnf","opts":["Rioja Alta","Sonsierra","Rioja Baja","Rioja Alavesa"],"q":"Which Rioja zone was renamed Rioja Oriental in 2018?"},{"accept":["1991","in 1991"],"ans":"1991","cat":"Rioja","exp":"Rioja was promoted to DOCa in 1991, the first region to hold Spain's top tier; Priorat followed as DOQ. Its original DO dates from 1925, also the first in Spain.","id":"r-b5cqkiaz","q":"In which year did Rioja become Spain's first Denominación de Origen Calificada?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Rioja","exp":"Graciano is the late-ripening native prized for its acidity, colour and perfume in Reserva and Gran Reserva blends. Garnacha brings flesh and alcohol, Mazuelo is Carignan's tannin and acid, and Maturana Tinta is a rarer recovered native.","id":"r-1tm63f3r","opts":["Maturana Tinta","Graciano","Mazuelo","Garnacha"],"q":"Which native red grape contributes acidity, colour and violet aromatics to a Rioja blend in small shares?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Rioja","exp":"Rioja's law caps ageing casks at 330 litres; the standard barrica is 225 litres, the Bordeaux size brought by the merchants of the nineteenth century. Larger vessels do not count towards Crianza, Reserva or Gran Reserva.","id":"r-3zufhhgh","opts":["330 litres","225 litres","600 litres","500 litres"],"q":"What is the largest cask permitted for the ageing counts of Rioja?"},{"accept":["Carignan","Cariñena","Carinena","Carignane","Carignan Noir","Samsó","Samso"],"ans":"Carignan","cat":"Rioja","exp":"Mazuelo is Carignan, Cariñena in Aragón and Samsó in Catalonia: a tannic, acidic blender used in small shares in Rioja and a lead grape in Priorat.","id":"r-nk3mgthy","q":"Mazuelo is the Rioja name for which grape?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Rioja","exp":"López de Heredia (1877), CVNE (1879), La Rioja Alta and others built beside Haro's railway station from the late 1870s, to ship to France by the line that had reached Haro in 1863. Logroño is the capital and seat of the Consejo, Laguardia the walled town of Alavesa, Calahorra a town of Rioja Oriental.","id":"r-lh461921","opts":["Haro","Logroño","Laguardia","Calahorra"],"q":"Which Rioja town's station district, the Barrio de la Estación, gathers the Bordeaux-era bodegas?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Rioja","exp":"A red Reserva needs at least 36 months of ageing with 12 in barrel and, under the current rules, at least 6 in bottle. Twenty-four months in bottle is the Gran Reserva minimum.","id":"r-c3brkt2v","opts":["Thirty-six","Six","Twelve","Twenty-four"],"q":"How many months in bottle must a red Rioja Reserva spend at minimum?"}],"sheet":"spain","slug":"rioja","summary":"Rioja is Spain's reference red: Tempranillo, with Garnacha, Graciano and Mazuelo, grown along the upper Ebro in the lee of the Sierra de Cantabria and aged to order in 225-litre barricas, traditionally American oak, under the legally fixed terms Crianza, Reserva and Gran Reserva. It was the first Spanish DO in 1925 and the first DOCa in 1991. Three zones share the valley: Rioja Alta in the cooler west around Haro, Rioja Alavesa on the north bank in Basque Álava, and the hotter, lower Rioja Oriental in the east, renamed from Rioja Baja in 2018. Since 2017 the label may also claim a zone, a village or a Viñedo Singular, and traditional method sparkling is permitted."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Garnacha, made by sangrado (bleeding the vat after a short maceration): dry, deep pink, fuller than a pressed rosé.","f":"Navarra rosado: grape and method"},{"b":"Baja Montaña, Valdizarbe, Tierra Estella, Ribera Alta, Ribera Baja. Valdizarbe coolest and wettest; Ribera Baja hottest and driest.","f":"Navarra's five subzones, north to south"},{"b":"Pago de Arínzano (2007), Prado de Irache (2008), Pago de Otazu (2009). Each is its own denomination, not part of DO Navarra.","f":"The three Vinos de Pago in Navarra"},{"b":"Tempranillo. Garnacha was the historic grape and still defines rosado and the old-vine reds of Baja Montaña.","f":"Most planted red grape in Navarra today"},{"b":"The regional viticultural research station, founded 1981, trialled them; growers planted on its results from the 1980s.","f":"Why Cabernet, Merlot and Chardonnay grow in Navarra"},{"b":"Crianza two years, six months cask. Reserva three years, twelve months cask. Gran Reserva five years, eighteen months cask. Rioja is stricter.","f":"National ageing floors for a Spanish red"},{"b":"Moscatel de Grano Menudo (small-berried Muscat), late harvested in Ribera Baja, unfortified.","f":"Navarra's sweet wine grape"},{"b":"Rioja: its Oriental zone includes towns in south-western Navarra such as San Adrián and Andosilla.","f":"Which neighbouring DOCa reaches into Navarra?"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Grenache / Garnacha","role":"lead"},{"name":"Tempranillo","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"blending"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"blending"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"},{"name":"Graciano","role":"minor"},{"name":"Moscatel de Grano Menudo","role":"minor"},{"name":"Viura","role":"minor"}],"index":5,"name":"Navarra","questions":[{"a":2,"cat":"Navarra","exp":"Garnacha, bled off the skins by sangrado, gives Navarra's deep dry pink. Tempranillo has overtaken it in the red plantings, but the rosado tradition belongs to Garnacha.","id":"r-fib9fgz3","opts":["Mazuelo","Tempranillo","Garnacha","Graciano"],"q":"Which grape makes the traditional dry rosado of Navarra?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Navarra","exp":"Ribera Baja lies on the Ebro plain around Tudela, semi-arid beside the Bardenas Reales badlands. Valdizarbe and Tierra Estella feel the Atlantic and are the coolest and wettest; Baja Montaña is high hill country under the Pyrenees.","id":"r-2813ybch","opts":["Ribera Baja","Valdizarbe","Baja Montaña","Tierra Estella"],"q":"Which of Navarra's five subzones is the hottest and driest?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Navarra","exp":"Each is a Vino de Pago, a single estate with its own denomination under the 2003 wine law, recognised in 2007, 2008 and 2009; none is a DO Navarra wine. DOCa is the rung of Rioja and Priorat, and Vino de la Tierra is country wine.","id":"r-6tuonc0c","opts":["DOCa","Vino de Pago","Vino de la Tierra","DO"],"q":"Which category of Spanish wine law covers Arínzano, Prado de Irache and Otazu in Navarra?"},{"accept":["Sangrado","sangrado","el sangrado","metodo de sangrado","método de sangrado"],"ans":"Sangrado","cat":"Navarra","exp":"Sangrado is the Spanish name for saignée: juice is bled off red grapes after some hours on the skins and fermented on its own, giving a deeper, fuller pink.","id":"r-eb9jhryx","q":"What is the Spanish name for the bleeding method by which Navarra's rosado is drawn off the skins?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Navarra","exp":"The Ebro runs along the south of the province, where its channels irrigate Ribera Baja. The Duero drains the northern plateau, the Tajo central Spain and the Guadalquivir Andalucía.","id":"r-ed280ind","opts":["Guadalquivir","Tajo","Duero","Ebro"],"q":"Which river flows through the south of Navarra past Tudela?"},{"accept":["Eighteen months","eighteen months","18 months","eighteen","18"],"ans":"Eighteen months","cat":"Navarra","exp":"Spanish law sets a red Gran Reserva at five years in all with at least eighteen months in cask of 330 litres or less. Rioja raises its own floor to twenty-four months in cask and twenty-four in bottle.","id":"r-ia2cl581","q":"Under the national ageing floors that apply in Navarra, how many months must a red Gran Reserva spend in cask?","sa":1},{"accept":["Baja Montaña","baja montana","baja montaña","la baja montana","montana baja"],"ans":"Baja Montaña","cat":"Navarra","exp":"Baja Montaña, the north-eastern hill country, keeps the oldest Garnacha on the poorest soils; its rosado and varietal reds are the region's calling card.","id":"r-ws9emx14","q":"Which subzone of Navarra holds the old bush-vine Garnacha of the Pyrenean foothills around Sangüesa?","sa":1}],"sheet":"spain","slug":"navarra","summary":"Navarra sits east of Rioja, running from the Pyrenean foothills down to the Ebro, and the exam remembers it for one thing first: dry Garnacha rosado, deep pink, made by bleeding the vat. The denomination is older than most, recognised under the 1933 wine statute, and it holds five subzones that climb from the hot southern Ribera Baja to the cool, damp Valdizarbe near Pamplona. Garnacha was the historic red; Tempranillo has since overtaken it in the plantings, with Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot and Chardonnay trialled here earlier than almost anywhere in Spain. Three single-estate Vinos de Pago sit inside its borders, and a corner of DOCa Rioja does too."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Macabeo: fruit and freshness. Xarel·lo: body, structure and ageing. Parellada: acidity and delicacy from the high Penedès.","f":"The three traditional Cava grapes and what each gives"},{"b":"Cava de Guarda 9. Guarda Superior: Reserva 18, Gran Reserva 30, Cava de Paraje Calificado 36. Gran Reserva only Brut Nature, Extra Brut or Brut.","f":"Cava lees ladder, in months"},{"b":"Comtats de Barcelona (Catalonia, most of the wine), Valle del Ebro (Rioja, Navarra, Álava, Aragón), Viñedos de Almendralejo (Extremadura), Zona de Levante (Requena, Valencia).","f":"Cava's four zones"},{"b":"Brut Nature under 3, no sugar added. Extra Brut under 6. Brut under 12. Extra Seco 12 to 17. Seco 17 to 32. Semi Seco 32 to 50. Dulce over 50.","f":"Cava dosage scale, grams per litre"},{"b":"Sant Sadurní d'Anoia, Penedès, 1872, by the Codorníu house after a study of Champagne, using the local white grapes.","f":"First Cava: where and when"},{"b":"A private collective mark of leading Penedès sparkling houses that left DO Cava in 2019: estate fruit, organic, hand picked, 18 months minimum on lees.","f":"What is Corpinnat?"},{"b":"DO Penedès's own traditional-method sparkling: organic by rule, at least 15 months on the lees, never called Cava.","f":"What is Clàssic Penedès?"},{"b":"At least a quarter of the blend must be red grapes (Trepat, Garnacha, Pinot Noir, Monastrell); no blending of finished white and red wine.","f":"Cava rosado rule"},{"b":"Marítim (coast): Monastrell, Garnacha, early Macabeo. Central (200 to 500 m): Macabeo, Xarel·lo. Superior (to 800 m): Parellada, Chardonnay.","f":"Three steps of Penedès and their grapes"},{"b":"Vines at least ten years old, hand harvested, registered plot, yield capped at 10,000 kg per hectare, vintage dated, organic from the 2025 harvest.","f":"Cava de Guarda Superior vineyard rules"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Macabeo","role":"lead"},{"name":"Xarel·lo","role":"lead"},{"name":"Parellada","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"blending"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"blending"},{"name":"Trepat","role":"minor"},{"name":"Grenache / Garnacha","role":"minor"},{"name":"Mourvèdre / Monastrell","role":"minor"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"minor"}],"index":6,"name":"Penedès (Cava)","questions":[{"a":3,"cat":"Penedès (Cava)","exp":"Xarel·lo is the structural grape; Macabeo brings fruit, Parellada acidity from the high vineyards. Trepat is a red permitted only for rosado.","id":"r-d53pcpkz","opts":["Trepat","Parellada","Macabeo","Xarel·lo"],"q":"Which Cava grape gives the blend its body and its capacity for long lees ageing?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Penedès (Cava)","exp":"Gran Reserva needs thirty months. Nine is the floor for Cava de Guarda, eighteen for Reserva and thirty-six for Cava de Paraje Calificado.","id":"r-qlj596yc","opts":["30 months","9 months","36 months","18 months"],"q":"What is the minimum time on the lees for a Cava Gran Reserva?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Penedès (Cava)","exp":"Sant Sadurní d'Anoia remains the Cava capital. Vilafranca is DO Penedès's administrative town, Vilanova i la Geltrú the coastal port of the Garraf and El Vendrell the chief town of the Baix Penedès.","id":"r-85fb0xp5","opts":["El Vendrell","Sant Sadurní d'Anoia","Vilanova i la Geltrú","Vilafranca del Penedès"],"q":"In which town of the Penedès was the first Cava made in 1872?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Penedès (Cava)","exp":"Brut Nature is the driest term and the only one with nothing added. Extra Brut allows under six grams, Brut under twelve, and Seco is in fact off-dry at seventeen to thirty-two.","id":"r-ibdfej7b","opts":["Brut Nature","Extra Seco","Extra Brut","Semi Seco"],"q":"Which sweetness term on a Cava label means fewer than three grams of sugar per litre with no dosage added?"},{"accept":["Comtats de Barcelona","comtats de barcelona","comtats","condados de barcelona","counties of barcelona"],"ans":"Comtats de Barcelona","cat":"Penedès (Cava)","exp":"Comtats de Barcelona is the Catalan zone, with subzones such as Valls d'Anoia-Foix; the other three are Valle del Ebro, Viñedos de Almendralejo and Zona de Levante.","id":"r-xafkiobj","q":"Which Cava zone covers Catalonia and produces most of the denomination's wine?","sa":1},{"accept":["Corpinnat","corpinnat","corpinat","the corpinnat association","corpinnat mark"],"ans":"Corpinnat","cat":"Penedès (Cava)","exp":"Corpinnat is a private brand, not a denomination; its members farm organically, pick by hand, vinify on the estate and age at least eighteen months on the lees.","id":"r-tebkgk3f","q":"What is the name of the collective mark founded by leading Penedès houses that left DO Cava in 2019?","sa":1},{"accept":["25 per cent","25 percent","25%","a quarter","one quarter","twenty-five per cent","twenty five per cent"],"ans":"25 per cent","cat":"Penedès (Cava)","exp":"A rosado must carry at least a quarter red grapes, usually Trepat, Garnacha or Pinot Noir, and may not be made by blending finished white and red wines.","id":"r-0er5ap9k","q":"What is the minimum share of red grapes in the blend of a Cava Rosado?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Penedès (Cava)","exp":"Parellada needs the altitude of the Penedès Superior to keep its acidity and is picked last; Macabeo ripens first on the lower land and Xarel·lo in the central basin.","id":"r-wer7ibmj","opts":["Xarel·lo","Macabeo","Parellada","Chardonnay"],"q":"Which Cava grape is grown highest, on the slopes of the Alt Penedès?"}],"sheet":"spain","slug":"penedes","summary":"Penedès is the Catalan country south west of Barcelona, climbing from the coast to 800 metres, and it is where Cava was born in 1872 at Sant Sadurní d'Anoia. Hold the two names apart: DO Penedès is a place, making still wines and its own organic sparkling called Clàssic Penedès; DO Cava is a traditional-method sparkling wine defined by method and registered vineyards, most of it from Penedès but with authorised zones in the Ebro valley, Extremadura and Valencia. Cava rests on Macabeo, Xarel·lo and Parellada, needs nine months on its lees, and since 2022 is sold as Cava de Guarda or, from eighteen months, Cava de Guarda Superior. Leading estates under the Corpinnat mark left the DO in 2019."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Llicorella: dark Carboniferous slate and schist with quartz veins, free draining, almost no organic matter; roots dig deep for water.","f":"Priorat's soil: name and description"},{"b":"Garnacha (Garnatxa) and Cariñena (Carinyena, Samsó, Mazuelo). Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah and Merlot arrived with the 1980s revival.","f":"Priorat's two historic grapes"},{"b":"DO 1954. DOQ (Catalan for DOCa) in Catalan law 2000, national confirmation 2009. Shares the top rung with Rioja only.","f":"Priorat's appellation status and dates"},{"b":"DOQ Priorat, Vi de Vila (12 villages, since the late 2000s), Vi de Paratge (named site), Vinya Classificada, Gran Vinya Classificada (full ladder 2019; L'Ermita first).","f":"Priorat's vineyard ladder, bottom to top"},{"b":"Gratallops, late 1980s: a group of newcomers made five clos wines in one cellar from the 1989 vintage, with new French oak and Cabernet in the blend.","f":"Where the Priorat revival began, and when"},{"b":"Montsant, a DO since 2001, carved from Tarragona's Falset subzone; same grapes, more mixed soils, lower prices.","f":"Which DO surrounds Priorat like a horseshoe?"},{"b":"Priory: the Carthusian monastery of Scala Dei, founded in the twelfth century, planted the vines and ruled the villages.","f":"What does Priorat mean, and why?"},{"b":"Porrera: high, cool, east side, century-old Cariñena, structured and long lived. Gratallops: lower, warmer, centre, Garnacha perfume, the revival village.","f":"Porrera versus Gratallops"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Grenache / Garnacha","role":"lead"},{"name":"Carignan","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"blending"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"blending"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"minor"},{"name":"Garnacha Blanca","role":"minor"}],"index":7,"name":"Priorat","questions":[{"accept":["Llicorella","llicorella","licorella","llicorella slate","licorela"],"ans":"Llicorella","cat":"Priorat","exp":"Llicorella is the local word for the dark, flaking Carboniferous slate shot with quartz; it drains instantly and forces roots deep, which is why yields are so low.","id":"r-28ye4ql5","q":"What is the name of the slate and quartz soil that defines Priorat?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Priorat","exp":"Garnacha and Cariñena were already on the terraces when the newcomers arrived; Cabernet, Syrah and Merlot were planted later. The other pairs belong to Rioja, the Levante and the north west.","id":"r-bi5peqr6","opts":["Monastrell and Bobal","Garnacha and Cariñena","Mencía and Garnacha Tintorera","Tempranillo and Graciano"],"q":"Which two grapes make up the old bush vines of Priorat?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Priorat","exp":"Priorat is a Denominación de Origen Calificada, written DOQ in Catalan, a rung it shares with Rioja alone: Catalonia raised it from DO in 2000 and national law confirmed it in 2009. Montsant and Penedès are plain DOs.","id":"r-y2ecopat","opts":["Vino de Pago","DO","Vino de la Tierra","DOCa"],"q":"Which tier of Spanish wine law does Priorat hold?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Priorat","exp":"Porrera sits high on the eastern side and its old Cariñena gives the most structured village wines. Gratallops and Bellmunt are lower and warmer, El Lloar a small western village.","id":"r-t24yu7wg","opts":["Porrera","Bellmunt del Priorat","Gratallops","El Lloar"],"q":"Which village of Priorat is known for its high, cool slopes and century-old Cariñena vines?"},{"accept":["Gran Vinya Classificada","gran vinya classificada","gran vina classificada","gran vinya clasificada","great classified vineyard"],"ans":"Gran Vinya Classificada","cat":"Priorat","exp":"The ladder climbs from Vi de Vila through Vi de Paratge and Vinya Classificada to Gran Vinya Classificada, the great classified vineyard; L'Ermita was the first so named.","id":"r-ucqgta28","q":"Which tier stands at the top of Priorat's own vineyard classification?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Priorat","exp":"Montsant was carved from DO Tarragona's Falset subzone in 2001 and shares Priorat's grapes on more mixed soils. Conca de Barberà, Terra Alta and Costers del Segre are other Catalan DOs further north or west.","id":"r-jf9e1tl2","opts":["Conca de Barberà","Terra Alta","Montsant","Costers del Segre"],"q":"Which DO surrounds Priorat on three sides like a horseshoe?"},{"accept":["Mazuelo","mazuelo","mazuela"],"ans":"Mazuelo","cat":"Priorat","exp":"Mazuelo in Rioja, Samsó or Carinyena in Catalonia and Carignan in France are one grape; in Priorat it gives acidity and iron to the old-vine blends.","id":"r-gapsst0v","q":"What is the name of the Cariñena grape in Rioja, where it plays a minor blending role?","sa":1}],"sheet":"spain","slug":"priorat","summary":"Priorat is a bowl of steep terraces in the Tarragona hills, inland from the Catalan coast, and its name comes from the Carthusian priory of Scala Dei founded there in the twelfth century. Its rock is llicorella, a black slate shot with quartz, and its grapes are old bush Garnacha and Cariñena, joined since the 1980s by Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah and Merlot. Yields are tiny, alcohol is high and the wines are dense, stony and long lived. Priorat is one of only two Spanish denominations at the top tier, written in Catalan as DOQ, and it has built a Burgundian ladder of its own: village wine, named site, classified vineyard and great classified vineyard."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Cencibel. Elsewhere: Tinto Fino and Tinta del País (Ribera del Duero), Tinta de Toro (Toro), Ull de Llebre (Catalonia), Tinta Roriz and Aragonez (Portugal).","f":"Tempranillo's name in La Mancha"},{"b":"A drought-tolerant white bush vine, long the most planted grape on earth; neutral dry whites and the base spirit for Brandy de Jerez.","f":"Airén: what it is and why it matters"},{"b":"Albacete, Ciudad Real, Cuenca and Toledo, administered from Alcázar de San Juan; the largest DO in Spain by area.","f":"La Mancha's four provinces"},{"b":"A separate DO, an enclave in the south of Ciudad Real: Cencibel and Airén, oak-aged Reserva and Gran Reserva reds at low prices.","f":"Valdepeñas: where and what"},{"b":"Dominio de Valdepusa, Malpica de Tajo, Toledo, 2003, with Finca Élez the same year; the category came from the 2003 wine law.","f":"Spain's first Vino de Pago"},{"b":"Extreme continental at 600 to 700 metres: winters below minus ten, summers above forty, 300 to 400 mm of rain, constant sun.","f":"La Mancha's climate in one line"},{"b":"Bush vines at about 1,600 per hectare in a square grid, so each vine draws on enough soil water to survive the rainless summer.","f":"Why La Mancha's old vineyards are planted so widely"},{"b":"Vino de la Tierra de Castilla, covering the whole autonomous community; not Castilla y León's.","f":"The country-wine label of Castilla-La Mancha"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Airén","role":"lead"},{"name":"Tempranillo","role":"lead"},{"name":"Grenache / Garnacha","role":"blending"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"blending"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"blending"},{"name":"Macabeo","role":"minor"},{"name":"Verdejo","role":"minor"},{"name":"Bobal","role":"minor"}],"index":8,"name":"La Mancha","questions":[{"accept":["Cencibel","cencibel","censibel","cencibel tempranillo"],"ans":"Cencibel","cat":"La Mancha","exp":"Cencibel is the plateau's name for Tempranillo; the same grape is Tinto Fino in Ribera del Duero and Tinta de Toro in Toro.","id":"r-y2bul153","q":"What is the local name for Tempranillo in La Mancha and Valdepeñas?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"La Mancha","exp":"Airén's drought tolerance made it the most planted vine in the world for decades, and much of its crop is distilled for Brandy de Jerez. Macabeo and Verdejo are newer plantings here; Palomino is Jerez's grape.","id":"r-4t7u70nx","opts":["Verdejo","Airén","Palomino","Macabeo"],"q":"Which white grape covers most of the La Mancha vineyard?"},{"a":0,"cat":"La Mancha","exp":"Valdepeñas has had its own DO since 1968 and is known for inexpensive oak-aged Cencibel. Manchuela lies to the east with Bobal, Méntrida to the north west with Garnacha, Uclés in Cuenca.","id":"r-0akayeoc","opts":["Valdepeñas","Manchuela","Uclés","Méntrida"],"q":"Which DO is a separate enclave at the southern edge of the La Mancha plain?"},{"a":3,"cat":"La Mancha","exp":"The Vino de Pago category was created by the 2003 wine law and Valdepusa, with Finca Élez, was recognised that year. 1991 is Rioja's DOCa, 1996 the legalisation of irrigation, 2009 Priorat's DOQ.","id":"r-psuj5lzp","opts":["1991","1996","2009","2003"],"q":"In which year was Dominio de Valdepusa recognised as Spain's first Vino de Pago?"},{"accept":["1996","nineteen ninety-six","nineteen ninety six"],"ans":"1996","cat":"La Mancha","exp":"Irrigation had been forbidden for quality vineyards until 1996; its legalisation let the plateau plant trellised Tempranillo, Cabernet and Syrah under drip lines.","id":"r-5gxczu4y","q":"In which year did Spain make vineyard irrigation legal, reshaping the plantings of La Mancha?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"La Mancha","exp":"Bobal is Manchuela's and Utiel-Requena's grape. Monastrell belongs to Jumilla and Yecla, Mencía to Bierzo, Garnacha Tintorera to Almansa.","id":"r-qc1b5kwc","opts":["Mencía","Garnacha Tintorera","Bobal","Monastrell"],"q":"Which red grape of neighbouring Manchuela gives dark, acid-fresh wines on the eastern edge of the plateau?"},{"accept":["Vino de la Tierra de Castilla","vino de la tierra de castilla","vt castilla","tierra de castilla","vino de la tierra castilla"],"ans":"Vino de la Tierra de Castilla","cat":"La Mancha","exp":"Vino de la Tierra de Castilla is the regional IGP for Castilla-La Mancha; Castilla y León to the north has its own, differently named label.","id":"r-qinc6as9","q":"What is the country-wine designation that covers the whole of Castilla-La Mancha and carries most of its varietal wines?","sa":1}],"sheet":"spain","slug":"la-mancha","summary":"La Mancha is the high dry plateau south of Madrid, Don Quixote's country, and the largest single denomination in Spain, spread across the provinces of Albacete, Ciudad Real, Cuenca and Toledo. It is the engine room of Spanish volume: Airén, the white grape that was long the most planted vine on earth, feeds the brandy stills and the bulk trade, while Tempranillo under its local name Cencibel makes the reds. Bush vines at low density on clay and limestone endure scorching summers and freezing winters with little rain. Around and inside it sit Valdepeñas, the separate DO to the south, and the largest cluster of Spain's single-estate Vinos de Pago."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Monastrell: Mourvèdre in France (Bandol, the southern Rhône), Mataro in Australia and California. Late ripening, thick skinned, needs heat.","f":"The grape of Jumilla and Yecla, and its names abroad"},{"b":"Jumilla DO 1966, Murcia plus six municipalities of Albacete. Yecla DO 1975, a single municipality in Murcia with subzones Campo Arriba and Campo Abajo.","f":"How do DO Jumilla and DO Yecla differ in date and extent?"},{"b":"Sandy soils over limestone kept phylloxera away until 1989; old bush Monastrell on its own roots (pie franco) survives from before.","f":"Why Jumilla has so many ungrafted vines"},{"b":"Blackberry, dried fig, earth and game; high tannin and alcohol, soft when ripe; young unoaked or blended with Syrah and Cabernet in oak.","f":"Taste profile of Jumilla Monastrell"},{"b":"Dulce Monastrell: Jumilla's sweet red, late harvest or fortified. Fondillón: Alicante's unfortified, naturally strong sweet Monastrell aged long in old casks.","f":"Dulce Monastrell versus Fondillón"},{"b":"Continental with Mediterranean summers: over 3,000 sun hours, 40 °C afternoons, winter frost, about 300 mm of rain, 400 to 800 metres.","f":"Jumilla and Yecla climate"},{"b":"Campo Arriba, the higher northern land towards 800 metres, where old bush Monastrell on poor limestone gives the freshest, most structured wines.","f":"Yecla's best subzone"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Mourvèdre / Monastrell","role":"lead"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"blending"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"blending"},{"name":"Tempranillo","role":"minor"},{"name":"Garnacha Tintorera","role":"minor"},{"name":"Petit Verdot","role":"minor"},{"name":"Airén","role":"minor"},{"name":"Moscatel","role":"minor"}],"index":9,"name":"Jumilla / Yecla","questions":[{"a":3,"cat":"Jumilla / Yecla","exp":"Monastrell, the Mourvèdre of France, is the grape of Murcia's hill country. Garnacha leads in Navarra and Priorat, Bobal in Manchuela and Utiel-Requena, Tempranillo in Rioja.","id":"r-3g4ok07d","opts":["Garnacha","Tempranillo","Bobal","Monastrell"],"q":"Which grape leads the red wines of both Jumilla and Yecla?"},{"accept":["Mourvèdre","mourvedre","mourvèdre"],"ans":"Mourvèdre","cat":"Jumilla / Yecla","exp":"Mourvèdre in Bandol and the southern Rhône, Mataro in Australia and California, Monastrell in Spain: one late-ripening, heat-loving grape.","id":"r-wmf1mjq1","q":"What is Monastrell called in France?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Jumilla / Yecla","exp":"The sandy soils delayed the louse until 1989, which is why ungrafted old Monastrell survives. 1966 and 1975 are the DO dates of Jumilla and Yecla; 1901 is the era of Rioja's crisis.","id":"r-hd5pehwn","opts":["1989","1901","1975","1966"],"q":"In which year did phylloxera finally reach Jumilla, a century after it devastated Rioja?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Jumilla / Yecla","exp":"Yecla is the one-town DO, split into Campo Arriba and Campo Abajo. Jumilla crosses into Albacete, Bullas covers several Murcian towns, Almansa is in Albacete.","id":"r-plk8xdsp","opts":["Jumilla","Yecla","Almansa","Bullas"],"q":"Which Spanish denomination consists of a single municipality in Murcia?"},{"accept":["Campo Arriba","campo arriba","arriba","campo de arriba"],"ans":"Campo Arriba","cat":"Jumilla / Yecla","exp":"Campo Arriba rises towards 800 metres and holds the oldest vines on the poorest limestone; Campo Abajo is the lower, warmer south of the municipality.","id":"r-1ddgnbu9","q":"Which subzone of Yecla, the higher northern land, is known for its old bush-vine Monastrell?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Jumilla / Yecla","exp":"Six municipalities of Albacete, in Castilla-La Mancha, lie inside DO Jumilla. Valencia holds Alicante and Utiel-Requena; Andalucía and Aragón do not border the DO.","id":"r-qu9qb06k","opts":["Andalucía","Aragón","Castilla-La Mancha","Comunidad Valenciana"],"q":"Into which neighbouring region does DO Jumilla extend from Murcia?"},{"accept":["Fondillón","fondillon","fondillón","alicante fondillon","vino fondillon"],"ans":"Fondillón","cat":"Jumilla / Yecla","exp":"Fondillón is Alicante's naturally strong late-harvest Monastrell matured for years in old casks without fortification; Jumilla's sweet red is Dulce Monastrell.","id":"r-b4oxudll","q":"What is the aged, unfortified sweet Monastrell of neighbouring Alicante called?","sa":1}],"sheet":"spain","slug":"jumilla-yecla","summary":"Jumilla and Yecla are two neighbouring denominations in the dry hill country of Murcia, inland from the Mediterranean, and they share one grape: Monastrell, the vine France calls Mourvèdre, which needs this kind of heat to ripen at all. Jumilla, a DO since 1966, spills from Murcia into six municipalities of Albacete; Yecla, a DO since 1975, is one municipality alone. Both grow old ungrafted bush vines on poor limestone and sand, because phylloxera did not reach Jumilla until 1989. The wines are dark, warm, tannic and generous, from juicy young reds through oaked blends with Syrah and Cabernet to the sweet Dulce Monastrell."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Fortify Palomino to about 15 per cent and a yeast veil, flor, grows: Fino and Manzanilla. Fortify to 17 or more and flor dies: Oloroso, aged in air.","f":"The flor rule in one line"},{"b":"Fino aged in Sanlúcar de Barrameda, where humid sea air keeps flor alive all year: lighter, saltier, sharper. A place, not a grape.","f":"What is Manzanilla?"},{"b":"Amontillado: a Fino whose flor died, refortified and aged in air, dry. Palo Cortado: a flor candidate that turned, Amontillado nose on Oloroso body, dry.","f":"How does Palo Cortado differ from Amontillado?"},{"b":"Palomino: every dry style. Pedro Ximénez: black sweet PX, sun-dried. Moscatel: lighter grapey sweet wine from the sands at Chipiona.","f":"Sherry's three grapes and their jobs"},{"b":"White chalky marl that soaks up winter rain and crusts in summer, feeding the vine through drought; the Jerez Superior zone and the great pagos are albariza.","f":"Albariza: what it is and why it matters"},{"b":"Scales of old butts: wine is drawn (saca) from the solera, the oldest row, and refilled (rocío) from the criaderas above; a blend of many years with no vintage.","f":"How a solera works"},{"b":"Dry styles under 5. Medium 5 to 115. Pale Cream 45 to 115. Cream 115 to 140. Moscatel at least 160. Pedro Ximénez at least 212.","f":"Sherry sugar scale, grams per litre"},{"b":"Certified average ages of 20 (Very Old Sherry) and 30 years (Very Old Rare Sherry), for Amontillado, Oloroso, Palo Cortado and PX only; 12 and 15 year marks sit below.","f":"VOS and VORS"},{"b":"Jerez de la Frontera (inland, fuller wines), El Puerto de Santa María (bay, between), Sanlúcar de Barrameda (river mouth, Manzanilla only).","f":"The three towns of the Sherry triangle"},{"b":"Fino and Manzanilla: cold, fresh bottle, drink within days. Amontillado: cool. Oloroso and Palo Cortado: cellar temperature. PX: slightly chilled, small pour.","f":"Serving the styles"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Palomino","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pedro Ximénez","role":"lead"},{"name":"Moscatel","role":"minor"},{"name":"Beba, Perruno and Vigiriega","role":"rising"}],"index":10,"name":"Jerez (Sherry)","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Jerez (Sherry)","exp":"Oloroso is fortified past flor's survival and ages in contact with air from the first day. Fino and Manzanilla live under flor; Amontillado begins under flor and finishes without it.","id":"r-3hqbetai","opts":["Oloroso","Amontillado","Manzanilla","Fino"],"q":"Which Sherry style is fortified to about seventeen per cent from the start of its ageing?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Jerez (Sherry)","exp":"Manzanilla is Fino aged at the mouth of the Guadalquivir in Sanlúcar, where the humid air keeps flor thick all year; the other three are production towns for Jerez.","id":"r-diwmsbrq","opts":["Sanlúcar de Barrameda","Chipiona","El Puerto de Santa María","Jerez de la Frontera"],"q":"In which town must Manzanilla be aged?"},{"accept":["Albariza","albariza","albarizas","albariza chalk","albariza marl"],"ans":"Albariza","cat":"Jerez (Sherry)","exp":"Albariza is the pale marl of chalk, clay and silica that stores winter rain under a summer crust; barros and arenas are the clay and sand soils of lesser rank.","id":"r-oy90w2xn","q":"What is the name of the white chalky soil on which the best Palomino is grown?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Jerez (Sherry)","exp":"Palomino Fino, neutral and low in acid, is the canvas for flor and solera. Pedro Ximénez and Moscatel make the sweet wines; Airén is the plateau's brandy grape.","id":"r-71uwk1ah","opts":["Moscatel","Airén","Palomino","Pedro Ximénez"],"q":"Which grape makes the dry styles of Sherry?"},{"accept":["30 years","thirty years","30","thirty"],"ans":"30 years","cat":"Jerez (Sherry)","exp":"VORS, Very Old Rare Sherry, certifies thirty years of average age by analysis and tasting panel; VOS is twenty. Both apply only to Amontillado, Oloroso, Palo Cortado and PX.","id":"r-6f4o88tv","q":"What is the certified minimum average age of a VORS Sherry?","sa":1},{"accept":["Flor","flor","the flor","flor yeast","velo de flor"],"ans":"Flor","cat":"Jerez (Sherry)","exp":"Flor consumes alcohol and glycerol, produces acetaldehyde and seals the wine from oxygen, which keeps Fino and Manzanilla pale, dry and saline.","id":"r-zohpnywo","q":"What is the living film of yeast that grows on the surface of a Fino cask called?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Jerez (Sherry)","exp":"Amontillado is a Fino whose flor died or was removed, refortified and aged oxidatively: dry, amber and nutty. Fino never leaves flor, Oloroso never had it, PX is a sweet raisined wine.","id":"r-6ey8bz8g","opts":["Pedro Ximénez","Oloroso","Fino","Amontillado"],"q":"Which Sherry style began under flor and finished its ageing in contact with air?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Jerez (Sherry)","exp":"PX must carry at least 212 grams; Cream sits at 115 to 140, Pale Cream and Medium top out at 115, and Moscatel needs at least 160.","id":"r-kul1dkqk","opts":["140","212","160","115"],"q":"What is the minimum residual sugar of a Pedro Ximénez Sherry in grams per litre?"}],"sheet":"spain","slug":"jerez","summary":"Sherry is the fortified wine of the far south west, grown on blinding white albariza chalk around Jerez de la Frontera between the Guadalquivir and the Atlantic. Palomino makes every dry style; Pedro Ximénez and Moscatel make the sweet ones. The strength of fortification decides the biology: at about fifteen per cent a yeast veil called flor grows and keeps Fino and Manzanilla pale and bone dry, at seventeen or more the flor dies and Oloroso ages in contact with air. Almost everything passes through a solera, a fractional blend of casks that has no vintage. Manzanilla is Fino aged in the sea air of Sanlúcar de Barrameda. Jerez was Spain's first regulated denomination, in 1933."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Young, not green: a wine meant to be drunk within a year of harvest. It can be white, red, rosé or sparkling.","f":"What does verde mean in Vinho Verde?"},{"b":"Cool, very wet Atlantic; granite weathered to acid sand. High training lifts the fruit above the damp.","f":"Vinho Verde: climate and soil in a breath"},{"b":"Monção e Melgaço, on the Minho river in the north-east, sheltered and drier than the coast.","f":"The sub-region of Alvarinho"},{"b":"Alvarinho, Loureiro, Trajadura, Arinto (called Pedernã), Avesso and Azal.","f":"The white grapes of Vinho Verde"},{"b":"Vinhão, a teinturier: opaque purple, tart, foaming, drunk cold from a bowl. It is Sousão in the Douro.","f":"The red grape of Vinho Verde"},{"b":"Nine: Monção e Melgaço, Lima, Cávado, Ave, Basto, Sousa, Amarante, Baião and Paiva, most of them named for rivers.","f":"How many sub-regions does DOC Vinho Verde name?"},{"b":"The Minho. Rías Baixas lies across it, where Alvarinho is Albariño.","f":"The river that borders Spain in Vinho Verde"},{"b":"Vinho Regional Minho.","f":"The IGP of the Vinho Verde region"},{"b":"The Lima, around Ponte de Lima.","f":"Loureiro's home valley"},{"b":"Once malolactic fermentation in bottle; now a measured dose of carbon dioxide allowed at bottling.","f":"Where the prickle comes from"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Alvarinho","role":"lead"},{"name":"Loureiro","role":"lead"},{"name":"Trajadura","role":"blending"},{"name":"Arinto","role":"blending"},{"name":"Avesso","role":"rising"},{"name":"Azal","role":"minor"},{"name":"Vinhão","role":"lead"},{"name":"Espadeiro","role":"minor"}],"index":0,"name":"Vinho Verde","questions":[{"accept":["Monção e Melgaço","Moncao e Melgaco","Monção and Melgaço","Monção","Melgaço","Moncao","Melgaco"],"ans":"Monção e Melgaço","cat":"Vinho Verde","exp":"Monção e Melgaço sits inland on the Minho river in the shelter of the Peneda hills, drier and warmer than the coast, and Alvarinho ripens there. Lima is Loureiro country, Baião grows Avesso and Basto is the cool inland sub-region.","id":"r-v2il0nrh","q":"Which sub-region of Vinho Verde is the home of varietal Alvarinho?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Vinho Verde","exp":"Vinhão is a teinturier with red flesh and gives the foaming purple red drunk cold from bowls; it is Sousão in the Douro. Espadeiro is mostly for rosé, Baga belongs to Bairrada and Castelão to the sandy south.","id":"r-n02c5oi8","opts":["Castelão","Vinhão","Espadeiro","Baga"],"q":"Which grape makes the opaque, tart red Vinho Verde?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Vinho Verde","exp":"Nine: Monção e Melgaço, Lima, Cávado, Ave, Basto, Sousa, Amarante, Baião and Paiva, most of them named for rivers. The Dão, for comparison, has seven.","id":"r-blhb4t5p","opts":["Eight","Ten","Seven","Nine"],"q":"How many sub-regions does DOC Vinho Verde contain?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Vinho Verde","exp":"Granite weathered to coarse acid sand is the soil of the Minho, as it is across the river in Rías Baixas. Schist is the Douro's rock, limestone Bairrada's and basalt Madeira's.","id":"r-z6p4qjyx","opts":["Limestone","Schist","Granite","Volcanic basalt"],"q":"Which rock underlies most of the vineyards of Vinho Verde?"},{"accept":["Young","Young wine","Green meaning young","Youthful","New","Green in the sense of young"],"ans":"Young","cat":"Vinho Verde","exp":"Verde describes the wine's youth, not a colour: it is made to be drunk within the year, light and fresh, and the category includes red and rosé as well as white.","id":"r-7grhf1fv","q":"What does the word verde mean on a bottle of Vinho Verde?","sa":1},{"accept":["Minho","Vinho Regional Minho","Regional Minho","IGP Minho","Minho IGP"],"ans":"Minho","cat":"Vinho Verde","exp":"Vinho Regional Minho is the IGP of the north-west, named for the province and the river; it gives fuller wines and wider grape choices than the DOC, and held the varietal Alvarinhos grown outside Monção e Melgaço before 2021.","id":"r-2y8g9irm","q":"Which Vinho Regional covers the same territory as DOC Vinho Verde?","sa":1},{"accept":["Lima","Lima valley","Ponte de Lima","the Lima","Vale do Lima"],"ans":"Lima","cat":"Vinho Verde","exp":"Loureiro, the laurel-scented white, has its home in the Lima valley west of Monção, where a few estates make varietal wines with lees and age. Alvarinho's home is the neighbouring Monção e Melgaço.","id":"r-dchvt61s","q":"Which sub-region of Vinho Verde is the heartland of Loureiro?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Vinho Verde","exp":"Albariño of Rías Baixas and Alvarinho of Monção e Melgaço are the same grape on two banks of the Minho. Treixadura is Trajadura, Loureira is Loureiro, and Godello is Gouveio in the Douro.","id":"r-ppv3tufm","opts":["Albariño","Godello","Treixadura","Loureira"],"q":"Across the Minho river in Spain, Alvarinho is known by which name?"}],"sheet":"portugal","slug":"vinho-verde","summary":"Vinho Verde is the Minho, Portugal's far north-west between the Minho river on the Spanish border and the Douro, the wettest wine country in the land. Granite, rain and high training make a wine that is light, sharp, low in alcohol and faintly prickly, bottled young: verde means young, not green, and it can be red or rosé as well as white. The region was demarcated in 1908 and holds nine sub-regions named for its rivers and towns. Monção e Melgaço, inland on the Minho in the hills' rain shadow, is the home of Alvarinho and the serious tier; Loureiro leads in the Lima valley; the inky, tart red is Vinhão. The IGP that covers it is Vinho Regional Minho."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Ruby: kept from air, red and fruity, Vintage aged in bottle, decanted. Tawny: aged in wood with air, amber and nutty, ready when bottled.","f":"Port's two families"},{"b":"Fermentation stopped at about half the sugar with grape spirit at 77 per cent, roughly one part in five; the wine finishes at 19 to 22 per cent.","f":"How Port is fortified"},{"b":"Baixo Corgo (wettest, fertile), Cima Corgo (Pinhão, the heart), Douro Superior (driest, to the Spanish border).","f":"The three sub-regions, downstream to upstream"},{"b":"Touriga Nacional, Touriga Franca (the most planted), Tinta Roriz, Tinta Barroca, Tinto Cão.","f":"The five favoured Port grapes"},{"b":"LBV: between the fourth and sixth year after harvest. Vintage: in the second or third year, then decades in bottle.","f":"LBV and Vintage: the bottling windows"},{"b":"A tawny from a single harvest, at least seven years in wood, labelled with its bottling date. Not a Vintage Port.","f":"What is a Colheita Port?"},{"b":"An indication of the blend's average age and character, approved by the IVDP's panel, not a vintage. Fifty Years was added in 2022.","f":"What a tawny's 10, 20, 30, 40 or 50 Years means"},{"b":"1756 demarcation by Pombal; 1933 the Port institute; 1952 Barca Velha; 1982 DOC Douro; 1986 ageing and shipping allowed from the Douro; 2001 World Heritage.","f":"The Douro's dates"},{"b":"Cadastro: the register grading every plot by letter, A the best. Beneficio: the yearly licence that says how much must may be made into Port, shared out by grade.","f":"Cadastro and beneficio"},{"b":"In the lodges of Vila Nova de Gaia, across the river from Porto; since 1986 also in the Douro itself.","f":"Where Port is aged by tradition"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Touriga Nacional","role":"lead"},{"name":"Touriga Franca","role":"lead"},{"name":"Tinta Roriz","role":"lead"},{"name":"Tinta Barroca","role":"blending"},{"name":"Tinto Cão","role":"blending"},{"name":"Sousão","role":"blending"},{"name":"Tinta Amarela","role":"minor"},{"name":"Gouveio","role":"minor"},{"name":"Rabigato","role":"minor"},{"name":"Viosinho","role":"minor"},{"name":"Moscatel Galego","role":"minor"}],"index":1,"name":"Douro / Port","questions":[{"a":1,"cat":"Douro / Port","exp":"The Douro Superior runs from the Valeira gorge east to Barca d'Alva on the border, with under 400 millimetres of rain. The Baixo Corgo is the wet western end, the Cima Corgo the heart around Pinhão; Trás-os-Montes is the separate region to the north.","id":"r-guge9aad","opts":["Baixo Corgo","Douro Superior","Trás-os-Montes","Cima Corgo"],"q":"Which sub-region of the Douro is the driest and reaches the Spanish border?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Douro / Port","exp":"Pombal demarcated the valley in 1756 and put its trade under a chartered company. 1703 is the Methuen Treaty, 1908 the year Vinho Verde, Dão and others were demarcated, and 1986 Portugal's entry into the European Community.","id":"r-i055kxgx","opts":["1908","1703","1986","1756"],"q":"In which year was the Douro demarcated by the Marquis of Pombal?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Douro / Port","exp":"Colheita is the single-harvest tawny, oxidative and amber, bottled with its bottling date. Vintage is bottled in its second or third year, LBV in its fourth to sixth, and Crusted is a blend of years.","id":"r-augwbq33","opts":["Colheita","Late Bottled Vintage","Crusted","Vintage"],"q":"Which style of Port comes from a single harvest and is aged at least seven years in wood?"},{"accept":["77 per cent","77","77%","Seventy-seven per cent","77 percent","77 abv","77 per cent abv"],"ans":"77 per cent","cat":"Douro / Port","exp":"Aguardente at 77 per cent is added when the must has fermented about half its sugar, near one part spirit to four of wine, so the Port finishes at 19 to 22 per cent with its own grape sugar intact.","id":"r-ov7y6rj0","q":"At what strength is the grape spirit used to fortify Port?","sa":1},{"accept":["Pinhão","Pinhao","Pinhão town"],"ans":"Pinhão","cat":"Douro / Port","exp":"Pinhão sits where the Pinhão river meets the Douro, with the great quintas of the Cima Corgo on the slopes around it. Peso da Régua is the town of the Baixo Corgo downstream.","id":"r-rmurhzz8","q":"Which town at the mouth of its namesake river is the heart of the Cima Corgo?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Douro / Port","exp":"LBV spends four to six years in wood before bottling, which is why it is Late. Vintage Port is bottled in its second or third year, and seven years is the minimum in wood for a Colheita.","id":"r-yx6dlue8","opts":["The fourth and sixth","The second and third","The first and second","The seventh and tenth"],"q":"Between which years after the harvest must a Late Bottled Vintage Port be bottled?"},{"accept":["Beneficio","Benefício","The beneficio","Beneficio licence"],"ans":"Beneficio","cat":"Douro / Port","exp":"The IVDP sets the beneficio each year and shares it out by the cadastro grade of each vineyard, so the best plots may fortify most of their crop and the rest goes to dry Douro wine.","id":"r-rlbnb9nk","q":"What is the name of the annual authorisation that fixes how much must may be made into Port?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Douro / Port","exp":"Touriga Franca, late ripening and dependable on hot sites, covers more of the valley than any other grape. Touriga Nacional is the most prized but lower yielding, Tinta Roriz is Tempranillo, and Tinto Cão is a small, fine component.","id":"r-ksi8jaas","opts":["Tinto Cão","Touriga Nacional","Touriga Franca","Tinta Roriz"],"q":"Which grape is the most widely planted in the Douro?"}],"sheet":"portugal","slug":"douro","summary":"The Douro is the river valley east of Porto, behind the Serra do Marão, where the Atlantic stops and schist terraces climb from the water. Demarcated in 1756, it is one of the oldest regulated wine regions anywhere and the home of Port, a red wine fortified during fermentation to keep its sugar and aged by tradition in the lodges of Vila Nova de Gaia. Since Barca Velha in 1952, and formally since DOC Douro in 1982, the same grapes make dry wines. Three sub-regions run upstream, Baixo Corgo, Cima Corgo and Douro Superior, each hotter and drier than the last. Port splits into two families: Ruby, kept from air and aged in bottle, and Tawny, aged in wood."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"A granite plateau around Viseu at 400 to 700 metres, sheltered by the Caramulo and the Serra da Estrela, vineyards among pine forest.","f":"Dão in a breath: place, soil, altitude"},{"b":"Touriga Nacional, Jaen (Mencía), Alfrocheiro and Tinta Roriz (Tempranillo), with Rufete and Bastardo in old plots.","f":"The red grapes of the Dão"},{"b":"Encruzado: textured, mineral, barrel fermented at its best, ages ten years.","f":"The white grape the exam places in the Dão"},{"b":"Alva, Besteiros, Castendo, Serra da Estrela, Silgueiros, Terras de Azurara and Terras de Senhorim.","f":"The seven sub-regions of the Dão"},{"b":"1908, the same year as Vinho Verde, Colares, Carcavelos, Setúbal and Madeira; the Douro had gone first in 1756.","f":"When was the Dão demarcated?"},{"b":"Vinho Regional Terras do Dão.","f":"The IGP of the Dão"},{"b":"From the 1940s the law pushed grapes into co-operatives; after Portugal joined the European Community in 1986 the estates took their fruit back and revived the region.","f":"Why the Dão went quiet, and when it woke"},{"b":"The Dão, at the village of Tourigo near Tondela in the Besteiros sub-region.","f":"Touriga Nacional's claimed birthplace"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Touriga Nacional","role":"lead"},{"name":"Jaen","role":"lead"},{"name":"Alfrocheiro","role":"blending"},{"name":"Tinta Roriz","role":"blending"},{"name":"Rufete","role":"minor"},{"name":"Bastardo","role":"minor"},{"name":"Encruzado","role":"lead"},{"name":"Bical","role":"blending"},{"name":"Cercial","role":"minor"},{"name":"Malvasia Fina","role":"minor"}],"index":2,"name":"Dão","questions":[{"a":3,"cat":"Dão","exp":"Encruzado is the Dão's own white, textured and ageworthy. Arinto is Bucelas and the Minho's Pedernã, Alvarinho is Vinho Verde's, and Antão Vaz belongs to the Alentejo.","id":"r-8bx06ncj","opts":["Antão Vaz","Arinto","Alvarinho","Encruzado"],"q":"Which white grape is the signature of the Dão?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Dão","exp":"The Serra do Caramulo and the Buçaco hills stand between the Dão and the coast; the Estrela lies to the south-east. The Marão shelters the Douro, and Montejunto the Alenquer vineyards of Lisboa.","id":"r-b6q5wpdo","opts":["Estrela","Montejunto","Caramulo","Marão"],"q":"Which range of hills shelters the Dão from the Atlantic weather on its western side?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Dão","exp":"Jaen is Mencía, the fragrant red of Bierzo and Galicia, early ripening and supple in a Dão blend. Tempranillo is Tinta Roriz in Portugal and Godello is Gouveio; Garnacha is barely grown in the Dão.","id":"r-10vdn8gk","opts":["Tempranillo","Mencía","Garnacha","Godello"],"q":"Jaen of the Dão is the same grape as which Spanish variety?"},{"accept":["Terras do Dão","Terras do Dao","Vinho Regional Terras do Dão","Regional Terras do Dão","Dão regional"],"ans":"Terras do Dão","cat":"Dão","exp":"Terras do Dão, lands of the Dão, is the IGP that surrounds the DOC and takes wines made outside its grape list. Beira Atlântico is Bairrada's IGP and Terras da Beira lies to the east.","id":"r-n73uylgi","q":"Which Vinho Regional covers the territory of the Dão?","sa":1},{"accept":["Viseu","Viseu city","City of Viseu"],"ans":"Viseu","cat":"Dão","exp":"Viseu is the cathedral city at the centre of the plateau; the seven sub-regions lie around it, and Nelas, Mangualde, Tondela and Seia are the smaller towns a label may name.","id":"r-0w4kg7zh","q":"Which city sits at the centre of the Dão plateau and houses its regional commission?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Dão","exp":"The Dão was demarcated in 1908, the same year as Vinho Verde, Colares and Carcavelos. 1756 is the Douro's date, 1979 Bairrada's, and 1986 the year Portugal joined the European Community.","id":"r-tmptn1fi","opts":["1908","1756","1986","1979"],"q":"In which year was the Dão demarcated?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Dão","exp":"Touriga Nacional's name is tied to the village of Tourigo near Tondela, and the Dão claims it as a native though the Douro made it famous. Alicante Bouschet is a French crossing, Trincadeira the Alentejo's grape, and Tinta Barroca the Douro's.","id":"r-niva999e","opts":["Touriga Nacional","Trincadeira","Tinta Barroca","Alicante Bouschet"],"q":"Which red grape is traditionally claimed to have originated in the Dão?"},{"accept":["Seven","7","seven sub-regions"],"ans":"Seven","cat":"Dão","exp":"Seven: Alva, Besteiros, Castendo, Serra da Estrela, Silgueiros, Terras de Azurara and Terras de Senhorim. Vinho Verde, by contrast, has nine.","id":"r-hgpig4rf","q":"How many sub-regions does DOC Dão contain?","sa":1}],"sheet":"portugal","slug":"dao","summary":"The Dão is a granite plateau in the centre-north of Portugal around the city of Viseu, ringed by mountains that shut out both the Atlantic rain and the inland extremes: the Caramulo to the west, the Serra da Estrela to the south-east. Vineyards sit at 400 to 700 metres in clearings among pine forest, demarcated in 1908, divided into seven sub-regions. Touriga Nacional is said to have been born here, and with Jaen, Alfrocheiro and Tinta Roriz it makes fragrant, firm, long-lived reds; Encruzado is Portugal's noblest white. Decades of co-operative rule dulled the region until estates revived it in the 1990s. The IGP is Terras do Dão."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Baga: late ripening, tight bunches, high acid and tannin, tart cherry and tobacco, hard young and long-lived; often called the Nebbiolo of the Atlantic.","f":"Bairrada's red grape and what it is like"},{"b":"Heavy clay (barro) over limestone, with sand near the coast; cool, wet Atlantic weather with autumn rain as the hazard.","f":"Bairrada: soil and climate"},{"b":"Traditional method sparkling wine from Bical, Maria Gomes, Arinto and Baga, served with leitão, roast suckling pig, at Mealhada.","f":"Bairrada's second signature"},{"b":"At least 50 per cent Baga, the rest from the traditional grapes: Alfrocheiro, Camarate, Castelão, Jaen and Touriga Nacional.","f":"What Bairrada Clássico guarantees"},{"b":"Demarcated 1979; rules opened to international grapes in 2003, when Clássico was created.","f":"Bairrada's two dates"},{"b":"Bical, Maria Gomes (Fernão Pires), Arinto and Cercial.","f":"The white grapes of Bairrada"},{"b":"Vinho Regional Beira Atlântico.","f":"The IGP around Bairrada"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Baga","role":"lead"},{"name":"Touriga Nacional","role":"blending"},{"name":"Alfrocheiro","role":"blending"},{"name":"Castelão","role":"minor"},{"name":"Camarate","role":"minor"},{"name":"Maria Gomes","role":"lead"},{"name":"Bical","role":"lead"},{"name":"Arinto","role":"blending"},{"name":"Cercial","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"}],"index":3,"name":"Bairrada","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Bairrada","exp":"Baga, late ripening, high in acid and tannin, is Bairrada's grape and the base of its Clássico reds and much of its sparkling wine. Touriga Nacional is a permitted partner, Castelão the sandy south's grape, Trincadeira the Alentejo's.","id":"r-oqdsoyyn","opts":["Baga","Castelão","Trincadeira","Touriga Nacional"],"q":"Which red grape is the signature of Bairrada?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Bairrada","exp":"Leitão à Bairrada, suckling pig roasted crisp, is served with the region's bone-dry espumante in the restaurants of Mealhada. Bacalhau is salt cod, caldo verde the Minho's kale soup, and francesinha Porto's sandwich.","id":"r-n1mluw2j","opts":["Francesinha","Bacalhau à Brás","Leitão","Caldo verde"],"q":"Which dish is the classic partner of Bairrada's sparkling wine along the road through Mealhada?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Bairrada","exp":"Heavy clay, the barro that names the region, over limestone gives Baga its structure; sand nearer the coast gives lighter wines. Granite is the Dão's and Vinho Verde's rock, schist the Douro's, basalt Madeira's.","id":"r-nu1pvm37","opts":["Volcanic basalt","Clay-limestone","Granite sand","Schist slabs"],"q":"Which soil type underlies the classic Baga vineyards of Bairrada?"},{"accept":["1979","Nineteen seventy-nine"],"ans":"1979","cat":"Bairrada","exp":"Bairrada was demarcated in 1979, seventy years after the Dão and Vinho Verde; its rules were rewritten in 2003 to admit international grapes and create the Clássico label.","id":"r-l7uc2lhc","q":"In which year was Bairrada demarcated?","sa":1},{"accept":["Beira Atlântico","Beira Atlantico","Vinho Regional Beira Atlântico","Regional Beira Atlântico"],"ans":"Beira Atlântico","cat":"Bairrada","exp":"Beira Atlântico is the IGP of the coastal Beiras around Bairrada; Terras do Dão is the Dão's and Terras da Beira lies inland to the east.","id":"r-hdnm4nt4","q":"Which Vinho Regional surrounds the Bairrada DOC?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Bairrada","exp":"Maria Gomes is Fernão Pires, Portugal's most planted white, aromatic and soft. Malvasia Fina belongs to the Douro's whites, Loureiro to Vinho Verde, and Encruzado to the Dão.","id":"r-ey83v8v2","opts":["Encruzado","Malvasia Fina","Loureiro","Fernão Pires"],"q":"Maria Gomes, the white grape of Bairrada, is the local name of which variety?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Bairrada","exp":"Clássico, created with the 2003 rules, requires at least 50 per cent Baga with the rest from the traditional grapes. Reserva is a ripeness word, Garrafeira an ageing word, and Escolha a producer's selection.","id":"r-pl64yi0k","opts":["Clássico","Reserva","Escolha","Garrafeira"],"q":"Which label term marks a traditional Bairrada red with at least half Baga?"},{"accept":["Traditional method","Método tradicional","Metodo tradicional","Método clássico","Metodo classico","Classic method","Champagne method","Second fermentation in bottle"],"ans":"Traditional method","cat":"Bairrada","exp":"Bairrada espumante is made by the traditional method, a second fermentation in the bottle with time on the lees, from Bical, Maria Gomes and Arinto for the whites or from Baga for rosé and blanc de noirs; the tank method is not the region's way.","id":"r-098d1u6u","q":"By which method is DOC Bairrada's sparkling wine made?","sa":1}],"sheet":"portugal","slug":"bairrada","summary":"Bairrada lies on the flat coastal plain between the hills of the Dão and the Atlantic, around Anadia and Mealhada, on the heavy clay, barro, that gives it its name, over limestone, with sand nearer the sea. Demarcated in 1979, it is the home of Baga, a late, small-berried red of high acid and high tannin that makes tart, tobacco-scented wines which age for decades and are often called the Nebbiolo of the Atlantic. It is also Portugal's sparkling capital: traditional method espumante from Baga, Bical and Maria Gomes, poured with the roast suckling pig of Mealhada. The rules were opened in 2003 to other grapes, and Clássico marks the traditional Baga-led wine. The IGP is Beira Atlântico."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Estremadura, the old province's name. Lisboa is the Vinho Regional of the coast north of the capital, holding nine small DOCs.","f":"The former name of Lisboa's regional wine"},{"b":"Alenquer, Arruda, Bucelas, Carcavelos, Colares, Encostas d'Aire, Lourinhã, Óbidos, Torres Vedras.","f":"The nine DOCs of Lisboa"},{"b":"White only, at least 75 per cent Arinto, from the Trancão valley north of Lisbon; Lisbon hock to the Georgians.","f":"Bucelas: grape and rule"},{"b":"They grow in dune sand under Sintra, where phylloxera cannot live, so Ramisco, and Malvasia for the white, is planted on its own roots.","f":"Why are the vines of Colares ungrafted?"},{"b":"A fortified wine from a few hectares on the Estoril coast, demarcated 1908, revived at Oeiras.","f":"Carcavelos is which style?"},{"b":"Lourinhã, for aguardente alone, a brandy demarcation of the kind Cognac and Armagnac hold.","f":"The brandy DOC of Lisboa"},{"b":"Alenquer, east of the Serra de Montejunto, which breaks the Atlantic wind; Touriga Nacional, Aragonez, Syrah and Cabernet ripen there.","f":"Lisboa's best red country"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Arinto","role":"lead"},{"name":"Fernão Pires","role":"blending"},{"name":"Vital","role":"minor"},{"name":"Malvasia","role":"minor"},{"name":"Ramisco","role":"minor"},{"name":"Castelão","role":"lead"},{"name":"Aragonez","role":"lead"},{"name":"Touriga Nacional","role":"blending"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"rising"},{"name":"Tinta Miúda","role":"minor"},{"name":"Alicante Bouschet","role":"minor"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"minor"}],"index":4,"name":"Lisboa","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Lisboa","exp":"Bucelas, north of Lisbon in the Trancão valley, requires at least 75 per cent Arinto and makes only white wine. Colares makes own-rooted reds and whites on sand, Carcavelos is fortified, and Alenquer is red country.","id":"r-jebvn5gb","opts":["Bucelas","Colares","Carcavelos","Alenquer"],"q":"Which Lisboa DOC is white only and built on the Arinto grape?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Lisboa","exp":"Colares, beneath the Sintra hills, plants Ramisco in dune sand where phylloxera cannot survive, so the vines are own-rooted. Bucelas is Arinto white, Óbidos is known for sparkling wine, and Arruda for everyday reds.","id":"r-u0fhpsbb","opts":["Óbidos","Colares","Bucelas","Arruda"],"q":"Which DOC grows ungrafted Ramisco vines in coastal sand dunes?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Lisboa","exp":"Lourinhã is a DOC for aguardente, a brandy demarcation of the kind Cognac and Armagnac hold. Torres Vedras, Encostas d'Aire and Alenquer are wine DOCs.","id":"r-cb5oryow","opts":["Encostas d'Aire","Alenquer","Lourinhã","Torres Vedras"],"q":"Which appellation within Lisboa covers grape brandy rather than wine?"},{"accept":["Estremadura","Vinho Regional Estremadura","Estremadura region"],"ans":"Estremadura","cat":"Lisboa","exp":"The regional wine was Estremadura, the old province's name, until it was renamed for the capital so that buyers abroad could place it; Spanish Extremadura, across the border, is a different place.","id":"r-sds7jua0","q":"What was the former name of the Vinho Regional now called Lisboa?","sa":1},{"accept":["Fortified","Fortified wine","Vinho licoroso","Sweet fortified wine","A fortified sweet wine"],"ans":"Fortified","cat":"Lisboa","exp":"Carcavelos is a fortified, nutty, off-dry to sweet wine aged in wood, demarcated in 1908 and nearly lost to the suburbs before its revival at Oeiras.","id":"r-zrauikpw","q":"Carcavelos, the small DOC on the Estoril coast, makes which style of wine?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Lisboa","exp":"The Serra de Montejunto rises to 666 metres between the coast and Alenquer and is why the reds ripen there. Sintra's granite shelters Colares, Arrábida belongs to the Setúbal peninsula, and the Estrela to the Dão.","id":"r-tvgfvoqo","opts":["Montejunto","Estrela","Sintra","Arrábida"],"q":"Which range shelters the Alenquer vineyards from the Atlantic wind?"},{"accept":["Nine","9","nine DOCs"],"ans":"Nine","cat":"Lisboa","exp":"Nine: Alenquer, Arruda, Bucelas, Carcavelos, Colares, Encostas d'Aire, Lourinhã, Óbidos and Torres Vedras, one of which, Lourinhã, is for brandy.","id":"r-tjb888kl","q":"How many DOCs lie within the Lisboa Vinho Regional?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Lisboa","exp":"Bucelas requires at least 75 per cent Arinto, the balance from local whites; 85 per cent is the usual varietal labelling threshold and 100 per cent is the rule for Rías Baixas Albariño, not here.","id":"r-e2zwiupd","opts":["50 per cent","85 per cent","100 per cent","75 per cent"],"q":"What minimum share of Arinto must a Bucelas white contain?"}],"sheet":"portugal","slug":"lisboa","summary":"Lisboa is the strip of coast and hills north of the capital, formerly the province of Estremadura, a Vinho Regional of volume that holds nine small DOCs, three of them historic and tiny. The Atlantic wind rules it; the Serra de Montejunto shelters the best red country at Alenquer. Bucelas is a white of Arinto; Colares grows ungrafted Ramisco in sand dunes under Sintra; Carcavelos is a fortified wine nearly swallowed by the suburbs; Arruda, Torres Vedras, Óbidos and Encostas d'Aire make the everyday wine, and Lourinhã is an appellation for brandy alone. Castelão, Aragonez, Touriga Nacional and Syrah lead the reds; Arinto, Fernão Pires and Vital the whites."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Ribatejo, the banks of the Tagus; the DOC and the Vinho Regional were both renamed Tejo in 2009.","f":"The Tejo's name before 2009"},{"b":"Bairro, clay and limestone hills on the north bank; Campo or Lezíria, the alluvial floodplain; Charneca, the sandy, dry south bank.","f":"Three zones of the Tejo and their soils"},{"b":"Fernão Pires, aromatic and soft, usually blended with Arinto for acidity; it is Maria Gomes in Bairrada.","f":"Lead white grape of the Tejo"},{"b":"Castelão and Trincadeira, with Touriga Nacional since the 1990s; Syrah and Cabernet Sauvignon go mostly into the Vinho Regional blends.","f":"Traditional red grapes of the Tejo"},{"b":"The regional tier carries the longer grape list (Syrah, Chardonnay and Sauvignon Blanc among them) and the looser rules; the DOC's lists are narrower and drawn sub-region by sub-region.","f":"Why most Tejo wine is Vinho Regional"},{"b":"Almeirim, Cartaxo, Chamusca, Coruche, Santarém and Tomar.","f":"Six sub-regions of DOC do Tejo"},{"b":"The Charneca, on the south bank: hot, poor and well drained, home to Castelão and the ripe international reds.","f":"Which Tejo zone is the sandy one?"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Fernão Pires","role":"lead"},{"name":"Castelão","role":"lead"},{"name":"Trincadeira","role":"lead"},{"name":"Touriga Nacional","role":"rising"},{"name":"Arinto","role":"blending"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"rising"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"minor"},{"name":"Tempranillo","role":"blending"}],"index":5,"name":"Tejo","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Tejo","exp":"Ribatejo, the banks of the Tagus, became Tejo in 2009. Estremadura became Lisboa and Terras do Sado became Península de Setúbal in the same wave of renaming; Beira Interior kept its name.","id":"r-b7avjow7","opts":["Ribatejo","Estremadura","Terras do Sado","Beira Interior"],"q":"Under which name was the Tejo wine region known until 2009?"},{"accept":["Tagus","the Tagus","River Tagus","Tagus River"],"ans":"Tagus","cat":"Tejo","exp":"The Tejo is the Portuguese name of the Tagus, which rises in Spain as the Tajo and meets the Atlantic at Lisbon; the Bairro hills lie north of it and the Charneca sand south.","id":"r-vnu9n0kr","q":"What is the English name of the river that gives the Tejo region its name?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Tejo","exp":"The Charneca is the poor sand and heath south of the river, warm and well drained. The Bairro is the clay and limestone hills north of it, and Campo and Lezíria are two names for the alluvial floodplain.","id":"r-jsro38ex","opts":["Bairro","Charneca","Campo","Lezíria"],"q":"Which zone of the Tejo is the sandy, dry plain of the south bank?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Tejo","exp":"Fernão Pires, aromatic and soft, is the Tejo's and Lisboa's white workhorse, usually with Arinto for acidity. Encruzado belongs to the Dão, Alvarinho to Vinho Verde and Antão Vaz to the Alentejo.","id":"r-8lgklvcl","opts":["Antão Vaz","Alvarinho","Fernão Pires","Encruzado"],"q":"Which white grape leads the blends of the Tejo?"},{"accept":["Maria Gomes","Maria-Gomes","Maria Gomez"],"ans":"Maria Gomes","cat":"Tejo","exp":"The same aromatic white carries two names: Fernão Pires in the Tejo, Lisboa and the south, Maria Gomes in Bairrada, where it goes into the sparkling wine.","id":"r-idv5olp9","q":"Under which name is Fernão Pires grown in Bairrada?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Tejo","exp":"Trincadeira, spicy and peppery when it ripens cleanly but prone to rot in a damp autumn, is Tinta Amarela in the Douro, where it goes into Port. Castelão is the Charneca's red, long sold as Periquita; Baga belongs to Bairrada and Alfrocheiro to the Dão.","id":"r-id85ihuk","opts":["Trincadeira","Castelão","Baga","Alfrocheiro"],"q":"Which red grape of the Tejo is known as Tinta Amarela in the Douro?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Tejo","exp":"The DOC's grape lists are narrower and drawn sub-region by sub-region, while Vinho Regional Tejo carries a longer list, Syrah and Chardonnay among them, and looser rules, which suits the estates' export blends; the DOC covers still wine of every colour.","id":"r-q1i09knn","opts":["The DOC rules permit only white and rosé wine to carry the Tejo name","The regional tier requires two years of ageing in new oak barrels","The DOC applies only to sparkling and sweet wines of the valley","The regional tier has a longer grape list and looser rules"],"q":"Why do most Tejo wines carry the Vinho Regional Tejo label rather than the DOC?"}],"sheet":"portugal","slug":"tejo","summary":"The Tejo is the wine country of the Tagus, the river that reaches the sea at Lisbon, and until 2009 it was called the Ribatejo. It is a region of value: warm, fertile and flat along the river, sandier and hotter on the south bank, hillier and cooler on the north. Fernão Pires leads the whites, Castelão and Trincadeira the traditional reds, and Touriga Nacional, Syrah and Cabernet Sauvignon the modern ones. Most of what is poured carries the Vinho Regional Tejo label rather than the DOC, because the regional tier has the longer grape list and the looser rules. For the exam, hold the three zones, the lead grapes and the old name."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Muscat of Alexandria, the big-berried Muscat, under its local name; Moscatel Roxo is its rare pink-skinned relative.","f":"Moscatel de Setúbal: which Muscat?"},{"b":"After fortification the skins go back into the wine for months, through the winter, for a deep orange-peel aroma and grip.","f":"The step that sets Moscatel de Setúbal apart from a vin doux naturel"},{"b":"Setúbal, the fortified Muscat; Palmela, Castelão table wine on sand. Around them, Vinho Regional Península de Setúbal.","f":"The two DOCs of the Setúbal peninsula"},{"b":"Two thirds, 67 per cent; Castelão is the grape sold for generations as Periquita.","f":"Minimum Castelão in a red Palmela DOC"},{"b":"Terras do Sado, after the estuary on the peninsula's south side.","f":"Península de Setúbal's name until 2009"},{"b":"At least 85 per cent of the must from the grape; a wine with 67 to 85 per cent is labelled plainly Setúbal.","f":"Moscatel percentage for the name Moscatel de Setúbal"},{"b":"José Maria da Fonseca, Azeitão, founded 1834: Moscatel de Setúbal and Periquita. Fonseca of Vila Nova de Gaia: Port.","f":"Two Fonsecas, two wines"},{"b":"Cool, in a small glass, with almond or orange desserts, crème caramel or blue cheese; an opened bottle keeps for weeks.","f":"Serving Moscatel de Setúbal"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Moscatel de Setúbal","role":"lead"},{"name":"Moscatel Roxo","role":"minor"},{"name":"Castelão","role":"lead"},{"name":"Fernão Pires","role":"blending"},{"name":"Arinto","role":"blending"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"rising"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"minor"},{"name":"Touriga Nacional","role":"blending"}],"index":6,"name":"Setúbal","questions":[{"a":2,"cat":"Setúbal","exp":"Moscatel de Setúbal is the local name of Muscat of Alexandria, the large-berried Muscat of hot climates. The small-berried Muscat Blanc makes Beaumes-de-Venise and Asti, Ottonel is Alsace's and Moscato Giallo is Alpine Italy's.","id":"r-4ilkxn55","opts":["Moscato Giallo","Muscat Blanc à Petits Grains","Muscat of Alexandria","Muscat Ottonel"],"q":"Which grape variety is Moscatel de Setúbal?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Setúbal","exp":"The maceration after fortification, through the winter, is the step that marks the wine: it gives the orange-peel intensity and grip that a vin doux naturel, racked off its skins at fortification, does not have.","id":"r-tbg20yfj","opts":["They are dried for a passito-style sweetening","They go back into the wine and macerate for months","They are distilled into the fortifying spirit","They are pressed again to make a second, drier wine"],"q":"What happens to the grape skins in the making of Moscatel de Setúbal after the wine has been fortified?"},{"accept":["Palmela","DOC Palmela","Palmela DOC"],"ans":"Palmela","cat":"Setúbal","exp":"Palmela is the DOC of the sandy plain north and east of the castle town, and its reds must be at least two thirds Castelão; the Setúbal DOC is for the fortified Muscat only.","id":"r-cfxv8v55","q":"Which DOC of the Setúbal peninsula covers its Castelão-led table reds grown on sand?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Setúbal","exp":"Castelão is the red of sandy southern Portugal, and Periquita is a brand of the oldest Setúbal house that became its nickname. Trincadeira is the spicy Alentejo partner, Aragonez is Tempranillo and Touriga Franca is the Douro's Port workhorse.","id":"r-h2plo3n3","opts":["Touriga Franca","Aragonez","Trincadeira","Castelão"],"q":"Which red grape makes the wine sold for generations under the brand name Periquita?"},{"accept":["Terras do Sado","Vinho Regional Terras do Sado","Terras de Sado"],"ans":"Terras do Sado","cat":"Setúbal","exp":"The regional wine took its old name from the Sado estuary on the peninsula's south side and was renamed in 2009, as Ribatejo became Tejo; Estremadura became Lisboa in the same years.","id":"r-uoai3dij","q":"What was the Vinho Regional Península de Setúbal called before 2009?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Setúbal","exp":"The Arrábida rises to about five hundred metres above the sea west of Setúbal, and its clay and limestone flanks around Azeitão ripen the Muscat. Sintra stands over the sand of Colares, the Estrela shelters the Dão and São Mamede is the Alentejo's Portalegre.","id":"r-4o7cde64","opts":["Serra da Arrábida","Serra de São Mamede","Serra da Estrela","Serra de Sintra"],"q":"Which limestone ridge along the peninsula's south coast grows the best Moscatel de Setúbal?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Setúbal","exp":"José Maria da Fonseca, founded in 1834, is the Setúbal house of Moscatel and Periquita; Bacalhôa is Azeitão's other house, known for Cabernet and aged Moscatel; Fonseca Guimaraens is the Port shipper of Gaia and Quinta do Noval a Port house of the Douro.","id":"r-cwibzvav","opts":["Quinta do Noval","José Maria da Fonseca","Bacalhôa Vinhos de Portugal","Fonseca Guimaraens"],"q":"Which house of Azeitão is the reference for Moscatel de Setúbal and created the Periquita brand?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Setúbal","exp":"The traditional names Moscatel de Setúbal and Moscatel Roxo de Setúbal need at least 85 per cent of the must from that grape; a wine with at least 67 per cent may be labelled plainly Setúbal, and 75 and 100 are not thresholds in this DOC.","id":"r-sp5wvh3p","opts":["85 per cent","100 per cent","67 per cent","75 per cent"],"q":"What is the minimum share of the named grape in a wine labelled Moscatel de Setúbal?"}],"sheet":"portugal","slug":"setubal","summary":"The Setúbal peninsula lies across the Tagus from Lisbon, between that estuary and the Sado's, with the limestone ridge of the Arrábida along its southern shore and warm sand inland. It gives the exam two things: Moscatel de Setúbal, a fortified Muscat of Alexandria macerated on its skins after fortification and aged in wood for years and sometimes for decades, and Palmela, the DOC of Castelão reds on sand. Around both sits Vinho Regional Península de Setúbal, called Terras do Sado until 2009, where Syrah, Cabernet and Touriga Nacional make the peninsula's modern reds. One family house has defined the region since 1834."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Portalegre, Borba, Redondo, Évora, Reguengos, Granja-Amareleja, Vidigueira and Moura.","f":"Eight sub-regions of DOC Alentejo"},{"b":"DOC: from one of the eight sub-regions, on the DOC's own grape list. Alentejano: the whole province, a longer list and looser rules; much fine wine chooses it.","f":"Alentejo DOC against Vinho Regional Alentejano"},{"b":"Aragonez (Tempranillo), Trincadeira and Alicante Bouschet, often with Castelão, Syrah or Touriga Nacional: ripe, soft, generous.","f":"The classic Alentejo red blend"},{"b":"A French teinturier crossing of 1866, red flesh and red juice, that found its best home on the Alentejo plain: colour, tannin, long life.","f":"Alicante Bouschet in one line"},{"b":"Antão Vaz for ripe body (Vidigueira's grape), Arinto for acidity, Roupeiro for aroma; some Verdelho and Viognier.","f":"The Alentejo's white grapes"},{"b":"A clay vessel of up to two thousand litres, lined with pitch, in which grapes ferment and rest with their skins under olive oil; opened on Saint Martin's Day, 11 November.","f":"What is a talha?"},{"b":"Portalegre: granite in the Serra de São Mamede at six to seven hundred metres, rain from the mountain, old mixed vines, picked weeks after the plain.","f":"The cool exception of the Alentejo"},{"b":"The montado of cork oak, protected by law, stripped by hand at least nine years apart; the Alentejo is the heart of the world's cork supply.","f":"Cork and the Alentejo"},{"b":"One grape, Tempranillo: Aragonez in the Alentejo and the south, Tinta Roriz in the Douro and the Dão.","f":"Aragonez and Tinta Roriz"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Aragonez","role":"lead"},{"name":"Trincadeira","role":"lead"},{"name":"Alicante Bouschet","role":"lead"},{"name":"Castelão","role":"blending"},{"name":"Touriga Nacional","role":"rising"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"rising"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"minor"},{"name":"Antão Vaz","role":"lead"},{"name":"Arinto","role":"blending"},{"name":"Roupeiro","role":"blending"}],"index":7,"name":"Alentejo","questions":[{"a":2,"cat":"Alentejo","exp":"Portalegre sits against the Spanish border at six to seven hundred metres, with rain from the mountain and old mixed vines picked weeks after the plain. Vidigueira and Moura are among the warmest sub-regions and Reguengos is the big central one.","id":"r-auezvwkz","opts":["Vidigueira","Reguengos","Portalegre","Moura"],"q":"Which sub-region of DOC Alentejo lies on granite at altitude in the Serra de São Mamede?"},{"accept":["Aragonez","Aragonês","Aragones"],"ans":"Aragonez","cat":"Alentejo","exp":"Aragonez is Tempranillo's southern Portuguese name and the backbone of the Alentejo's red blends; the same grape is Tinta Roriz in the Douro and the Dão.","id":"r-hqsskw3w","q":"Under which name does the Alentejo grow Tempranillo?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Alentejo","exp":"Alicante Bouschet is a teinturier, a French crossing of 1866 whose pulp is coloured, and it gives Alentejo blends their depth and tannin; Trincadeira, Touriga Nacional and Tinta Caiada all have clear juice like almost every other red grape.","id":"r-1uj65dd4","opts":["Touriga Nacional","Alicante Bouschet","Tinta Caiada","Trincadeira"],"q":"Which red grape of the Alentejo has red flesh and red juice?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Alentejo","exp":"The talha is a large clay vessel lined with pitch in which the grapes ferment with their skins and rest under olive oil until Saint Martin's Day; the stone lagar is for treading, and casks and concrete belong to the modern cellar.","id":"r-ba4otjnd","opts":["A stone treading trough","A clay amphora","A chestnut cask","A concrete egg"],"q":"In which vessel is Vinho de Talha fermented?"},{"accept":["11 November","November 11","11th November","Saint Martin's Day","St Martin's Day","St. Martin's Day","Dia de São Martinho","São Martinho"],"ans":"11 November","cat":"Alentejo","exp":"Saint Martin's Day, 11 November, is the feast on which the talhas are opened across the Alentejo, and the Vinho de Talha rules require the wine to stay in the vessel with its skins until then.","id":"r-dacfxxh3","q":"On which date is the talha traditionally opened and the new wine first drawn?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Alentejo","exp":"Antão Vaz, at home in Vidigueira, is drought-tolerant and ripens to full tropical fruit with little acid, so Arinto is blended in. Encruzado is the Dão's white, Loureiro Vinho Verde's and Fernão Pires the Tejo's and Lisboa's.","id":"r-xs3j3h4j","opts":["Encruzado","Fernão Pires","Loureiro","Antão Vaz"],"q":"Which white grape gives Alentejo whites their body and is nearly always blended with Arinto for acidity?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Alentejo","exp":"Eight: Portalegre, Borba, Redondo, Évora, Reguengos, Granja-Amareleja, Vidigueira and Moura; the land between them is Vinho Regional Alentejano. The Tejo has six sub-regions and Vinho Verde nine.","id":"r-1f0oh6cy","opts":["Eight","Four","Twelve","Six"],"q":"How many demarcated sub-regions make up DOC Alentejo?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Alentejo","exp":"Alentejano is the IGP for the whole province, with a longer grape list and looser rules, and many of the region's best estates choose it; Alentejo DOC is reserved for wine from the eight demarcated sub-regions.","id":"r-2jjxa3qw","opts":["It may come from anywhere in the province","It comes from one of the eight demarcated sub-regions","It was grown on the granite of Portalegre at altitude","It was fermented in a clay talha until Saint Martin's Day"],"q":"A label reads Vinho Regional Alentejano rather than Alentejo DOC. What does that tell you about the wine's origin?"}],"sheet":"portugal","slug":"alentejo","summary":"The Alentejo is the land beyond the Tagus, a third of Portugal of rolling plain, cork oak, olive and wheat, hot and dry through a long summer. Its reds are ripe, soft and generous, blends of Aragonez, Trincadeira, Alicante Bouschet and Castelão with Syrah and Touriga Nacional beside them, and its whites lean on Antão Vaz with Arinto for acidity. The DOC Alentejo has eight sub-regions, Portalegre on granite at altitude the cool exception, and the regional wine Alentejano covers the rest. Two traditions stand out: wine fermented in clay talhas, and the cork forests that stopper the world's bottles."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Sercial, Verdelho, Boal (Bual), Malvasia (Malmsey); Terrantez is the rare fifth, medium in style.","f":"Madeira's noble grapes, dry to sweet"},{"b":"Estufagem: tanks heated to about forty-five to fifty degrees for at least three months, for young blends. Canteiro: casks in warm lofts for years, for the fine wines.","f":"Estufagem against canteiro"},{"b":"By fortifying early, while most of the sugar is unfermented; a dry Sercial is fortified after the ferment has nearly finished. Spirit to seventeen to eighteen per cent.","f":"How is a sweet Madeira made sweet?"},{"b":"Single harvest, a single grape, nearly always a noble one, at least twenty years in cask by canteiro before bottling; also called Garrafeira or Vintage Madeira.","f":"Frasqueira: define it"},{"b":"A single harvest bottled after at least five years in cask, from any grape, Tinta Negra included; younger and cheaper than Frasqueira.","f":"Colheita Madeira: define it"},{"b":"Tinta Negra, a red, hardy and high yielding, vinified at every sweetness level; it may be named on the label since 2015.","f":"The grape that makes most Madeira"},{"b":"3, 5 (Reserve), 10 (Special Reserve), 15 (Extra Reserve), 20, 30, 40, 50 and over 50 years: blends approved by tasting as showing the character of that age.","f":"Madeira age statements"},{"b":"The wine is already oxidised and heated by its making, so air and warmth can do little more; months open, decades sealed.","f":"Why does an open bottle of Madeira keep?"},{"b":"A light, pale, medium-dry style, usually Tinta Negra, named for casks said to have been rained on at the quay; the traditional American market's Madeira.","f":"Rainwater Madeira: what is it?"},{"b":"IVBAM, the Instituto do Vinho, do Bordado e do Artesanato da Madeira, which approves every lot by tasting and seals the bottles.","f":"Who regulates Madeira wine?"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Tinta Negra","role":"lead"},{"name":"Sercial","role":"lead"},{"name":"Verdelho","role":"lead"},{"name":"Boal","role":"lead"},{"name":"Malvasia","role":"lead"},{"name":"Terrantez","role":"minor"},{"name":"Bastardo","role":"minor"}],"index":8,"name":"Madeira","questions":[{"a":3,"cat":"Madeira","exp":"The ladder runs Sercial, Verdelho, Boal, Malvasia from driest to sweetest; Sercial is grown highest and coolest and is fortified late, after the ferment has nearly finished.","id":"r-cdeko9f4","opts":["Malvasia","Verdelho","Boal","Sercial"],"q":"Which of Madeira's noble grapes makes the driest style?"},{"accept":["Canteiro","canteiro ageing","the canteiro method"],"ans":"Canteiro","cat":"Madeira","exp":"Canteiro is the rack the cask rests on under the lodge roof, where the sun warms the wine over years; it is the route for the noble grapes, Colheita and Frasqueira, while estufagem heats young blends in tanks for months.","id":"r-ki42khwy","q":"What is the name of the slow ageing method in which Madeira rests in cask in a warm loft for years?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Madeira","exp":"Tinta Negra, a red, hardy and high yielding, is the island's dominant grape and the base of most three and five year blends; the noble whites together are a small minority, and Terrantez is nearly extinct.","id":"r-ooneusq7","opts":["Tinta Negra","Terrantez","Sercial","Verdelho"],"q":"Which grape makes the greater part of Madeira's wine?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Madeira","exp":"Frasqueira, the single-harvest, single-grape vintage category, needs at least twenty years in cask by canteiro; Colheita needs five, Special Reserve is a ten-year blend and three years is the youngest Madeira.","id":"r-f1wkf9p7","opts":["Five","Ten","Twenty","Three"],"q":"How many years must a Frasqueira Madeira spend in cask before it may be bottled?"},{"accept":["Malmsey","Malmsey Madeira"],"ans":"Malmsey","cat":"Madeira","exp":"Malmsey is the English corruption of Malvasia, the sweetest noble style; in the same way Bual is the export spelling of Boal.","id":"r-qwqb7mx0","q":"Which English name appears on export labels for Madeira made from Malvasia?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Madeira","exp":"Fortification stops the yeast, so an early addition leaves a sweet Malvasia and a late one a dry Sercial; heating, blending and bottling come later and change the flavour, not the sugar.","id":"r-yp67xbd6","opts":["When the wine is heated in the estufa","When spirit is added to stop the fermentation","When the wine is bottled and sealed","When the blend is assembled for its age statement"],"q":"At what moment in the process is the sweetness of a Madeira decided?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Madeira","exp":"IVBAM is the Madeira institute for wine, embroidery and handicraft; the IVDP governs Port and Douro, the IVV is the national vine and wine institute, and the CVRA is the Alentejo's regional commission.","id":"r-b9s5v1cz","opts":["IVBAM","IVDP","CVRA","IVV"],"q":"Which body regulates Madeira wine, approving each lot by tasting and sealing the bottles?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Madeira","exp":"A solera is a blend refreshed over time, and its date is the year it began, not a vintage; only Colheita and Frasqueira carry a true harvest year.","id":"r-vvn7tfva","opts":["The year the lodge was founded","The year the solera was first laid down","The harvest of every wine in the bottle","The year the wine was heated"],"q":"What does the year on a solera Madeira record?"}],"sheet":"portugal","slug":"madeira","summary":"Madeira is a volcanic island in the Atlantic, nearer Morocco than Lisbon, and its wine is the one great wine made by heat on purpose. It is fortified, then either warmed in tanks for months, estufagem, or left in cask in warm lofts for years, canteiro, so that it arrives already oxidised and almost indestructible: an opened bottle keeps for months. Four noble white grapes run dry to sweet, Sercial, Verdelho, Boal and Malvasia, with Terrantez the rarity, and the red Tinta Negra makes most of the wine. Age statements run from three years to over fifty; Colheita is one harvest after five years in cask, Frasqueira one harvest and a single grape after twenty."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Germany's red wine valley: Spätburgunder on steep slate and greywacke in a sheltered gorge south of Bonn, dry and barrel aged since the 1980s.","f":"The Ahr in one line"},{"b":"Spätburgunder (lead, about two thirds), Frühburgunder (early Pinot mutation, the speciality), Portugieser, a little Riesling.","f":"Ahr grapes to name"},{"b":"Upper: Altenahr to Dernau, slate and greywacke, steep, mineral and tense. Lower: Ahrweiler to Heimersheim, loess and loam, rounder, earlier.","f":"Upper valley against lower valley"},{"b":"Walporzheimer Kräuterberg and Gärkammer, Dernauer Pfarrwingert and Hardtberg, Ahrweiler Rosenthal and Silberberg, Neuenahrer Sonnenberg, Recher Herrenberg, Mayschosser Mönchberg.","f":"Ahr sites to know"},{"b":"One Bereich, Walporzheim/Ahrtal; one Grosslage, Klosterberg; around forty Einzellagen, so a vineyard name is nearly always a true single site.","f":"Ahr Bereich and Grosslage"},{"b":"1868 the Mayschoss cooperative, Germany's oldest; the 1980s the dry Burgundian turn led at Dernau; July 2021 the flood that the valley has rebuilt from.","f":"The Ahr's history in three dates"},{"b":"White wine from Spätburgunder pressed before the skins give colour: dry, pale, crisp; serve it as an aperitif or with river fish.","f":"Blanc de Noir on an Ahr list"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"lead"},{"name":"Frühburgunder","role":"minor"},{"name":"Portugieser","role":"minor"},{"name":"Riesling","role":"minor"},{"name":"Dornfelder","role":"minor"}],"index":0,"name":"Ahr","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Ahr","exp":"Spätburgunder, Pinot Noir, fills about two thirds of the Ahr; Portugieser and Riesling are small shares and Dornfelder smaller still.","id":"r-phszb6b3","opts":["Spätburgunder","Dornfelder","Portugieser","Riesling"],"q":"Which grape covers the greatest share of the Ahr's vineyard?"},{"accept":["Pinot Noir","pinot noir","Spätburgunder","Spatburgunder","Pinot Noir Precoce"],"ans":"Pinot Noir","cat":"Ahr","exp":"Frühburgunder, Pinot Noir Précoce, ripens two to three weeks before Spätburgunder and gives softer, darker wine; the Ahr is its stronghold.","id":"r-44azzntu","q":"Frühburgunder is an early ripening mutation of which grape?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Ahr","exp":"The Ahr runs east out of the Eifel and joins the Rhine between Remagen and Sinzig, south of Bonn; it has nothing to do with the Mosel.","id":"r-xxqf9e4x","opts":["Nahe","Rhine","Mosel","Main"],"q":"Into which river does the Ahr flow?"},{"accept":["Slate","slate","Devonian slate","slate and greywacke","greywacke"],"ans":"Slate","cat":"Ahr","exp":"Dark Devonian slate with greywacke stores the day's sun and radiates it at night, and drains fast, which is why Pinot Noir ripens this far north.","id":"r-dllzrx5t","q":"Which rock makes the upper Ahr's steep vineyards so warm?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Ahr","exp":"The Gärkammer sits in Walporzheim beside the Kräuterberg; Dernau has the Pfarrwingert, Ahrweiler the Rosenthal and Mayschoss the Mönchberg.","id":"r-8kb58s6i","opts":["Dernau","Mayschoss","Ahrweiler","Walporzheim"],"q":"Which village holds the Gärkammer, one of Germany's smallest single vineyards?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Ahr","exp":"Blanc de Noir is a white wine from red grapes, pressed at once so the skins give no colour; the rosé of one red grape is Weissherbst, the red and white co-ferment is Rotling and the sparkling wine is Sekt.","id":"r-yogh9pdd","opts":["a pink wine from red and white grapes fermented together","a sparkling wine from Pinot Noir by the traditional method","a white wine from red grapes, pressed before colour","a rosé from a single red grape"],"q":"What does Blanc de Noir on an Ahr label describe?"},{"accept":["Walporzheim/Ahrtal","Walporzheim Ahrtal","Walporzheim","Ahrtal"],"ans":"Walporzheim/Ahrtal","cat":"Ahr","exp":"The whole valley is one Bereich, Walporzheim/Ahrtal, with one Grosslage, Klosterberg; the 2021 law will label the Grosslage as a Region.","id":"r-dnrkb570","q":"What is the single Bereich of the Ahr called?","sa":1}],"sheet":"germany","slug":"ahr","summary":"The Ahr is a narrow valley running east into the Rhine south of Bonn, one of the smallest and most northerly of the thirteen regions and the one that grows red. Spätburgunder, Pinot Noir, fills about two thirds of its slopes, with Frühburgunder, its early ripening cousin, as the local speciality and a little Portugieser and Riesling behind. The upper valley is a slate and greywacke gorge that traps heat; the lower valley around Ahrweiler and Bad Neuenahr opens onto loess and loam. Once a source of pale, sweetish reds for day trippers, since the 1980s it has made dry, oak aged Pinot Noir of Burgundian ambition. Say it: AHR."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Riesling on Devonian slate, slopes to 65 degrees, a cool winding valley: the lightest great white wine, 7.5 to 9 per cent when sweet, austere and stony when dry.","f":"The Mosel in one line"},{"b":"Mosel (Mittelmosel: Bernkastel, Wehlen, Ürzig, Piesport); Saar (Wiltingen, Ockfen, Ayl, Saarburg: colder, higher acid); Ruwer (Maximin Grünhaus, Karthäuserhof: delicate). Mosel-Saar-Ruwer until 2007.","f":"The three valleys"},{"b":"Kabinett 70, Spätlese 76, Auslese 83, BA and Eiswein 110, TBA 150 degrees Oechsle: the lowest Kabinett floor in Germany.","f":"Mosel Riesling minimum must weights"},{"b":"Burg Cochem (the terraces), Bernkastel (Mittelmosel), Obermosel, Moseltor, Saar, Ruwertal.","f":"The six Bereiche of the Mosel"},{"b":"Piesporter Michelsberg, Zeller Schwarze Katz, Kröver Nacktarsch, Wiltinger Scharzberg, Bernkasteler Kurfürstlay. Badstube is the small, respectable exception.","f":"Grosslagen that fool the guest"},{"b":"Scharzhofberger, Wehlener Sonnenuhr, Bernkasteler Doctor, Graacher Domprobst, Ürziger Würzgarten, Erdener Prälat, Brauneberger Juffer-Sonnenuhr.","f":"Seven Mosel sites to recite"},{"b":"A gold capsule on an Auslese marks a richer, more botrytised selection within the Prädikat; Lange Goldkapsel richer again, often the auction lot.","f":"Goldkapsel: what it marks"},{"b":"The Mosel's traditional cask of about a thousand litres, old and neutral; the Rhine's Stück holds twelve hundred.","f":"Fuder: the Mosel's cask"},{"b":"The lower Mosel from Zell to Koblenz: terraces on hard slate and quartzite, Winninger Uhlen and Röttgen, Bremmer Calmont; the dry Mosel's stronghold.","f":"Terrassenmosel: where and what"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Riesling","role":"lead"},{"name":"Müller-Thurgau","role":"minor"},{"name":"Elbling","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Blanc","role":"rising"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"rising"}],"index":1,"name":"Mosel","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Mosel","exp":"Wiltingen and its Scharzhofberg are on the Saar, which joins the Mosel above Trier; the Ruwer is the smaller stream below Trier, and the Nahe and Lahn are Rhine tributaries.","id":"r-cq27c5x2","opts":["Saar","Ruwer","Lahn","Nahe"],"q":"On which tributary of the Mosel does Wiltingen stand?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Mosel","exp":"The Bernkasteler Doctor is the tiny grey slate site above the town; Wehlen has the Sonnenuhr, Graach the Himmelreich and Domprobst, Piesport the Goldtröpfchen.","id":"r-jkshy3f1","opts":["Piesport","Bernkastel","Wehlen","Graach"],"q":"In which village does the Doctor vineyard lie?"},{"accept":["2007","in 2007","2007 vintage","the 2007 vintage"],"ans":"2007","cat":"Mosel","exp":"The region was Mosel-Saar-Ruwer until 2007, when the name became simply Mosel; an old bottle carrying the long name is the same region, not a different one.","id":"r-itq4pbfj","q":"In which year was the region’s name shortened from Mosel-Saar-Ruwer to Mosel?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Mosel","exp":"The Mosel's floors are the lowest in Germany: Kabinett 70, Spätlese 76, Auslese 83, Beerenauslese and Eiswein 110 degrees Oechsle; the Rheingau asks 73 for Kabinett.","id":"r-ihiqs9mb","opts":["70 °Oe","76 °Oe","110 °Oe","83 °Oe"],"q":"What is the minimum must weight for a Mosel Riesling Kabinett?"},{"accept":["Elbling","elbling","Kleinberger"],"ans":"Elbling","cat":"Mosel","exp":"Upstream of Trier the slate ends and Muschelkalk limestone begins; Elbling, the Roman grape, makes light, tart still wine and Sekt base there.","id":"r-9kcbyo2t","q":"Which grape grows on the Obermosel's limestone, where Riesling gives way?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Mosel","exp":"The Treppchen is a registered Einzellage at Erden; the black cat of Zell, the bare bottom of Kröv and the Michelsberg of Piesport are collective Grosslage names.","id":"r-6gsnwoj7","opts":["Piesporter Michelsberg","Erdener Treppchen","Kröver Nacktarsch","Zeller Schwarze Katz"],"q":"Which of these Mosel names is a single vineyard rather than a Grosslage?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Mosel","exp":"Ürzig and the Erdener Prälat sit on red slate coloured by iron and volcanic rhyolite; blue slate is the Mittelmosel norm and the limestone and marl belong to the Obermosel.","id":"r-u6r8ghuq","opts":["blue Devonian slate","Keuper marl","red volcanic slate","Muschelkalk limestone"],"q":"Which rock underlies the Ürziger Würzgarten and gives it its spicy character?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Mosel","exp":"Fermentation is stopped early to keep natural sugar, so a sweet Kabinett lands at 7.5 to 9 per cent; the dry school and the GG run at 11 to 13.","id":"r-qgy31qje","opts":["10 to 11 per cent","14 to 15 per cent","12 to 13 per cent","7.5 to 9 per cent"],"q":"What alcohol does a classic sweet Mosel Riesling Kabinett carry?"}],"sheet":"germany","slug":"mosel","summary":"The Mosel is the lightest great white wine on earth: Riesling on Devonian slate, on slopes so steep they are worked by hand, in a cool valley at the northern edge of ripeness. The river winds from Luxembourg through Trier and the Mittelmosel to Koblenz, and its two tributaries at Trier, the Saar and the Ruwer, carry the region's coolest, most austere wines. Kabinett at eight per cent with sugar and acid in balance, Spätlese and Auslese from sun and botrytis, Eiswein from frost and a growing dry school under the VDP's Grosses Gewächs all come from the same slate. Until 2007 the label said Mosel-Saar-Ruwer. Say MOH-zul."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Thirty kilometres of south facing north bank where the Rhine runs west; Riesling four fifths, firmer and drier than the Mosel; Spätburgunder at Assmannshausen; Hochheim apart on the Main.","f":"The Rheingau in one line"},{"b":"Kabinett 73, Spätlese 85, Auslese 95, BA and Eiswein 125, TBA 150 degrees Oechsle: higher floors than the Mosel's at every step.","f":"Rheingau Riesling minimum must weights"},{"b":"1136 Kloster Eberbach founded; 1720 Riesling alone at Johannisberg; 1775 the Spätlese; 1872 Geisenheim; 1984 Charta; 1999 Erstes Gewächs in law; 2012 VDP members move to GG.","f":"Rheingau dates to hold"},{"b":"Lorch, Assmannshausen, Rüdesheim, Geisenheim, Johannisberg, Winkel, Oestrich, Hallgarten, Hattenheim, Erbach, Kiedrich, Rauenthal, Eltville, Walluf; Hochheim apart on the Main.","f":"Villages west to east"},{"b":"Rüdesheimer Berg Schlossberg, Schloss Johannisberg, Winkeler Jesuitengarten, Hattenheimer Wisselbrunnen, Steinberg, Erbacher Marcobrunn, Kiedricher Gräfenberg, Rauenthaler Baiken, Hochheimer Domdechaney, Assmannshäuser Höllenberg.","f":"Rheingau sites to recite"},{"b":"A growers' association of 1984 for dry and halbtrocken Riesling from Rheingau sites at higher ripeness, marked by a double Romanesque arch; the seed of Erstes Gewächs.","f":"Charta: what it was"},{"b":"Schloss Johannisberg, Schloss Vollrads, Steinberg and Schloss Reichartshausen: estates with village standing, labelled without a village name.","f":"Ortsteile of the Rheingau"},{"b":"The English word for Rhine wine, from Hochheim; Queen Victoria's visit of 1845 gave the Königin Victoriaberg its name.","f":"Hock: the word and its origin"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Riesling","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"minor"},{"name":"Müller-Thurgau","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Blanc","role":"rising"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"}],"index":2,"name":"Rheingau","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Rheingau","exp":"The Taunus ridge turns the Rhine west and blocks the north wind; the Hunsrück shelters the Nahe, the Eifel the Ahr and the Haardt the Pfalz.","id":"r-jiazjq3v","opts":["Taunus","Hunsrück","Haardt","Eifel"],"q":"Which hills shelter the Rheingau from the north?"},{"accept":["Schloss Johannisberg","Johannisberg","schloss johannisberg"],"ans":"Schloss Johannisberg","cat":"Rheingau","exp":"The courier with the abbot's permission to pick arrived late at Johannisberg in 1775; the rotted grapes made a famous wine and gave the Spätlese its name.","id":"r-rx2myizk","q":"Which estate claims the first Spätlese, from a late harvest in 1775?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Rheingau","exp":"Assmannshausen’s Höllenberg, on slate where the river turns north, has grown Pinot Noir for centuries; Rüdesheim, Hattenheim and Geisenheim are Riesling villages.","id":"r-e2vwsalr","opts":["Rüdesheim","Hattenheim","Geisenheim","Assmannshausen"],"q":"Which Rheingau village is known for Spätburgunder rather than Riesling?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Rheingau","exp":"Hochheim lies on the Main just before it meets the Rhine at Mainz, apart from the rest of the Rheingau and on limestone rather than slate or loess.","id":"r-imgqs70z","opts":["Rhine","Main","Nahe","Lahn"],"q":"On which river does Hochheim stand?"},{"accept":["Hock","hock"],"ans":"Hock","cat":"Rheingau","exp":"English merchants shortened Hochheimer to Hock and used it for any Rhine wine; Queen Victoria's 1845 visit fixed the association.","id":"r-wluuirid","q":"Which English word for Rhine wine comes from the name of Hochheim?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Rheingau","exp":"Erstes Gewächs entered Hessian law in 1999 after the Charta growers' campaign; 1984 is Charta's founding and 2002 the VDP's first Grosses Gewächs elsewhere.","id":"r-nb6mrn03","opts":["1999","2002","1984","1994"],"q":"In which year did Hesse recognise Erstes Gewächs as a legal term for the Rheingau?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Rheingau","exp":"The Steinberg is the Cistercians' walled vineyard above Hattenheim, an Ortsteil labelled Steinberger; Marcobrunn is Erbach's, Gräfenberg Kiedrich's and Höllenberg Assmannshausen's.","id":"r-qtnp64do","opts":["Höllenberg","Marcobrunn","Steinberg","Gräfenberg"],"q":"Which site is the walled vineyard of Kloster Eberbach?"},{"accept":["Kiedrich","kiedrich","Kiedrich (Rheingau)"],"ans":"Kiedrich","cat":"Rheingau","exp":"The Kiedricher Gräfenberg sits on phyllite and slate set back from the river, a source of dry GG and of some of Germany's finest TBA.","id":"r-s6bdc9cp","q":"In which village is the Gräfenberg vineyard?","sa":1}],"sheet":"germany","slug":"rheingau","summary":"The Rheingau is thirty kilometres of north bank where the Rhine, blocked by the Taunus, turns west between Wiesbaden and Rüdesheim, so the whole slope looks south across a broad stretch of water. It is the Riesling region of history: the Cistercians at Kloster Eberbach, Schloss Johannisberg's Spätlese legend of 1775, the Charta growers who put dry Riesling back on the table in 1984 and the Erstes Gewächs law of 1999. Riesling fills nearly four fifths of the vineyard; Spätburgunder owns Assmannshausen, where the river turns north into its gorge. The wines are fuller and firmer than the Mosel's, more often dry, with peach, quince and a smoky mineral grip. Hochheim, apart on the Main, gave English the word Hock."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"A small valley between Mosel and Rheingau in place and style: Riesling on slate, quartzite and volcanic porphyry, taut, smoky, mineral; dry and sweet alike.","f":"The Nahe in one line"},{"b":"Schlossböckelheimer Kupfergrube and Felsenberg, Niederhäuser Hermannshöhle, Oberhäuser Brücke, Traiser Bastei, Norheimer Dellchen, Monzinger Halenberg and Frühlingsplätzchen, Bockenauer Felseneck, Dorsheimer Goldloch.","f":"Nahe sites to recite"},{"b":"Upper (Monzingen, Meddersheim: slate, cool); middle (Schlossböckelheim to Bad Kreuznach: porphyry, the grand crus); lower (Dorsheim, Münster-Sarmsheim: loess, warmer, towards Bingen).","f":"Three stretches of the Nahe"},{"b":"The copper mine: a vineyard at Schlossböckelheim cleared from 1902 by the Prussian state domaine on an old mine; porphyry; the Nahe's most austere, longest lived dry Riesling.","f":"Kupfergrube: what and where"},{"b":"Rüdesheimer Rosengarten is a Nahe Grosslage from Rüdesheim an der Nahe; the Rheingau's Rüdesheim gives Berg Schlossberg. Check the region on the label.","f":"The two Rüdesheims"},{"b":"Slate and quartzite (upper), porphyry and melaphyre (middle), sandstone and loess (Bad Kreuznach), loess and gravel (lower): the most varied region in Germany.","f":"Nahe soils in one breath"},{"b":"Sold as Rhine wine for centuries; the 1971 wine law made it an Anbaugebiet of its own, with one Bereich, Nahetal.","f":"The Nahe since 1971"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Riesling","role":"lead"},{"name":"Müller-Thurgau","role":"minor"},{"name":"Dornfelder","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Gris / Grigio","role":"rising"},{"name":"Pinot Blanc","role":"rising"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"minor"}],"index":3,"name":"Nahe","questions":[{"a":2,"cat":"Nahe","exp":"The Nahe comes down from the hills beyond Idar-Oberstein and meets the Rhine at Bingen, opposite the western end of the Rheingau; the Saar and the Ruwer are the Mosel's tributaries.","id":"r-3p8vejsz","opts":["Mosel","Main","Rhine","Saar"],"q":"Into which river does the Nahe flow?"},{"accept":["Niederhausen","niederhausen","Niederhausen an der Nahe"],"ans":"Niederhausen","cat":"Nahe","exp":"The Niederhäuser Hermannshöhle is the middle valley's most complete site on porphyry and slate; its neighbour Oberhausen holds the Brücke.","id":"r-g33spmnq","q":"Which Nahe village gives its name to the Hermannshöhle vineyard?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Nahe","exp":"Kupfergrube means copper mine: the Prussian state domaine founded in 1902 cleared the worked out mine and the scrub above it into terraces of porphyry.","id":"r-4zas8b8r","opts":["a copper mine","a quarry","a monastery","a castle"],"q":"The Kupfergrube vineyard at Schlossböckelheim was cleared on the site of what?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Nahe","exp":"The middle Nahe's grand crus sit on volcanic porphyry and melaphyre; slate rules the upper valley at Monzingen, loess the lower valley, and Muschelkalk belongs to Franken and the Obermosel.","id":"r-i9o1ymlf","opts":["loess","volcanic porphyry","Devonian slate","Muschelkalk limestone"],"q":"Which rock underlies the Hermannshöhle, the Kupfergrube and the Bastei?"},{"accept":["1971","the 1971 wine law","1971 wine law"],"ans":"1971","cat":"Nahe","exp":"The 1971 German wine law drew the regions as they stand and gave the Nahe, long sold as Rhine wine, its own name and its single Bereich, Nahetal.","id":"r-kufjy97w","q":"In which year did the wine law make the Nahe an Anbaugebiet in its own right?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Nahe","exp":"Riesling is a little under a third of the Nahe's vines and carries all its fame; Müller-Thurgau, Dornfelder and the Pinots together outnumber it, and four fifths is the Rheingau's Riesling share.","id":"r-11x1k1fr","opts":["under a third","about four fifths","about a tenth","about a half"],"q":"Roughly what share of the Nahe's vineyard is planted to Riesling?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Nahe","exp":"Rüdesheimer Rosengarten is a Nahe Grosslage around Rüdesheim an der Nahe; the Rheingau's Rüdesheim gives the Berg Schlossberg and is a different town.","id":"r-j1ujtyef","opts":["Rheinhessen","Mittelrhein","Rheingau","Nahe"],"q":"To which region does the Grosslage Rüdesheimer Rosengarten belong?"},{"accept":["Nahetal","nahetal","Nahe Valley","Bereich Nahetal"],"ans":"Nahetal","cat":"Nahe","exp":"The whole region from Martinstein to Bingen is one Bereich, Nahetal; under the 2021 law a Bereich is labelled as a Region.","id":"r-z2qo61uz","q":"What is the single Bereich of the Nahe?","sa":1}],"sheet":"germany","slug":"nahe","summary":"The Nahe flows north east out of the hills beyond Idar-Oberstein to the Rhine at Bingen, and its valley, between the Mosel and the Rheingau on the map and in the glass, is a small region of broken geology: slate, quartzite, volcanic porphyry, sandstone and loess within a few kilometres. Riesling is a little under a third of the vineyard and all of its fame, carrying the Mosel's delicacy with a little more flesh and a mineral, blackcurrant leaf tang. The middle valley from Schlossböckelheim to Bad Kreuznach holds the great names, Kupfergrube, Hermannshöhle and Brücke; the upper valley at Monzingen and the lower valley towards Bingen complete it. Say NAH-uh."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Germany's largest region, dry rolling hills between Mainz, Worms and Bingen; once Liebfraumilch, now dry limestone Riesling in the Wonnegau and red slate Riesling on the Roter Hang.","f":"Rheinhessen in one line"},{"b":"Bingen (north west: Scharlachberg, Ingelheim), Nierstein (the Rheinterrasse and Roter Hang), Wonnegau (south: Westhofen, Dalsheim, Worms).","f":"Three Bereiche of Rheinhessen"},{"b":"The red slope: Permian Rotliegend sandstone and slate falling to the Rhine from Nackenheim to Nierstein; Rothenberg, Pettenthal, Hipping, Brudersberg, Ölberg, Orbel.","f":"Roter Hang: what it is"},{"b":"Westhofener Morstein, Kirchspiel, Brunnenhäuschen, Aulerde; Dalsheimer Hubacker, Bürgel; Hohen-Sülzener Kirchenstück: limestone and clay, dry Riesling of weight.","f":"Wonnegau sites to recite"},{"b":"Lieblich Qualitätswein from Rheinhessen, Pfalz, Nahe or Rheingau; at least 70 per cent Riesling, Silvaner, Müller-Thurgau or Kerner; at least 18 g/L sugar; named for Worms' Liebfrauenkirche.","f":"Liebfraumilch: the rule"},{"b":"A Grosslage centred on Dexheim's flat land that borrows Nierstein's name; the single sites of Nierstein are Pettenthal, Hipping, Ölberg, Orbel, Brudersberg.","f":"Niersteiner Gutes Domtal"},{"b":"Riesling leads; Müller-Thurgau, Dornfelder, Silvaner (the world's largest Silvaner area), Grauburgunder, Weissburgunder, Spätburgunder, Scheurebe (Alzey, 1916), Portugieser.","f":"Rheinhessen grapes to name"},{"b":"The hilly west around Siefersheim: volcanic porphyry, Heerkretz and Höllberg; smoky, taut Riesling far from the river.","f":"Rheinhessische Schweiz: what it is"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Riesling","role":"lead"},{"name":"Müller-Thurgau","role":"minor"},{"name":"Dornfelder","role":"minor"},{"name":"Silvaner","role":"minor"},{"name":"Scheurebe","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Gris / Grigio","role":"rising"},{"name":"Pinot Blanc","role":"rising"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"rising"}],"index":4,"name":"Rheinhessen","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Rheinhessen","exp":"The Roter Hang runs from Nackenheim to Nierstein on red Rotliegend sandstone; Westhofen is limestone in the Wonnegau, Bingen quartzite at the Nahe's mouth and Alzey the inland town.","id":"r-rm24cg00","opts":["Nierstein","Bingen","Alzey","Westhofen"],"q":"At which village does the Roter Hang, the red slope, fall to the Rhine?"},{"accept":["Niersteiner Glöck","Glöck","Glock","Niersteiner Glock","Nierstein Glöck"],"ans":"Niersteiner Glöck","cat":"Rheinhessen","exp":"The Glöck, a small walled vineyard beside Nierstein's church, appears in a deed of 742 and is counted the oldest named single vineyard in Germany.","id":"r-3as17k1p","q":"Which Rheinhessen vineyard, documented in 742, is the oldest named site in Germany?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Rheinhessen","exp":"Scheurebe was crossed at the Alzey breeding station in 1916; Müller-Thurgau is Geisenheim's of 1882, Kerner and Dornfelder are later crossings from Weinsberg.","id":"r-wg4w6lse","opts":["Dornfelder","Müller-Thurgau","Scheurebe","Kerner"],"q":"Which grape was bred at Alzey in 1916?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Rheinhessen","exp":"Liebfraumilch must be at least 70 per cent from those four grapes, lieblich with at least 18 g/L of sugar, and from Rheinhessen, the Pfalz, the Nahe or the Rheingau.","id":"r-m3ejusd2","opts":["85 per cent","70 per cent","60 per cent","51 per cent"],"q":"What is the minimum share of Riesling, Silvaner, Müller-Thurgau or Kerner in Liebfraumilch?"},{"accept":["Worms","worms"],"ans":"Worms","cat":"Rheinhessen","exp":"The Liebfrauenstift-Kirchenstück beside the church of Our Lady at Worms was the original Liebfraumilch; the law later made the name generic across four regions.","id":"r-agl1ugt7","q":"Which city holds the Liebfrauenkirche whose vineyard gave Liebfraumilch its name?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Rheinhessen","exp":"Morstein is Westhofen's limestone site in the Wonnegau; Hubacker belongs to Dalsheim, Pettenthal to Nierstein on the Roter Hang and Scharlachberg to Bingen.","id":"r-8x8qt24b","opts":["Pettenthal","Hubacker","Scharlachberg","Morstein"],"q":"Which of these vineyards lies in Westhofen?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Rheinhessen","exp":"Rheinhessen is the triangle inside the Rhine's bend with Mainz in the north, Worms in the south and Bingen in the west; Speyer and Landau belong to the Pfalz, Trier to the Mosel.","id":"r-bn46xjt7","opts":["Mainz, Worms and Bingen","Worms, Speyer and Landau","Bingen, Bonn and Koblenz","Mainz, Koblenz and Trier"],"q":"Which three towns mark the corners of Rheinhessen?"},{"accept":["Wonnegau","wonnegau","Bereich Wonnegau"],"ans":"Wonnegau","cat":"Rheinhessen","exp":"The Wonnegau is the southern Bereich around Worms and Alzey; Nierstein covers the Rheinterrasse and Bingen the north west.","id":"r-9npjwwqj","q":"Which Bereich of Rheinhessen holds Westhofen and Dalsheim?","sa":1}],"sheet":"germany","slug":"rheinhessen","summary":"Rheinhessen is Germany's largest region, a gentle, dry, rolling country inside the great bend of the Rhine between Mainz, Worms and Bingen, the land of a thousand hills. For a century it meant Liebfraumilch and Niersteiner Gutes Domtal, bulk wine from flat land; since the 1990s a generation of growers on the Roter Hang at Nierstein and Nackenheim and on the limestone of the Wonnegau at Westhofen and Dalsheim has made it a home of Germany's most sought after dry Riesling. Riesling has led the plantings since it overtook Müller-Thurgau in the 2010s, with Müller-Thurgau, Dornfelder, Silvaner, which has its largest home here, Grauburgunder, Weissburgunder and Scheurebe behind. Say RINE-hess-en."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"The Haardt hills, the northern end of the Vosges, cast a rain shadow: the Pfalz is among Germany's driest, sunniest regions, warm enough for figs and almonds.","f":"Pfalz: what shelters it and what does that do?"},{"b":"Mittelhaardt-Deutsche Weinstrasse in the north (the classic Riesling villages); Südliche Weinstrasse in the south (warmer, Pinot family, Dornfelder).","f":"The two Bereiche of the Pfalz, north and south"},{"b":"Forst, Deidesheim, Ruppertsberg and Wachenheim, with Bad Dürkheim and Kallstadt to the north.","f":"Four classic Mittelhaardt Riesling villages"},{"b":"Kirchenstück, Jesuitengarten, Ungeheuer and Pechstein, all Grosse Lage sites; the 1828 survey placed Kirchenstück at the top of the Pfalz.","f":"Forst's four famous single vineyards"},{"b":"They quarried black basalt from the Pechsteinkopf above the village and spread it over the sandstone vineyards to darken and warm the soil; Pechstein takes its name from it.","f":"What did Forst's growers do to their soil?"},{"b":"Pfalz: riper, fuller, dry, spicy, higher alcohol, on sandstone, limestone and basalt. Mosel: lighter, lower alcohol, often off-dry, on slate.","f":"Pfalz Riesling versus Mosel Riesling in one line"},{"b":"The Bavarian land tax survey of 1828, made while the Pfalz was Bavarian; one estate still prints P.C. and G.C. from it.","f":"Which classification ranked Forster Kirchenstück top, and when?"},{"b":"Dornfelder (the most planted red), Spätburgunder, Weissburgunder, Grauburgunder, Müller-Thurgau, Scheurebe, Rieslaner, Gewürztraminer.","f":"Grapes the Pfalz grows beyond Riesling"},{"b":"Riesling, Weissburgunder and Spätburgunder.","f":"VDP Grosses Gewächs grapes allowed in the Pfalz"},{"b":"Much of its Sonnenberg vineyard, with the Kammerberg and Sankt Paul parcels, lies over the border in France, yet the wine is vinified and sold as German wine.","f":"Schweigen: why is it a trap?"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Riesling","role":"lead"},{"name":"Dornfelder","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"rising"},{"name":"Pinot Blanc","role":"rising"},{"name":"Pinot Gris / Grigio","role":"rising"},{"name":"Müller-Thurgau","role":"minor"},{"name":"Scheurebe","role":"minor"},{"name":"Rieslaner","role":"minor"},{"name":"Gewürztraminer","role":"minor"}],"index":5,"name":"Pfalz","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Pfalz","exp":"The Haardt is the northern continuation of the Vosges and wrings the rain out of the westerlies. The Taunus shelters the Rheingau, the Odenwald backs the Hessische Bergstrasse and the Hunsrück lies between the Mosel and the Nahe.","id":"r-ndqlb1yp","opts":["The Haardt","The Hunsrück","The Odenwald","The Taunus"],"q":"The Pfalz is one of the driest and warmest of Germany's thirteen regions. Which hill range shelters it from the west and makes that so?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Pfalz","exp":"The Mittelhaardt is the northern Bereich and the historic heart of Pfalz Riesling. The Südliche Weinstrasse is the warmer southern Bereich, the Wonnegau is in Rheinhessen and the Ortenau is in Baden.","id":"r-5hzm6110","opts":["Wonnegau","Südliche Weinstrasse","Ortenau","Mittelhaardt"],"q":"Which Bereich of the Pfalz holds the classic Riesling villages of Forst, Deidesheim and Wachenheim?"},{"accept":["Forst","forst","forst an der weinstrasse","forst an der weinstraße","forster"],"ans":"Forst","cat":"Pfalz","exp":"Basalt from the Pechsteinkopf above Forst was quarried and carted onto the sandstone below. The Pechstein vineyard takes its name from that stone, and Kirchenstück, Jesuitengarten and Ungeheuer are its neighbours.","id":"r-8f0d1xsu","q":"Growers in one Mittelhaardt village spread crushed black volcanic rock over their vineyards to warm them. Name the village.","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Pfalz","exp":"Grosses Gewächs is the dry wine of a top classified site. The VDP keeps the Prädikat words Kabinett, Spätlese and Auslese for its wines that carry sweetness, so a GG is always trocken.","id":"r-m0mziv6y","opts":["It is lightly sweet","It is dry","It is off-dry","It is a dessert wine"],"q":"A Pfalz label reads Riesling Grosses Gewächs from a VDP member. What does the term tell a guest about the wine's sweetness?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Pfalz","exp":"Dornfelder, bred at Weinsberg in 1955 for its deep colour, leads the red plantings of the Pfalz, which with Rheinhessen grows most of Germany's crop of it. Spätburgunder is the region's serious red, Portugieser its old everyday grape and Regent a disease resistant crossing from Geilweilerhof.","id":"r-z1sorjg7","opts":["Portugieser","Regent","Dornfelder","Spätburgunder"],"q":"Which grape is the most planted red variety of the Pfalz?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Pfalz","exp":"The Bavarian land tax survey of 1828 placed Forster Kirchenstück at the top of the Pfalz. The Spätlese legend at Schloss Johannisberg dates from 1775, the Bordeaux classification from 1855 and the German wine law that created the Grosslagen from 1971.","id":"r-1ezajpe9","opts":["1971","1828","1855","1775"],"q":"The vineyards of the Pfalz were ranked for tax while the region belonged to another German state, and one estate still prints that ranking on its labels. In which year was that survey made?"},{"accept":["Schweigen","schweigen","schweigen-rechtenbach","schweigen rechtenbach"],"ans":"Schweigen","cat":"Pfalz","exp":"Schweigen sits at the Deutsches Weintor at the southern end of the German Wine Road. Much of its Sonnenberg vineyard, with the Kammerberg parcel, is on French soil, and the grapes are brought back and vinified in Germany under German law.","id":"r-0l5nmno1","q":"The last wine village of the Pfalz before France is known for Spätburgunder whose best sites lie across the border. Name the village.","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Pfalz","exp":"Rieslaner was bred at Würzburg in 1921 and ripens late with high acid, which keeps its botrytis wines fresh. Scheurebe is Riesling crossed with Bukettraube, Kerner is Trollinger with Riesling, and Bacchus is a softer, early ripening crossing.","id":"r-0691s8hk","opts":["Rieslaner","Bacchus","Scheurebe","Kerner"],"q":"Which aromatic crossing of Silvaner and Riesling, with piercing acidity, do Pfalz estates keep for taut Auslese and sweeter wines?"}],"sheet":"germany","slug":"pfalz","summary":"The Pfalz is the warm, dry strip of vineyard that runs north to south along the eastern foot of the Haardt hills, from the Rheinhessen border down to the French frontier at Schweigen, where Alsace begins. It is Germany's second largest region by vineyard and rivals the Mosel for the largest planting of Riesling anywhere in the world. Its heart is the Mittelhaardt, the run of villages around Forst, Deidesheim, Ruppertsberg and Wachenheim that made full, dry Riesling famous. The south, the Südliche Weinstrasse, is warmer again and belongs as much to the Pinot family as to Riesling. Think ripe, dry, broad and spicy, with figs and almonds growing between the rows."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Dry Silvaner, in the Bocksbeutel, on Muschelkalk shell limestone around Würzburg on the river Main.","f":"Franken in one line: grape, bottle, rock"},{"b":"Mainviereck (west): Buntsandstein red sandstone. Maindreieck (centre, Würzburg): Muschelkalk shell limestone. Steigerwald (east): Keuper gypsum marl.","f":"The three Bereiche of Franken and their rocks"},{"b":"The Main; Bavaria; Würzburg. The Main flows west to join the Rhine at Mainz.","f":"Place Franken: its river, its state and its capital"},{"b":"At Castell in the Steigerwald, in 1659.","f":"Where and when was Silvaner first planted in Germany?"},{"b":"Bürgerspital zum Heiligen Geist (1316), Juliusspital (1576), Staatlicher Hofkeller (the state cellar under the Residenz).","f":"The three historic Würzburg estates"},{"b":"Würzburger Stein (Würzburg), Escherndorfer Lump (Escherndorf, on the Mainschleife), Julius-Echter-Berg (Iphofen).","f":"Franken's famous sites: name three and their villages"},{"b":"A continental climate with spring frost and a short season: Silvaner ripens a week or two earlier and sets reliably. Riesling is kept for the warmest sites.","f":"Why does Franken grow Silvaner rather than Riesling?"},{"b":"Two pockets of Baden: Tauberfranken in the north and the Neuweier vineyards near Baden-Baden.","f":"Where else may the Bocksbeutel be used?"},{"b":"The Mainviereck on red sandstone: Bürgstadt (Centgrafenberg) and Klingenberg's terraces, for Spätburgunder and Frühburgunder.","f":"Franken's red wine corner"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Silvaner","role":"lead"},{"name":"Müller-Thurgau","role":"lead"},{"name":"Bacchus","role":"minor"},{"name":"Riesling","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"rising"},{"name":"Scheurebe","role":"minor"},{"name":"Rieslaner","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Blanc","role":"rising"},{"name":"Domina","role":"minor"}],"index":6,"name":"Franken","questions":[{"a":1,"cat":"Franken","exp":"Silvaner ripens earlier than Riesling and takes its stony character from the Muschelkalk around Würzburg. Müller-Thurgau and Bacchus are grown for volume and aroma, and Riesling is kept for the warmest sites.","id":"r-8jluxw7s","opts":["Müller-Thurgau","Silvaner","Riesling","Bacchus"],"q":"Which grape is Franken's signature, finished dry and showing an earthy, herbal, pear scented character?"},{"accept":["Bocksbeutel","bocksbeutel","the bocksbeutel","bocksbeutel flask","boxbeutel"],"ans":"Bocksbeutel","cat":"Franken","exp":"The Bocksbeutel is reserved for Franken and for two small pockets of Baden, Tauberfranken and the Neuweier area. It is instantly recognisable and will not lie flat in a rack.","id":"r-cp8qmfbc","q":"A squat, round bellied flask marks the wine of Franken and is protected for it under European law. What is the bottle called?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Franken","exp":"The Steigerwald is the eastern Bereich on Keuper and gives broader, softer Silvaner. The Mainviereck is red sandstone, the Maindreieck is shell limestone, and Tauberfranken is a Bereich of Baden.","id":"r-kx4zf11t","opts":["Steigerwald","Tauberfranken","Maindreieck","Mainviereck"],"q":"Which Bereich of Franken sits on Keuper, the gypsum bearing marl, around Iphofen and Castell?"},{"accept":["Castell","castell","schloss castell","castell in the steigerwald"],"ans":"Castell","cat":"Franken","exp":"The counts of Castell recorded the planting on their Schlossberg in 1659, and the estate still makes Silvaner from it. The date is the anchor for every question on the grape's German history.","id":"r-5ie9piss","q":"Germany's first documented planting of Silvaner was made in 1659 in a Steigerwald village. Name the village.","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Franken","exp":"The Main runs through Würzburg and west to the Rhine at Mainz, and its bends make the region's south facing sites. The Neckar is Württemberg's river, the Nahe has its own region, and the Tauber is a small tributary shared with Baden.","id":"r-52wqg0ig","opts":["The Main","The Nahe","The Tauber","The Neckar"],"q":"Along which river do Franken's vineyards lie?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Franken","exp":"Julius Echter von Mespelbrunn founded the Juliusspital in 1576. The Bürgerspital is older, from 1316, the Hofkeller is the Bavarian state's cellar under the Residenz, and Kloster Eberbach is a Rheingau monastery.","id":"r-btzl60xt","opts":["Bürgerspital zum Heiligen Geist","Kloster Eberbach","Juliusspital","Staatlicher Hofkeller"],"q":"Which Würzburg estate was founded in 1576 by a prince-bishop as a charitable hospital that wine still funds?"},{"accept":["Lump","lump","escherndorfer lump","the lump","escherndorfer lump vineyard"],"ans":"Lump","cat":"Franken","exp":"The Escherndorfer Lump sits on the Mainschleife near Volkach, and despite its unflattering name it is a single Einzellage. The leading Escherndorf estate built its reputation on it.","id":"r-fygb2yx2","q":"A steep limestone amphitheatre above a loop of the Main at Escherndorf gives Franken's richest Silvaner and its rare botrytis wines. What is the vineyard called?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Franken","exp":"The Mainviereck is the western square of the Main where red sandstone surfaces, and its Pinot Noir ranks with the Ahr's and Baden's. The Maindreieck is limestone, the Steigerwald is marl, and the Kraichgau is in Baden.","id":"r-m11sompw","opts":["Maindreieck","Steigerwald","Kraichgau","Mainviereck"],"q":"Which Bereich of Franken grows serious Spätburgunder on red Buntsandstein at Bürgstadt and Klingenberg?"}],"sheet":"germany","slug":"franken","summary":"Franken is Bavaria's wine region, strung along the river Main around Würzburg, far from the Rhine and the easternmost of the western group, looking towards Nuremberg rather than France. Its signature is dry Silvaner in the squat, flat Bocksbeutel flask, grown on shell limestone above Würzburg, on red sandstone to the west and on gypsum marl to the east. The climate is continental, with hard winters and a real spring frost risk, so early ripening grapes have always mattered more than Riesling. Würzburg's three great charitable and state estates carry centuries of history, and the Mainviereck in the west has become serious Spätburgunder country."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"The Neckar region around Stuttgart and Heilbronn, Germany's red wine region: Trollinger for everyday, Lemberger for serious, Riesling the white, cooperatives the makers.","f":"Württemberg in one line"},{"b":"Trollinger = Schiava (Vernatsch). Lemberger = Blaufränkisch. Schwarzriesling = Pinot Meunier (Müllerrebe).","f":"Three Württemberg synonyms a server must decode"},{"b":"A Württemberg Rotling: red and white grapes fermented together, pale and fresh, Qualitätswein or above. Not a blend of finished wines and not Weissherbst.","f":"What is Schillerwein?"},{"b":"A rosé made from a single named red variety at Qualitätswein level or above, common in Baden and Württemberg.","f":"What is Weissherbst?"},{"b":"Founded 1868, Germany's oldest viticultural school. Bred Kerner (Trollinger x Riesling, 1929) and Dornfelder (1955), selected Samtrot.","f":"The Weinsberg school: when, and which grapes?"},{"b":"Remstal-Stuttgart, Württembergisch Unterland (largest), Kocher-Jagst-Tauber, Oberer Neckar, Württembergischer Bodensee, Bayerischer Bodensee.","f":"The six Bereiche of Württemberg"},{"b":"Keuper gypsum marl under the Remstal, Stuttgart and Heilbronn slopes; Muschelkalk shell limestone on the terraced middle Neckar at Besigheim, Hessigheim and Mundelsheim.","f":"Württemberg's two soils and where each shows"},{"b":"Lemberger and Spätburgunder, alongside Riesling for the whites.","f":"Which red grapes may be VDP Grosses Gewächs in Württemberg?"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Trollinger","role":"lead"},{"name":"Lemberger","role":"lead"},{"name":"Riesling","role":"lead"},{"name":"Schwarzriesling","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"rising"},{"name":"Samtrot","role":"minor"},{"name":"Dornfelder","role":"minor"},{"name":"Kerner","role":"minor"},{"name":"Müller-Thurgau","role":"minor"}],"index":7,"name":"Württemberg","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Württemberg","exp":"The Neckar winds past Stuttgart and Heilbronn, joined by the Rems, Enz, Kocher and Jagst. The Main is Franken's river, the Rhine is Baden's western edge, and the Danube crosses the south of the state without a wine region on its banks.","id":"r-yilwcvgd","opts":["The Neckar","The Danube","The Main","The Rhine"],"q":"Along which river and its tributaries do Württemberg's vineyards lie?"},{"accept":["Trollinger","trollinger","trollinger grape","blauer trollinger"],"ans":"Trollinger","cat":"Württemberg","exp":"Trollinger ripens very late and gives a soft, strawberry scented red drunk young and cool by the quarter litre. The name is a worn down form of Tirolinger, from the Tyrol.","id":"r-mcel165g","q":"Württemberg's pale, light everyday red is the grape the Alto Adige calls Schiava. What does the German label say?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Württemberg","exp":"Lemberger and Blaufränkisch are one grape, Kékfrankos in Hungary. Zweigelt is a crossing of Blaufränkisch and Sankt Laurent, and Portugieser is an older, paler red.","id":"r-nprw0xvy","opts":["Blauer Portugieser","Zweigelt","Sankt Laurent","Blaufränkisch"],"q":"Lemberger, the serious red of Württemberg, is the same grape as which Austrian variety?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Württemberg","exp":"Schillerwein is a Rotling, which by law means red and white grapes or their must fermented together. A single variety rosé is a Weissherbst, and blending finished red and white wine is not permitted for a quality wine.","id":"r-mqo96dnb","opts":["Red and white grapes fermented together","A rosé pressed from Trollinger alone","A blend of finished red and white wines","A red wine bottled with a light sparkle"],"q":"A Württemberg Schillerwein arrives pale pink at the table. How was it made?"},{"accept":["Weinsberg","weinsberg","the weinsberg school","staatliche lehr- und versuchsanstalt weinsberg","weinsberg institute"],"ans":"Weinsberg","cat":"Württemberg","exp":"Weinsberg near Heilbronn is Germany's oldest viticultural school and keeps its own estate. Kerner came from it in 1929 and Dornfelder in 1955.","id":"r-rn175ypa","q":"Which Württemberg town holds the state viticultural school, founded in 1868, where Kerner and Dornfelder were bred?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Württemberg","exp":"Schwarzriesling, also called Müllerrebe, is Meunier, the Champagne grape, and its name has nothing to do with Riesling. Frühburgunder is the early ripening mutation of Pinot Noir, and Samtrot is a velvety mutation of Schwarzriesling selected at Weinsberg.","id":"r-bjf6b3pm","opts":["Frühburgunder","Pinot Meunier","Riesling","Pinot Noir"],"q":"Schwarzriesling is a speciality red of Württemberg. Which grape is it?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Württemberg","exp":"Remstal-Stuttgart takes in the city slopes, Fellbach and the Rems villages. The Unterland is the larger Heilbronn country to the north, Kocher-Jagst-Tauber is the cool northern fringe and the Oberer Neckar is a tiny upstream outpost.","id":"r-zk5lcyfs","opts":["Kocher-Jagst-Tauber","Württembergisch Unterland","Remstal-Stuttgart","Oberer Neckar"],"q":"Which Bereich of Württemberg holds the vineyards inside the state capital and the Rems valley to its east?"}],"sheet":"germany","slug":"wurttemberg","summary":"Württemberg is the vineyard of the Neckar and its tributaries around Stuttgart and Heilbronn, and it is Germany's red wine region: unusually, more of it is planted to red grapes than white. Trollinger, the pale, light red the Alto Adige calls Schiava, fills the local quarter litre glass, Lemberger, which Austria calls Blaufränkisch, is the serious red, and Schwarzriesling, Spätburgunder and Dornfelder follow. Riesling leads the whites. Keuper marl and Muschelkalk on terraced valley slopes, a sheltered continental climate, powerful cooperatives and a population that drinks nearly everything it makes are the rest of the story, with the Weinsberg school breeding the region's crossings since 1868."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Germany's southernmost, warmest region, zone B alone, from Lake Constance up the Rhine to Heidelberg: Pinot family country, Spätburgunder first, cooperatives making most of it.","f":"Baden in one line"},{"b":"An extinct volcano in the Rhine plain near Freiburg, capped with loess and cut into terraces; Germany's hottest vineyard, for Spätburgunder and Grauburgunder.","f":"What is the Kaiserstuhl?"},{"b":"Tauberfranken, Badische Bergstrasse, Kraichgau, Ortenau, Breisgau, Kaiserstuhl, Tuniberg, Markgräflerland, Bodensee.","f":"The nine Bereiche of Baden, north to south"},{"b":"Ortenau: Riesling (Klingelberger) on granite. Markgräflerland: Gutedel (Chasselas). Kaiserstuhl: Spätburgunder and Grauburgunder on volcanic rock and loess.","f":"Grape by Bereich: Ortenau, Markgräflerland, Kaiserstuhl"},{"b":"Both are Pinot Gris. Grauburgunder signals the dry style; Ruländer the older, richer, often sweeter one.","f":"Grauburgunder versus Ruländer"},{"b":"Weissherbst: a rosé from one named red grape. Badisch Rotgold: a protected Baden Rotling, Grauburgunder and Spätburgunder fermented together.","f":"Weissherbst versus Badisch Rotgold"},{"b":"Tauberfranken in the north and the Neuweier vineyards near Baden-Baden in the Ortenau.","f":"Where in Baden may the Bocksbeutel be used?"},{"b":"Baden is the only German region in EU zone B, so its permitted enrichment is lower (2 per cent by volume against 3 in zone A): the sun does the work.","f":"Why does zone B matter for Baden?"},{"b":"Spätburgunder, Weissburgunder and Grauburgunder, with Riesling in the Ortenau: one of the broadest lists in Germany.","f":"Which grapes lead Baden's VDP Grosses Gewächs list?"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Gris / Grigio","role":"lead"},{"name":"Müller-Thurgau","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Blanc","role":"rising"},{"name":"Riesling","role":"minor"},{"name":"Gutedel","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"rising"},{"name":"Gewürztraminer","role":"minor"}],"index":8,"name":"Baden","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Baden","exp":"The other twelve regions sit in zone A, the coolest. Zone B holds Baden with much of northern France, and it caps enrichment of the must at a lower level because the climate ripens the grapes itself.","id":"r-3q9fpryd","opts":["Zone B","Zone C II","Zone C I","Zone A"],"q":"Baden alone among Germany's thirteen regions is placed in a warmer European wine growing zone. Which zone?"},{"accept":["Kaiserstuhl","kaiserstuhl","the kaiserstuhl","kaiserstuhl volcano"],"ans":"Kaiserstuhl","cat":"Baden","exp":"The Kaiserstuhl's volcanic rock under deep loess records the highest temperatures in German viticulture. Ihringen, Achkarren, Oberrotweil and Burkheim are its leading villages, planted to Spätburgunder and Grauburgunder.","id":"r-f8juowdd","q":"An extinct volcano rising from the Rhine plain near Freiburg, capped with loess terraces, is Baden's hottest and most famous vineyard area. Name it.","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Baden","exp":"Gutedel was brought to the Markgräflerland from Vevey in 1780 and gives a soft, low acid wine drunk young. Elbling is the Obermosel's grape, Silvaner belongs to Franken and Tauberfranken, and Müller-Thurgau is grown across Baden for volume.","id":"r-ryhj67p0","opts":["Elbling","Gutedel","Müller-Thurgau","Silvaner"],"q":"Which grape, known as Chasselas in Switzerland, is the speciality of the Markgräflerland in the far south of Baden?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Baden","exp":"Klingelberger is the Ortenau's traditional name for Riesling, taken from the Klingelberg vineyard at Durbach. Traminer is called Clevner in the same villages, and Pinot Gris is Grauburgunder or Ruländer.","id":"r-821ndbit","opts":["Silvaner","Riesling","Traminer","Pinot Gris"],"q":"Klingelberger appears on labels from Durbach in the Ortenau. Which grape is it?"},{"accept":["Badisch Rotgold","badisch rotgold","rotgold","badischer rotgold"],"ans":"Badisch Rotgold","cat":"Baden","exp":"Badisch Rotgold must be a Qualitätswein made by co-fermenting the two Pinot grapes, which separates it from a Weissherbst, the single variety rosé. Württemberg's equivalent Rotling is Schillerwein.","id":"r-k3015d01","q":"Baden's own Rotling, Grauburgunder and Spätburgunder fermented together, carries a protected name. What is it?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Baden","exp":"Malterdingen sits on limestone in the Breisgau, the foothills north of Freiburg, and its name was once used for the Pinot Noir grape itself. Students routinely misplace it in the Kaiserstuhl.","id":"r-rwe8g9lo","opts":["Breisgau","Tuniberg","Kaiserstuhl","Ortenau"],"q":"Malterdingen, home of Baden's reference Spätburgunder estate, lies in which Bereich?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Baden","exp":"Tauberfranken on the river Tauber tastes and bottles like Franken, and shares the flask's protection with the Neuweier vineyards near Baden-Baden. The Kraichgau and Bergstrasse are loess hill country and the Bodensee is the lake outpost in the south.","id":"r-qskuvovd","opts":["Bodensee","Badische Bergstrasse","Tauberfranken","Kraichgau"],"q":"Which northern Bereich of Baden, on Muschelkalk and planted to Silvaner, is allowed to use the Bocksbeutel?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Baden","exp":"Deep wind blown loess was laid over the volcano during the ice ages and holds both water and warmth. Slate belongs to the Mosel and Ahr, granite to the Ortenau foothills and Muschelkalk to Franken and Tauberfranken.","id":"r-9i1m9cv2","opts":["Granite","Muschelkalk","Slate","Loess"],"q":"What covers the volcanic rock on the terraced slopes of the Kaiserstuhl?"}],"sheet":"germany","slug":"baden","summary":"Baden is Germany's southernmost and warmest region, a long, broken strip that runs from Lake Constance up the Rhine past Freiburg, the Kaiserstuhl and the Ortenau to Heidelberg, with an outpost on the Tauber in the north, facing Alsace across the river for most of its length. It alone of the thirteen sits in the warmer European wine zone B. It belongs to the Pinot family: Spätburgunder above all, then Grauburgunder and Weissburgunder, with Müller-Thurgau for volume, Riesling in the Ortenau and Gutedel in the Markgräflerland. The Kaiserstuhl, an extinct volcano capped with loess terraces, is its hottest and most famous corner, and cooperatives make most of its wine."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Steinfeder, at most 11.5% (a feathery grass); Federspiel, 11.5 to 12.5% (a falconer's lure); Smaragd, from 12.5% (the emerald lizard). Dry wines, a growers' scale from 1983, not law.","f":"The Vinea Wachau scale, with its alcohol lines"},{"b":"Gebietswein, 17 grapes; Ortswein, 9 grapes from 22 villages; Riedenwein, Grüner Veltliner and Riesling only. Hand harvest at every tier; from the 2020 vintage.","f":"Wachau DAC: the three tiers"},{"b":"Loess holds water and lime and feeds the vigorous Grüner Veltliner; thin gneiss terraces starve and warm the vine, which suits late ripening Riesling.","f":"Grüner on loess, Riesling on rock: why"},{"b":"Grüner Veltliner and Riesling only. Traisental 2006, Kremstal 2007, Kamptal 2008. Klassik and Reserve (13% minimum), now also Gebietswein, Ortswein and Riedenwein.","f":"Kamptal, Kremstal, Traisental DAC: grapes and years"},{"b":"The Kamptal's great hill above Zöbing: Permian sandstone with volcanic grit, about 270 million years old. Riesling. The Lamm at its foot is loess and clay for Grüner Veltliner.","f":"The Heiligenstein in one line"},{"b":"Spitz, Weissenkirchen (with Joching and Wösendorf), Dürnstein, Loiben; Mautern faces them on the south bank. Krems and Stein begin the Kremstal.","f":"Wachau villages west to east"},{"b":"Singerriedel and Honivogl (Spitz), Achleiten and Klaus (Weissenkirchen), Kellerberg (Dürnstein), Loibenberg and Schütt (Loiben), Pfaffenberg (Stein), Heiligenstein (Zöbing), Lamm (Kammern).","f":"Famous Rieden and their village"},{"b":"Wagram 2021 (Roter Veltliner on loess), Carnuntum 2019 (Zweigelt and Blaufränkisch), Thermenregion 2023 (Rotgipfler, Zierfandler, Pinot Noir, St. Laurent).","f":"Niederösterreich's other regions and their DAC years"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Grüner Veltliner","role":"lead"},{"name":"Riesling","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Neuburger","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"},{"name":"Roter Veltliner","role":"minor"},{"name":"Zweigelt","role":"minor"}],"index":0,"name":"Wachau / Kamptal / Kremstal","questions":[{"a":1,"cat":"Wachau / Kamptal / Kremstal","exp":"Smaragd, named for the emerald lizard of the terraces, starts at 12.5%; Federspiel runs 11.5 to 12.5% and Steinfeder stops at 11.5%. Klassik is a Kamptal and Kremstal DAC term.","id":"r-lf9n9dwv","opts":["Federspiel","Smaragd","Klassik","Steinfeder"],"q":"Which Vinea Wachau category carries at least 12.5% alcohol?"},{"accept":["Riesling","riesling","Rheinriesling","Weisser Riesling"],"ans":"Riesling","cat":"Wachau / Kamptal / Kremstal","exp":"The Heiligenstein's hot Permian sandstone is the Kamptal's Riesling summit; the Lamm at its foot, on loess and clay, is the Grüner Veltliner site.","id":"r-t5sl99mb","q":"Which grape is the Zöbinger Heiligenstein famous for?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Wachau / Kamptal / Kremstal","exp":"Riesling takes the thin, water poor primary rock terraces of gneiss and amphibolite; Grüner Veltliner takes the deep loess beside them. Limestone marks the Traisental and basalt the Styrian Vulkanland.","id":"r-qyguyg31","opts":["Limestone","Basalt","Gneiss","Loess"],"q":"On which soil is Riesling planted in the Wachau?"},{"accept":["Grüner Veltliner and Riesling","Gruner Veltliner and Riesling","Riesling and Grüner Veltliner","Riesling and Gruner Veltliner","Grüner Veltliner, Riesling"],"ans":"Grüner Veltliner and Riesling","cat":"Wachau / Kamptal / Kremstal","exp":"Kamptal DAC, like Kremstal and Traisental, is reserved for Grüner Veltliner and Riesling, dry; any other grape from the valley is sold as Niederösterreich Qualitätswein.","id":"r-onu4psuu","q":"Which two grapes may be labelled as Kamptal DAC?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Wachau / Kamptal / Kremstal","exp":"The Wachau DAC ordinance was issued in 2020 for that vintage; Kremstal came in 2007, Kamptal in 2008 and Carnuntum in 2019.","id":"r-gdi5kajy","opts":["2020","2019","2007","2008"],"q":"From which vintage has the Wachau been a DAC?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Wachau / Kamptal / Kremstal","exp":"The Wachau is the gorge between Melk upstream and Krems downstream; Krems itself and Stein belong to the Kremstal, and Tulln lies in the Wagram region further east.","id":"r-4ufwzle2","opts":["Krems and Tulln","Melk and Krems","Tulln and Vienna","Linz and Melk"],"q":"Which two towns mark the ends of the Wachau along the Danube?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Wachau / Kamptal / Kremstal","exp":"The Kellerberg rises above Dürnstein; Spitz has the Singerriedel, Weissenkirchen the Achleiten, and Loiben the Loibenberg and Schütt.","id":"r-8446vg1y","opts":["Spitz","Loiben","Weissenkirchen","Dürnstein"],"q":"Which village is the Kellerberg vineyard in?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Wachau / Kamptal / Kremstal","exp":"The Wachau DAC narrows from 17 grapes at Gebietswein and 9 at Ortswein to Grüner Veltliner and Riesling alone for a Riedenwein, hand picked and never chaptalised. Reserve is a rung of the Kamptal and Kremstal DACs, not the Wachau's.","id":"r-fibnjuit","opts":["Riedenwein","Ortswein","Gebietswein","Reserve"],"q":"Which tier of the Wachau DAC is limited to Grüner Veltliner and Riesling?"}],"sheet":"austria","slug":"wachau-kamptal-kremstal","summary":"Three separate regions of Niederösterreich sharing the Danube, two grapes and one idea. The Wachau is the gorge between Melk and Krems, gneiss terraces above the river, with its own growers' scale of Steinfeder, Federspiel and Smaragd and, since the 2020 vintage, a DAC. The Kremstal wraps the town of Krems, primary rock on the west side and deep loess to the east. The Kamptal runs north up the Kamp river through Langenlois, with the Heiligenstein above Zöbing as its great Riesling hill. In all three Grüner Veltliner takes the loess and Riesling the bare rock, the wines are dry, and the DACs name only those two grapes at the top."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"At least three white Qualitätswein grapes co planted in one Vienna vineyard, picked and fermented together; largest share at most 50%, third largest at least 10%. DAC from the 2013 vintage.","f":"Wiener Gemischter Satz DAC: the rule"},{"b":"Plain: dry, at most 12.5% alcohol, no dominant oak. With a Ried or village name: at least 12.5% alcohol, need not be dry.","f":"Plain DAC versus Ried or village DAC"},{"b":"Nussberg and the Döbling villages (Grinzing, Sievering, Neustift) on the right bank; Bisamberg over Stammersdorf on the left; Mauer and Oberlaa in the south.","f":"Vienna's vineyard hills"},{"b":"A field blend grows, is picked and ferments as one wine; a cuvée is assembled in the cellar from separately made wines. Only the first may be called Gemischter Satz.","f":"Field blend versus cuvée"},{"b":"A grower's tavern selling only its own wine, open on set days under a pine bough; also the current year's wine. Right granted by Joseph II's decree of 1784.","f":"The Heuriger, defined"},{"b":"Limestone and calcareous sandstone on the Nussberg (saline Riesling and Gemischter Satz); loess over limestone on the Bisamberg (broad Grüner Veltliner); brown earth and gravel in the south.","f":"Vienna's soils and what they give"},{"b":"Grape must caught mid fermentation: cloudy, sweet, faintly fizzy, drunk within days in autumn. Not a wine category and not for ageing.","f":"Sturm: what it is"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Grüner Veltliner","role":"lead"},{"name":"Riesling","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Blanc","role":"blending"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"blending"},{"name":"Neuburger","role":"minor"},{"name":"Rotgipfler","role":"minor"},{"name":"Zierfandler","role":"minor"},{"name":"Zweigelt","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"minor"}],"index":1,"name":"Wien (Vienna)","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Wien (Vienna)","exp":"The DAC asks for at least three white Qualitätswein grapes co planted in one Vienna vineyard, with the largest at most half and the third largest at least a tenth of the planting.","id":"r-0f3ox1hi","opts":["Three","Five","Two","Seven"],"q":"How many white grape varieties, at the least, must a Wiener Gemischter Satz DAC contain?"},{"accept":["12.5%","12.5","12,5%","twelve and a half percent","12.5 percent"],"ans":"12.5%","cat":"Wien (Vienna)","exp":"The plain DAC is dry and capped at 12.5% alcohol; a bottling with a Ried or village name reverses the rule and must reach at least 12.5%.","id":"r-exuguotq","q":"What is the maximum alcohol of a Wiener Gemischter Satz DAC that names no vineyard or village?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Wien (Vienna)","exp":"The Nussberg rises over Nussdorf and Heiligenstadt in Döbling, the 19th district, on the right bank; the Bisamberg faces it from the left bank in the 21st.","id":"r-z43nyuqb","opts":["The right bank, in the 23rd district","The left bank, in the 21st district","The right bank, in the 19th district","The left bank, in the 22nd district"],"q":"On which bank of the Danube does the Nussberg lie?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Wien (Vienna)","exp":"Wiener Gemischter Satz DAC was created for the 2013 vintage; 2008 was the Kamptal's DAC year and 2016 the vintage from which Ried became compulsory before vineyard names.","id":"r-kxa44g16","opts":["2016","2008","2010","2013"],"q":"From which vintage has Wiener Gemischter Satz been a DAC?"},{"accept":["Sturm","sturm","Der Sturm"],"ans":"Sturm","cat":"Wien (Vienna)","exp":"Sturm is cloudy, sweet, fizzy must caught mid ferment and sold for a few weeks; it is drunk fresh and never kept.","id":"r-pxws6l1m","q":"What is the Austrian name for partly fermented grape must sold in autumn?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Wien (Vienna)","exp":"A Heuriger is a grower's own tavern: Vienna's statute, rooted in Joseph II's decree of 1784, lets it sell only wine from its own vineyards, on its own premises, for a set number of days each year, under the pine bough that shows it is open.","id":"r-jeq0wqvx","opts":["Only the grower's own","Any Vienna grower's","Any Austrian grower's","Any wine bought from a merchant"],"q":"Under Vienna's Heuriger rule, whose wine may a Heuriger sell?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Wien (Vienna)","exp":"The Bisamberg rises over Stammersdorf and Strebersdorf on the left bank; the Nussberg and Kahlenberg are on the right bank and the Laaer Berg is in the city's south.","id":"r-w50kzlg4","opts":["Laaer Berg","Bisamberg","Nussberg","Kahlenberg"],"q":"Which hill carries Vienna's vineyards on the left bank of the Danube?"}],"sheet":"austria","slug":"wien","summary":"Vienna is the only capital city with a serious commercial vineyard inside its limits, and its wine is drunk in the Heuriger taverns where it grows. The vines sit on the city's northern and southern edges: the Nussberg and the Heurigen villages of the 19th district on the right bank of the Danube, the Bisamberg across the river, and Mauer and Oberlaa in the south. The signature is Wiener Gemischter Satz, a field blend of at least three white grapes planted, picked and fermented together, a DAC since the 2013 vintage. Grüner Veltliner, Riesling and Weissburgunder are bottled alone too, with a little Zweigelt. Everything is dry, fresh and meant for the table."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Grüner Veltliner only, dry, at least 12% alcohol, fresh and peppery with no obvious oak or botrytis; Austria's first DAC, created in 2002 for that vintage.","f":"Weinviertel DAC: the rule"},{"b":"From the 2009 vintage: Grüner Veltliner, dry, at least 13% alcohol, a delicate wood or botrytis note allowed, released later.","f":"Weinviertel DAC Reserve"},{"b":"North east Niederösterreich: north of Vienna and the Danube, Manhartsberg in the west, Czech border north, March river and Slovakia east. Austria's largest region.","f":"Where the Weinviertel is"},{"b":"Granite and gneiss at Retz; loess nearly everywhere; Jurassic limestone klippen at Falkenstein and Staatz; sand, gravel and marl towards the March.","f":"Weinviertel soils, west to east"},{"b":"Retz (west, cellars), Mailberg (reds), Poysdorf (north, Sekt, Eiswein), Falkenstein (limestone), Wolkersdorf and Stetten (warm south by the Bisamberg).","f":"Weinviertel towns to know"},{"b":"The little pepper: the Weinviertel's word for the white pepper and radish bite of its Grüner Veltliner, the DAC's required character.","f":"Pfefferl: what it means"},{"b":"A lane of press houses with cellars tunnelled into the loess behind, found in most Weinviertel villages; a cultural feature, not a vineyard rank.","f":"Kellergasse: what it is"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Grüner Veltliner","role":"lead"},{"name":"Welschriesling","role":"blending"},{"name":"Riesling","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Zweigelt","role":"minor"},{"name":"Blaufränkisch","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"minor"}],"index":2,"name":"Weinviertel","questions":[{"a":1,"cat":"Weinviertel","exp":"Weinviertel DAC was created in 2002 for that vintage; Mittelburgenland followed in 2005, Kamptal in 2008 and the Wachau only in 2020.","id":"r-u6vb0fir","opts":["Mittelburgenland","Weinviertel","Wachau","Kamptal"],"q":"Which was the first Austrian region to receive a DAC?"},{"accept":["Grüner Veltliner","Gruner Veltliner","Gruener Veltliner","Grüner","GV"],"ans":"Grüner Veltliner","cat":"Weinviertel","exp":"The Weinviertel DAC protects dry, peppery Grüner Veltliner and no other grape; Welschriesling, Riesling and Zweigelt from the region are Niederösterreich Qualitätswein.","id":"r-srfs6sjc","q":"Which grape alone may be labelled Weinviertel DAC?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Weinviertel","exp":"The Reserve, added with the 2009 vintage, asks at least 13% alcohol and a dry wine, where the Klassik DAC asks 12%.","id":"r-48hzlxz9","opts":["12%","13.5%","13%","12.5%"],"q":"What is the minimum alcohol of a Weinviertel DAC Reserve?"},{"accept":["The white pepper note of Grüner Veltliner","white pepper","pepper","the peppery character","peppery note","white pepper note"],"ans":"The white pepper note of Grüner Veltliner","cat":"Weinviertel","exp":"Pfefferl, the little pepper, is the local name for the white pepper and radish bite that the DAC requires of the region's Grüner Veltliner.","id":"r-tl6ux27t","q":"What does the Weinviertel word Pfefferl describe?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Weinviertel","exp":"Deep, lime rich loess underlies most of the plateau and feeds Grüner Veltliner; gneiss is the Wachau's terrace rock, slate the Mosel's, basalt the Styrian Vulkanland's.","id":"r-ksdao77t","opts":["Loess","Slate","Basalt","Gneiss"],"q":"Which soil covers most of the Weinviertel's vineyard?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Weinviertel","exp":"Poysdorf in the cool north has long made sparkling base wine from Welschriesling and Grüner Veltliner; Retz is known for its cellars, Mailberg for reds and Falkenstein for limestone.","id":"r-4v7y2x9f","opts":["Poysdorf","Retz","Falkenstein","Mailberg"],"q":"Which Weinviertel town is regarded as Austria's Sekt capital?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Weinviertel","exp":"The Weinviertel fills the north east of Niederösterreich north of Vienna and the Danube; the Thermenregion lies south of the city, Carnuntum east and the Wagram and Danube regions west.","id":"r-ym2zqd9c","opts":["South","West","East","North"],"q":"In which direction from Vienna does the Weinviertel lie?"}],"sheet":"austria","slug":"weinviertel","summary":"The Weinviertel, the wine quarter, is the largest wine region in Austria, a rolling plateau of loess and limestone that fills the north east of Niederösterreich between the Manhartsberg, the Danube and the Czech and Slovak borders. It is Grüner Veltliner's homeland and the birthplace of the DAC: Weinviertel DAC, created in 2002 for that vintage, was Austria's first and is reserved for peppery Grüner Veltliner alone, Klassik and, since 2009, Reserve. Retz, Poysdorf, Falkenstein and Wolkersdorf are the names to hold, Poysdorf as the Sekt town. The wines are dry, fresh, modestly priced and marked by the local word Pfefferl, the little pepper."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Mittelburgenland 2005 (Blaufränkisch), Leithaberg 2008 red and 2009 white (Blaufränkisch; four whites), Eisenberg 2009 (Blaufränkisch), Neusiedlersee 2012 (Zweigelt), Rosalia 2018 (reds and rosé), Ruster Ausbruch 2020 (sweet).","f":"Burgenland's six DACs: grape and year"},{"b":"A steppe lake about 36 km long and one to two metres deep: it warms fast, holds warmth into autumn, and its morning mists burn off by midday, the humid then dry cycle noble rot needs.","f":"Why the Neusiedlersee breeds botrytis"},{"b":"From the 2020 vintage: white grapes grown and vinified in Rust, hand picked botrytised berries at 30° KMW, at least 45 g/l residual sugar. The only lawful Ausbruch.","f":"Ruster Ausbruch DAC: the rule"},{"b":"Blaufränkisch: dark cherry, black pepper, iron, high acid, firm tannin, ages. Zweigelt: sour cherry, soft tannin, juicy, drink young; its parent is Blaufränkisch.","f":"Blaufränkisch versus Zweigelt on the palate"},{"b":"At least 85% Blaufränkisch, with Zweigelt, St. Laurent or Pinot Noir as partners; at least 12.5% alcohol; released in the September of the second year.","f":"Leithaberg DAC red: the blend rule"},{"b":"Seewinkel: sand, gravel, loam, salt pans. Leithaberg: Leitha limestone and mica schist. Mittelburgenland: heavy clay and loam. Eisenberg: green schist, slate, iron rich loam.","f":"Burgenland soils, north to south"},{"b":"Reed wine: healthy grapes dried at least three months on reed mats from the lake, pressed at 25° KMW or more; Austria's Strohwein in the Seewinkel's version. Not botrytised.","f":"Schilfwein: how it is made"},{"b":"Illmitz, Apetlon and Podersdorf on the east shore for sweet wines; Gols on the north shore for Zweigelt, Blaufränkisch and the Pannobile blends.","f":"The Seewinkel's villages and the reds village"},{"b":"A free town since 1681 on the west shore, limestone and schist, home of Ruster Ausbruch DAC and of the Mariental Blaufränkisch.","f":"Rust in one line"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Blaufränkisch","role":"lead"},{"name":"Zweigelt","role":"lead"},{"name":"Welschriesling","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"blending"},{"name":"Pinot Blanc","role":"blending"},{"name":"Neuburger","role":"minor"},{"name":"Grüner Veltliner","role":"minor"},{"name":"St. Laurent","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"minor"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"minor"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"minor"},{"name":"Furmint","role":"minor"}],"index":3,"name":"Burgenland","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Burgenland","exp":"Mittelburgenland is the Blaufränkischland and its DAC, from the 2005 vintage, admits Blaufränkisch alone; Zweigelt is the Neusiedlersee DAC's grape.","id":"r-ny1gwetu","opts":["Blaufränkisch","Zweigelt","St. Laurent","Pinot Noir"],"q":"Which grape is named by the Mittelburgenland DAC?"},{"accept":["30","30 KMW","30° KMW","30 degrees KMW","thirty"],"ans":"30","cat":"Burgenland","exp":"Since the 2020 vintage Ruster Ausbruch DAC is picked at 30° KMW, the same as Trockenbeerenauslese, from hand picked botrytised berries grown and made in Rust.","id":"r-0wpe4qp7","q":"What is the minimum must weight, in KMW, for Ruster Ausbruch DAC?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Burgenland","exp":"Neusiedlersee DAC, created in 2012, is a red from Zweigelt, Klassik and Reserve; the lake's sweet wines from Welschriesling and Chardonnay are Prädikatswein outside the DAC.","id":"r-wimdlutc","opts":["Zweigelt","Blaufränkisch","Welschriesling","Chardonnay"],"q":"Which grape does the Neusiedlersee DAC protect?"},{"accept":["East","the east shore","eastern","east shore","the eastern shore"],"ans":"East","cat":"Burgenland","exp":"The Seewinkel, the lake corner, is the flat east shore around Illmitz, Apetlon and Podersdorf; Rust and the Leithaberg are on the west shore.","id":"r-w20d69dl","q":"On which shore of the Neusiedlersee does the Seewinkel lie?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Burgenland","exp":"Leithaberg DAC red is at least 85% Blaufränkisch with Zweigelt, St. Laurent or Pinot Noir as partners; 60% is the Zweigelt floor of a Neusiedlersee DAC Reserve blend.","id":"r-697wjy9a","opts":["75%","60%","100%","85%"],"q":"What is the minimum share of Blaufränkisch in a Leithaberg DAC red?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Burgenland","exp":"Schilfwein is the Seewinkel's Strohwein, healthy grapes dried on mats of reed cut from the lake shore for at least three months before pressing at 25° KMW or above.","id":"r-x7r4jgug","opts":["Straw","Pine boughs","Reed mats","Wooden racks"],"q":"What is a Schilfwein dried on before pressing?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Burgenland","exp":"Illmitz, between the lake and the salt pans, is where the Seewinkel's Beerenauslese and Trockenbeerenauslese are made; Gols is the reds village, Rust the Ausbruch town across the water and Deutschkreutz the heart of Mittelburgenland.","id":"r-st8ar5c1","opts":["Rust","Illmitz","Deutschkreutz","Gols"],"q":"Which village is the capital of the Seewinkel's sweet wines?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Burgenland","exp":"Eisenberg DAC, from the 2009 vintage, covers Südburgenland's Blaufränkisch on green schist, slate and iron rich loam along the Pinka; Leithaberg is limestone and schist by the lake, Rosalia the hills between.","id":"r-nnfyyadq","opts":["Leithaberg DAC","Eisenberg DAC","Neusiedlersee DAC","Rosalia DAC"],"q":"Which Burgenland DAC names Blaufränkisch from the slate soils of the far south?"}],"sheet":"austria","slug":"burgenland","summary":"Burgenland is the long, thin federal state down Austria's eastern border with Hungary, and the country's red and sweet wine heartland. The Neusiedlersee, a vast steppe lake barely a metre or two deep, sits at its northern end: the Seewinkel on its east shore makes botrytis sweet wines and Zweigelt reds, the Leithaberg on the west shore makes mineral whites and Blaufränkisch on limestone and schist, and the free town of Rust makes Ruster Ausbruch. South of the lake the Rosalia hills, then Mittelburgenland, the Blaufränkisch country, then the Eisenberg in the far south on iron rich slate. Six DACs cover it, five of them naming Blaufränkisch or Zweigelt. The climate is Pannonian, Austria's warmest."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Südsteiermark: Sauvignon Blanc, Morillon, Muskateller on opok (Gamlitz, Zieregg). Vulkanland Steiermark: basalt, Traminer at Klöch. Weststeiermark: Schilcher rosé from Blauer Wildbacher on gneiss and schist.","f":"Styria's three regions and what each is known for"},{"b":"All three from the 2018 vintage, on the origin pyramid: Gebietswein, Ortswein (named villages), Riedenwein (hand picked, released from May of the second year). No single grape; a list of nine.","f":"The Styrian DACs: year and shape"},{"b":"Chardonnay under its Styrian name, treated as a local grape: lean, mineral and unoaked at the base, nutty and cask aged from the Rieden. Nothing else.","f":"Morillon: decode it"},{"b":"A pale, high acid rosé of Blauer Wildbacher from the Weststeiermark alone: redcurrant and rhubarb, drunk young and cold, still or sparkling.","f":"Schilcher: decode it"},{"b":"Klassik: fresh, steel, gooseberry, elderflower, nettle, early bottled. Riedenwein: riper, whole bunch pressed, large old casks, long lees, smoky and dense, built to age.","f":"Styrian Sauvignon Blanc: two faces"},{"b":"Südsteiermark: opok (calcareous marl), sand, gravel, conglomerate; schist and gneiss in the Sausal. Vulkanland: basalt, tuff, volcanic ash. Weststeiermark: gneiss and schist of the Koralpe.","f":"Styrian soils by region"},{"b":"Mediterranean warmth over the Alps' southern edge: humid summers, high rainfall, hail, mild autumns, cold winters; steep airy slopes and hand work follow from it.","f":"The Illyrian climate"},{"b":"Südoststeiermark, renamed Vulkanland Steiermark in 2016 for its extinct volcanoes at Klöch, Kapfenstein, Straden and Riegersburg.","f":"Vulkanland Steiermark's old name and date"},{"b":"The wooden wind rattle on a tall post that turns in Styrian vineyards each autumn to scare birds off the ripening grapes; the region's emblem.","f":"Klapotetz: what it is"},{"b":"The young wine of the vintage, released each November under a shared Styrian label: light, fruity, Styria's answer to the Heuriger wine.","f":"Steirischer Junker: what it is"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"lead"},{"name":"Welschriesling","role":"blending"},{"name":"Gelber Muskateller","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Blanc","role":"blending"},{"name":"Pinot Gris / Grigio","role":"minor"},{"name":"Traminer","role":"minor"},{"name":"Blauer Wildbacher","role":"lead"},{"name":"Zweigelt","role":"minor"}],"index":4,"name":"Steiermark (Styria)","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Steiermark (Styria)","exp":"Morillon is the Styrian name for Chardonnay, made lean and mineral or cask aged from the Rieden; the other three are grown in Styria under their own names.","id":"r-hjj8hv0f","opts":["Chardonnay","Welschriesling","Pinot Blanc","Sauvignon Blanc"],"q":"Which grape is sold under the name Morillon in Steiermark?"},{"accept":["Blauer Wildbacher","blauer wildbacher","Wildbacher","Blauer Wildbacher grape"],"ans":"Blauer Wildbacher","cat":"Steiermark (Styria)","exp":"Schilcher is the pale, high acid rosé of Blauer Wildbacher, the red grape of the Weststeiermark around Stainz and Deutschlandsberg; a little is also made red and sparkling.","id":"r-wxda20ed","q":"From which grape is Schilcher made?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Steiermark (Styria)","exp":"Südsteiermark, Vulkanland Steiermark and Weststeiermark DAC were all created for the 2018 vintage; 2016 was the year Südoststeiermark was renamed Vulkanland and 2020 the Wachau's DAC vintage.","id":"r-3kq7rsgj","opts":["2012","2020","2018","2016"],"q":"From which vintage have the three Styrian DACs applied?"},{"accept":["Calcareous marl","marl","a calcareous marl","limestone marl","marl that crumbles like slate","clay marl"],"ans":"Calcareous marl","cat":"Steiermark (Styria)","exp":"Opok is the local name for a calcareous marl that dries hard and splits like slate; it holds water in the Südsteiermark's wet climate and gives the Sauvignon Blanc its stony edge.","id":"r-1c58bc73","q":"What is opok, the soil of the Südsteiermark's hills?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Steiermark (Styria)","exp":"Klöch's basalt slopes on the Slovenian border are the home of Styrian Traminer; Gamlitz and Kitzeck are Südsteiermark villages and Stainz is the Schilcher town of the west.","id":"r-nkljgwed","opts":["Klöch","Stainz","Kitzeck","Gamlitz"],"q":"Which Vulkanland Steiermark village is famous for Traminer?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Steiermark (Styria)","exp":"Vulkanland Steiermark, called Südoststeiermark until 2016, sits on the basalt, tuff and ash of extinct volcanoes at Klöch, Kapfenstein and Straden. Opok marl is the Südsteiermark's soil, gneiss and schist belong to the Weststeiermark and the Sausal, and loess to the Danube and the Weinviertel.","id":"r-lctfzent","opts":["Loess","Basalt and tuff","Gneiss and schist","Opok marl"],"q":"Which soil marks the vineyards of Vulkanland Steiermark?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Steiermark (Styria)","exp":"Sauvignon Blanc is Styria's flag, grown on the opok hills of Gamlitz, Ehrenhausen and Leutschach; Grüner Veltliner belongs to the Danube and Traminer to Klöch in the Vulkanland.","id":"r-69p0n4rn","opts":["Riesling","Gewürztraminer","Grüner Veltliner","Sauvignon Blanc"],"q":"Which grape is the signature of the Südsteiermark?"},{"accept":["Gelber Muskateller","gelber muskateller","Muskateller","Muscat Blanc à Petits Grains","Muscat Blanc a Petits Grains","Muscat à Petits Grains","Muscat a Petits Grains"],"ans":"Gelber Muskateller","cat":"Steiermark (Styria)","exp":"Gelber Muskateller is Muscat Blanc à Petits Grains under its Austrian name, made bone dry in Styria with orange blossom, elderflower and sharp acidity. Welschriesling is the neutral everyday white, and Traminer the Klöch speciality of the Vulkanland.","id":"r-284h9tgj","q":"Which Muscat variety does Steiermark make as a dry, fragrant aperitif white?","sa":1}],"sheet":"austria","slug":"steiermark","summary":"Steiermark, Styria, is Austria's green south east: steep, wooded hills south of Graz running to the Slovenian border, wetter than the Danube and worked almost entirely by hand. It is white wine country of a different kind, Sauvignon Blanc above all, with Chardonnay under its local name Morillon, Gelber Muskateller, Welschriesling and Weissburgunder, all dry. Three regions share it: Südsteiermark along the border, the land of Gamlitz and the great Rieden; Vulkanland Steiermark to the east on old volcanoes, with Traminer at Klöch; Weststeiermark around Stainz and Deutschlandsberg, home of Schilcher, the piercing rosé of Blauer Wildbacher. Each has had a DAC since the 2018 vintage, built on the origin pyramid of Gebietswein, Ortswein and Riedenwein."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Los Carneros, Oak Knoll District, Yountville, Stags Leap District (east side), Oakville, Rutherford, St Helena, Calistoga; Coombsville sits east of Napa city and Crystal Springs below Howell Mountain.","f":"Napa's valley-floor and bench AVAs, south to north"},{"b":"Mayacamas, west: Mount Veeder, Spring Mountain District, Diamond Mountain District. Vaca Range, east: Howell Mountain, Atlas Peak, Chiles Valley. Pritchard Hill in the east is a vineyard name, not an AVA.","f":"Napa's mountain AVAs: which range"},{"b":"Fog and wind enter from San Pablo Bay at the southern end and weaken as they travel north, so Carneros grows Pinot Noir and Chardonnay and Calistoga, about 50 kilometres north, ripens Cabernet in heat tempered by cool nights.","f":"Why Carneros is cool and Calistoga hot"},{"b":"Russian River Valley: Pinot Noir, Chardonnay. Sonoma Coast and Fort Ross-Seaview: cold Pinot Noir. Dry Creek Valley: Zinfandel. Alexander Valley: Cabernet. Knights Valley: warmest, Cabernet. Petaluma Gap: windy Pinot and Syrah.","f":"Sonoma AVAs and their grapes"},{"b":"Stags Leap District is the AVA, no apostrophe. Stag's Leap Wine Cellars, apostrophe before the s, won Paris 1976. Stags' Leap Winery, apostrophe after, is a separate house.","f":"The three Stags Leap spellings"},{"b":"A fine sandy loam of old marine sediment under the Russian River Valley and Green Valley; free-draining and low in vigour, it gives the valley's Pinot Noir and Chardonnay their finesse.","f":"Goldridge soil: where and why"},{"b":"Insignia (Joseph Phelps) 1974, the first. Opus One (Mondavi with a Bordeaux first growth) 1979. Dominus (Napanook, Yountville) 1983. Harlan Estate 1990. Screaming Eagle 1992.","f":"Napa proprietary reds and first vintages"},{"b":"Napa: a sub-AVA label must also print Napa Valley, state law of 1990. Sonoma: any AVA lying wholly in the county must also print Sonoma County, on wine bottled from 1 January 2014.","f":"Conjunctive labelling in the two counties"},{"b":"Seventeen by November 2024. The first were Los Carneros and Howell Mountain in 1983; the seventeenth was Crystal Springs of Napa Valley, in force from 15 November 2024, on the Vaca slopes between St Helena and Calistoga.","f":"Napa Valley's nested AVAs: how many by late 2024, and the last added"},{"b":"The only nested Napa Valley AVA that crosses into Sonoma County, approved in 1983 at the southern end, open to San Pablo Bay: cool, windy and foggy, for Pinot Noir, Chardonnay and sparkling base wine.","f":"Los Carneros: what makes it unusual"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"lead"},{"name":"Zinfandel","role":"lead"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"blending"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Cabernet Franc","role":"blending"},{"name":"Petit Verdot","role":"blending"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"minor"},{"name":"Petite Sirah","role":"minor"}],"index":0,"name":"Napa & Sonoma (CA)","questions":[{"a":1,"cat":"Napa & Sonoma (CA)","exp":"Los Carneros straddles the county line at the southern end of both counties, open to San Pablo Bay; Oak Knoll, Yountville and Coombsville lie wholly in Napa.","id":"r-jfisgk27","opts":["Coombsville","Los Carneros","Yountville","Oak Knoll District"],"q":"Which nested Napa Valley AVA extends into Sonoma County?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Napa & Sonoma (CA)","exp":"Howell Mountain rises on the Vaca side, east of St Helena; Mount Veeder, Spring Mountain and Diamond Mountain are the Mayacamas AVAs on the west, and the Santa Cruz Mountains and the Diablo Range lie well to the south.","id":"r-a5bf1hwz","opts":["Vaca Range","Santa Cruz Mountains","Mayacamas Mountains","Diablo Range"],"q":"In which mountain range does Howell Mountain lie?"},{"accept":["Russian River Valley","Russian River","Russian River Valley AVA","RRV"],"ans":"Russian River Valley","cat":"Napa & Sonoma (CA)","exp":"Goldridge, a fine, free-draining sandy loam of marine origin, underlies the Russian River Valley and gives its Pinot Noir and Chardonnay their finesse; Chalk Hill is its volcanic corner, and the benches of Dry Creek and Alexander valleys are gravel.","id":"r-nbte2rv8","q":"Goldridge sandy loam is the signature soil of which Sonoma AVA?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Napa & Sonoma (CA)","exp":"Stag's Leap Wine Cellars' 1973 from the Stags Leap District took first place; Heitz entered its 1970 Martha's Vineyard and placed lower, Chateau Montelena won the white flight with Chardonnay, and Trefethen did not compete.","id":"r-hvl5s9vf","opts":["Trefethen Family Vineyards","Chateau Montelena","Heitz Wine Cellars","Stag's Leap Wine Cellars"],"q":"Which house's 1973 Cabernet Sauvignon won the red flight of the 1976 Paris tasting?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Napa & Sonoma (CA)","exp":"Dry Creek Valley's warm, gravelly benches north-west of Healdsburg carry head-trained Zinfandel, some of it a century old; the other three are Pinot Noir country in the fog.","id":"r-b4lcgwuj","opts":["Russian River Valley","Petaluma Gap","Dry Creek Valley","Sonoma Coast"],"q":"Which Sonoma AVA is known above all for old-vine Zinfandel?"},{"accept":["Sonoma County","Sonoma County appellation","the words Sonoma County"],"ans":"Sonoma County","cat":"Napa & Sonoma (CA)","exp":"State law makes any AVA lying wholly in Sonoma County print Sonoma County beside it on wine bottled from 1 January 2014; Napa has required Napa Valley beside its sub-AVAs since 1990.","id":"r-olr31f10","q":"Under California's conjunctive labelling law, which appellation must a Dry Creek Valley label also carry?","sa":1},{"accept":["To Kalon","To-Kalon","To Kalon Vineyard","Tokalon"],"ans":"To Kalon","cat":"Napa & Sonoma (CA)","exp":"To Kalon was planted on the Oakville bench from 1868, and the Robert Mondavi Winery was built on part of it in 1966; like Heitz's Martha's Vineyard nearby, it is a vineyard name, not an AVA.","id":"r-mvt12gz9","q":"Which historic Oakville vineyard, first planted in 1868, became the home of the Robert Mondavi Winery in 1966?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Napa & Sonoma (CA)","exp":"Silver Oak has aged its Cabernet Sauvignon in American oak alone since its founding in 1972, for both its Napa Valley and Alexander Valley bottlings, where new French oak is the Napa norm.","id":"r-ycjji2uf","opts":["Silver Oak Cellars","Shafer Vineyards","Caymus Vineyards","Duckhorn Vineyards"],"q":"Which Napa house ages its Cabernet Sauvignon in American oak only?"}],"sheet":"california","slug":"napa-sonoma","summary":"Napa and Sonoma are neighbouring counties north of San Francisco Bay, parted by the Mayacamas Mountains: one course heading, two appellations. Napa is a narrow valley of Cabernet Sauvignon, cool at Carneros by the bay and hot at Calistoga, with nested AVAs on floor, bench and mountain. Sonoma is wider and more varied: Pinot Noir and Chardonnay in the fog of the Russian River Valley and the Sonoma Coast, Zinfandel in Dry Creek, rounder Cabernet in Alexander Valley. Napa won the 1976 Judgment of Paris, and a sub-AVA label must name its parent."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Distance from the Pacific and the gaps in the Coast Ranges decide the climate more than latitude: Sta. Rita Hills, well south of Paso Robles, is far cooler because its valley opens straight to the sea.","f":"The Central Coast's governing rule"},{"b":"The Santa Ynez and San Rafael mountains run east to west, so the valleys open onto the Pacific: Sta. Rita Hills in the cold west grows Pinot Noir and Chardonnay, Happy Canyon in the warm east grows Cabernet.","f":"Santa Barbara's transverse ranges: why they matter"},{"b":"Inland San Luis Obispo County, 1983: hot days, cold nights, calcareous shale and limestone on the west, alluvium on the east; Cabernet most planted, old Zinfandel, Rhône blends; eleven districts since 2014; Tablas Creek the Rhône nursery.","f":"Paso Robles in one line"},{"b":"Monte Bello Ridge, Santa Cruz Mountains, limestone from about 400 to 820 metres; Cabernet-led Bordeaux blend; American oak; first vintage 1962; fifth in Paris 1976, first at the 2006 thirtieth-anniversary rematch.","f":"Ridge Monte Bello: place, grape, oak, Paris"},{"b":"Monterey Bay's cold water sends an afternoon wind down the Salinas Valley; the Santa Lucia Highlands, terraces on its west wall, 1991, grow dark, lush Pinot Noir, Chardonnay and cool Syrah.","f":"What cools the Santa Lucia Highlands, and what grows there"},{"b":"Sta. Rita Hills (2001, west, Pinot and Chardonnay), Ballard Canyon (2013, Syrah), Los Olivos District (2016, centre), Happy Canyon of Santa Barbara (2009, east, Cabernet), Alisos Canyon (2020, Rhône grapes).","f":"The Santa Ynez Valley's five nested AVAs"},{"b":"Bien Nacido (Santa Maria Valley, 1973: Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Syrah). Sanford & Benedict (Sta. Rita Hills, 1971: the area's first Pinot Noir). Monte Bello (Santa Cruz Mountains, Ridge's Cabernet).","f":"Three vineyards every Central Coast list names"},{"b":"Central Coast houses of the 1980s that planted Syrah, Grenache, Mourvèdre, Viognier and Roussanne: Bonny Doon's Le Cigare Volant, Qupé's Syrah, Alban in Edna Valley, Tablas Creek's nursery from Beaucastel in Paso Robles.","f":"The Rhône Rangers: who and what"},{"b":"Chalone (Monterey side, about 550 metres, vines from 1919, also Pinot Blanc and Chenin Blanc) and Mount Harlan (San Benito side, Calera, 1975, single-vineyard Pinot Noir and Viognier).","f":"Limestone Pinot Noir in the Gavilan Range: the two AVAs"},{"b":"Business and Professions Code section 25244: a wine from any Paso Robles district must print Paso Robles beside the district's name, for wine bottled from 1 January 2008; the eleven districts arrived in 2014.","f":"Conjunctive labelling for Paso Robles"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"lead"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"lead"},{"name":"Zinfandel","role":"minor"},{"name":"Grenache","role":"blending"},{"name":"Mourvèdre","role":"blending"},{"name":"Viognier","role":"minor"},{"name":"Roussanne","role":"minor"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Riesling","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chenin Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Blanc","role":"minor"}],"index":1,"name":"Central Coast (CA)","questions":[{"a":2,"cat":"Central Coast (CA)","exp":"The Santa Cruz Mountains AVA of 1981 follows the 122-metre (400-foot) contour on its ocean side and 244 metres (800 feet) on its bay side; Howell Mountain's 427-metre (1,400-foot) line came in 1983, Mount Harlan in 1990 and the Santa Lucia Highlands in 1991.","id":"r-h2bkxj0e","opts":["Howell Mountain","Santa Lucia Highlands","Santa Cruz Mountains","Mount Harlan"],"q":"Which was the first California AVA to have its boundary drawn by elevation?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Central Coast (CA)","exp":"Sta. Rita Hills takes the cold western end between Lompoc and Buellton, nearest the Pacific, for Pinot Noir and Chardonnay; Ballard Canyon and Los Olivos District lie in the middle and Happy Canyon at the warm eastern end.","id":"r-g3lia2ky","opts":["Los Olivos District","Sta. Rita Hills","Happy Canyon of Santa Barbara","Ballard Canyon"],"q":"Which AVA occupies the western end of the Santa Ynez Valley?"},{"accept":["Tablas Creek","Tablas Creek Vineyard","Tablas"],"ans":"Tablas Creek","cat":"Central Coast (CA)","exp":"Tablas Creek in the Adelaida District imported Rhône cuttings from Beaucastel, ran them through quarantine and sold them on, which is why so many Grenache, Mourvèdre and Roussanne vines across the state trace to it.","id":"r-j6atrj0x","q":"Which Paso Robles estate was founded in 1989 as a partnership with the family of Château de Beaucastel?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Central Coast (CA)","exp":"Paso Robles, behind the Santa Lucia Range, swings from hot days to cold nights; Edna Valley and Arroyo Grande Valley are the county's cool coastal valleys, and York Mountain is a tiny, cool AVA in the hills to its west.","id":"r-dhoww9sb","opts":["Paso Robles","York Mountain","Arroyo Grande Valley","Edna Valley"],"q":"Which San Luis Obispo County AVA lies inland of the Santa Lucia Range, with hot days and cold nights?"},{"accept":["Bien Nacido","Bien Nacido Vineyard","Bien Nacido Vineyards"],"ans":"Bien Nacido","cat":"Central Coast (CA)","exp":"Bien Nacido is the valley's most named vineyard, sold to many houses for Pinot Noir, Chardonnay and Syrah; a vineyard designation needs 95 per cent from that site under federal law.","id":"r-zdi690yk","q":"Which Santa Maria Valley vineyard, planted in 1973, appears on labels from Au Bon Climat, Qupé and many other houses?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Central Coast (CA)","exp":"Paso Robles was divided into eleven districts in 2014, from Adelaida and Willow Creek on the limestone west to Estrella and Geneseo on the warm eastern floor; As of 2026 Napa Valley holds seventeen nested AVAs and the Santa Ynez Valley five.","id":"r-v57c4zwq","opts":["Eight","Five","Seventeen","Eleven"],"q":"How many district AVAs were approved inside Paso Robles in 2014?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Central Coast (CA)","exp":"Calera's founder searched California for limestone after working in Burgundy and found it at about 670 metres (2,200 feet) in the Gavilan Range; Goldridge is the Russian River Valley's soil, red volcanic soil Howell Mountain's, and granite lies beside limestone at Chalone.","id":"r-niqpylbg","opts":["Limestone","Goldridge sandy loam","Red volcanic soil","Granite"],"q":"On which kind of soil does Calera grow its Pinot Noir on Mount Harlan?"},{"accept":["Monte Bello","Montebello","Ridge Monte Bello","Monte Bello 1971"],"ans":"Monte Bello","cat":"Central Coast (CA)","exp":"The 1971 Monte Bello, a Cabernet-led blend from the Santa Cruz Mountains, placed fifth in 1976 and first among the reds at the thirtieth-anniversary retasting in 2006, when the Californian wines outlasted the Bordeaux.","id":"r-1jyz0aqd","q":"Which Ridge wine led the reds at the 2006 rematch of the 1976 Paris tasting?","sa":1}],"sheet":"california","slug":"central-coast","summary":"The Central Coast is the Pacific strip from San Francisco Bay to Santa Barbara, an umbrella AVA of 1985 holding a set of climates; its rule is distance from the sea and whether the hills let the marine air in. The Santa Cruz Mountains make structured Cabernet above the fog; Monterey's Salinas Valley funnels wind to the Santa Lucia Highlands' Pinot Noir; Paso Robles, inland and hot by day, is Rhône and Zinfandel country on limestone; Santa Barbara's east to west valleys pour cold air onto the Pinot Noir and Chardonnay of Sta. Rita Hills."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Approved in 1983. The Coast Range west, the Cascades east, the Columbia River north, the Calapooya Mountains south of Eugene; about 240 kilometres (150 miles) long.","f":"Willamette Valley AVA: year, and its boundaries"},{"b":"Red, iron-rich clay weathered from basalt, the Dundee Hills' signature and Oregon's state soil since 2011; also much of the Eola-Amity Hills.","f":"Jory soil: what it is and where"},{"b":"Ancient marine sediment, sandstone and siltstone, under Yamhill-Carlton, Ribbon Ridge and McMinnville; darker, earthier Pinot Noir.","f":"Willakenzie soil: what it is and where"},{"b":"Dundee Hills, Yamhill-Carlton, McMinnville and Ribbon Ridge in 2005; Chehalem Mountains and Eola-Amity Hills in 2006.","f":"The six older nested AVAs and their years"},{"b":"Van Duzer Corridor 2019; Laurelwood District and Tualatin Hills 2020; Lower Long Tom 2021; Mount Pisgah, Polk County, Oregon 2022.","f":"The five newer nested AVAs"},{"b":"A gap in the Coast Range west of Salem that funnels cool Pacific air across the Eola-Amity Hills each afternoon; an AVA itself since 2019.","f":"The Van Duzer Corridor: what and why it matters"},{"b":"The South Block Reserve placed among the top Pinot Noirs at a Paris tasting in 1979 and nearly beat Drouhin's own Burgundy in a 1980 rematch; Drouhin came to Oregon in 1987.","f":"Eyrie's 1975 Pinot Noir: why it matters"},{"b":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay and Meunier, traditional method, aged on lees; Argyle's house style since 1987.","f":"The three grapes of a Willamette Brut"},{"b":"A label naming a Willamette nested AVA must also carry the words Willamette Valley, with 95 per cent of the grapes from the named area.","f":"Nested AVA labels: the 2023 rule"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Gris / Grigio","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"lead"},{"name":"Riesling","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Gamay","role":"rising"},{"name":"Pinot Meunier","role":"blending"}],"index":0,"name":"Willamette Valley (OR)","questions":[{"a":2,"cat":"Willamette Valley (OR)","exp":"Jory is the iron-rich clay weathered from basalt that covers the Dundee Hills. Willakenzie and Bellpine are marine sediments; Laurelwood is windblown loess.","id":"r-6cw44p9k","opts":["Willakenzie","Bellpine","Jory","Laurelwood"],"q":"Which soil series is the red volcanic clay of the Dundee Hills?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Willamette Valley (OR)","exp":"The valley became an AVA in 1983, eighteen years after Eyrie's first Pinot Noir vines and four years before Drouhin arrived.","id":"r-3s9p2l2p","opts":["1978","1988","1991","1983"],"q":"In which year was the Willamette Valley AVA approved?"},{"accept":["Van Duzer Corridor","Van Duzer","the Van Duzer Corridor","Van Duzer gap","Van Duser Corridor"],"ans":"Van Duzer Corridor","cat":"Willamette Valley (OR)","exp":"The Van Duzer Corridor west of Salem lets ocean air into the valley each afternoon, cooling the Eola-Amity Hills; it became an AVA of its own in 2019.","id":"r-gm4a7hnt","q":"Which gap in the Coast Range funnels cool Pacific air across the Eola-Amity Hills?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Willamette Valley (OR)","exp":"Ribbon Ridge, approved in 2005, is a small sedimentary ridge within the Chehalem Mountains; the Laurelwood District of 2020 is the other. The rest stand alone.","id":"r-3fddqpnh","opts":["Dundee Hills","Ribbon Ridge","McMinnville","Yamhill-Carlton"],"q":"Which nested AVA lies entirely inside the Chehalem Mountains AVA?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Willamette Valley (OR)","exp":"Pinot Gris, first planted at Eyrie in the 1960s, is the leading white; Chardonnay has risen since the Dijon clones, and Riesling and Pinot Blanc are small.","id":"r-0tsylvg8","opts":["Pinot Gris","Chardonnay","Pinot Blanc","Riesling"],"q":"Which grape leads the white plantings of the Willamette Valley?"},{"accept":["90 per cent","90","90%","ninety per cent","90 percent","ninety percent"],"ans":"90 per cent","cat":"Willamette Valley (OR)","exp":"Oregon sets the varietal minimum at 90 per cent for Pinot Noir and most grapes, above the federal 75; only a listed group of blended grapes may use 75.","id":"r-swon9p3r","q":"What minimum share of Pinot Noir must a Willamette Valley wine labelled Pinot Noir contain?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Willamette Valley (OR)","exp":"Eyrie's 1975 South Block Reserve was the wine; the showing led Joseph Drouhin to a rematch in 1980 and to Oregon in 1987. The other three are fellow pioneers.","id":"r-jqh0qd69","opts":["The Eyrie Vineyards","Adelsheim Vineyard","Erath","Ponzi Vineyards"],"q":"Which house's 1975 Pinot Noir placed among the best at the Paris tasting of 1979?"},{"accept":["Joseph Drouhin","Maison Joseph Drouhin","Drouhin","Maison Drouhin","Domaine Drouhin"],"ans":"Joseph Drouhin","cat":"Willamette Valley (OR)","exp":"Joseph Drouhin of Beaune bought the land in 1987 and made the first vintage of Domaine Drouhin Oregon in 1988, the first great Burgundian investment in the valley.","id":"r-0udlu6rm","q":"Which Beaune négociant bought land in Oregon's Dundee Hills in 1987?","sa":1}],"sheet":"oregon","slug":"willamette-valley","summary":"The Willamette Valley is Oregon's Pinot Noir country: a long, cool valley south of Portland held between the Coast Range and the Cascades, wet in winter, dry in summer, where Pinot Noir has grown since 1965 and is by far the leading grape. The AVA dates from 1983; by 2022 eleven AVAs were nested inside it, Dundee Hills, Yamhill-Carlton and Eola-Amity Hills the names a server meets most. Soil is the local argument: red volcanic Jory against marine sediment against windblown loess. Pinot Gris leads the whites, Chardonnay has climbed since the Dijon clones arrived, and the valley makes serious traditional-method sparkling wine. Oregon's label law is stricter than federal law, and the exam loves that fact."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Approved 1984; about 4.5 million hectares (eleven million acres), about a quarter of Washington; crosses into Oregon; holds nearly every vineyard in the state.","f":"Columbia Valley AVA: year, size, reach"},{"b":"Yakima Valley, 1983, a year before the Columbia Valley umbrella.","f":"Washington's first AVA and its year"},{"b":"Ice-age floods from glacial Lake Missoula spread sand, silt and gravel over the basalt; windblown loess settled on top. Deep, free-draining, alkaline, phylloxera-poor soils.","f":"The Missoula Floods: what they left"},{"b":"AVA of 2001 near Benton City inside the Yakima Valley; the state's warmest slope, and windy; lime-rich sand; dense Cabernet Sauvignon; Ciel du Cheval and Klipsun; Col Solare.","f":"Red Mountain in one breath"},{"b":"Basalt cobbles on the Walla Walla River's alluvial fan; savoury, meaty Syrah; wholly in Oregon inside the Walla Walla Valley AVA; approved 2015.","f":"The Rocks District: soil, wine, state"},{"b":"Leonetti Cellar 1977, Woodward Canyon 1981, L'Ecole No 41 in 1983.","f":"Walla Walla's three founding houses"},{"b":"Withholding drip water after fruit set to shrink berries and concentrate flavour; possible only because the basin's 150 to 200 millimetres of rain leave the grower in control.","f":"Deficit irrigation: what and why"},{"b":"Royal Slope and Candy Mountain 2020; Goose Gap, White Bluffs and The Burn 2021; Rocky Reach 2022; Beverly 2024; Columbia Hills 2026.","f":"The Columbia Valley's nested AVAs of 2020 and after"},{"b":"An arctic freeze every several years kills buds and trunks; growers plant on slopes for cold-air drainage, keep two trunks, and retrain own-rooted vines from their suckers.","f":"Washington's winter hazard and the grower's answer"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"lead"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"lead"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"lead"},{"name":"Riesling","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Franc","role":"blending"},{"name":"Malbec","role":"blending"},{"name":"Petit Verdot","role":"blending"},{"name":"Grenache / Garnacha","role":"minor"},{"name":"Mourvèdre / Monastrell","role":"minor"},{"name":"Viognier","role":"minor"},{"name":"Gewürztraminer","role":"minor"},{"name":"Blaufränkisch","role":"minor"}],"index":0,"name":"Columbia Valley (WA)","questions":[{"accept":["phylloxera","grape phylloxera","vine phylloxera","Phylloxera vastatrix","Daktulosphaira vitifoliae"],"ans":"phylloxera","cat":"Columbia Valley (WA)","exp":"Phylloxera has never prospered in the basin's deep, sandy, free-draining soils and cold winters, so most vines are own-rooted. It was confirmed in Walla Walla vineyards in 2019, and new plantings are more often grafted.","id":"r-2oi7ie79","q":"Which pest's rarity in the sandy flood soils lets most Columbia Valley vines grow on their own roots?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Columbia Valley (WA)","exp":"The Yakima Valley was first in 1983. Walla Walla followed in 1984, Red Mountain in 2001 and the Horse Heaven Hills in 2005.","id":"r-vn3og1af","opts":["Yakima Valley","Red Mountain","Horse Heaven Hills","Walla Walla Valley"],"q":"Which was Washington's first AVA, approved in 1983?"},{"accept":["the Cascades","Cascades","Cascade Range","Cascade Mountains","the Cascade Range"],"ans":"the Cascades","cat":"Columbia Valley (WA)","exp":"Pacific storms drop their rain on the western slopes of the Cascades; the Columbia basin east of them receives 150 to 200 millimetres (six to eight inches) a year and is irrigated throughout.","id":"r-mf7g774e","q":"Which mountain range's rain shadow makes the Columbia Valley a near desert?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Columbia Valley (WA)","exp":"Red Mountain, approved in 2001 inside the Yakima Valley, is a warm, windy slope of lime-rich sand. Rattlesnake Hills and Snipes Mountain are other Yakima Valley AVAs; Naches Heights is a cool plateau west of Yakima.","id":"r-0o8fzvac","opts":["Rattlesnake Hills","Snipes Mountain","Naches Heights","Red Mountain"],"q":"Which AVA near Benton City is Washington's benchmark for dense, tannic Cabernet Sauvignon?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Columbia Valley (WA)","exp":"The Walla Walla Valley AVA of 1984 lies on both sides of the line and holds The Rocks District on the Oregon side. The Horse Heaven Hills, the Wahluke Slope and the Yakima Valley lie wholly in Washington.","id":"r-mzxt5ldr","opts":["Walla Walla Valley","Wahluke Slope","Yakima Valley","Horse Heaven Hills"],"q":"Which AVA nested in the Columbia Valley crosses the state line into Oregon?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Columbia Valley (WA)","exp":"The Rocks District is the Walla Walla River's alluvial fan, paved with fist-sized basalt cobbles that store heat and drain fast. Loess blankets much of the basin, red volcanic clay is Oregon's Jory, and chalk belongs to Champagne.","id":"r-ofiy43z4","opts":["Windblown loess","Chalk","Basalt cobbles","Red volcanic clay"],"q":"Which soil feature marks the vineyards of The Rocks District of Milton-Freewater?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Columbia Valley (WA)","exp":"Cabernet Sauvignon leads the state's plantings, with Merlot and Syrah behind it among the reds; Chardonnay leads the whites, and Gewürztraminer and Sauvignon Blanc are small.","id":"r-3dute36l","opts":["Chardonnay","Cabernet Sauvignon","Sauvignon Blanc","Gewürztraminer"],"q":"Which grape is the most planted in Washington?"},{"accept":["Missoula Floods","the Missoula Floods","Missoula","Lake Missoula floods","glacial Lake Missoula","the Missoula flood"],"ans":"Missoula Floods","cat":"Columbia Valley (WA)","exp":"Repeated bursts of glacial Lake Missoula in Montana swept the basin at the end of the ice age, leaving the deep sands, silts and gravels that windblown loess later covered.","id":"r-aef04uqq","q":"Which ice-age floods spread the sands and gravels across the Columbia basin?","sa":1}],"sheet":"washington","slug":"columbia-valley","summary":"The Columbia Valley is nearly all of Washington's wine: a vast AVA of 1984 east of the Cascades, in the mountains' rain shadow, where 150 to 200 millimetres (six to eight inches) of rain fall a year and every vine is irrigated. It crosses into Oregon and holds the state's working appellations: the Yakima Valley, Washington's first AVA of 1983, Red Mountain, the Walla Walla Valley, the Horse Heaven Hills and the Wahluke Slope. Cabernet Sauvignon is the most planted grape, Merlot and Syrah follow, and Riesling and Chardonnay lead the whites. The vines stand on windblown loess and flood sands over basalt, most on their own roots, and the long summer days of 46 degrees north ripen them fully."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Finger Lakes 1982; Cayuga Lake 1988; Seneca Lake 2003.","f":"Finger Lakes AVA and its nested AVAs, with years"},{"b":"Seneca, deepest and most wineries; Cayuga, longest and first nested AVA; Keuka, Y-shaped, Frank's lake; Canandaigua, westernmost, the Canandaigua Wine Company.","f":"The four wine lakes and one fact each"},{"b":"Cold water delays bud break past the frosts; warm, unfrozen water in winter keeps the slopes a few degrees above a killing freeze.","f":"The lake effect, spring and winter"},{"b":"Hired at Gold Seal by Charles Fournier in 1953; Vinifera Wine Cellars on Keuka Lake in 1962; vinifera on hardy rootstock.","f":"Konstantin Frank: the dates"},{"b":"Dry, semi-dry, semi-sweet, late harvest and ice wine; the sweetness often declared by the International Riesling Foundation's 2008 scale.","f":"Finger Lakes Riesling styles"},{"b":"Cabernet Franc: late budding, early ripening, cold tolerant. Lemberger (Blaufränkisch) and Pinot Noir follow.","f":"The Finger Lakes red grape"},{"b":"Dundee, on the west shore of Seneca Lake, founded 1979 by a Mosel-born grower; benchmark dry Riesling and a nursery that supplied the east.","f":"Hermann J. Wiemer: where and when"},{"b":"Founded 1880 at the north end of Seneca Lake, Cornell's since 1923; released Cayuga White (1972) and Traminette (1996), and trials the region's grapes.","f":"Cornell's Geneva station: what it did"},{"b":"Grapes frozen naturally on the vine and pressed frozen; wine from fruit frozen after picking may not be called ice wine.","f":"Ice wine: the American rule"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Riesling","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Franc","role":"lead"},{"name":"Gewürztraminer","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"minor"},{"name":"Blaufränkisch","role":"minor"},{"name":"Grüner Veltliner","role":"rising"},{"name":"Cayuga White","role":"minor"},{"name":"Vidal Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Vignoles","role":"minor"},{"name":"Concord","role":"minor"},{"name":"Catawba","role":"minor"}],"index":0,"name":"Finger Lakes (NY)","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Finger Lakes (NY)","exp":"Seneca Lake is about 188 metres (618 feet) deep and almost never freezes, which is why its shores hold the most wineries. Cayuga is the longest, Keuka the Y-shaped one.","id":"r-3y66txn9","opts":["Seneca","Canandaigua","Keuka","Cayuga"],"q":"Which of the Finger Lakes is the deepest?"},{"accept":["Konstantin Frank","Dr. Konstantin Frank","Dr Konstantin Frank","Frank","Dr. Frank","Doctor Frank"],"ans":"Konstantin Frank","cat":"Finger Lakes (NY)","exp":"Konstantin Frank, hired at Gold Seal in 1953, grafted Riesling and Chardonnay onto cold-hardy roots and founded his own winery in 1962; the house still bears his name.","id":"r-8jdvodc6","q":"Who founded Vinifera Wine Cellars on Keuka Lake in 1962?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Finger Lakes (NY)","exp":"Cabernet Franc buds late, ripens early and tolerates cold, so it finishes where Merlot and Cabernet Sauvignon do not; Pinot Noir is a smaller planting.","id":"r-q32shc4q","opts":["Merlot","Pinot Noir","Cabernet Sauvignon","Cabernet Franc"],"q":"Which red vinifera grape leads the Finger Lakes plantings?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Finger Lakes (NY)","exp":"The Finger Lakes AVA came in 1982, six years after the Farm Winery Act of 1976 opened the lakes to small wineries; 1988 and 2003 are the years of its two nested lake AVAs.","id":"r-5myaw1bl","opts":["1982","1976","1988","2003"],"q":"In which year was the Finger Lakes AVA approved?"},{"accept":["Cayuga Lake","Cayuga","Cayuga Lake AVA","Lake Cayuga"],"ans":"Cayuga Lake","cat":"Finger Lakes (NY)","exp":"Cayuga Lake was approved in 1988, fifteen years before Seneca Lake in 2003, and gave its name to the Cornell hybrid Cayuga White.","id":"r-31gi8unh","q":"Which nested AVA inside the Finger Lakes was approved first, in 1988?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Finger Lakes (NY)","exp":"Riesling is the finest wine of the lakes, made dry to sweet with keen acidity and a wet-shale edge. Gewürztraminer is a long-standing speciality beside it, Chardonnay is lean and used for sparkling, and Grüner Veltliner is a recent arrival.","id":"r-0u7y0ywz","opts":["Gewürztraminer","Grüner Veltliner","Riesling","Chardonnay"],"q":"Which white grape is the flagship of the Finger Lakes?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Finger Lakes (NY)","exp":"Keuka Lake forks into a Y; Hammondsport at its foot was the cradle of the region's wine trade and the site of Frank's Vinifera Wine Cellars.","id":"r-d792nxs7","opts":["Cayuga","Keuka","Seneca","Skaneateles"],"q":"Which Finger Lake is shaped like a Y, with Hammondsport at its southern tip?"},{"accept":["Geneva","Geneva, New York","Geneva, NY","Geneva NY"],"ans":"Geneva","cat":"Finger Lakes (NY)","exp":"Cornell's station at Geneva, at the northern end of Seneca Lake, released Cayuga White in 1972 and later Traminette, and remains the region's research centre.","id":"r-muffxjah","q":"In which town is the Cornell experiment station that bred the hybrid Cayuga White?","sa":1}],"sheet":"new-york","slug":"finger-lakes","summary":"The Finger Lakes are eleven long glacial lakes in west-central New York, and the vineyards climb the slopes above four of them: Canandaigua, Keuka, Seneca and Cayuga. The lakes are deep enough to hold summer heat through the winter and spring cold through the frosts, which is the only reason vinifera lives here. Riesling is the flagship, dry to sweet, and Cabernet Franc the red that ripens; Gewürztraminer, Chardonnay, Pinot Noir and Lemberger follow, with hybrids and native grapes beside them. The AVA dates from 1982 and holds Cayuga Lake and Seneca Lake inside it. Konstantin Frank proved vinifera here in the 1950s and opened his Keuka Lake winery in 1962; Hermann J. Wiemer followed on Seneca in 1979."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"The Hamptons, Long Island 1985; North Fork of Long Island 1986; Long Island (island-wide, Nassau and Suffolk) 2001.","f":"Long Island's three AVAs, with years"},{"b":"Long Island Sound north and the Peconic Bay between the forks warm slowly and cool slowly, lengthening the North Fork's season; the Atlantic makes the South Fork cooler and foggier.","f":"The three waters and what each does"},{"b":"Planted in 1973 at Cutchogue on the North Fork, on a former potato farm; the fork's potato fields have been turning to vines ever since.","f":"Long Island's first vinifera vineyard"},{"b":"Merlot first, then Cabernet Franc, Chardonnay, Cabernet Sauvignon and Sauvignon Blanc; Bordeaux varieties in a maritime climate.","f":"Long Island's leading grapes"},{"b":"North: warmer, drier, most of the vines, Merlot and Bordeaux blends. South (the Hamptons): cooler, foggier, rosé, whites and sparkling.","f":"North Fork against South Fork"},{"b":"Sandy loam over glacial outwash, Haven and Riverhead on the North Fork, Bridgehampton on the South; fast draining, low fertility, no bedrock.","f":"Long Island's soils"},{"b":"Humidity bringing mildew and botrytis, autumn hurricanes, birds and deer; sea breezes and open canopies are the defence.","f":"Long Island's hazards"},{"b":"A trademarked American term, used under licence, for a blend of two or more Bordeaux grapes with none above 90 per cent; coined for blends that could not carry a varietal name.","f":"Meritage: what the word means"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Merlot","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Franc","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"blending"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Petit Verdot","role":"blending"},{"name":"Malbec","role":"minor"},{"name":"Riesling","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chenin Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"minor"},{"name":"Albariño","role":"rising"}],"index":1,"name":"Long Island (NY)","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Long Island (NY)","exp":"Merlot leads the island, ripening fully in the long maritime autumn where Cabernet Sauvignon only sometimes does; Pinot Noir is small and Zinfandel is not grown.","id":"r-9gh7krti","opts":["Merlot","Zinfandel","Pinot Noir","Cabernet Sauvignon"],"q":"Which grape is the most planted on Long Island?"},{"accept":["The Hamptons, Long Island","The Hamptons","Hamptons","Hamptons, Long Island","The Hamptons AVA"],"ans":"The Hamptons, Long Island","cat":"Long Island (NY)","exp":"The Hamptons, Long Island, the AVA of the South Fork, came in 1985, a year before the North Fork of Long Island; the island-wide AVA that covers both forks came later.","id":"r-ga2u2ult","q":"Which was the first Long Island AVA to be approved, in 1985?","sa":1},{"accept":["Peconic Bay","the Peconic Bay","Peconic","Great Peconic Bay","the Peconic"],"ans":"Peconic Bay","cat":"Long Island (NY)","exp":"The Peconic Bay separates the forks and, with Long Island Sound to the north, warms and cools slowly enough to give the North Fork the longest season in the state.","id":"r-op2p23ip","q":"Which body of water lies between the North Fork and the South Fork?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Long Island (NY)","exp":"The first vinifera went into a former potato farm at Cutchogue in 1973. Oregon's HillCrest was 1961; Pindar was planted in 1979; the Hamptons AVA came in 1985.","id":"r-u4mguubt","opts":["1961","1985","1973","1979"],"q":"In which year was the first vinifera vineyard planted on Long Island?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Long Island (NY)","exp":"Chardonnay is the island's leading white, from steely unoaked bottlings to barrel-fermented wines and sparkling. Sauvignon Blanc is a smaller planting, Riesling suits only a few cooler sites, and Albariño is a recent arrival.","id":"r-7ar9qxdb","opts":["Riesling","Sauvignon Blanc","Albariño","Chardonnay"],"q":"Which white grape leads Long Island's plantings?"},{"accept":["North Fork","the North Fork","North Fork of Long Island","the North Fork of Long Island","northern fork"],"ans":"North Fork","cat":"Long Island (NY)","exp":"The North Fork, between Long Island Sound and the Peconic Bay, is warmer and drier and holds most of the vines and the Merlot; the Atlantic keeps the South Fork, the Hamptons, cooler and foggier.","id":"r-73kufpur","q":"Which of Long Island's two forks holds most of its vineyards?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Long Island (NY)","exp":"The Long Island AVA of 2001 covers both forks and the whole of Nassau and Suffolk counties; the Hamptons and North Fork AVAs date from 1985 and 1986.","id":"r-whtfeydm","opts":["2001","1986","1985","1995"],"q":"In which year was the island-wide Long Island AVA approved?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Long Island (NY)","exp":"Wölffer Estate, planted from 1988 at Sagaponack, made rosé the Hamptons' summer wine. Bedell, Paumanok and Macari are North Fork estates.","id":"r-4sbaggxd","opts":["Bedell Cellars","Wölffer Estate","Paumanok Vineyards","Macari Vineyards"],"q":"Which South Fork estate at Sagaponack is best known for its pale rosé?"}],"sheet":"new-york","slug":"long-island","summary":"Long Island is New York's maritime wine region: the two forks at the island's eastern end, with Long Island Sound to the north, the Atlantic to the south and the Peconic Bay between them, which give it the longest and mildest growing season in the state. The first vinifera went into a former potato farm at Cutchogue on the North Fork in 1973, and Bordeaux grapes followed: Merlot leads, with Cabernet Franc, Cabernet Sauvignon, Chardonnay and Sauvignon Blanc beside it. The soils are sandy glacial outwash that drains fast. Three AVAs cover it: The Hamptons, Long Island (1985), North Fork of Long Island (1986) and the island-wide Long Island AVA of 2001. The hazards are humidity, fungus and autumn hurricanes."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Argentina's far north: Torrontés and inky Malbec from high desert valleys, Cafayate at about 1,700 metres, the upper Calchaquí above 3,000; hot days, cold nights, fierce ultraviolet light.","f":"Salta / Cafayate in one line"},{"b":"Roses, jasmine, peach and orange peel on the nose; dry, medium bodied and brisk, with a faint bitter finish. Drink young and cold.","f":"Cafayate Torrontés: nose and palate"},{"b":"Up the valley beyond Molinos and Cachi, near Payogasta; Colomé's Altura Máxima at roughly 3,100 metres is among the highest commercial vineyards in the world.","f":"The highest vineyards of the Calchaquí Valleys"},{"b":"Altitude: at 1,700 to over 3,000 metres the nights are cold, the air dry and the ultraviolet strong, so grapes ripen with thick skins and keep their acidity.","f":"Why Salta can grow wine near the tropics"},{"b":"Founded in 1831 at Molinos, among Argentina's oldest working wineries; pre-phylloxera vines and the Altura Máxima vineyard.","f":"Bodega Colomé in one line"},{"b":"The Calchaquí and the Santa María meet near Cafayate to form the Río de las Conchas, which cuts north-east through the red sandstone Quebrada de las Conchas.","f":"The rivers at Cafayate"},{"b":"Tannat: dark, tannic and spicy; after Uruguay, Cafayate is its best-known South American home.","f":"Cafayate's speciality red besides Malbec"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Torrontés","role":"lead"},{"name":"Malbec","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"blending"},{"name":"Tannat","role":"minor"}],"index":0,"name":"Salta / Cafayate","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Salta / Cafayate","exp":"Torrontés Riojano is Cafayate's signature, floral on the nose and dry on the palate. Chardonnay and Sauvignon Blanc are grown but are not the identity, and Pedro Giménez is a bulk white of Mendoza and San Juan.","id":"r-ork8kjiq","opts":["Torrontés","Pedro Giménez","Chardonnay","Sauvignon Blanc"],"q":"Which white grape is the signature of Cafayate?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Salta / Cafayate","exp":"Cafayate's vineyards sit at about 1,650 to 2,000 metres. 3,000 metres and more is reached only further up the valley near Payogasta, and 700 to 1,000 metres is the altitude of Mendoza's lower zones.","id":"r-f21urlpa","opts":["1,000 metres","1,700 metres","3,000 metres","700 metres"],"q":"At roughly what altitude do the vineyards around the town of Cafayate lie?"},{"accept":["Bodega Colomé","Colomé","Colome","Bodega Colome"],"ans":"Bodega Colomé","cat":"Salta / Cafayate","exp":"Colomé, at Molinos in the upper Calchaquí Valleys, dates from 1831 and farms Altura Máxima at roughly 3,100 metres, among the highest commercial vineyards in the world.","id":"r-zz810jk3","q":"Which Salta winery, founded in 1831, farms the Altura Máxima vineyard?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Salta / Cafayate","exp":"Cafayate lies in the south of the Valles Calchaquíes, the Calchaquí Valleys, which run north past Molinos and Cachi and south into Tucumán and Catamarca. Famatina is La Rioja's valley, Calingasta San Juan's and Uco Mendoza's.","id":"r-07xb5z9k","opts":["Valle de Calingasta","Valle de Uco","Valles Calchaquíes","Valle de Famatina"],"q":"In which valley system does the town of Cafayate lie?"},{"accept":["Tannat","Harriague"],"ans":"Tannat","cat":"Salta / Cafayate","exp":"Tannat, the grape of Madiran and Uruguay, where it was once called Harriague, ripens to dark, spicy, firmly tannic wine in Cafayate's sun, and several valley houses bottle it on its own.","id":"r-1d78yybs","q":"Apart from Malbec and Cabernet Sauvignon, which red grape is a speciality of Cafayate?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Salta / Cafayate","exp":"The parral is Argentina's traditional overhead pergola, and around Cafayate its canopy shades Torrontés bunches from sunburn. Guyot is cane pruning on a low wire, gobelet a free-standing bush and the lyre a divided canopy.","id":"r-n7wshqdu","opts":["Gobelet","Lyre","Guyot","Parral"],"q":"Which traditional Argentine training system is a high overhead pergola?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Salta / Cafayate","exp":"Jujuy is Argentina's northernmost wine province, with a few high vineyards in the Quebrada de Humahuaca. Tucumán and Catamarca lie south of Salta and share the Calchaquí system, and La Rioja is further south still.","id":"r-q3nnnz8b","opts":["Jujuy","Catamarca","La Rioja","Tucumán"],"q":"Which province lies north of Salta and holds vineyards in the Quebrada de Humahuaca?"}],"sheet":"argentina","slug":"salta","summary":"Salta is the far north of Argentine wine, and Cafayate, a town at about 1,700 metres in the south of the Calchaquí Valleys, is its centre. Here, not far south of the Tropic of Capricorn, altitude alone makes viticulture possible: hot, brilliant days, cold nights and fierce ultraviolet light. Torrontés Riojano is the signature, a white that smells of roses, jasmine and peach yet finishes dry, often with a faint bitter twist. The reds, Malbec, Cabernet Sauvignon and Tannat, are inky, thick skinned and firm. Up the valley, through Molinos and Cachi to Payogasta, the vineyards climb past 3,000 metres, among the highest anywhere."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"A hot, dry province between Catamarca and San Juan; the Famatina Valley around Chilecito; Torrontés Riojano named for it, with Malbec and Cabernet. Not Spain's Rioja.","f":"La Rioja (Argentina) in one line"},{"b":"Muscat of Alexandria crossed naturally with Criolla Chica, the mission grape Spain calls Listán Prieto. Torrontés Sanjuanino has the same parents.","f":"Torrontés Riojano's two parents"},{"b":"The Sierra de Famatina to the west, above 6,000 metres and snow capped, and the lower Sierra de Velasco to the east.","f":"The Famatina Valley's two walls"},{"b":"The Famatina Valley's main town, about 1,100 metres up, home of the large La Riojana cooperative.","f":"Chilecito in one line"},{"b":"Many small growers, a large cooperative and a leading role in Fairtrade and organic wine, helped by dry air that keeps disease away.","f":"La Rioja's trade story"},{"b":"Argentine province: Torrontés and Malbec, IG and the 85 per cent rule. Spanish DOCa: Tempranillo and the Crianza to Gran Reserva ageing ladder. Same name, nothing else shared.","f":"La Rioja, Argentina, against Rioja, Spain"},{"b":"The overhead parral shades bunches from fierce sun, keeping aroma and acidity and avoiding the coarse, bitter finish of sunburnt fruit.","f":"Why Torrontés is grown on pergolas here"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Torrontés","role":"lead"},{"name":"Muscat","role":"minor"},{"name":"Malbec","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"blending"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"minor"},{"name":"Bonarda","role":"minor"}],"index":1,"name":"La Rioja","questions":[{"a":3,"cat":"La Rioja","exp":"Torrontés Riojano, named for the province, is La Rioja's leading white, perfumed yet dry. Moscatel de Alejandría grows here in small amounts, Pedro Giménez is the bulk white of Mendoza and San Juan, and Chardonnay is Mendoza's leading quality white.","id":"r-66fl2m7y","opts":["Moscatel de Alejandría","Pedro Giménez","Chardonnay","Torrontés"],"q":"Which grape is La Rioja's leading white?"},{"a":1,"cat":"La Rioja","exp":"DNA profiling shows Torrontés Riojano is a natural cross of Muscat of Alexandria and Criolla Chica, the Listán Prieto of the Spanish missions; its orange blossom perfume comes from that Muscat, not from the small-berried Muscat Blanc à Petits Grains.","id":"r-i8qf3hwr","opts":["Muscat Blanc à Petits Grains","Muscat of Alexandria","Gewürztraminer","Malvasia Bianca"],"q":"Which grape is one parent of Torrontés Riojano?"},{"accept":["Famatina Valley","Valle de Famatina","Famatina"],"ans":"Famatina Valley","cat":"La Rioja","exp":"The Famatina Valley runs between the Sierra de Famatina and the Sierra de Velasco, with Chilecito its main town; it grows most of the province's Torrontés Riojano and Malbec.","id":"r-coelgpcf","q":"Which valley around Chilecito is La Rioja's main wine area?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"La Rioja","exp":"La Rioja's main vineyards lie on a hot valley floor at roughly 900 to 1,500 metres, where clear skies bring cool nights. 200 to 400 metres is Patagonia's range, and 2,000 metres and more belongs to Salta's Calchaquí Valleys.","id":"r-gkk4lyxf","opts":["900 to 1,500 metres","200 to 400 metres","Above 3,000 metres","2,000 to 2,500 metres"],"q":"At roughly what altitude do La Rioja's main vineyards lie?"},{"a":2,"cat":"La Rioja","exp":"San Juan lies between La Rioja and Mendoza. Catamarca and Salta are north of La Rioja, and Neuquén is in northern Patagonia, south of Mendoza.","id":"r-0h4o6wb1","opts":["Salta","Neuquén","San Juan","Catamarca"],"q":"Which wine province lies between La Rioja and Mendoza?"},{"a":0,"cat":"La Rioja","exp":"The air is so dry that fungal disease rarely takes hold, so growers need few sprays and organic farming comes easily. La Rioja's summers are hot, its soils alluvial, and it lies far from any sea.","id":"r-bng9hoyz","opts":["Very low humidity","Volcanic soils","Steady sea breezes","Cool summers"],"q":"What lets La Rioja's growers farm organically with ease?"},{"accept":["Criolla Chica","criolla chica"],"ans":"Criolla Chica","cat":"La Rioja","exp":"Criolla Chica is Argentina's name for Listán Prieto, the mission grape that Chile calls País and California Mission; crossed with Muscat of Alexandria it gave Torrontés Riojano.","id":"r-0i1ku2jb","q":"Under what name does Argentina grow the Listán Prieto that is a parent of Torrontés?","sa":1}],"sheet":"argentina","slug":"la-rioja","summary":"La Rioja is an inland province of north west Argentina, between Catamarca and San Juan, and shares nothing with Spain's Rioja beyond a name the colonists carried. Its vineyards lie mostly in the Famatina Valley around Chilecito, a hot, dry trough at roughly 900 to 1,500 metres between the Sierra de Famatina and the Sierra de Velasco. The province gave its name to Torrontés Riojano, the finest of the three Torrontés, and the grape remains its leading white: perfumed, dry and best young. Malbec, Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah and Bonarda fill out the reds, and much of the crop passes through a large cooperative."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Argentina's second wine province, north of Mendoza: hot, dry Tulum Valley Syrah, Bonarda and Malbec, table grapes and concentrate; high, cool Pedernal and Calingasta for finer wine.","f":"San Juan in one line"},{"b":"Syrah: ripe blackberry, plum, pepper and smoke from the Tulum Valley, fresher and finer from Pedernal.","f":"San Juan's signature red grape"},{"b":"The hot, dry Andean foehn of western Argentina is named for the Zonda valley just west of San Juan city.","f":"Where the Zonda gets its name"},{"b":"Pedernal, about 1,350 metres, in the south west; Calingasta, 1,400 metres and more around Barreal, behind the precordillera. Cool nights, stony soils, fresher wines.","f":"San Juan's two high valleys"},{"b":"Around 100 millimetres a year or less, among the driest of Argentine wine regions; the San Juan river and the Ullum reservoir make the valley farmable.","f":"Rain in the Tulum Valley"},{"b":"The second of the three Torrontés, named for San Juan; the same parents as Riojano, Muscat of Alexandria and Criolla Chica, but less perfumed.","f":"Torrontés Sanjuanino in one line"},{"b":"San Juan's best-known historic house, founded in 1870.","f":"Graffigna in one line"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"lead"},{"name":"Bonarda","role":"lead"},{"name":"Malbec","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"blending"},{"name":"Torrontés","role":"minor"},{"name":"Viognier","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"rising"},{"name":"Pedro Giménez","role":"minor"}],"index":2,"name":"San Juan","questions":[{"a":1,"cat":"San Juan","exp":"Syrah made San Juan's reputation from the 1990s, ripe and spicy from the Tulum Valley. Tannat is Cafayate's speciality, Pinot Noir Patagonia's and Cabernet Franc a rising grape of the Uco Valley.","id":"r-ilibizkg","opts":["Cabernet Franc","Syrah","Tannat","Pinot Noir"],"q":"Which red grape is San Juan best known for?"},{"accept":["Tulum Valley","Valle de Tulum","Tulum"],"ans":"Tulum Valley","cat":"San Juan","exp":"The Tulum Valley, a broad basin at 600 to 700 metres irrigated from the San Juan river, carries most of the province's wine grapes, table grapes and raisins.","id":"r-0tm42oac","q":"Which valley around San Juan city grows most of the province's grapes?","sa":1},{"accept":["Calingasta Valley","Valle de Calingasta","Calingasta"],"ans":"Calingasta Valley","cat":"San Juan","exp":"The Calingasta Valley lies at 1,400 metres and more beneath the high Andes, watered by the Los Patos river; Barreal, its southern town, keeps old pergola Criolla beside new plantings of Malbec.","id":"r-i43u4qj2","q":"Which valley of western San Juan, behind the precordillera, holds the town of Barreal?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"San Juan","exp":"Pedernal sits at about 1,350 metres in the south west of the province, far above the Tulum Valley's 600 to 700 metres; 3,000 metres belongs to Salta's upper Calchaquí and 300 metres to Patagonia.","id":"r-l8kz5gcv","opts":["1,350 metres","650 metres","3,000 metres","300 metres"],"q":"At roughly what altitude does San Juan's Pedernal Valley lie?"},{"a":3,"cat":"San Juan","exp":"San Juan is second only to Mendoza in vineyard and volume, and much of its crop becomes table grapes, raisins and grape concentrate as well as wine.","id":"r-rigzsx9o","opts":["First","Fourth","Third","Second"],"q":"By vineyard area, where does San Juan rank among Argentina's wine provinces?"},{"a":0,"cat":"San Juan","exp":"San Juan receives around 100 millimetres a year or less, among the driest of Argentina's wine regions, so snowmelt from the San Juan river, stored behind the Ullum dam, waters the vines. Mendoza city sees about 200.","id":"r-0j46mi38","opts":["100 millimetres","1,200 millimetres","400 millimetres","750 millimetres"],"q":"Roughly how much rain does the city of San Juan receive in a year?"},{"a":2,"cat":"San Juan","exp":"Mendoza lies directly south of San Juan. La Rioja is to the north with Catamarca beyond it, while Neuquén lies further south, past Mendoza, in northern Patagonia.","id":"r-gy0d4l9o","opts":["Neuquén","Catamarca","Mendoza","La Rioja"],"q":"Which wine province lies directly south of San Juan?"}],"sheet":"argentina","slug":"san-juan","summary":"San Juan, north of Mendoza, is second only to Mendoza in Argentine vineyard and volume, and the hottest and driest of the major provinces. Most vines grow in the Tulum Valley around the city of San Juan at about 600 to 700 metres, where heat, sun and around a hundred millimetres of rain a year make big, ripe wines, alongside much of the country's table grapes, raisins and grape concentrate. Syrah is the grape the province made its name with, warm and spicy; Bonarda, Malbec and Cabernet follow. The quality frontier lies higher and to the west, in the Zonda, Ullum, Pedernal and Calingasta valleys, where altitude restores freshness."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Most of Argentina's vineyard, on the eastern foot of the Andes and irrigated by snowmelt: Malbec, plush from Luján de Cuyo, taut and floral from the high Uco Valley.","f":"Mendoza in one line"},{"b":"Luján: first zone, 900 to 1,100 metres, first DOC (1989), classic plush Malbec. Uco: 900 to over 1,500 metres, alluvial fans, cooler, tauter, more floral Malbec and fine Chardonnay.","f":"Luján de Cuyo against the Uco Valley"},{"b":"Tupungato (north: Gualtallary, El Peral), Tunuyán (middle: Los Chacayes, Vista Flores, San Pablo), San Carlos (south: Paraje Altamira, La Consulta, El Cepillo).","f":"The Uco Valley's three departments"},{"b":"The Mendoza: Luján de Cuyo, Maipú and the north. The Tunuyán: the Uco Valley, then the East. The Diamante and Atuel: San Rafael.","f":"Mendoza's rivers and what they water"},{"b":"An IG since 2013, drawn by soil survey, in San Carlos; stones coated with calcium carbonate on the Tunuyán river's fan; chalky, tense Malbec.","f":"Paraje Altamira in one line"},{"b":"Summer hail, met with nets; the Zonda, a hot, dry Andean wind of late winter and spring; and spring frost in the high Uco Valley.","f":"Mendoza's three vineyard hazards"},{"b":"Founded by Moët & Chandon in 1959, its first winery outside France; traditional-method sparkling wine from high Uco Chardonnay and Pinot Noir.","f":"Bodegas Chandon in Mendoza"},{"b":"Luján de Cuyo (1989, built around Malbec) and San Rafael in the south. Both rarely appear on labels; the IG is the working tier.","f":"Mendoza's two DOCs"},{"b":"Château Cheval Blanc with Terrazas de los Andes, first vintage 1999; Malbec and Cabernet Sauvignon, Malbec usually leading.","f":"Cheval des Andes in one line"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Malbec","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"lead"},{"name":"Bonarda","role":"blending"},{"name":"Cabernet Franc","role":"rising"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"minor"},{"name":"Sémillon","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chenin Blanc","role":"minor"}],"index":3,"name":"Mendoza (Uco, Luján de Cuyo)","questions":[{"a":1,"cat":"Mendoza (Uco, Luján de Cuyo)","exp":"Luján de Cuyo was recognised as a DOC in 1989 for its Malbec. Maipú is the lower, warmer neighbour in the first zone, Tupungato an Uco Valley department and General Alvear lies in the south beside San Rafael.","id":"r-emkj88p0","opts":["General Alvear","Luján de Cuyo","Maipú","Tupungato"],"q":"Which Mendoza department holds Argentina's first DOC?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Mendoza (Uco, Luján de Cuyo)","exp":"Paraje Altamira lies in San Carlos, the Uco Valley's southern department, on the Tunuyán river's fan, and became an IG in 2013. Tupungato and Tunuyán are the valley's other departments, and Luján de Cuyo is the first zone south of the city.","id":"r-19nfsusp","opts":["San Carlos","Luján de Cuyo","Tupungato","Tunuyán"],"q":"In which Mendoza department does Paraje Altamira lie?"},{"accept":["Tupungato","tupungato"],"ans":"Tupungato","cat":"Mendoza (Uco, Luján de Cuyo)","exp":"Gualtallary lies in the north of Tupungato, the highest of the Uco Valley's three departments; Catena Zapata planted its Adrianna vineyard there at about 1,450 metres in 1992.","id":"r-s1dw9b4f","q":"Which Uco Valley department holds the district of Gualtallary?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Mendoza (Uco, Luján de Cuyo)","exp":"The Tunuyán crosses the Uco Valley before watering the East. The Mendoza river feeds Luján de Cuyo and Maipú, and the Diamante and Atuel serve San Rafael in the south.","id":"r-x2qftame","opts":["Mendoza","Tunuyán","Diamante","Atuel"],"q":"Which river irrigates the Uco Valley?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Mendoza (Uco, Luján de Cuyo)","exp":"San Rafael, the southern oasis watered by the Diamante and Atuel rivers, holds the second DOC after Luján de Cuyo. Maipú and Tupungato are department origins without DOC status, and Paraje Altamira is an IG.","id":"r-sjdmkisq","opts":["Tupungato","Maipú","San Rafael","Paraje Altamira"],"q":"Which Mendoza zone holds Argentina's second DOC?"},{"accept":["Château Cheval Blanc","Chateau Cheval Blanc","Cheval Blanc"],"ans":"Château Cheval Blanc","cat":"Mendoza (Uco, Luján de Cuyo)","exp":"Cheval des Andes joins the Saint-Émilion estate Château Cheval Blanc with Terrazas de los Andes; the first vintage was 1999, a blend of Malbec and Cabernet Sauvignon.","id":"r-secoei9p","q":"Which Bordeaux estate partners Terrazas de los Andes in Cheval des Andes?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Mendoza (Uco, Luján de Cuyo)","exp":"Black or white hail nets stretched over the rows protect the fruit from summer hailstorms. Wind machines, sprinklers and smudge pots are frost defences, used in the Uco Valley in spring.","id":"r-f5s9mc8r","opts":["Overhead sprinklers","Smudge pots","Wind machines","Hail nets"],"q":"Which measure protects Mendoza's best vineyards from summer hail?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Mendoza (Uco, Luján de Cuyo)","exp":"Maipú, lower and warmer than Luján at 750 to 950 metres and known for old-vine Cabernet Sauvignon as well as Malbec, completes the first zone. Tupungato and San Carlos are Uco Valley departments, and Lavalle lies in the hot north.","id":"r-bccrj0lv","opts":["Maipú","Lavalle","San Carlos","Tupungato"],"q":"Which department joins Luján de Cuyo in Mendoza's Primera Zona?"}],"sheet":"argentina","slug":"mendoza","summary":"Mendoza is Argentine wine: the province holds most of the country's vineyard and nearly all of its famous names. It lies on the eastern foot of the Andes in a rain shadow so complete that every vine is irrigated with snowmelt. Malbec is the grape, and two addresses carry the exam: Luján de Cuyo, the historic first zone just south of Mendoza city at 900 to 1,100 metres, home of the country's first DOC and of plush, classic Malbec; and the Uco Valley, higher at 900 to over 1,500 metres, whose stony fans give tauter, more floral Malbec and fine Chardonnay. Maipú, the East and San Rafael complete the picture."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Argentina's cool south: low, windswept, irrigated valleys of Río Negro and Neuquén at 38 to 39 degrees; Pinot Noir, fresh Malbec and Merlot.","f":"Patagonia in one line"},{"b":"Latitude, the strong westerly wind, cold nights and an early autumn; the vineyards are low, at 200 to 400 metres, so altitude plays no part.","f":"Where Patagonia's cool comes from"},{"b":"The Neuquén and Limay meet at Neuquén city to form the Río Negro; the Alto Valle lies just below the confluence.","f":"The rivers of northern Patagonia"},{"b":"Founded in 2004 at Mainque, Río Negro; biodynamic Pinot Noir from own-rooted vines of 1932 and 1955, bottled as Treinta y Dos and Cincuenta y Cinco.","f":"Bodega Chacra in one line"},{"b":"Neuquén's modern zone, a desert plateau irrigated from the Neuquén river and planted from around 2000; Malbec, Pinot Noir, Merlot.","f":"San Patricio del Chañar in one line"},{"b":"Apples and pears: canals of the early twentieth century made it Argentina's orchard, and vines were planted among the fruit trees.","f":"The Alto Valle's other crop"},{"b":"Chubut: Trevelin and El Hoyo near the Andes and Sarmiento on the steppe, beyond 42 degrees south, among the southernmost vineyards in the world.","f":"Patagonia's southern outposts"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"lead"},{"name":"Malbec","role":"lead"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"minor"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Sémillon","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"}],"index":4,"name":"Patagonia (Río Negro/Neuquén)","questions":[{"a":2,"cat":"Patagonia (Río Negro/Neuquén)","exp":"The Neuquén and the Limay join at Neuquén city, and the Río Negro flows east through the Alto Valle. The Diamante and Atuel water San Rafael, the Mendoza and Tunuyán the rest of Mendoza, and the Colorado and Chubut are separate rivers.","id":"r-62wd93br","opts":["Colorado and Chubut","Mendoza and Tunuyán","Neuquén and Limay","Diamante and Atuel"],"q":"Which two rivers meet to form the Río Negro?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Patagonia (Río Negro/Neuquén)","exp":"Pinot Noir, from old own-rooted Río Negro vines and newer Neuquén plantings, is Patagonia's signature. Syrah is San Juan's grape, Bonarda the everyday red of East Mendoza and Cabernet Sauvignon a blending partner almost everywhere.","id":"r-3zvklx4r","opts":["Bonarda","Syrah","Cabernet Sauvignon","Pinot Noir"],"q":"Which red grape has earned Patagonia most of its critical praise?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Patagonia (Río Negro/Neuquén)","exp":"Patagonia's vineyards are low, at about 200 to 400 metres, and owe their coolness to latitude and wind. The other ranges match Luján de Cuyo, the Uco Valley and Cafayate.","id":"r-ugojvfv5","opts":["200 to 400 metres","900 to 1,100 metres","1,700 to 2,000 metres","1,200 to 1,500 metres"],"q":"At roughly what altitude do the Río Negro and Neuquén vineyards lie?"},{"accept":["Bodega Chacra","Chacra"],"ans":"Bodega Chacra","cat":"Patagonia (Río Negro/Neuquén)","exp":"Bodega Chacra, founded in 2004 in the Río Negro valley, farms abandoned own-rooted Pinot Noir biodynamically and names its wines for the planting years, 1932 and 1955.","id":"r-4txun8jh","q":"Which Mainque winery bottles Pinot Noir from vines planted in 1932 as Treinta y Dos?","sa":1},{"accept":["San Patricio del Chañar","San Patricio del Chanar","San Patricio"],"ans":"San Patricio del Chañar","cat":"Patagonia (Río Negro/Neuquén)","exp":"San Patricio del Chañar, irrigated from the Neuquén river, was turned from desert to vineyard by large modern estates around the turn of the century and is Neuquén's main wine zone.","id":"r-057zbece","q":"Which Neuquén zone, on a plateau north west of the city, was planted from around 2000?","sa":1},{"accept":["Alto Valle del Río Negro","Alto Valle del Rio Negro","Alto Valle","Upper Río Negro Valley","Upper Rio Negro Valley"],"ans":"Alto Valle del Río Negro","cat":"Patagonia (Río Negro/Neuquén)","exp":"The Alto Valle, the upper Río Negro valley around General Roca, Allen and Cipolletti, holds Patagonia's oldest vineyards, planted among apple and pear orchards in the early twentieth century; the Valle Medio lies further downstream.","id":"r-cr32e1ov","q":"Which valley of Río Negro province, around General Roca, is Patagonia's historic wine heart?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Patagonia (Río Negro/Neuquén)","exp":"Latitude and the constant westerly wind cool Patagonia, whose vineyards sit only 200 to 400 metres up. Altitude cools Mendoza and Salta, Pacific fog cools coastal Chile, and the Atlantic is too far east to matter.","id":"r-38h2sivj","opts":["Pacific fog","Latitude and wind","Atlantic sea breezes","High altitude"],"q":"What chiefly keeps the Patagonian vineyards cool?"}],"sheet":"argentina","slug":"patagonia","summary":"Patagonia is Argentina's cool south, though its vineyards lie in the north of that vast region, in the irrigated river valleys of Río Negro and Neuquén around 38 to 39 degrees south. Unlike the Andean north, the vineyards are low, at roughly 200 to 400 metres: the cooling comes from latitude, cold nights, an early autumn and an almost constant westerly wind. Pinot Noir is the grape the region is praised for, pale, spicy and fresh, and Malbec here is red fruited and lifted. The Alto Valle del Río Negro is the historic heart and San Patricio del Chañar in Neuquén the modern one; La Pampa and Chubut add outposts."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Chile's northernmost fine wine valley and pisco country: the Andes corridor east of La Serena, Syrah and Sauvignon Blanc at altitude under cloudless skies, irrigated from the Elqui river.","f":"Elqui in one line"},{"b":"A broad valley around Ovalle, open to the camanchaca fog, with calcium carbonate in the soil: taut Chardonnay, peppery Syrah, saline Sauvignon Blanc.","f":"Limarí in one line"},{"b":"Elqui, Limarí, Choapa.","f":"The Coquimbo region's three valleys, north to south"},{"b":"Granted in 1931, limited to the Atacama and Coquimbo regions; distilled from Muscat family grapes, Pedro Jiménez and Torontel.","f":"Pisco: its denomination of origin"},{"b":"Upper Elqui terraces above 2,000 metres, among Chile's highest vineyards: Syrah and Garnacha trodden by foot in stone lagares.","f":"Alcohuaz: where and why it matters"},{"b":"Calcium carbonate left by an old sea over clay, rare in Chile, plus fog-cooled mornings that keep acidity high.","f":"Why Limarí tastes mineral"},{"b":"Camanchaca: low cloud pushed inland by the cold Humboldt Current; it burns off by late morning and cuts the sun's hours.","f":"The fog's name and its source"},{"b":"Elqui: Vicuña and Paiguano. Limarí: Ovalle, Monte Patria, Punitaqui and Río Hurtado.","f":"The areas of Elqui and Limarí"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"lead"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"rising"},{"name":"Carménère","role":"minor"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"minor"},{"name":"Muscat","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pedro Jiménez","role":"minor"}],"index":0,"name":"Elqui / Limarí","questions":[{"a":3,"cat":"Elqui / Limarí","exp":"Limarí's soils around Ovalle and at coastal Talinay carry calcium carbonate left by an ancient sea, rare in Chile. Elqui is alluvial sand and stone, Maipo is Andean gravel and Colchagua is clay and granite.","id":"r-yj9sy6s5","opts":["Maipo","Elqui","Colchagua","Limarí"],"q":"Which Chilean valley is known for a layer of calcium carbonate beneath its vineyards?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Elqui / Limarí","exp":"Chilean pisco has carried a denomination since 1931 for the Atacama and Coquimbo regions, distilled from Muscat family grapes. Singani is Bolivia's Muscat brandy, cachaça Brazil's cane spirit and grappa Italy's pomace brandy.","id":"r-c7nkjy8a","opts":["Grappa","Pisco","Singani","Cachaça"],"q":"Which spirit holds a denomination of origin shared by Chile's Atacama and Coquimbo regions?"},{"accept":["Camanchaca","the camanchaca","camanchaca fog"],"ans":"Camanchaca","cat":"Elqui / Limarí","exp":"The camanchaca is the low coastal cloud the Humboldt Current pushes inland each morning; it hangs over Limarí until late morning and shortens the sun's hours, which keeps the whites fresh.","id":"r-rvbvtcl8","q":"What is the name of the morning fog that cools Limarí's vineyards?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Elqui / Limarí","exp":"Coquimbo is the northern wine region of Decreto 464 with those three valleys. Atacama lies further north with Copiapó and Huasco, Aconcagua holds Casablanca and San Antonio, and the Valle Central runs from Maipo to Maule.","id":"r-x5m5iq11","opts":["Coquimbo","Atacama","Valle Central","Aconcagua"],"q":"Which viticultural region of Chile's 1994 decree holds Elqui, Limarí and Choapa?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Elqui / Limarí","exp":"Syrah from Elqui's high terraces, peppery and floral, made the valley's reputation after 1998. Cabernet and Carménère are minor plantings there, and Pinot Noir belongs to Limarí's coastal limestone.","id":"r-yhpsane6","opts":["Pinot Noir","Cabernet Sauvignon","Syrah","Carménère"],"q":"Which red grape leads the fine wine of the Elqui valley?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Elqui / Limarí","exp":"Chardonnay from Limarí's calcareous soils and fog-cooled slopes near the coast, as at Talinay, is the valley's signature: lemon, chalk and a saline cut. Riesling and Gewürztraminer are southern specialities of Bío Bío, and Viognier is a minor Chilean planting.","id":"r-v23ypa1v","opts":["Chardonnay","Riesling","Viognier","Gewürztraminer"],"q":"Which white grape is the signature of the Limarí Valley?"},{"accept":["Choapa","Choapa Valley","Valle del Choapa","Valle de Choapa"],"ans":"Choapa","cat":"Elqui / Limarí","exp":"Choapa, around Salamanca and Illapel where Chile is at its narrowest, completes Coquimbo's three subregions and is a small source of stony Syrah. Copiapó and Huasco, further north, belong to the Atacama region.","id":"r-aozdbk3u","q":"Which valley is the third subregion of the Coquimbo region, alongside Elqui and Limarí?","sa":1}],"sheet":"chile","slug":"elqui-limari","summary":"Elqui and Limarí are the two wine valleys of the Coquimbo region, Chile's north, around 30 to 31 degrees south, with the smaller Choapa a third. Rain is scant, so every vine drinks from a river and a reservoir. Elqui is the pisco valley, a narrow cleft climbing from La Serena into the Andes, where Syrah and Sauvignon Blanc now grow at altitude under the clearest skies in the country. Limarí is broader, centred on Ovalle and open to the sea, where the camanchaca fog and limestone, rare in Chile, give Chardonnay, Syrah and Sauvignon Blanc a taut, saline cut. Hold the pair as cool north, sun with fog, never as hot desert."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Aconcagua (the river valley, warm inland, schist coast), Casablanca (fog-cooled basin, whites and Pinot), San Antonio with its Leyda zone (coldest, nearest the sea).","f":"The three valleys of the Aconcagua region"},{"b":"Planted in 1982 for the sea fog. Spring frost in the basin and scarce water from wells; it has no river.","f":"Casablanca: first planting and two hazards"},{"b":"A zone of San Antonio a dozen kilometres from the Pacific, dry until a pipeline brought Maipo river water at the end of the 1990s; Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Noir, Syrah.","f":"Why Leyda exists"},{"b":"Aconcagua's icon: Cabernet led with Carménère from a biodynamic hillside at Ocoa, begun in 1995 with Robert Mondavi, second in the 2004 Berlin Tasting.","f":"Seña in one line"},{"b":"Schist and slate near the coast, rare in Chile; Chardonnay and Pinot Noir of marked tension (the Las Pizarras wines).","f":"Aconcagua Costa's soil"},{"b":"San Antonio area created in 2018, four kilometres from the sea, Costa term, 85 per cent rule; Casa Marín's home.","f":"Lo Abarca: what and when"},{"b":"Pepper, olive, restraint: whole bunch ferments, modest alcohol, large oak; San Antonio, Casablanca and Elqui, unlike inland Colchagua's style.","f":"Coastal Chilean Syrah in three words"},{"b":"Errázuriz founded there in 1870, the warm interior of the Aconcagua valley; the Don Maximiano blend is its flagship.","f":"Panquehue: which house, and since when"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"lead"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"lead"},{"name":"Carménère","role":"blending"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"blending"},{"name":"Riesling","role":"minor"},{"name":"Gewürztraminer","role":"minor"}],"index":1,"name":"Aconcagua / Casablanca","questions":[{"a":2,"cat":"Aconcagua / Casablanca","exp":"Casablanca was planted in 1982 once the sea fog was understood as a cooling asset. 1870 is the founding of Errázuriz at Panquehue, 1994 the Carménère identification and 2004 the Berlin Tasting.","id":"r-mq4ncyrt","opts":["1994","2004","1982","1870"],"q":"In which year was the Casablanca valley first planted for wine?"},{"accept":["San Antonio","San Antonio Valley","Valle de San Antonio","Valle San Antonio"],"ans":"San Antonio","cat":"Aconcagua / Casablanca","exp":"Leyda is a zone of the San Antonio valley, south of Casablanca and nearer the sea. It is irrigated with Maipo river water by pipeline, which is why Maipo is a tempting wrong answer.","id":"r-3otpypx0","q":"Which valley holds the Leyda zone?","sa":1},{"accept":["Robert Mondavi","Mondavi","Robert Mondavi Winery","the Mondavi family"],"ans":"Robert Mondavi","cat":"Aconcagua / Casablanca","exp":"Seña began in 1995 as a joint venture between Errázuriz and Robert Mondavi of Napa; the Chilean family later took sole control. Almaviva, the venture often confused with it, pairs the Mouton Rothschild company with Concha y Toro in the Maipo.","id":"r-f750qhdi","q":"Which Napa producer was the original partner in the Aconcagua blend Seña?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Aconcagua / Casablanca","exp":"Sauvignon Blanc is Casablanca's signature, grassy and citrus at high acidity, with Chardonnay close behind. Gewürztraminer is a Casablanca curiosity, Muscat of Alexandria belongs to the pisco north and old Itata vines, and Pinot Gris is a minor grape in Chile.","id":"r-idm0m8d2","opts":["Gewürztraminer","Sauvignon Blanc","Muscat of Alexandria","Pinot Gris"],"q":"Which white grape is Casablanca best known for?"},{"accept":["Frost","spring frost","frosts","spring frosts"],"ans":"Frost","cat":"Aconcagua / Casablanca","exp":"The basin collects cold air at night in spring, so frost is the chronic threat and wind machines and sprinklers are standard; water scarcity is the valley's other limit but not a spring event.","id":"r-e46ws8l1","q":"What is the chief spring hazard for growers in the Casablanca basin?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Aconcagua / Casablanca","exp":"Aconcagua Costa sits on schist and slate, rare in a country of granite and alluvium; the Las Pizarras wines take their name from the slate. Limestone is Limarí's story, basalt belongs to volcanic soils further south.","id":"r-emwg5txa","opts":["Limestone","Sandstone","Basalt","Schist"],"q":"Which bedrock gives the Aconcagua Costa vineyards their character?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Aconcagua / Casablanca","exp":"Leyda's cold, windy ridges a dozen kilometres from the Pacific make savoury, dark cherried Pinot Noir, with Syrah close behind. Carménère and Cabernet Sauvignon need the warmth of the Central Valley, and Malbec is a supporting red of Colchagua and Maule.","id":"r-ph2co7nd","opts":["Pinot Noir","Malbec","Cabernet Sauvignon","Carménère"],"q":"Which red grape is the Leyda zone best known for?"}],"sheet":"chile","slug":"aconcagua-casablanca","summary":"The Aconcagua viticultural region holds three valleys that must be studied apart. The Aconcagua valley proper follows its river from the Andes north of Santiago to the sea: warm and inland at Panquehue, home of Errázuriz, and at Ocoa, where Seña grows, both for Cabernet blends; cool and schisty at Aconcagua Costa. Casablanca, in the Coastal Range west of Santiago, was planted in 1982 for the sea fog and became Chile's first cool white valley: Sauvignon Blanc, Chardonnay and Pinot Noir. San Antonio and its Leyda zone lie closer to the Pacific again and make the country's sharpest Sauvignon Blanc and most savoury Pinot Noir and Syrah. The Humboldt Current is the teacher here, not the latitude."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Santiago's valley, the cradle of Chilean fine wine: Cabernet Sauvignon on Andean gravels at Puente Alto and Pirque, cooled by mountain night air; cassis, cedar, mint.","f":"Maipo in one line"},{"b":"Don Melchor (Concha y Toro, from 1987), Almaviva (Rothschild and Concha y Toro, from 1996), Viñedo Chadwick (the Errázuriz family, first in the 2004 Berlin Tasting).","f":"The three Puente Alto icons"},{"b":"Trade terms for the Andean bench (Puente Alto, Pirque, Alto Jahuel), the floor (Isla de Maipo, Talagante, Buin) and the western reaches (Melipilla, María Pinto, Alhué).","f":"Alto, Medio and Bajo Maipo: what each names"},{"b":"Santiago, Pirque, Puente Alto, Buin, Isla de Maipo, Talagante, Melipilla, Alhué, María Pinto.","f":"The nine areas of the Maipo"},{"b":"Concha y Toro and Baron Philippe de Rothschild, the Mouton company, from the 1996 vintage; a Cabernet led blend with Carménère, Cabernet Franc, Merlot and Petit Verdot from Puente Alto.","f":"Almaviva: the partners behind it"},{"b":"Stony, poor alluvial fans at 500 to 800 metres and cold air draining off the Andes each night: a twenty degree swing holds acidity and the herbal, minty lift.","f":"Why Alto Maipo Cabernet stays fresh"},{"b":"Carmen 1850, Cousiño Macul 1856, Santa Carolina 1875, Santa Rita 1880, Concha y Toro 1883, Undurraga 1885.","f":"The Santiago houses by date"},{"b":"Chilean beech, the traditional vat wood of the old houses, replaced by stainless steel and French oak from the 1980s onward.","f":"Raulí: the old vat wood"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"lead"},{"name":"Carménère","role":"blending"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"blending"},{"name":"Cabernet Franc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Petit Verdot","role":"minor"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"minor"}],"index":2,"name":"Maipo Valley","questions":[{"a":3,"cat":"Maipo Valley","exp":"All three come from Puente Alto, the stony Alto Maipo bench where the river leaves the Andes. Isla de Maipo and Talagante are the warmer middle valley and Melipilla the western reaches.","id":"r-bz8kyyak","opts":["Talagante","Isla de Maipo","Melipilla","Puente Alto"],"q":"Which Maipo area grows Don Melchor, Almaviva and Viñedo Chadwick?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Maipo Valley","exp":"Almaviva, first made in 1996, pairs Concha y Toro with Baron Philippe de Rothschild, the company behind Mouton Rothschild. Lafite's owners hold Los Vascos in Colchagua, the classic swap, and Margaux and Latour are simply other first growths.","id":"r-yun8ejv2","opts":["Château Lafite Rothschild","Château Mouton Rothschild","Château Margaux","Château Latour"],"q":"Which Bordeaux first growth's owners are Concha y Toro's partners in Almaviva?"},{"accept":["Cabernet Sauvignon","Cabernet","Cabernet-Sauvignon"],"ans":"Cabernet Sauvignon","cat":"Maipo Valley","exp":"Cabernet Sauvignon is the Maipo's identity, grown on the Alto Maipo gravels since the French cuttings of the 1850s; the valley is often called the Bordeaux of South America.","id":"r-g4z60bxi","q":"Which grape is the Maipo valley best known for?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Maipo Valley","exp":"Puente Alto and Pirque sit on rounded stones and gravel laid down by the Maipo where it leaves the Andes, poor and free draining. Limestone marks Limarí, decomposed granite the Coastal Range hills and schist the Aconcagua coast.","id":"r-taaju4jq","opts":["Schist","Limestone","Alluvial gravel","Decomposed granite"],"q":"Which soil underlies the best Cabernet vineyards of Puente Alto and Pirque?"},{"accept":["Isla de Maipo","Isla del Maipo"],"ans":"Isla de Maipo","cat":"Maipo Valley","exp":"De Martino was founded at Isla de Maipo in 1934, and the warm clay and sand of that middle-valley area ripen Carménère well; the house is credited with the first wine labelled Carménère, in 1996.","id":"r-rpa7pnl6","q":"Which Maipo area on the warmer valley floor is best known for Carménère?","sa":1},{"accept":["Concha y Toro","Viña Concha y Toro","Vina Concha y Toro"],"ans":"Concha y Toro","cat":"Maipo Valley","exp":"Concha y Toro, founded at Pirque in 1883, first made Don Melchor from Puente Alto in 1987, often called Chile's first icon Cabernet. Almaviva is its venture with the Mouton company, and Viñedo Chadwick belongs to the Errázuriz family.","id":"r-f4uolmr5","q":"Which house created Don Melchor, the Puente Alto Cabernet first made in 1987?","sa":1},{"accept":["Andes","Maipo Andes","Valle del Maipo Andes"],"ans":"Andes","cat":"Maipo Valley","exp":"Puente Alto and Pirque lie on the Andean bench, so their wine may be labelled Maipo Andes. Entre Cordilleras covers the floor around Isla de Maipo and Talagante, and Costa the side of a valley towards the sea; the terms describe position, not quality.","id":"r-b8a3av62","q":"Which of the 2011 complementary terms may a label add for Puente Alto and Pirque?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Maipo Valley","exp":"Raulí, a Chilean beech, made the great vats of the nineteenth-century houses and shaped the old Maipo style until stainless steel and French barriques replaced it from the 1980s; chestnut, acacia and cherry are European cooperage woods.","id":"r-yy6vfg7c","opts":["Raulí","Cherry","Chestnut","Acacia"],"q":"What was Chile's traditional vat wood before stainless steel and French oak arrived?"}],"sheet":"chile","slug":"maipo-valley","summary":"The Maipo is Santiago's valley and the cradle of Chilean fine wine: the French cuttings of the 1850s were planted here, and the great houses, Cousiño Macul, Santa Rita, Concha y Toro, Carmen and Undurraga, still stand around the capital. Cabernet Sauvignon is the grape, and the address is the Alto Maipo, the Andean foothills at Puente Alto and Pirque, where stony river gravels and cold mountain night air give cassis, cedar and mint with fine tannin. Don Melchor, Almaviva and Viñedo Chadwick all come from Puente Alto. The valley warms and softens westward through Isla de Maipo and Talagante towards the Coastal Range. Study notes call it the Bordeaux of South America."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Cachapoal (Rancagua, Peumo) then Colchagua (San Fernando, Santa Cruz, Apalta). Labels name the zone; Rapel appears mostly on blends.","f":"Rapel's two zones, north to south"},{"b":"A horseshoe of granite hills near Santa Cruz, sheltered and slow ripening, with old own-rooted vines; Clos Apalta, Montes Alpha M, Folly and Neyen, Purple Angel in part. An area since 2018.","f":"Apalta in one line"},{"b":"Cachapoal area near Lake Rapel, warm, sheltered, deep clay: Chile's best Carménère ground and the source of Carmín de Peumo.","f":"Peumo: where and why"},{"b":"It ripens two to three weeks after Merlot and carries green pyrazine until late; Colchagua picks it in late April or May for paprika, plum and chocolate.","f":"Carménère's harvest rule"},{"b":"Apalta, the horseshoe near Santa Cruz, and Los Lingues, at the Andean foot near San Fernando; each needs 85 per cent from the named ground. Published May 2018.","f":"The 2018 Colchagua areas"},{"b":"Clos Apalta and Purple Angel: Carménère led. Montes Alpha M: Cabernet led. Montes Folly: Syrah. Coyam: a biodynamic Colchagua blend.","f":"The Colchagua icons: which grape leads each"},{"b":"San Fernando, Chimbarongo, Nancagua, Santa Cruz, Palmilla, Peralillo, Lolol, Marchigüe; plus Apalta and Los Lingues from 2018.","f":"The areas of Colchagua"},{"b":"Los Vascos at Peralillo in Colchagua, which the Lafite company bought into in 1988. The Mouton company's venture is Almaviva in the Maipo.","f":"Lafite in Chile: which estate, and where"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"lead"},{"name":"Carménère","role":"lead"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"lead"},{"name":"Malbec","role":"blending"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"blending"},{"name":"Petit Verdot","role":"minor"},{"name":"Cabernet Franc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"rising"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"rising"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"}],"index":3,"name":"Rapel / Colchagua","questions":[{"a":3,"cat":"Rapel / Colchagua","exp":"Cachapoal, around Rancagua and Peumo, is Rapel's northern zone and Colchagua, around San Fernando and Santa Cruz, its southern. Teno and Lontué are the two zones of Curicó, the next valley south.","id":"r-zvctwppq","opts":["Lontué","Teno","Colchagua","Cachapoal"],"q":"Which of the Rapel valley's two zones lies to the north?"},{"accept":["Apalta","Valle de Apalta","Apalta Valley"],"ans":"Apalta","cat":"Rapel / Colchagua","exp":"Apalta, near Santa Cruz, is the granite horseshoe whose old vines grow Clos Apalta and Neyen; it became an area of the decree in 2018.","id":"r-h9pc7133","q":"Which Colchagua locality is a horseshoe of hills famous for Carménère?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Rapel / Colchagua","exp":"Peumo, on the lower Cachapoal near Lake Rapel, is the warm clay pocket that ripens Carménère best and the source of Concha y Toro's Carmín de Peumo. Rancagua, Requínoa and Rengo are the zone's other areas, further up the valley.","id":"r-kwqgpffz","opts":["Requínoa","Peumo","Rancagua","Rengo"],"q":"Which Cachapoal area is the most cited site in Chile for Carménère?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Rapel / Colchagua","exp":"Clos Apalta is Carménère led, with Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot in support, from old Apalta vines. Montes Alpha M from the same hill is Cabernet led and Montes Folly is Syrah.","id":"r-442fefoh","opts":["Carménère","Syrah","Cabernet Sauvignon","Merlot"],"q":"Which grape leads the blend of Clos Apalta?"},{"accept":["Château Lafite Rothschild","Chateau Lafite Rothschild","Lafite Rothschild","Château Lafite","Chateau Lafite","Lafite","Domaines Barons de Rothschild"],"ans":"Château Lafite Rothschild","cat":"Rapel / Colchagua","exp":"The Lafite Rothschild company bought into Los Vascos at Peralillo in 1988. The other Rothschild in Chile is the Mouton company, partner in Almaviva in the Maipo, which the exam likes to swap.","id":"r-xwmgymek","q":"Which Bordeaux first growth's owners bought into Los Vascos in Colchagua in 1988?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Rapel / Colchagua","exp":"A decree published on 25 May 2018 created Apalta and Los Lingues, with Lo Abarca and Licantén, each needing 85 per cent from the named ground. 1994 is Decreto 464 itself, 2004 the Berlin Tasting and 2011 the year of the position terms.","id":"r-1pdwkxh6","opts":["1994","2011","2018","2004"],"q":"In which year were Apalta and Los Lingues gazetted as areas of Chile's origin decree?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Rapel / Colchagua","exp":"Carménère ripens two to three weeks after Merlot, which is why it tasted green when the two were picked together before 1994; Colchagua often harvests it in late April or May.","id":"r-yb6jh8h5","opts":["Two to three weeks","A few days","Two to three months","Six to eight weeks"],"q":"How long after Merlot does Carménère usually ripen in Colchagua?"},{"accept":["Lapostolle","Casa Lapostolle","Viña Lapostolle","Vina Lapostolle","Lapostolle Wines"],"ans":"Lapostolle","cat":"Rapel / Colchagua","exp":"Lapostolle, founded in 1994 by the family behind Grand Marnier, makes Clos Apalta from old Carménère led vines on the Apalta hill. Montes Alpha M and Neyen are its neighbours there, and Carmín de Peumo is Concha y Toro's.","id":"r-52b5t6l6","q":"Which house makes the Colchagua icon Clos Apalta?","sa":1}],"sheet":"chile","slug":"colchagua","summary":"Rapel is the legal valley south of the Maipo, and it is really two: Cachapoal to the north and Colchagua to the south, each a zone of the decree, and labels nearly always name the zone. This is Chile's big-red country. Colchagua grows ripe, generous Cabernet Sauvignon, Carménère, Syrah and Malbec, with Apalta, a horseshoe of hills near Santa Cruz, as its grand address: Clos Apalta, Montes Alpha M and Neyen come from there, and Purple Angel draws on it. Cachapoal's claim is Peumo, the warm clay pocket near Lake Rapel that many rank first for Carménère. Both valleys cool at their edges, towards the Andes at Los Lingues and towards the sea at Marchigüe and Paredones."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Curicó: Teno (Rauco, Romeral) and Lontué (Molina, Sagrada Familia). Maule: Claro (Talca), Loncomilla (San Javier, Linares, Parral) and Tutuvén (Cauquenes).","f":"Curicó's zones and Maule's zones"},{"b":"At least 65 per cent Carignan, dry-farmed bush vines of thirty years or more from the Maule secano, two years of ageing before release. A private seal from the start of the 2010s.","f":"The VIGNO rules"},{"b":"Planted after the Chillán earthquake of 1939, with state encouragement, to give colour and acidity to País; the bush vines were left to age in the secano.","f":"Why Maule has old Carignan"},{"b":"A protected term of the decree for País or Cinsault from the listed dry-farmed communes of the Coastal Range; forbidden for any other grape.","f":"Secano Interior: the rule"},{"b":"The Torres family of Penedès brought temperature-controlled stainless steel to Chile and helped open the export era.","f":"What the Torres family began at Curicó in 1979"},{"b":"Young, cloudy País or Cinsault from the south, traditionally sold in bulk; now a serious chilled red on export lists.","f":"Pipeño: what it is"},{"b":"Slate hills near Constitución on the Maule coast, terraced for Pinot Noir; one of Chile's rare slate vineyards.","f":"Empedrado: the slate vineyard"},{"b":"The sieve the secano uses to destem by hand, rubbing the bunches across it; a word that marks a traditional País cellar.","f":"The zaranda: what it does"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"lead"},{"name":"Carménère","role":"lead"},{"name":"Carignan","role":"minor"},{"name":"País","role":"lead"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"lead"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"blending"},{"name":"Malbec","role":"minor"},{"name":"Cinsault","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"rising"}],"index":4,"name":"Curicó / Maule","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Curicó / Maule","exp":"VIGNO is the Maule Carignan association: at least 65 per cent Carignan from dry-farmed bush vines of thirty years or more, aged two years. País and Cinsault are the Secano Interior grapes and Cabernet is the floor's volume red.","id":"r-ghkm4ils","opts":["Carignan","País","Cinsault","Cabernet Sauvignon"],"q":"Which grape must make up at least 65 per cent of a wine under the VIGNO charter?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Curicó / Maule","exp":"The Chillán earthquake of 1939 led the state to encourage Carignan to improve País. Chile also suffered great earthquakes in 1960, 1985 and 2010, which makes each a plausible distractor.","id":"r-mfjjlk42","opts":["1985","1960","2010","1939"],"q":"After which year's earthquake was Carignan planted across the Maule secano?"},{"accept":["País and Cinsault","Pais and Cinsault","Cinsault and País","Cinsault and Pais","País, Cinsault"],"ans":"País and Cinsault","cat":"Curicó / Maule","exp":"Decreto 464 reserves Secano Interior for País or Cinsault from the dry-farmed communes of the Coastal Range; a Carignan or Cabernet from the same hills cannot use the term.","id":"r-7umw197l","q":"Which two grapes alone may carry the Secano Interior denomination?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Curicó / Maule","exp":"Torres arrived at Curicó in 1979 with stainless steel and an export mindset. 1865 is San Pedro's founding at Molina, 1988 is Montes and 1994 is Lapostolle and the Carménère identification.","id":"r-c74jjbjq","opts":["1988","1865","1979","1994"],"q":"In which year did the Torres family begin making wine at Curicó?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Curicó / Maule","exp":"Cauquenes is the only area of the Tutuvén zone, in the Coastal Range secano. Claro is around Talca, Loncomilla around San Javier and Linares, and Lontué is a zone of Curicó.","id":"r-vpwfvoe6","opts":["Tutuvén","Lontué","Claro","Loncomilla"],"q":"Which Maule zone holds the single area of Cauquenes?"},{"accept":["Dry farmed","dry-farmed","dry farming","unirrigated","without irrigation","dryland"],"ans":"Dry farmed","cat":"Curicó / Maule","exp":"Secano means dry farmed: the vines live on winter rain alone with no irrigation, as in the Cauquenes hills of Maule. Secano Interior is the legal denomination built on the word, reserved for País and Cinsault.","id":"r-hrxsau0u","q":"What does the word secano mean on a Chilean label?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Curicó / Maule","exp":"Empedrado near Constitución is an outcrop of slate, rare in Chile, terraced for Pinot Noir. Granite is the usual rock of the Coastal Range secano, limestone belongs to Limarí and basalt to the volcanic foothills.","id":"r-dsryc7us","opts":["Granite","Slate","Limestone","Basalt"],"q":"Which rock underlies the terraced Pinot Noir vineyard at Empedrado?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Curicó / Maule","exp":"Curicó divides into Teno in the north, with Rauco and Romeral, and Lontué in the south, with Molina and Sagrada Familia. Cachapoal and Colchagua make up Rapel, Claro and Loncomilla are two of Maule's three zones, and Leyda and Lo Abarca lie in San Antonio.","id":"r-im23act5","opts":["Claro and Loncomilla","Teno and Lontué","Leyda and Lo Abarca","Cachapoal and Colchagua"],"q":"Which two zones make up the Curicó valley?"}],"sheet":"chile","slug":"curico-maule","summary":"Curicó and Maule are the southern half of the Central Valley, and together they carry more vines than any other part of Chile. Curicó, with its zones of Teno and Lontué, is where the Torres family arrived in 1979 and the modern era began: Cabernet, Sauvignon Blanc and Carménère at scale. Maule, the largest single valley, is two countries: an irrigated floor of Cabernet, Merlot and Carménère along the Claro and Loncomilla rivers, and the dry-farmed secano of the Coastal Range around Cauquenes, where old bush-vine País and Carignan grow without a drop of irrigation. The Carignan planted after the 1939 earthquake is the valley's modern pride under the VIGNO banner, and its País is the country's past made new."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Among Chile's oldest vineyard land, around Chillán and the coastal hills: dry-farmed bush-vine País, Cinsault and Moscatel of a century or more on granitic sand, revived since about 2010.","f":"Itata in one line"},{"b":"The cool, wet, windy valley south of Itata: Riesling and Gewürztraminer at Mulchén, Pinot Noir, Chardonnay and Sauvignon Blanc, with old País at Yumbel.","f":"Bío Bío in one line"},{"b":"Itata, Bío Bío, Malleco. The Austral region (Cautín, Osorno and, since 2025, Chiloé) lies south again.","f":"The three subregions of the Sur, north to south"},{"b":"Itata: Chillán, Quillón, Portezuelo, Coelemu. Bío Bío: Yumbel, Mulchén. Malleco: Traiguén.","f":"The areas of Itata, Bío Bío and Malleco"},{"b":"The south's farmhouse wine: País or Cinsault destemmed on the zaranda, wild ferment in raulí or clay, pressed by hand, drunk young and cloudy, once sold by the pipe.","f":"Pipeño in one line"},{"b":"Around 1,000 millimetres of winter rain fills deep granitic sand, and century-old bush vines reach it with deep roots; no river is needed.","f":"Why Itata can dry farm"},{"b":"Viña Aquitania's Chardonnay from Traiguén in Malleco, the wine that proved the far south of Chile; lean, mineral, barrel fermented.","f":"Sol de Sol: what it is and where it grows"},{"b":"Planted in the 1940s after the 1939 Chillán earthquake, as Carignan was in Maule; now the valley's juiciest old-vine red.","f":"When and why Cinsault came to Itata"}],"grapes":[{"name":"País","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cinsault","role":"lead"},{"name":"Muscat","role":"lead"},{"name":"Riesling","role":"minor"},{"name":"Gewürztraminer","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"rising"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"rising"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Carignan","role":"minor"}],"index":5,"name":"Itata / Bío Bío","questions":[{"accept":["Muscat of Alexandria","Moscatel de Alejandría","Moscatel de Alejandria","Muscat d'Alexandrie","Moscatel","Muscat"],"ans":"Muscat of Alexandria","cat":"Itata / Bío Bío","exp":"Moscatel de Alejandría, Muscat of Alexandria, grows on Itata's old dry-farmed bush vines and makes dry, floral whites, often left on the skins for an orange style. The same grape is a pisco variety in the north.","id":"r-l56i3bes","q":"Which white grape do Itata's old bush vines yield for dry and skin-fermented whites?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Itata / Bío Bío","exp":"Riesling, with Gewürztraminer beside it, is Bío Bío's speciality, dry and lime scented from the cool, wet valley, notably at Mulchén. Viognier is a minor Chilean planting, Albariño is Galicia's grape and Torrontés is Argentina's aromatic white.","id":"r-z4ueqlpt","opts":["Riesling","Albariño","Viognier","Torrontés"],"q":"Which aromatic white grape is Bío Bío's best-known speciality?"},{"accept":["Pipeño","Pipeno","vino pipeño","vino pipeno"],"ans":"Pipeño","cat":"Itata / Bío Bío","exp":"Pipeño is the farmhouse wine of Itata, Bío Bío and Maule: destemmed on the zaranda, wild ferment, pressed by hand and drunk within months, traditionally sold in bulk by the pipe.","id":"r-r48l70es","q":"What is the name of the south's young, cloudy country wine made from País or Cinsault?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Itata / Bío Bío","exp":"The Sur region holds the three southern subregions. The Austral region lies further south with Cautín, Osorno and Chiloé, the Valle Central ends at Maule and Aconcagua is the coastal north of Santiago.","id":"r-ffiq2erk","opts":["Valle Central","Sur","Austral","Aconcagua"],"q":"Which viticultural region of Decreto 464 holds Itata, Bío Bío and Malleco?"},{"accept":["Tinaja","Tinajas","clay tinaja"],"ans":"Tinaja","cat":"Itata / Bío Bío","exp":"The tinaja is the clay jar of the old Chilean south, used for pipeño and revived by De Martino and others; it breathes a little and adds no wood character.","id":"r-axgegmg2","q":"Which vessel of clay do the traditional cellars of the south use for fermentation and storage?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Itata / Bío Bío","exp":"Cinsault came to Itata in the 1940s after the Chillán earthquake of 1939, the same push that planted Carignan in Maule. The 1850s brought French grapes to the Maipo, the 1980s planted Casablanca and the 2010s were the revival.","id":"r-tastmq2i","opts":["2010s","1850s","1980s","1940s"],"q":"In which decade was Cinsault planted across the Itata hills?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Itata / Bío Bío","exp":"Malleco, around Traiguén, is the Sur's southernmost subregion and the home of the Sol de Sol Chardonnay. Itata and Bío Bío lie north of it, and Cautín belongs to the Austral region beyond.","id":"r-cbo5bobn","opts":["Bío Bío","Itata","Malleco","Cautín"],"q":"Which subregion lies furthest south in the Sur region?"}],"sheet":"chile","slug":"itata-bio-bio","summary":"Itata and Bío Bío are the two valleys of Chile's southern region, the Sur, with Malleco a third further south again. Itata, around Chillán and the hills towards the coast, is among the oldest wine country in Chile, planted within a generation of the Spanish settlement of Concepción in 1550, and its hills still carry head-trained País, Cinsault and Moscatel of great age, dry farmed on granitic sand. Bío Bío, along the great river to the south, is cooler, wetter and windier, and grows Riesling, Gewürztraminer, Chardonnay, Pinot Noir and Sauvignon Blanc beside its own old País. The south joins Chile's past and its newest fashion: pipeño, clay tinajas and bush vines beside cool aromatic whites."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Cape Naturaliste in the north and Cape Leeuwin in the south, about 100 kilometres apart; the Indian Ocean on the west and Geographe Bay to the north.","f":"Margaret River: the two capes that bound it"},{"b":"The Leeuwin Current, a warm flow running south along the coast, keeps winters mild and springs frost-free, giving the most even climate in Australia.","f":"Margaret River: the current and what it does"},{"b":"Cabernet Sauvignon and Chardonnay lead; the Semillon Sauvignon Blanc blend, SSB, is the everyday white.","f":"Margaret River: the two lead grapes and the third wine"},{"b":"Gravelly, ironstone-flecked sandy loam over granite and gneiss (the Forest Grove gravels); limestone and sand nearer the coast.","f":"Margaret River: the soil in one line"},{"b":"Margaret River's Chardonnay clone, brought in through the Gingin research station in the 1950s; its hen-and-chicken bunches cut yield and concentrate flavour.","f":"The Gingin clone: what it is and why it matters"},{"b":"Vasse Felix, planted in 1967 in Wilyabrup by a Perth doctor; Moss Wood followed in 1969, Cape Mentelle in 1970, Cullen in 1971, Leeuwin Estate in 1974.","f":"Margaret River: first commercial vineyard and year"},{"b":"Yallingup, Carbunup, Wilyabrup, Treeton, Wallcliffe, Karridale, north to south, drawn in a 1999 study; Wilyabrup is the Cabernet heartland.","f":"Margaret River's six unofficial districts"},{"b":"Cloudy Bay, founded in 1985 by the founder of Cape Mentelle, who had just won two Jimmy Watson Trophies with Margaret River Cabernet.","f":"Which Marlborough house did a Margaret River founder start"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"lead"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"blending"},{"name":"Sémillon","role":"blending"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"blending"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"minor"},{"name":"Malbec","role":"blending"},{"name":"Petit Verdot","role":"blending"},{"name":"Chenin Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Verdelho","role":"minor"}],"index":0,"name":"Margaret River (WA)","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Margaret River (WA)","exp":"The warm Leeuwin Current runs south along the Western Australian coast and keeps Margaret River's winters mild and its springs even; the Humboldt and Benguela currents are cold flows off Chile and South Africa.","id":"r-r0mhtm2y","opts":["Leeuwin Current","Benguela Current","Humboldt Current","East Australian Current"],"q":"Which ocean current moderates the climate of Margaret River?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Margaret River (WA)","exp":"A 1965 study matched the cape's maritime, even climate to Bordeaux, which is why Cabernet Sauvignon was the grape planted first and still leads.","id":"r-rfb1t27w","opts":["Alsace","the Rhône","Burgundy","Bordeaux"],"q":"To which French region is Margaret River's climate most often compared?"},{"accept":["Gingin","Gin Gin","Gingin clone","Mendoza","Mendoza clone"],"ans":"Gingin","cat":"Margaret River (WA)","exp":"The Gingin clone came into Western Australia through the Gingin research station in the 1950s; its uneven berry set lowers yield and concentrates flavour, and some references call it the Mendoza clone.","id":"r-dfqz8mku","q":"Which clone of Chardonnay, with its hen-and-chicken bunches, gives Margaret River its concentrated whites?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Margaret River (WA)","exp":"Wilyabrup, in the north-centre on gravel over granite, holds the founding vineyards and the most structured Cabernet; Karridale in the south is the coolest district and Yallingup the exposed northern tip.","id":"r-jdxamnhv","opts":["Yallingup","Karridale","Wilyabrup","Carbunup"],"q":"Which unofficial district is regarded as the heartland of Margaret River Cabernet Sauvignon?"},{"accept":["Vasse Felix","Vasse-Felix","Vasse Felix Estate","Vasse Felix Winery"],"ans":"Vasse Felix","cat":"Margaret River (WA)","exp":"Vasse Felix was planted in 1967 by a Perth doctor acting on the 1965 climate study; Moss Wood, Cape Mentelle, Cullen and Leeuwin Estate followed within seven years.","id":"r-zejattc2","q":"Which estate planted Margaret River's first commercial vineyard in 1967?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Margaret River (WA)","exp":"The Semillon Sauvignon Blanc blend, labelled SSB or SBS by proportion, is the region's zesty everyday white, mostly unoaked and drunk young.","id":"r-gz8vo9hl","opts":["Pinot Gris and Gewürztraminer","Sauvignon Blanc and Semillon","Chardonnay and Chenin Blanc","Marsanne and Viognier"],"q":"Which two grapes make Margaret River's classic dry white blend?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Margaret River (WA)","exp":"Margaret River is a region of the South West Australia zone with Great Southern, Pemberton, Manjimup, Geographe and Blackwood Valley; Greater Perth holds the Swan District, Perth Hills and Peel, and the other two zones hold almost no vines.","id":"r-obo1bca0","opts":["South West Australia","Greater Perth","West Australian South East Coastal","Central Western Australia"],"q":"In which zone of Western Australia does the Margaret River region lie?"},{"accept":["Leeuwin Estate","Leeuwin","Leeuwin Estate Art Series","Leeuwin Estate Winery"],"ans":"Leeuwin Estate","cat":"Margaret River (WA)","exp":"Leeuwin Estate was planted in 1974 on a Napa vintner's advice; its Art Series Chardonnay, named for the commissioned paintings on its labels, put Australian Chardonnay among the world's best and is the region's most famous white.","id":"r-e61gue7x","q":"Which Margaret River estate makes the Art Series Chardonnay?","sa":1}],"sheet":"australia","slug":"margaret-river","summary":"Margaret River is the far south-western corner of Australia, a cape of granite and gravel between Cape Naturaliste and Cape Leeuwin, with the Indian Ocean to the west and north and the Southern Ocean to the south. The warm Leeuwin Current and constant sea breezes give it Australia's most even climate, which is why a 1965 study compared it to Bordeaux, whose Cabernet Sauvignon ripens here with a reliability Bordeaux can only envy. The first commercial vines went in at Vasse Felix in 1967. A server holds three wines: Cabernet Sauvignon with cedar, bay leaf and fine gravelly tannin; intense, stone-fruited Chardonnay from the Gingin clone; and the zesty unoaked Semillon Sauvignon Blanc blend the trade calls SSB."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Zone: Barossa. Regions: Barossa Valley (the warm floor) and Eden Valley (the cool hills to the east). Subregion: High Eden, the highest southern part of Eden Valley.","f":"Barossa: zone, regions and subregion"},{"b":"Full-bodied, ripe plum, blackberry, dark chocolate and liquorice from old own-rooted bush vines, traditionally in American oak; around 14.5 per cent alcohol.","f":"Barossa Valley Shiraz in one line"},{"b":"Bone-dry Riesling of lime, flowers and slate under screwcap, and perfumed, peppery Shiraz with firmer acid, from vineyards such as Hill of Grace and Mount Edelstone.","f":"Eden Valley: its two wines"},{"b":"Henschke, Eden Valley near Keyneton, Shiraz; oldest vines planted in the 1860s, first bottled as a single vineyard in 1958. The exam's partner to Grange.","f":"Hill of Grace: house, region, grape, dates"},{"b":"Penfolds; Shiraz with sometimes a little Cabernet; new American oak hogsheads for about eighteen months; a blend across South Australian regions, first made in 1951, labelled Grange Hermitage until about 1990.","f":"Grange: house, grape, oak, origin"},{"b":"Old Vine 35 years, Survivor Vine 70, Centenarian Vine 100, Ancestor Vine 125; a voluntary regional charter of 2009.","f":"The Barossa Old Vine Charter tiers"},{"b":"Grenache, Shiraz and Mataro (Mourvèdre), from old bush vines, in the manner of the southern Rhône's Grenache, Syrah and Mourvèdre.","f":"GSM: the three grapes and the model"},{"b":"South Australia has never had phylloxera and quarantines its border, so vines on their own roots planted by the first settlers still crop.","f":"Why 1840s vines survive in the Barossa"},{"b":"Settlement 1842; Jacob's Creek vines 1847; Pewsey Vale 1847; Yalumba 1849; Seppeltsfield 1851; Henschke at Keyneton 1868; Penfolds at Magill, Adelaide, 1844.","f":"Barossa founding dates to know"},{"b":"Penfolds' Cabernet Shiraz, more Cabernet than Shiraz by the name order, matured in the American oak barrels that held the previous vintage's Grange.","f":"Bin 389: what it is and why Baby Grange"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"lead"},{"name":"Riesling","role":"lead"},{"name":"Grenache","role":"blending"},{"name":"Mourvèdre","role":"blending"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"blending"},{"name":"Sémillon","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"},{"name":"Viognier","role":"minor"}],"index":1,"name":"Barossa / Eden Valley (SA)","questions":[{"a":2,"cat":"Barossa / Eden Valley (SA)","exp":"Barossa is the zone and the two valleys are its registered regions; Mount Lofty Ranges holds Clare Valley and the Adelaide Hills, Fleurieu holds McLaren Vale and Limestone Coast holds Coonawarra.","id":"r-v9dqq37g","opts":["Mount Lofty Ranges","Limestone Coast","Barossa","Fleurieu"],"q":"Which zone holds both the Barossa Valley and Eden Valley regions?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Barossa / Eden Valley (SA)","exp":"Hill of Grace is Henschke's single-vineyard Eden Valley Shiraz, from vines planted in the 1860s and first bottled alone in 1958; the house also makes Riesling, but not from that site.","id":"r-xxavz3pu","opts":["Cabernet Sauvignon","Grenache","Riesling","Shiraz"],"q":"To which grape is the Hill of Grace vineyard planted?"},{"accept":["125 years","125","one hundred and twenty-five years","one hundred and twenty five","125 years old"],"ans":"125 years","cat":"Barossa / Eden Valley (SA)","exp":"The 2009 charter sets Old Vine at 35 years, Survivor at 70, Centenarian at 100 and Ancestor at 125; it is a voluntary regional code, not national law.","id":"r-q6ku0vg1","q":"At what age does a vine qualify as an Ancestor Vine under the Barossa Old Vine Charter?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Barossa / Eden Valley (SA)","exp":"Eden Valley's height and wind keep acid in Riesling, giving the dry lime-and-slate style; Semillon is the old Barossa floor white and Viognier a Yalumba speciality.","id":"r-px982glk","opts":["Riesling","Viognier","Chardonnay","Semillon"],"q":"Which white grape is Eden Valley's signature?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Barossa / Eden Valley (SA)","exp":"Yalumba was founded by an English brewer at Angaston in 1849 and remains in the same family; Seppeltsfield dates from 1851, Penfolds from 1844 at Magill and Henschke from 1868.","id":"r-59z3hxgq","opts":["Yalumba","Seppeltsfield","Penfolds","Henschke"],"q":"Which house, founded at Angaston in 1849, is Australia's oldest family-owned winery?"},{"accept":["High Eden","High Eden Valley","high eden"],"ans":"High Eden","cat":"Barossa / Eden Valley (SA)","exp":"High Eden is the highest, coolest southern part of Eden Valley and the only registered subregion in the Barossa zone; the Barossa Grounds on the floor are unofficial.","id":"r-0rlu5jop","q":"Which subregion is registered within Eden Valley?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Barossa / Eden Valley (SA)","exp":"The wine was sold as Grange Hermitage, Hermitage being the old Australian word for Shiraz, until the name was retired under the agreement with Europe; the other three are northern Rhône appellations never borrowed.","id":"r-g2513wti","opts":["Saint-Joseph","Hermitage","Cornas","Côte-Rôtie"],"q":"Which French name did Penfolds Grange carry on its label until about 1990?"},{"accept":["American oak","new American oak","American","American oak hogsheads"],"ans":"American oak","cat":"Barossa / Eden Valley (SA)","exp":"Grange matures for about eighteen months in new American oak hogsheads, the source of its mocha and vanilla; Bin 389 then uses the same barrels, earning the name Baby Grange.","id":"r-347ee68r","q":"In which kind of oak is Penfolds Grange matured?","sa":1}],"sheet":"australia","slug":"barossa-eden","summary":"Barossa is a zone an hour north-east of Adelaide that holds two registered regions: the warm Barossa Valley on the floor, and the higher, cooler Eden Valley in the ranges to its east, with High Eden as a subregion. Silesian Lutherans and English settlers planted it from 1842, South Australia has never had phylloxera, and so Shiraz on its own roots from the 1840s still crops here. The valley floor makes Australia's most famous red, ripe, chocolatey Shiraz, with old bush-vine Grenache and Mataro beside it; Eden Valley makes bone-dry, lime-scented Riesling and finer, more perfumed Shiraz, including Henschke's Hill of Grace. Grange, Penfolds' multi-region Shiraz, is the Barossa's other flagship, and the exam asks the pair every time."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Mount Lofty Ranges zone (with Adelaide Hills and Adelaide Plains), about 130 kilometres north of Adelaide, vineyards at 400 to 550 metres with hot days and cold nights.","f":"Clare Valley: zone, distance and height"},{"b":"Bone dry, lime, slate, high acid, screwcap. Alcohol 11.5 to 13 per cent, no oak, no malolactic; toast and honey after ten years in bottle.","f":"Clare Riesling in five words"},{"b":"Watervale: red loam over limestone, floral lime, rounder, earlier. Polish Hill River: slatey shale over clay, flinty, austere, slower to age. Grosset's Springvale and Polish Hill show the pair.","f":"Watervale against Polish Hill River"},{"b":"The 2000 vintage: thirteen Clare Valley Riesling producers bottled under screwcap together, and the rest of Australia followed within a few years.","f":"The screwcap vintage and who led it"},{"b":"Cabernet Sauvignon with Malbec, a nineteenth-century habit kept by Wendouree and Jim Barry; firm, minty, long-lived.","f":"Clare's old red blend"},{"b":"First vines 1840 at Penwortham; Sevenhill Cellars by the Jesuits 1851, the valley's oldest winery; Wendouree 1895; Taylors 1969; Grosset 1981.","f":"Clare founding dates"},{"b":"Jim Barry Wines' flagship Shiraz, from the Armagh hamlet west of the town of Clare; first vintage 1985.","f":"The Armagh: whose wine, what grape, where"},{"b":"Auburn, Watervale, Penwortham, Sevenhill, Clare (with Armagh to the west); Polish Hill River lies in a side valley to the east. None is a registered subregion.","f":"Clare sub-districts south to north"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Riesling","role":"lead"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"lead"},{"name":"Malbec","role":"blending"},{"name":"Grenache","role":"minor"},{"name":"Sémillon","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"}],"index":2,"name":"Clare Valley (SA)","questions":[{"a":2,"cat":"Clare Valley (SA)","exp":"Clare Valley shares the Mount Lofty Ranges zone with the Adelaide Hills and Adelaide Plains; it lies outside the Barossa zone, a frequent exam trap, while Limestone Coast holds Coonawarra and South West Australia is Margaret River's zone.","id":"r-umt5z5l7","opts":["South West Australia","Barossa","Mount Lofty Ranges","Limestone Coast"],"q":"In which zone does the Clare Valley region lie?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Clare Valley (SA)","exp":"Watervale, in the south-centre, sits on red loam over limestone with shale and gives the rounder, lime-blossom style; Polish Hill River, on hard slatey shale to the east, gives the flintier, more austere, slower-ageing Riesling.","id":"r-g26dk9e0","opts":["Watervale","Sevenhill","Polish Hill River","Auburn"],"q":"Which Clare sub-district gives the softer, more floral lime Riesling on red loam over limestone?"},{"accept":["2000","the 2000 vintage","2000 vintage","two thousand"],"ans":"2000","cat":"Clare Valley (SA)","exp":"Thirteen Clare Valley producers put their 2000 Rieslings under screwcap to end cork taint and random oxidation; within a few years screwcap became the national norm for white wine.","id":"r-7r8vo4ul","q":"For which vintage did Clare's Riesling makers bottle under screwcap together, leading Australia?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Clare Valley (SA)","exp":"Austrian Jesuits founded Sevenhill in 1851 to make sacramental wine, and the winery still does; it is the oldest in the Clare Valley.","id":"r-wz7op0ky","opts":["Franciscans","Jesuits","Cistercians","Benedictines"],"q":"Which religious order founded Sevenhill Cellars in 1851?"},{"accept":["The Armagh","Armagh","the armagh shiraz","Armagh Shiraz"],"ans":"The Armagh","cat":"Clare Valley (SA)","exp":"The Armagh is named for the hamlet west of the town of Clare where it is grown; its first vintage was 1985 and it sits among Australia's most collected Shiraz.","id":"r-c0ak99t9","q":"What is the name of Jim Barry's flagship Clare Valley Shiraz?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Clare Valley (SA)","exp":"Clare Riesling is fermented to dryness with no oak or malolactic conversion, at 11.5 to 13 per cent alcohol; an off-dry or late-harvest version is the exception and says so on the label.","id":"r-jofows4n","opts":["Dry","Off-dry","Medium-sweet","Sweet"],"q":"How is a typical Clare Valley Riesling finished for sweetness?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Clare Valley (SA)","exp":"Cabernet Malbec goes back to the nineteenth century in Clare, Malbec adding plum and softness to firm Cabernet; Cabernet Shiraz is the wider South Australian pairing and Grenache Mataro a Barossa one.","id":"r-ni870vw9","opts":["Merlot and Cabernet Franc","Grenache and Mataro","Cabernet Sauvignon and Shiraz","Cabernet Sauvignon and Malbec"],"q":"Which red blend is a Clare Valley tradition at houses such as Wendouree and Jim Barry?"}],"sheet":"australia","slug":"clare-valley","summary":"Clare Valley is a string of narrow valleys about 130 kilometres north of Adelaide, in the Mount Lofty Ranges zone, not the Barossa, standing 400 to 500 metres up where hot days give way to cold nights. It is Australia's Riesling capital: bone dry, lime and slate, high in acid, bottled under screwcap since the Riesling makers switched together for the 2000 vintage. Watervale gives the floral, limey style and Polish Hill River the flintier, slower one. The reds are serious too: firm, minty Shiraz and the old Clare pairing of Cabernet Sauvignon with Malbec. The Jesuits founded Sevenhill in 1851, the oldest winery in the valley, and the vines have never seen phylloxera."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Fleurieu zone, south of Adelaide between the Mount Lofty Ranges and Gulf St Vincent; warm Mediterranean with a daily sea breeze, almost no frost, 550 to 650 millimetres of winter rain.","f":"McLaren Vale: zone, position and climate"},{"b":"Full-bodied, blackberry, plum and dark chocolate with a savoury, saline edge and soft tannin; French oak now, about 14 to 14.5 per cent.","f":"McLaren Vale Shiraz in one line"},{"b":"Grenache, Shiraz, Mataro (Mourvèdre), named in descending order of proportion; the southern Rhône model from old South Australian bush vines.","f":"GSM: the grapes and the order"},{"b":"Deep pale sand over clay in the elevated north of McLaren Vale; the heartland of old bush-vine Grenache, some planted in the 1940s, and fine perfumed Shiraz.","f":"Blewitt Springs: soil and grape"},{"b":"First vines at Reynella 1838; Hardy founded 1853 and bought Tintara 1876; Kay Brothers 1890; Wirra Wirra 1894; d'Arenberg 1912. Never any phylloxera.","f":"McLaren Vale founding dates"},{"b":"McLaren Vale, Langhorne Creek, Currency Creek, Southern Fleurieu and Kangaroo Island.","f":"The Fleurieu zone's five regions"},{"b":"d'Arenberg's flagship Shiraz, named for Eutypa dieback, the trunk disease that kills one arm of an old vine and concentrates the crop on the other.","f":"The Dead Arm: house, grape, name"},{"b":"Fiano and Vermentino for whites, Tempranillo, Nero d'Avola and Sangiovese for reds; Coriole planted the first commercial Sangiovese in Australia in 1985.","f":"McLaren Vale's Mediterranean varieties"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"lead"},{"name":"Grenache","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"blending"},{"name":"Mourvèdre","role":"blending"},{"name":"Tempranillo","role":"rising"},{"name":"Fiano","role":"rising"},{"name":"Vermentino","role":"rising"},{"name":"Nero d’Avola","role":"rising"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"},{"name":"Sangiovese","role":"minor"}],"index":3,"name":"McLaren Vale (SA)","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"McLaren Vale (SA)","exp":"McLaren Vale is a region of the Fleurieu zone on the peninsula south of Adelaide, with Langhorne Creek, Currency Creek, Southern Fleurieu and Kangaroo Island; the Mount Lofty Ranges zone holds Clare and the Adelaide Hills.","id":"r-thy3qvwn","opts":["Fleurieu","Limestone Coast","Mount Lofty Ranges","Barossa"],"q":"In which zone does the McLaren Vale region lie?"},{"a":3,"cat":"McLaren Vale (SA)","exp":"GSM is Grenache, Shiraz and Mataro, the Australian name for Mourvèdre, in descending order of proportion; it follows the southern Rhône's Grenache, Syrah and Mourvèdre.","id":"r-7y2zuxs6","opts":["Grenache, Sangiovese and Merlot","Gamay, Syrah and Malbec","Graciano, Shiraz and Mencía","Grenache, Shiraz and Mataro"],"q":"Which three grapes make up a GSM blend?"},{"accept":["Gulf St Vincent","Gulf of St Vincent","St Vincent Gulf","Saint Vincent Gulf","Gulf Saint Vincent","st vincent"],"ans":"Gulf St Vincent","cat":"McLaren Vale (SA)","exp":"The gulf west of Adelaide lies at the region's feet; its afternoon breeze drops the summer temperature, keeps acid in the fruit and gives the Shiraz its saline note.","id":"r-q2fxgryy","q":"Which body of water sends the afternoon breeze that cools McLaren Vale?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"McLaren Vale (SA)","exp":"Blewitt Springs' pale sand over clay in the elevated north grows the region's finest Grenache, some vines from the 1940s; Clarendon is the cool hill district and the Sellicks Foothills the warm south.","id":"r-pagc7meu","opts":["Clarendon","Blewitt Springs","Sellicks Foothills","McLaren Flat"],"q":"Which McLaren Vale district, on deep sands, is the heartland of old bush-vine Grenache?"},{"a":0,"cat":"McLaren Vale (SA)","exp":"Eutypa dieback is a trunk disease that kills one arm of an old vine and leaves the other to carry a small, concentrated crop; the two mildews are leaf and bunch diseases, and phylloxera has never reached South Australia.","id":"r-3o2wat4a","opts":["Eutypa dieback","Powdery mildew","Downy mildew","Phylloxera"],"q":"After which vine disorder is d'Arenberg's flagship Shiraz, The Dead Arm, named?"},{"accept":["Reynella","Old Reynella","Reynella Estate","Chateau Reynella"],"ans":"Reynella","cat":"McLaren Vale (SA)","exp":"Vines went in at Reynella, the region's northern gateway near the Onkaparinga River, in 1838, which makes McLaren Vale among Australia's oldest regions; Thomas Hardy's purchase of Tintara followed in 1876, and phylloxera has never arrived.","id":"r-8ekk2uan","q":"Where were McLaren Vale's first vines planted in 1838?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"McLaren Vale (SA)","exp":"Langhorne Creek near Lake Alexandrina diverted the Bremer's winter floods across its flat vineyards, growing soft, generous Cabernet and Shiraz that fed many South Australian blends.","id":"r-uoj27nym","opts":["Kangaroo Island","Southern Fleurieu","Langhorne Creek","Currency Creek"],"q":"Which Fleurieu region east of the ranges was traditionally irrigated by the winter flooding of the Bremer River?"},{"a":1,"cat":"McLaren Vale (SA)","exp":"Hardy bought Tintara in 1876 and made it the McLaren Vale home of the company he had founded in 1853; Reynella is where the first vines of 1838 were planted and Amery is the Kay Brothers' 1890 estate.","id":"r-scrg7y35","opts":["Reynella","Tintara","Tatachilla","Amery"],"q":"Which historic McLaren Vale winery did Thomas Hardy buy in 1876?"}],"sheet":"australia","slug":"mclaren-vale","summary":"McLaren Vale lies half an hour south of Adelaide in the Fleurieu zone, a bowl of vineyards between the Mount Lofty Ranges and the beaches of Gulf St Vincent, warm in the Mediterranean way but cooled every afternoon by the sea breeze. Vines were planted at Reynella in 1838 and Thomas Hardy bought Tintara in 1876, so it is among the oldest regions in the country and has never seen phylloxera. Shiraz leads, dark, savoury and chocolatey with a saline tang, and old bush-vine Grenache is the second claim, alone or in the GSM blend with Mataro. The geology is famously diverse, more than forty mapped units, and Fiano, Vermentino, Tempranillo and Nero d’Avola are the modern experiment."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Limestone Coast zone, far south-east South Australia near the Victorian border, some 60 to 70 kilometres from the Southern Ocean; cool maritime, cloudy afternoons, late harvest into April and May, spring frost risk.","f":"Coonawarra: zone, position and climate"},{"b":"Friable red-brown loam, 30 to 100 centimetres deep, coloured by iron oxide, over soft porous limestone over a water table a metre or two down; free draining yet never dry.","f":"Terra rossa: define it"},{"b":"A flat ridge of old dune limestone about 20 kilometres long and two kilometres wide, running north from Penola through the hamlet of Coonawarra; the cigar on the map.","f":"The Coonawarra strip: shape and size"},{"b":"Blackcurrant, mulberry, mint and eucalypt with cedar and tobacco in age; firm acid, fine dusty tannin, moderate alcohol, twenty years of life. Australia's most structured Cabernet.","f":"Coonawarra Cabernet in one line"},{"b":"John Riddoch's Penola Fruit Colony 1890 and his triple-gabled winery of the 1890s; the Wynn family bought it in 1951; Black Label Cabernet since 1954; John Riddoch since 1982.","f":"Coonawarra dates and names"},{"b":"A boundary dispute through the 1990s, settled in the early 2000s, brought growers on the flanking black rendzina soil inside the line; the GI promises origin, not terra rossa.","f":"Why the Coonawarra GI is wider than its soil"},{"b":"Coonawarra, Wrattonbully (just north, terra rossa too), Padthaway (further north, the big companies), Mount Benson and Robe (on the coast), Mount Gambier (south, coolest).","f":"The Limestone Coast's six regions"},{"b":"Bin 128 is Penfolds' Coonawarra Shiraz, one of the few single-region wines in the Bin series; Bin 707 is its flagship Cabernet, a multi-region blend in which Coonawarra fruit has long been central.","f":"Penfolds Bin 128 and Bin 707"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"lead"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"lead"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"blending"},{"name":"Cabernet Franc","role":"blending"},{"name":"Petit Verdot","role":"blending"},{"name":"Malbec","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"},{"name":"Riesling","role":"minor"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"minor"}],"index":4,"name":"Coonawarra (SA)","questions":[{"a":1,"cat":"Coonawarra (SA)","exp":"Coonawarra is one of six regions in the Limestone Coast zone of south-eastern South Australia, with Wrattonbully, Padthaway, Mount Benson, Robe and Mount Gambier.","id":"r-zywcct78","opts":["Mount Lofty Ranges","Limestone Coast","Fleurieu","Barossa"],"q":"In which zone does the Coonawarra region lie?"},{"accept":["terra rossa","terra rosa","terrarossa","Terra Rossa soil"],"ans":"terra rossa","cat":"Coonawarra (SA)","exp":"Terra rossa, red earth, is a friable iron-rich loam over soft porous limestone with a shallow water table beneath; it covers a strip about 20 kilometres long and two wide.","id":"r-bw8j6imf","q":"What is the name of the red loam over limestone on which Coonawarra's famous Cabernet grows?","sa":1},{"accept":["Cabernet Sauvignon","Cabernet","Cab Sav","Cabernet-Sauvignon"],"ans":"Cabernet Sauvignon","cat":"Coonawarra (SA)","exp":"Cabernet Sauvignon overtook Shiraz in the 1960s and 1970s and now defines the region; Cabernet Franc is only a blending seasoning and Pinot Noir belongs to cooler Mount Gambier.","id":"r-j9hkw0fz","q":"Which grape variety is the most planted in Coonawarra?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Coonawarra (SA)","exp":"The Wynn family bought Riddoch's winery in 1951 and put its three gables on the label of Wynns Coonawarra Estate; Black Label Cabernet has been made there since 1954.","id":"r-5wlgifku","opts":["Wynns","Penfolds","Yalumba","Lindeman's"],"q":"Which house's label shows the triple-gabled winery John Riddoch built in the 1890s?"},{"accept":["Penola","penola","Penola township"],"ans":"Penola","cat":"Coonawarra (SA)","exp":"Riddoch founded the Penola Fruit Colony in 1890 and the strip runs north from the town; the hamlet of Coonawarra, further up the ridge, later gave the region its name.","id":"r-46bqjqzh","q":"Which town at the southern end of the terra rossa strip gave John Riddoch's 1890 fruit colony its name?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Coonawarra (SA)","exp":"The strip is about 20 kilometres long and two kilometres wide, the cigar shape on every map; about 60 kilometres is roughly the distance to the Southern Ocean.","id":"r-uggnh66z","opts":["2 kilometres","200 kilometres","60 kilometres","20 kilometres"],"q":"About how long is the terra rossa strip of Coonawarra?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Coonawarra (SA)","exp":"The Southern Ocean lies some 60 to 70 kilometres west and its cold offshore upwelling sends cloud and breeze over the flat land through summer; the two gulfs cool Adelaide's regions and Bass Strait cools Tasmania and Victoria.","id":"r-pm5afho0","opts":["Gulf St Vincent","Bass Strait","Southern Ocean","Spencer Gulf"],"q":"Which body of water moderates Coonawarra's climate?"},{"accept":["Wrattonbully","wrattonbully","Wrattonbully region"],"ans":"Wrattonbully","cat":"Coonawarra (SA)","exp":"Wrattonbully adjoins Coonawarra's northern end with terra rossa of its own and vineyards mostly planted in the 1990s; Padthaway lies further north and is warmer.","id":"r-jjba1r33","q":"Which Limestone Coast region lies immediately north of Coonawarra on the same limestone ridge?","sa":1}],"sheet":"australia","slug":"coonawarra","summary":"Coonawarra is a flat, cool strip in the south-eastern corner of South Australia, near the Victorian border and some 60 to 70 kilometres from the Southern Ocean, in the Limestone Coast zone. Its fame rests on one soil, terra rossa, a red-brown loam over soft porous limestone over a shallow water table, along a low ancient dune ridge about 20 kilometres long and two wide, running north from Penola. On it Cabernet Sauvignon ripens slowly into blackcurrant, mint and cedar with firm acid and fine tannin, the most structured Cabernet in Australia and the exam’s answer to Bordeaux. John Riddoch planted the colony in 1890, Wynns gave it its label in 1951, and the vines are on their own roots."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Picked early at ten to eleven and a half per cent, fermented cool in steel, no oak, no malolactic; bottled young and aged in bottle into toast, honey and lanolin.","f":"Hunter Semillon: picking alcohol and oak regime"},{"b":"Zone Hunter Valley, region Hunter, subregions Broke Fordwich (1997), Pokolbin (2010) and Upper Hunter Valley (2010).","f":"The Hunter's GI ladder, top to bottom"},{"b":"Humid subtropical; summer cloud and sea breezes temper the heat, and an early harvest beats most of the late-summer rain.","f":"Hunter climate in one phrase, and why it works"},{"b":"Medium-bodied, earthy, leathery, softer tannin and lower alcohol; ages for decades. Barossa is fuller, riper and chocolatey.","f":"Hunter Shiraz style against Barossa"},{"b":"The house's Chardonnay, first made in 1971, widely credited as the first commercial varietal Chardonnay in Australia. Vat 1 is the Semillon.","f":"Tyrrell's Vat 47: what it is and why it matters"},{"b":"Hunter River Riesling meant Semillon; Hunter Burgundy and Hermitage meant Shiraz. None is allowed today.","f":"Old label names for Hunter grapes"},{"b":"Red volcanic duplex soils on the slopes for Shiraz; sandy alluvial creek flats for Semillon; the Brokenback Range frames the west.","f":"Soils of the Lower Hunter: which grape where"},{"b":"Founded 1963 by Max Lake at Pokolbin, the first boutique winery of modern Australia; an elegant Cabernet blend and a Chardonnay.","f":"Lake's Folly: the fact to keep"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Sémillon","role":"lead"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"},{"name":"Verdelho","role":"minor"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"minor"}],"index":5,"name":"Hunter Valley (NSW)","questions":[{"a":2,"cat":"Hunter Valley (NSW)","exp":"Hunter Semillon is picked early, bottled without oak and ages into toast and honey. Chardonnay and Verdelho are grown here too; Riesling was only an old misnomer for Semillon.","id":"r-ifbimakv","opts":["Verdelho","Riesling","Sémillon","Chardonnay"],"q":"Which white grape makes the Hunter Valley's most celebrated long-lived dry wine?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Hunter Valley (NSW)","exp":"The Hunter is humid and subtropical, with cloud, sea breeze and an early harvest keeping the wines moderate; rain at vintage is the hazard. Barossa is Mediterranean, Margaret River maritime.","id":"r-vogm0por","opts":["Humid subtropical","Maritime","Mediterranean","Continental"],"q":"What climate classification describes the Hunter Valley?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Hunter Valley (NSW)","exp":"Broke Fordwich was registered in 1997; Pokolbin and Upper Hunter Valley followed in 2010, and the three are the Hunter's only registered subregions. Lovedale and Mount View are Lower Hunter localities without registration; Muswellbrook is an Upper Hunter town.","id":"r-jt7jan5l","opts":["Muswellbrook","Broke Fordwich","Mount View","Lovedale"],"q":"Which Hunter locality is one of the region's three registered subregions?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Hunter Valley (NSW)","exp":"Tyrrell's Vat 47 from 1971 is the wine the trade credits as the first commercial varietal Chardonnay in Australia. Lindeman's is the older Semillon house, Rosemount the Upper Hunter Chardonnay name, Brokenwood the Graveyard Shiraz house.","id":"r-4jfblr7f","opts":["Rosemount","Brokenwood","Lindeman's","Tyrrell's"],"q":"Which house's Vat 47 of 1971 is credited as Australia's first commercial varietal Chardonnay?"},{"accept":["10 to 11.5 per cent","10 to 11.5 percent","10 to 11.5%","ten to eleven and a half percent","10-11.5%","around 11 percent","10.5%","11%"],"ans":"10 to 11.5 per cent","cat":"Hunter Valley (NSW)","exp":"The grapes come in early, before the rain and with high acid, giving wines of ten to eleven and a half per cent that are bone dry and need no oak to become toasty with age.","id":"r-dw5ktbx9","q":"In roughly what range of alcohol is Hunter Semillon picked and bottled?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Hunter Valley (NSW)","exp":"The Hunter's wettest months fall in late summer, right over picking, so Semillon comes in from January and a dry year is a great one. Humidity and rain, not frost or drought, are the burden of this subtropical climate.","id":"r-e8n6oygu","opts":["Summer rain","Drought","Spring frost","Hailstorms"],"q":"Which weather hazard most often spoils a Hunter Valley vintage?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Hunter Valley (NSW)","exp":"Lovedale's low sandy flats north-east of Pokolbin give pure, long-lived Semillon. Mount View is a Shiraz slope; Denman and Muswellbrook are Upper Hunter towns.","id":"r-ffezphim","opts":["Denman","Lovedale","Mount View","Muswellbrook"],"q":"Which Hunter locality is known above all for long-lived Semillon on sandy soils?"},{"accept":["Hunter River Riesling","Hunter Riesling","Riesling"],"ans":"Hunter River Riesling","cat":"Hunter Valley (NSW)","exp":"Nineteenth and twentieth century labels called Semillon Hunter River Riesling; the grape was never Riesling, and truthful varietal labelling ended the name. Hunter Burgundy and Hermitage were the old names for Shiraz.","id":"r-l7jr5gwl","q":"What was the historical Hunter name for Semillon that appeared on old labels?","sa":1}],"sheet":"australia","slug":"hunter-valley","summary":"The Hunter is Australia's oldest surviving wine region and its least likely: humid, subtropical, two hours north of Sydney, with rain that arrives at harvest. It works because summer cloud and sea breezes take the edge off the heat and because the growers pick early. Its two signatures are Semillon, picked lean at ten to eleven and a half per cent alcohol, unoaked, and transformed by bottle age into toast and honey, and a medium-bodied, earthy Shiraz that ages for decades. The Hunter Valley zone holds one region, Hunter, with three registered subregions: Broke Fordwich, Pokolbin and Upper Hunter Valley. Say Semillon the Australian way, and never promise that a young one will taste of much."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Port Phillip zone, with Mornington Peninsula, Geelong, Macedon Ranges and Sunbury. No registered subregions; Upper and Lower Yarra are descriptions.","f":"Yarra Valley: zone and neighbours"},{"b":"Planted 1838 at Yering; last old vintage 1921; revival with Yarra Yering 1969 and Mount Mary 1971; Domaine Chandon 1986.","f":"Yarra Valley history in four dates"},{"b":"Upper: higher, cooler, later, red volcanic soils, Pinot, Chardonnay, sparkling. Lower: valley floor, warmer, grey loams, the historic estates, Cabernet and Shiraz as well.","f":"Upper Yarra against Lower Yarra"},{"b":"Pinot Noir (perfumed, silky), Chardonnay (taut, flinty, the modern lean style), traditional-method sparkling; Cabernet blends and peppery Shiraz at the old estates.","f":"The Yarra's lead grapes and styles"},{"b":"A Cabernet-led Bordeaux blend of five grapes from Lilydale, made since the 1970s; a house name, not a legal term.","f":"Mount Mary Quintet: what is it"},{"b":"No. 1 is the Cabernet blend; No. 2 is Shiraz-based with small shares of Rhône partners. The estate was founded in 1969 at Gruyere.","f":"Yarra Yering's Dry Red No. 1 and No. 2"},{"b":"Found in the valley in 2006; growers replant on resistant rootstock and Victoria runs infested and exclusion zones. South Australia remains free.","f":"Phylloxera and the Yarra"},{"b":"Moët & Chandon built at Green Point, Coldstream, in 1986: proof the cool Yarra could grow traditional-method sparkling base, and a tourist magnet for the region.","f":"What Domaine Chandon's arrival meant"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"minor"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"minor"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"blending"}],"index":6,"name":"Yarra Valley (VIC)","questions":[{"a":3,"cat":"Yarra Valley (VIC)","exp":"The Yarra is in the Port Phillip zone with Mornington Peninsula, Geelong, Macedon Ranges and Sunbury. North East Victoria holds Rutherglen; Central Victoria holds Heathcote and Bendigo; Western Victoria holds the Grampians.","id":"r-h7gme3ah","opts":["Central Victoria","Western Victoria","North East Victoria","Port Phillip"],"q":"In which zone does the Yarra Valley region sit?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Yarra Valley (VIC)","exp":"Pinot Noir leads with Chardonnay; Cabernet was the historic grape and survives at the old estates, Shiraz is a cool, peppery minority and Merlot a blending partner.","id":"r-iaoqpsr2","opts":["Pinot Noir","Shiraz","Merlot","Cabernet Sauvignon"],"q":"Which red grape is the Yarra Valley's flagship today?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Yarra Valley (VIC)","exp":"Yering was planted in 1838, the start of Victorian viticulture; Yeringberg followed in 1863, the old industry's last vintage was 1921, and Yarra Yering's founding in 1969 marks the revival.","id":"r-dh0q5g16","opts":["1863","1921","1838","1969"],"q":"In which year were the first Yarra Valley vines planted at Yering?"},{"accept":["Mount Mary","Mt Mary","Mount Mary Vineyard","Mt Mary Vineyard"],"ans":"Mount Mary","cat":"Yarra Valley (VIC)","exp":"Mount Mary at Lilydale, founded 1971, makes Quintet from the five Bordeaux grapes; Yarra Yering's equivalent is Dry Red No. 1.","id":"r-i5zkf6yc","q":"Which Yarra Valley house makes the Cabernet-led blend called Quintet?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Yarra Valley (VIC)","exp":"The Upper Yarra sits on young, fertile red basalt soils; the Lower Yarra's valley floor is older grey-brown sedimentary loam. Terra rossa belongs to Coonawarra.","id":"r-260q26ie","opts":["Red volcanic soil","Terra rossa over limestone","Alluvial river sand","Grey sandy clay loam"],"q":"Which soil type marks the Upper Yarra around Seville and Hoddles Creek?"},{"accept":["Moët & Chandon","Moet & Chandon","Moët et Chandon","Moet et Chandon","Moët Chandon","Moet Chandon","Moët","Moet"],"ans":"Moët & Chandon","cat":"Yarra Valley (VIC)","exp":"Moët & Chandon built Domaine Chandon at Green Point, Coldstream, in 1986 to make traditional-method sparkling wine from cool Victorian fruit.","id":"r-vrsuv3tn","q":"Which Champagne house established Domaine Chandon in the Yarra Valley in 1986?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Yarra Valley (VIC)","exp":"Mornington Peninsula, south of Melbourne, is a Port Phillip region like Geelong, Macedon Ranges and Sunbury. King Valley is North East Victoria; Goulburn Valley and Strathbogie Ranges are Central Victoria.","id":"r-3sun4l54","opts":["Goulburn Valley","Mornington Peninsula","Strathbogie Ranges","King Valley"],"q":"Which neighbouring region shares the Port Phillip zone with the Yarra Valley?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Yarra Valley (VIC)","exp":"A small share of Viognier fermented with Shiraz, in the Côte-Rôtie manner, lifts the perfume and steadies the colour; the label reads Shiraz Viognier because Shiraz is the larger share. Marsanne and Roussanne make Yeringberg's white blend, and Chardonnay is not co-fermented with red grapes here.","id":"r-ysq3b3ns","opts":["Marsanne","Viognier","Roussanne","Chardonnay"],"q":"Which white grape do Yarra Valley houses such as Yering Station co-ferment with Shiraz?"}],"sheet":"australia","slug":"yarra-valley","summary":"The Yarra Valley is Victoria's first wine region, planted in 1838, abandoned by 1921 and replanted from the 1960s, and today the Australian address for cool-climate Pinot Noir, Chardonnay and traditional-method sparkling wine an hour north-east of Melbourne. It is a region in the Port Phillip zone with no registered subregions, but the trade splits it into a warmer, lower, older Lower Yarra on grey loams and a cooler, higher Upper Yarra on red volcanic soils. Cabernet blends and peppery Shiraz hold their ground at the historic estates. Think of it as Australia's answer to Burgundy with a Bordeaux past, and sell its reds for their perfume rather than their power."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Rutherglen, Classic, Grand, Rare: the Muscat of Rutherglen Network's classification from 1995, rising in richness and indicative average age (about 3 to 5, 6 to 10, 11 to 19, 20 and more years).","f":"The Rutherglen Muscat ladder, bottom to top"},{"b":"Brown Muscat, the red-skinned Muscat à Petits Grains Rouge, raisined on the vine before picking.","f":"Grape of Rutherglen Muscat"},{"b":"Muscadelle, the minor white grape of Bordeaux; labelled Tokay until the 2008 EU wine agreement retired the name.","f":"Topaque: grape and former name"},{"b":"Raisin on the vine; crush; short partial ferment; fortify with grape spirit to about 17 to 18 per cent; age for years in old oak in hot sheds and blend across ages.","f":"How Rutherglen Muscat is made, in five steps"},{"b":"North East Victoria zone: Rutherglen, Glenrowan, Beechworth, King Valley, Alpine Valleys. Glenrowan is the other fortified Muscat region.","f":"Rutherglen's zone and its neighbours"},{"b":"Durif, the same grape as Petite Sirah: inky, dense, tannic. Big Shiraz beside it.","f":"Rutherglen's signature dry red grape"},{"b":"Cool, in a small glass, a small pour; after dinner with blue cheese, chocolate or toffee desserts, or alone. The open bottle keeps for weeks.","f":"Serving a Rare Rutherglen Muscat"},{"b":"Hot continental, dry summers, cool nights; a long dry warm autumn lets Muscat shrivel on the vine without rot.","f":"Rutherglen climate and the autumn that matters"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Muscat","role":"lead"},{"name":"Muscadelle","role":"lead"},{"name":"Durif","role":"lead"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"lead"},{"name":"Touriga Nacional","role":"minor"}],"index":7,"name":"Rutherglen (VIC)","questions":[{"a":1,"cat":"Rutherglen (VIC)","exp":"Topaque is fortified Muscadelle, once called Tokay. Furmint is Hungary's Tokaji grape and has no part in it; Muscat makes the sister wine; Semillon makes Riverina's botrytis stickies.","id":"r-vprjugig","opts":["Muscat Blanc","Muscadelle","Sémillon","Furmint"],"q":"Which grape makes Rutherglen Topaque?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Rutherglen (VIC)","exp":"The ladder rises Rutherglen, Classic, Grand, Rare; Rare is built on the oldest reserves. Reserve is a common label word elsewhere but not a rung here.","id":"r-8ui7gbl8","opts":["Grand","Reserve","Rare","Classic"],"q":"Which is the top tier of the Rutherglen Muscat classification?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Rutherglen (VIC)","exp":"North East Victoria holds Rutherglen with Glenrowan, Beechworth, King Valley and Alpine Valleys. Port Phillip is Melbourne's zone, Central Victoria holds Heathcote, Western Victoria the Grampians.","id":"r-ejdklyzc","opts":["North East Victoria","Western Victoria","Port Phillip","Central Victoria"],"q":"In which Victorian zone does Rutherglen sit?"},{"accept":["Tokay","Rutherglen Tokay","Liqueur Tokay"],"ans":"Tokay","cat":"Rutherglen (VIC)","exp":"Tokay was the borrowed Hungarian name; the 2008 agreement between Australia and the European Community retired it and the producers chose Topaque.","id":"r-fzkhyeh8","q":"Under what name was Rutherglen Topaque sold before the EU wine agreement?","sa":1},{"accept":["Muscat à Petits Grains Rouge","Muscat a Petits Grains Rouge","Muscat à Petits Grains","Muscat a Petits Grains","Muscat Rouge à Petits Grains","Muscat Rouge a Petits Grains","Muscat Blanc à Petits Grains","Muscat Blanc a Petits Grains"],"ans":"Muscat à Petits Grains Rouge","cat":"Rutherglen (VIC)","exp":"Brown Muscat is the red-skinned form of Muscat à Petits Grains, the finest of the Muscat family and the grape of Asti and Beaumes-de-Venise; it is not Muscat of Alexandria, the coarser grape behind much Spanish Moscatel.","id":"r-y334rcwd","q":"What is the formal variety name of the Brown Muscat that makes Rutherglen Muscat?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Rutherglen (VIC)","exp":"Durif and Petite Sirah are the same grape, a cross of Peloursin and Syrah; Petit Verdot is a Bordeaux grape, Syrah is one parent and Mourvèdre is unrelated.","id":"r-8tol0cym","opts":["Petite Sirah","Petit Verdot","Syrah","Mourvèdre"],"q":"What is Durif called in California?"},{"accept":["17 to 18 per cent","17 to 18 percent","17-18%","about 18 percent","around 17.5%","18%","17%"],"ans":"17 to 18 per cent","cat":"Rutherglen (VIC)","exp":"Grape spirit stops the brief fermentation and lifts the wine to about 17 to 18 per cent, leaving the raisined grapes' sugar in the wine.","id":"r-hofyhg0i","q":"Roughly what alcohol level does Rutherglen Muscat reach after fortification?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Rutherglen (VIC)","exp":"The Murray marks the Victoria and New South Wales border beside Rutherglen. The Murrumbidgee waters the Riverina, the Goulburn runs through Nagambie, the Ovens through the Alpine Valleys.","id":"r-wbdck22c","opts":["Murrumbidgee","Goulburn","Ovens","Murray"],"q":"Which river runs along Rutherglen's northern boundary?"}],"sheet":"australia","slug":"rutherglen","summary":"Rutherglen is a hot, dry corner of north-east Victoria beside the Murray River where vines were planted in the 1850s and the fortified wine never stopped. Its claim to the world is Rutherglen Muscat, made from Brown Muscat (the red-skinned Muscat à Petits Grains) left to raisin on the vine, barely fermented, fortified with spirit and aged for years in old wood in sheds that bake. Beside it stands Topaque, the same wine from Muscadelle, once called Tokay. The producers' own ladder, Rutherglen, Classic, Grand and Rare, grades the blends by richness and average age. The dry reds are big: Durif and Shiraz. Pour the Muscat cool, in small glasses, with blue cheese or nothing at all."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"One GI for the whole state, zone and region in one; no registered subregions; district names are description only.","f":"Tasmania in law: one line"},{"b":"Tamar Valley, Pipers River, North West, East Coast, Coal River Valley, Derwent Valley, Huon Valley. All unofficial.","f":"Tasmania's seven trade districts"},{"b":"The central highlands shelter it from the westerlies of the Roaring Forties; the west is soaked and vineless, the east needs irrigation.","f":"Why Tasmania's east is dry"},{"b":"A 1986 joint venture of Heemskerk and Louis Roederer at Pipers River; traditional-method sparkling, Tasmania's best known fizz abroad.","f":"Jansz: origin and district"},{"b":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Gris, Riesling; Pinot and Chardonnay also feed the sparkling blends.","f":"Tasmania's grapes, most planted first"},{"b":"Dolerite clays (Coal River, Derwent), red basalt ferrosols (Pipers River, North West), mixed loams (Tamar), sandstone and alluvium (east and south).","f":"Soils to name in Tasmania"},{"b":"About 41 to 43 degrees south; cool maritime, the coolest mainstream climate in Australia; phylloxera free.","f":"Tasmania's latitude and climate class"},{"b":"Founded 1995; Tasmanian traditional-method sparkling aged for years on lees, vintage, Blanc de Blancs and late-disgorged cuvées; the island's prestige name.","f":"House of Arras: what to say"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"lead"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Gris / Grigio","role":"minor"},{"name":"Riesling","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Meunier","role":"blending"}],"index":8,"name":"Tasmania","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Tasmania","exp":"Tasmania is one state-wide GI with no registered subregions; the seven district names are trade descriptions. The Hunter, by contrast, has three registered subregions.","id":"r-m20eixok","opts":["None","Five","Three","Seven"],"q":"How many registered subregions does the Tasmania GI contain?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Tasmania","exp":"Pipers River, cool and wet on red basalt in the north-east, grew Pipers Brook, Jansz and Clover Hill and supplies much of the sparkling base. Coal River and the East Coast are drier Pinot and Chardonnay country; the Huon is the far south.","id":"r-tabba0w4","opts":["Huon Valley","East Coast","Coal River Valley","Pipers River"],"q":"Which Tasmanian district, the home of Jansz, is the island's sparkling heartland?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Tasmania","exp":"Pinot Noir leads the island's plantings, feeding both table wine and the sparkling blends with Chardonnay; Sauvignon Blanc and Riesling follow at a distance.","id":"r-p40o0rkf","opts":["Pinot Noir","Riesling","Chardonnay","Sauvignon Blanc"],"q":"Which grape is the most planted in Tasmania?"},{"accept":["Louis Roederer","Roederer","Champagne Louis Roederer"],"ans":"Louis Roederer","cat":"Tasmania","exp":"Louis Roederer's joint venture with Heemskerk at Pipers River in 1986 began Tasmania's traditional-method sparkling story; the Jansz label followed.","id":"r-wajg2lmm","q":"Which Champagne house partnered with Heemskerk in 1986 to found what became Jansz?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Tasmania","exp":"Bass Strait separates Tasmania from Victoria; the Tasman Sea lies east, the Southern Ocean south and west, the Coral Sea off Queensland.","id":"r-b5lbjkqq","opts":["Coral Sea","Tasman Sea","Bass Strait","Southern Ocean"],"q":"Which sea lies between Tasmania's north coast and the mainland?"},{"accept":["rain shadow","a rain shadow","rainshadow","the rain shadow of the central highlands","rain shadow of the mountains","Central Plateau rain shadow"],"ans":"rain shadow","cat":"Tasmania","exp":"The island's central highlands catch the Roaring Forties' rain, leaving the Tamar, Coal River and East Coast in a rain shadow where vineyards irrigate.","id":"r-qjcketzx","q":"What weather feature keeps eastern Tasmania dry while the west is soaked?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Tasmania","exp":"The Coal River Valley around Richmond and Cambridge is dry and warm for the island; the Derwent is north-west of Hobart, the Tamar is near Launceston in the north.","id":"r-y279ivad","opts":["Derwent Valley","Coal River Valley","North West","Tamar Valley"],"q":"Which Tasmanian district lies around Richmond, north-east of Hobart?"},{"accept":["85 per cent","85%","85 percent","eighty-five percent","85"],"ans":"85 per cent","cat":"Tasmania","exp":"The 85 per cent rule applies to every Australian GI claim, and to the variety and the vintage as well; a mainland house that buys Tasmanian fruit may label the wine Tasmania on that basis, which is how the island's name appears on bottles finished far away.","id":"r-c4jos0lw","q":"What minimum share of a wine's fruit must come from the island for the label to claim Tasmania?","sa":1}],"sheet":"australia","slug":"tasmania","summary":"Tasmania is Australia's cool island, forty-one to forty-three degrees south in the path of the Roaring Forties, and its one Geographical Indication: the whole state is a zone and a region in one, with no registered subregions, so the district names a label carries are description, not law. It grows Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Gris and Riesling, and it is the country's premier source of traditional-method sparkling wine, both under island labels and as base wine shipped to mainland houses. The vineyards cluster on the drier north and east: the Tamar Valley and Pipers River in the north, the East Coast, and the Coal River, Derwent and Huon valleys around Hobart. It is phylloxera free."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"The North Island's east coast around Poverty Bay, north of Hawke's Bay; warm, sunny, wet in autumn. Chardonnay leads, with Gewürztraminer, Pinot Gris, Chenin Blanc and Viognier.","f":"Where is Gisborne?"},{"b":"Deep fertile alluvial clay loam from the Waipaoa River: vigorous vines, divided canopies, high yields on the flats and a move by the estates towards the better-drained hill edges.","f":"What do Gisborne's deep alluvial soils do to the vine?"},{"b":"Autumn rain and ex-tropical cyclones from the north-east, with humidity and botrytis; harvest is early, from late February or early March, to beat them.","f":"What weather threatens the Gisborne vintage?"},{"b":"Millton, founded 1984 at Manutuke, certified organic in 1989 and later biodynamic; known for Chenin Blanc, Chardonnay and Viognier.","f":"Which Gisborne house was New Zealand's first certified organic estate?"},{"b":"Matawhero's, in the 1970s and 1980s: lychee, rose and ginger, oily and usually just off-dry.","f":"Whose Gewürztraminer first made Gisborne's name?"},{"b":"Early-picked Chardonnay and Pinot Noir from the flats feed the big companies' sparkling lines, Lindauer above all, launched by Montana in 1981.","f":"What does Gisborne give New Zealand's sparkling wine?"},{"b":"Ormond and the Ormond Valley to the north-west, Patutahi west, Manutuke south, Hexton on the city's northern hills; the Waipaoa flats between them.","f":"Name Gisborne's sub-areas"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"lead"},{"name":"Gewürztraminer","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Gris / Grigio","role":"rising"},{"name":"Chenin Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Viognier","role":"minor"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"minor"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"blending"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"blending"}],"index":0,"name":"Gisborne","questions":[{"a":1,"cat":"Gisborne","exp":"Gisborne calls itself the Chardonnay capital; it is the most planted grape and the region's best wine. Sauvignon Blanc here goes into blends, and Pinot Noir is grown for sparkling base.","id":"r-inn0ebc4","opts":["Sauvignon Blanc","Chardonnay","Riesling","Pinot Noir"],"q":"Which grape is Gisborne best known for?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Gisborne","exp":"Matawhero's Gewürztraminer was the region's first wine of fine reputation. Chenin Blanc and Viognier are Millton's specialities, and the Sauvignon Blanc grown here goes into blends.","id":"r-9qpnup31","opts":["Gewürztraminer","Chenin Blanc","Sauvignon Blanc","Viognier"],"q":"Which aromatic white made Gisborne's name at Matawhero in the 1970s and 1980s?"},{"accept":["Hawke's Bay","hawkes bay","hawkes bay region"],"ans":"Hawke's Bay","cat":"Gisborne","exp":"Gisborne lies around Poverty Bay, north of Hawke's Bay; down the east coast the order runs Gisborne, Hawke's Bay, then the Wairarapa. Gisborne is the wetter of the two and picks earlier.","id":"r-wmr6l3wu","q":"Which region is Gisborne's neighbour to the south on the North Island's east coast?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Gisborne","exp":"Gisborne sits on Poverty Bay, Cook's name of 1769. Hawke Bay is the water off Napier, Tasman Bay is Nelson's and Pegasus Bay lies north of Christchurch.","id":"r-gx18dtnc","opts":["Poverty Bay","Hawke Bay","Tasman Bay","Pegasus Bay"],"q":"On which bay does the city of Gisborne sit?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Gisborne","exp":"Millton, founded 1984 at Manutuke, was certified organic in 1989 and went on to biodynamics. Matawhero is the Gewürztraminer name and Lindauer the sparkling brand.","id":"r-c7hk45kr","opts":["Lindauer","Matawhero","TW Wines","Millton"],"q":"Which Gisborne house was New Zealand's first certified organic vineyard?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Gisborne","exp":"Ex-tropical cyclones bring rain from the north-east at vintage, so harvest is early and canopies are kept open. Frost is rare on this warm coast, and drought is not the worry of a wet one.","id":"r-3p3emw7h","opts":["summer hail","cyclone rain","summer drought","spring frost"],"q":"Which weather hazard most threatens the Gisborne vintage?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Gisborne","exp":"The Waipaoa River laid deep, fertile silts and clay loams across the flats, so vines grow vigorously and yield heavily. Greywacke gravel marks the Gimblett Gravels, schist Central Otago and chalky limestone Waikari.","id":"r-1rxqo1v4","opts":["weathered schist","chalky limestone","deep alluvial clay loam","free-draining greywacke gravel"],"q":"What soil covers the Poverty Bay flats, where most of Gisborne's vines grow?"}],"sheet":"new-zealand","slug":"gisborne","summary":"Gisborne sits on the North Island's east coast around Poverty Bay, a long way north and east, warm, sunny and wetter than the regions south of it. The Waipaoa River's deep alluvial clay loams are fertile and make vigorous vines, so the region was for decades the engine of bulk white wine, Müller-Thurgau first and then Chardonnay. Chardonnay remains its claim, ripe and peachy, with Gewürztraminer, Pinot Gris, Chenin Blanc and Viognier as the aromatic supporting cast and Merlot the main red. Autumn rain and cyclones from the tropics are the hazards. Millton, certified organic in 1989 and later biodynamic, is the house the exam knows."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"The North Island's east coast around Napier and Hastings, south of Gisborne; the warmest region of size, home of the oldest winery (Mission Estate 1851), second largest; Chardonnay, Merlot blends and Syrah.","f":"Where is Hawke's Bay?"},{"b":"The Ngaruroro's old bed, abandoned in the 1867 flood; greywacke stones, low fertility, warm and drip irrigated; a growers' trademark drawn on the soil boundary since 2001; Syrah, Merlot and Cabernet Sauvignon.","f":"What are the Gimblett Gravels?"},{"b":"Chardonnay the finest white and among the most planted; Merlot the leading red; Sauvignon Blanc and Pinot Gris for volume; Syrah the signature; Cabernet Sauvignon, Cabernet Franc and Malbec for the blends.","f":"Hawke's Bay's grapes: give each its role"},{"b":"Cool-climate: white pepper, violets, raspberry, medium body and fresh acidity; nearer the northern Rhône than Barossa, which is why the label says Syrah.","f":"How does Hawke's Bay Syrah taste?"},{"b":"Te Mata Estate's Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot and Cabernet Franc blend from the Havelock North hills, made since 1982; one of the country's longest-standing fine reds.","f":"What is Te Mata's Coleraine?"},{"b":"Ngaruroro (the Gimblett Gravels' maker), Tutaekuri and Tukituki: their fans built the Heretaunga Plains and its patchwork of silt, loam and gravel.","f":"Name the three rivers of Hawke's Bay"},{"b":"The warm district south-west of Hastings on red metal soils, older alluvium over gravel that holds more water than the Gimblett; softer, more aromatic Syrah and Merlot. A growers' name, not a GI.","f":"What is the Bridge Pa Triangle?"},{"b":"Te Awanga on the coast towards Cape Kidnappers: sea breeze, slower ripening, Chardonnay and Pinot Noir rather than Bordeaux grapes.","f":"Where is Hawke's Bay's cool coastal corner?"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"lead"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"lead"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"blending"},{"name":"Cabernet Franc","role":"blending"},{"name":"Malbec","role":"blending"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Gris / Grigio","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"minor"},{"name":"Viognier","role":"minor"}],"index":1,"name":"Hawke's Bay","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Hawke's Bay","exp":"The Ngaruroro changed course in the flood of 1867 and left its stony bed dry west of Hastings. The Tukituki and Tutaekuri are the plain's other rivers; the Waipaoa is Gisborne's.","id":"r-nb420ail","opts":["Ngaruroro","Tutaekuri","Waipaoa","Tukituki"],"q":"Which river's 1867 flood left behind the Gimblett Gravels?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Hawke's Bay","exp":"Merlot ripens earlier than Cabernet Sauvignon, so it leads the red plantings and most of the blends. Syrah is the famous red, Cabernet Sauvignon the warm-year leader and Pinot Noir a coastal crop.","id":"r-rupms56k","opts":["Merlot","Syrah","Pinot Noir","Cabernet Sauvignon"],"q":"Which red grape is the most planted in Hawke's Bay?"},{"accept":["Mission Estate","mission","mission estate winery"],"ans":"Mission Estate","cat":"Hawke's Bay","exp":"The Marist fathers founded it in 1851 and it has long stood at Taradale, the oldest working winery in the country. Te Mata's vines date from the 1890s and Church Road's cellars from 1897.","id":"r-so6qjyoz","q":"Which Hawke's Bay winery, founded in 1851, is the oldest still working in New Zealand?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Hawke's Bay","exp":"The growers' association drew the district on the soil boundary in 2001 and holds the name as a trademark; it is neither a regional nor a local GI, and it covers many vineyards, not one.","id":"r-or2ep5o6","opts":["a local GI","a single vineyard name","a regional GI","a growers' trademark"],"q":"What legal standing does the name Gimblett Gravels have?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Hawke's Bay","exp":"Coleraine is Cabernet Sauvignon with Merlot and Cabernet Franc from the Havelock North hills, made since 1982. Te Mata's Syrah is the Bullnose and its Chardonnay the Elston.","id":"r-meeyrsi1","opts":["a Rhône blend","a varietal Syrah","a Bordeaux blend","a Chardonnay"],"q":"What style of wine is Te Mata's Coleraine?"},{"accept":["Syrah","shiraz"],"ans":"Syrah","cat":"Hawke's Bay","exp":"Syrah from the Gimblett Gravels and the Bridge Pa Triangle is cool-climate in style, peppery and medium-bodied, nearer the northern Rhône than Barossa, which is why the labels say Syrah rather than Shiraz.","id":"r-nc5vxrpk","q":"Which red grape, small in area, gives Hawke's Bay its signature wine of white pepper and violets?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Hawke's Bay","exp":"Chardonnay, barrel fermented, with ripe stone fruit, a struck-match edge and the acidity to age a decade, is the best white and among the most planted. Sauvignon Blanc and Pinot Gris are volume grapes; Viognier is a small crop.","id":"r-a3wp7vpi","opts":["Sauvignon Blanc","Chardonnay","Viognier","Pinot Gris"],"q":"Which grape makes Hawke's Bay's finest white wine?"}],"sheet":"new-zealand","slug":"hawkes-bay","summary":"Hawke's Bay is the North Island's east coast south of Gisborne, the country's second largest region and home of its oldest winery, Mission Estate, founded by Marist missionaries in 1851, with Te Mata's vines at Havelock North from the 1890s. It is the warmest region of any size, maritime but sheltered by the ranges to the west, and its Heretaunga Plains are a mosaic of river soils. The Gimblett Gravels, the stony bed the Ngaruroro River abandoned in an 1867 flood, ripen Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon and a peppery, cool-climate Syrah; Chardonnay, grown across the plain, is the region's best white. The Bridge Pa Triangle, the hills of Havelock North and the cool coast at Te Awanga complete the map."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"A town at the dry southern end of the Wairarapa, east of the ranges from Wellington; a gravel terrace growing the North Island's most celebrated Pinot Noir since 1979 and 1980.","f":"Where is Martinborough?"},{"b":"Thin silt loam over deep free-draining river gravel around the town; low vigour, low rainfall, a 1979 soil survey that likened it to Burgundy.","f":"What is the Martinborough Terrace?"},{"b":"The north-westerly, dry off the ranges: it strips flowers and cuts the crop, and it keeps the fruit clean. Small yields are the style's source.","f":"What does the north-westerly do to Martinborough's vines?"},{"b":"Dry River (1979), Ata Rangi and Martinborough Vineyard (1980), then Te Kairanga and Palliser Estate in the 1980s; Escarpment on Te Muna Road from 1998.","f":"Name Martinborough's founding houses"},{"b":"Wairarapa for the whole valley; Martinborough and Gladstone as local GIs inside it. Masterton and Te Muna Road are addresses.","f":"Name the three GIs of the Wairarapa"},{"b":"Martinborough: darker, firmer, savoury, dried herb, wind-pruned crops. Central Otago: plusher dark cherry and thyme, riper tannin, continental extremes.","f":"How does Martinborough Pinot Noir differ from Central Otago's?"},{"b":"A higher terrace east of the town above the Huangarua, planted from the late 1990s by Escarpment and Craggy Range; fresher, more floral Pinot Noir.","f":"What is Te Muna Road?"},{"b":"A Pinot Noir clone of uncertain origin, nicknamed the Gumboot clone, that gives Ata Rangi and others their dark, spicy backbone.","f":"What is the Abel clone?"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"lead"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Gris / Grigio","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"},{"name":"Riesling","role":"minor"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"minor"},{"name":"Gewürztraminer","role":"minor"}],"index":2,"name":"Martinborough (Wairarapa)","questions":[{"a":3,"cat":"Martinborough (Wairarapa)","exp":"Pinot Noir from the gravel terrace is the region's fame; Sauvignon Blanc is the wider valley's volume wine, and Syrah and Chardonnay are small crops.","id":"r-w3xs2fmm","opts":["Chardonnay","Sauvignon Blanc","Syrah","Pinot Noir"],"q":"Which grape made Martinborough's reputation?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Martinborough (Wairarapa)","exp":"A thin silt loam caps old river gravel that drains for many metres, so roots go deep and vigour stays low. Clay over limestone is Waikari's, loess over schist Central Otago's.","id":"r-sgk2505b","opts":["clay over limestone","silt loam over river gravel","wind-blown loess over schist","volcanic ash over basalt"],"q":"Which soil profile makes the Martinborough Terrace?"},{"accept":["Ata Rangi","atarangi","ata rangi vineyard"],"ans":"Ata Rangi","cat":"Martinborough (Wairarapa)","exp":"Ata Rangi was planted on the terrace in 1980 and its Pinot Noir, built in large part on the Abel clone, is the region's benchmark. Dry River is the other founding name, from 1979.","id":"r-lfaddgjg","q":"Which house, founded in 1980, is the Martinborough name every sommelier knows for Pinot Noir?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Martinborough (Wairarapa)","exp":"The dry north-westerly off the ranges blows through flowering and shrinks the crop, then keeps the fruit clean in autumn. The southerly brings cold and rain, not the region's signature.","id":"r-t25yz8hp","opts":["the north-westerly","the southerly","the easterly sea breeze","the nor'easter"],"q":"Which wind shapes Martinborough's small crops?"},{"accept":["Wellington","wellington city"],"ans":"Wellington","cat":"Martinborough (Wairarapa)","exp":"Martinborough sits at the dry southern end of the Wairarapa, and the road from Wellington climbs the Remutaka Hill to reach it; the ranges that shelter the valley also put it in their rain shadow.","id":"r-r5h811sh","q":"Which city lies about an hour's drive south-west of Martinborough, over the Remutaka Range?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Martinborough (Wairarapa)","exp":"Wind-pruned crops from the gravel terrace give dark cherry, dried herb and earth with a firm, savoury tannin. Central Otago's continental heat gives the plusher, riper fruit; the exam's favourite Pinot contrast.","id":"r-xiv80kyt","opts":["softer and sweeter","plusher and riper","firmer and more savoury","lighter, paler and more floral"],"q":"Set beside Central Otago Pinot Noir, how is Martinborough's usually described?"},{"accept":["Gladstone","gladstone gi","gladstone district"],"ans":"Gladstone","cat":"Martinborough (Wairarapa)","exp":"The register holds Wairarapa for the whole valley, with Martinborough and Gladstone as local GIs inside it; Masterton and Te Muna Road are addresses with no GI of their own.","id":"r-w6kdfd5i","q":"Besides Martinborough, which registered GI lies inside the Wairarapa GI?","sa":1}],"sheet":"new-zealand","slug":"martinborough","summary":"Martinborough is a small town at the southern end of the Wairarapa, the valley east of the Tararua and Remutaka ranges an hour from Wellington, and the terrace of old river gravel around the town grows the North Island's most celebrated Pinot Noir. A soil survey of 1979 matched the place to Burgundy for climate and drainage, and the first vineyards followed within a year: Ata Rangi, Dry River and Martinborough Vineyard among them. Rain is scarce in the ranges' shadow, the autumn is dry and the north-westerly wind cuts the crop, so the wines are concentrated and savoury. The wider Wairarapa, with Gladstone and Masterton to the north, adds Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Gris, Chardonnay, Riesling and Syrah."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"More than two thirds, most of it Sauvignon Blanc, in the north-eastern corner of the South Island around Blenheim, the country's sunniest region.","f":"How much of New Zealand's vineyard does Marlborough hold?"},{"b":"1973 Montana plants Brancott; 1975 first Sauvignon Blanc planted; 1979 Montana's first Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc; 1985 Cloudy Bay's first vintage; 1986 Hunter's wins in London; 1988 Deutz Marlborough Cuvée; 2018 Appellation Marlborough Wine.","f":"Marlborough milestones: what happened in 1973, 1975, 1979, 1985, 1986, 1988 and 2018?"},{"b":"Wairau: broad, flat, stony, sunny, riper and tropical. Awatere: south over the Wither Hills, higher, windier, cooler, drier, a fortnight later, green, herbal and saline.","f":"How does the Awatere differ from the Wairau?"},{"b":"Brancott, Omaka, Fairhall, Waihopai and Ben Morven: clay-bound loess over gravel on the Wairau's southern flank; the region's Pinot Noir and Chardonnay.","f":"Name Marlborough's Southern Valleys"},{"b":"Pyrazines: capsicum, grass, tomato leaf; made in shade, lost in sun. Thiols: passionfruit, grapefruit, boxwood; precursors raised by skin contact, released by yeast in fermentation.","f":"Which compounds give Sauvignon Blanc its green notes, and which its tropical ones?"},{"b":"Night machine harvest, gentle press with sulphur dioxide and ascorbic acid, cold settle, cool tank ferment with selected yeast, no malolactic, no oak, a few grams of sugar, early bottling under screwcap.","f":"How is classic Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc made?"},{"b":"Wild yeast in old barrels with lees and time: Cloudy Bay Te Koko, Greywacke Wild Sauvignon, Dog Point Section 94. Smoky, waxy, textured.","f":"Name the models of Marlborough's wild-ferment Sauvignon Blanc"},{"b":"A growers' seal from 2018: 100 per cent Marlborough grapes, bottled in New Zealand, from vineyards certified sustainable. A promise above the GI, not law.","f":"What does the Appellation Marlborough Wine seal promise?"},{"b":"Pinot Noir and Chardonnay by the traditional method: Deutz Marlborough Cuvée (Montana with Deutz, 1988), Cloudy Bay Pelorus; Méthode Marlborough is the producers' mark.","f":"Name Marlborough's benchmark traditional-method sparkling wines"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Gris / Grigio","role":"minor"},{"name":"Riesling","role":"minor"},{"name":"Gewürztraminer","role":"minor"},{"name":"Grüner Veltliner","role":"rising"},{"name":"Albariño","role":"rising"}],"index":3,"name":"Marlborough","questions":[{"a":1,"cat":"Marlborough","exp":"The Awatere is higher, windier and cooler beyond the Withers, so it picks later and tastes greener. Omaka, Waihopai and Brancott are Southern Valleys on the Wairau's flank.","id":"r-q3qqdcl1","opts":["Omaka","Awatere","Waihopai","Brancott"],"q":"Which valley lies south of the Wither Hills and ripens about a fortnight later than the Wairau?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Marlborough","exp":"Montana, now the Brancott Estate brand, planted at Brancott in 1973 and Sauvignon Blanc in 1975. Hunter's came in the early 1980s and Cloudy Bay's first vintage was 1985.","id":"r-falrjdmd","opts":["Montana","Villa Maria","Cloudy Bay","Hunter's"],"q":"Which company planted the first vines of Marlborough's modern industry in 1973?"},{"accept":["Wairau","wairau valley","wairau river valley"],"ans":"Wairau","cat":"Marlborough","exp":"The broad, stony Wairau between the Richmond Range and the Wither Hills was planted first and is still the heart of the region; the Awatere lies south over the Withers, and the Southern Valleys are the Wairau's side valleys.","id":"r-aupr2044","q":"Which valley, the largest, holds most of Marlborough's vineyards?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Marlborough","exp":"Cloudy Bay was founded at Rapaura in 1985 by the owners of Cape Mentelle and its first vintage was that year. 1979 was Montana's first Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc and 1988 the Deutz joint venture.","id":"r-ksl6iwx9","opts":["1979","1991","1985","1988"],"q":"What was Cloudy Bay's first vintage?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Marlborough","exp":"The Southern Valleys, Brancott, Omaka, Fairhall, Waihopai and Ben Morven, hold water in their clay and grow the Pinot Noir and Chardonnay. Rapaura and the lower Wairau are Sauvignon Blanc ground; the Kekerengu coast is a small, cool outpost.","id":"r-tmr7qi7q","opts":["the Kekerengu coast","the Southern Valleys","the lower Wairau silts","the Rapaura stones"],"q":"Which part of Marlborough, on clay-bound loess, carries most of the region's Pinot Noir?"},{"accept":["Appellation Marlborough Wine","amw","appellation marlborough"],"ans":"Appellation Marlborough Wine","cat":"Marlborough","exp":"Appellation Marlborough Wine is a private seal above the GI's 85 per cent rule, and it also asks for vineyards certified sustainable. It is a promise, not law.","id":"r-9c93kbgc","q":"Which growers' certification, launched in 2018, requires every grape to be from Marlborough and the wine to be bottled in New Zealand?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Marlborough","exp":"Methoxypyrazines, the capsicum and cut-grass notes, form in shaded fruit and are broken down by sun and warmth, so leaf plucking is a stylistic decision. Thiols come from fermentation; terpenes and norisoprenoids are the floral and kerosene families.","id":"r-q4dqv02t","opts":["pyrazines","terpenes","norisoprenoids","thiols"],"q":"Which aroma family in Sauvignon Blanc does sunlight on the fruit reduce?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Marlborough","exp":"Deutz's joint venture with Montana in 1988 produced the Deutz Marlborough Cuvée, one of the region's first serious traditional-method sparkling wines. Moët's venture was in Australia's Yarra Valley and Roederer's in California.","id":"r-pkqqt9k1","opts":["Louis Roederer","Moët et Chandon","Bollinger","Deutz"],"q":"Which Champagne house joined Montana in 1988 to make a traditional-method Marlborough Cuvée?"}],"sheet":"new-zealand","slug":"marlborough","summary":"Marlborough fills the north-eastern corner of the South Island around Blenheim and holds more than two thirds of New Zealand's vineyard, most of it Sauvignon Blanc. Montana planted the first vines of the modern industry at Brancott in 1973, the first Sauvignon Blanc went in in 1975, and Cloudy Bay's 1985 vintage made the pungent passionfruit and gooseberry style famous within a decade. The region is the country's sunniest, dry and cool at night, on the stony old river floor of the Wairau Valley; the Southern Valleys add clay for Pinot Noir and the Awatere Valley to the south is higher, windier, cooler and greener. Chardonnay, Pinot Gris, Riesling and traditional-method sparkling complete the list."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"On Tasman Bay at the top of the South Island, west of Marlborough over the Richmond Range; sunny, wetter, small, family estates; aromatic whites, Chardonnay and Pinot Noir.","f":"Where is Nelson?"},{"b":"Waimea Plains: young stony alluvium, flat, irrigated, Sauvignon Blanc and Pinot Gris. Moutere Hills: ancient weathered clay gravel, water-holding, low yields, Chardonnay and Pinot Noir.","f":"Name Nelson's two districts and their soils"},{"b":"Sauvignon Blanc most planted; Pinot Noir and Chardonnay the fine wines; Riesling and Gewürztraminer the aromatic calling card; Pinot Gris for volume.","f":"Nelson's grapes: give each its role"},{"b":"Neudorf, founded 1978 at Upper Moutere; its Moutere Chardonnay is the region's benchmark, textured, long and age-worthy.","f":"What is Neudorf known for?"},{"b":"1840s German settlers at Upper Moutere grow the first grapes; 1973 the first modern vineyard there (Seifried); 1978 Neudorf; GI applied for in the first intake of 2017.","f":"Nelson's dates: what happened in the 1840s, 1973, 1978 and 2017?"},{"b":"As sunny as Blenheim, around a metre of rain a year, autumn rain the hazard; sheltered from the westerlies by the mountains, moderated by the bay.","f":"What is Nelson's weather hazard?"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Gris / Grigio","role":"minor"},{"name":"Riesling","role":"minor"},{"name":"Gewürztraminer","role":"minor"},{"name":"Albariño","role":"rising"}],"index":4,"name":"Nelson","questions":[{"accept":["Sauvignon Blanc","fume blanc","fumé blanc","sauv blanc"],"ans":"Sauvignon Blanc","cat":"Nelson","exp":"Sauvignon Blanc covers most of the Waimea Plains, riper and rounder than Marlborough's; the region's reputation rests instead on Moutere Chardonnay, Pinot Noir and its aromatic whites.","id":"r-hq89acf3","q":"Which grape is the most planted in Nelson?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Nelson","exp":"The Moutere Hills are an ancient gravel formation weathered into clay, water-holding and low in fertility, giving small crops of dense Chardonnay and Pinot Noir. The young river stones are the Waimea Plains.","id":"r-5u872h7l","opts":["limestone marl","weathered clay gravel","young river stones","wind-blown loess over schist"],"q":"Which soil marks the Moutere Hills?"},{"accept":["Neudorf","neudorf vineyards","neudorf vineyard"],"ans":"Neudorf","cat":"Nelson","exp":"Neudorf at Upper Moutere made the Chardonnay that put Nelson among the country's best. Seifried, from 1973, is the founding family's larger house.","id":"r-qg9ege0y","q":"Which Nelson estate, founded in 1978, carries the region's name for Moutere Chardonnay?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Nelson","exp":"Nelson faces Tasman Bay at the top of the South Island. Cloudy Bay is Marlborough's, Hawke Bay is off Napier and Poverty Bay is Gisborne's.","id":"r-dqyn46f5","opts":["Poverty Bay","Hawke Bay","Tasman Bay","Cloudy Bay"],"q":"On which bay does the Nelson wine region sit?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Nelson","exp":"Riesling, Gewürztraminer and Pinot Gris are the region's reputation, with Moutere Chardonnay and Pinot Noir as its fine wines. Bordeaux grapes do not ripen reliably in this wet, cool season.","id":"r-yjfz1fpa","opts":["fortified wines","sparkling wines","Bordeaux reds","aromatic whites"],"q":"Which family of wines is Nelson's calling card?"},{"accept":["Waimea Plains","waimea","waimea plain"],"ans":"Waimea Plains","cat":"Nelson","exp":"The Waimea Plains around Richmond and Brightwater are young river gravels, flat and drip irrigated, where Sauvignon Blanc and Pinot Gris grow; the Moutere Hills are old weathered clay gravel.","id":"r-i34bbqpw","q":"Which of Nelson's two districts lies on young, stony alluvial soils?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Nelson","exp":"Nelson is as sunny as Marlborough but receives around a metre of rain, some of it at vintage, so botrytis and harvest timing are the worry. Frost and drought are Marlborough's problems more than Nelson's.","id":"r-r97da108","opts":["autumn rain","strong wind","spring frost","summer drought"],"q":"Which hazard sets Nelson apart from Marlborough in the vineyard?"}],"sheet":"new-zealand","slug":"nelson","summary":"Nelson sits on Tasman Bay at the top of the South Island, west of Marlborough across the Richmond Range, and is a region of a different temper: as sunny, wetter, hillier and small, a place of family estates rather than corporations. The Waimea Plains near the city are flat alluvial gravels that grow Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Gris and Riesling in the Marlborough manner; the Moutere Hills to the west are old weathered clay gravels that hold water and give the region's concentrated Chardonnay and Pinot Noir. Aromatic whites are the calling card, Riesling and Gewürztraminer above all. The first modern vines went in at Upper Moutere in 1973, and Neudorf's Moutere Chardonnay has carried the name since the 1980s."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"The Teviotdale Hills on its seaward side block the cold easterly sea breeze, so the valley, an hour north of Christchurch, runs warmer and drier, with a long autumn for Riesling and Pinot Noir.","f":"Why is the Waipara Valley warmer than the Canterbury Plains?"},{"b":"Canterbury (the whole coast), North Canterbury (the northern part, registered 2020), Waipara Valley (the valley itself). Waikari and Omihi are addresses.","f":"Name Canterbury's three nested GIs"},{"b":"Glasnevin gravels on the valley floor: free-draining, Riesling and Sauvignon Blanc. Omihi hills: clay and limestone over calcareous rock, Pinot Noir and Chardonnay.","f":"Name Waipara's two soils and what each grows"},{"b":"Dry, off-dry, late harvest, botrytised: the same grape picked at four moments of the dry autumn; lime and honeysuckle, taut acidity, a decade of life.","f":"In what styles is Waipara Riesling made?"},{"b":"Canterbury's hot, dry föhn wind off the Southern Alps: scorches, dehydrates and strips flowers; shelter belts and row orientation are planned against it.","f":"What is the nor'wester?"},{"b":"Bell Hill (1997) and Pyramid Valley (2000): tiny estates on limestone in the cooler, higher inland basin north-west of Waipara, farmed organically or biodynamically; cult Pinot Noir and Chardonnay.","f":"Which two estates made Waikari's name?"},{"b":"1840 French vines at Akaroa; 1973 Lincoln College trials; 1978 Belfast and 1981 Burnham on the plains; 1981 Waipara's first commercial vineyard; Pegasus Bay's first vintage 1991.","f":"Canterbury milestones: what happened in 1840, 1973, 1978, 1981 and 1991?"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"lead"},{"name":"Riesling","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Gris / Grigio","role":"minor"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Gewürztraminer","role":"minor"}],"index":5,"name":"Canterbury / Waipara","questions":[{"a":1,"cat":"Canterbury / Waipara","exp":"Riesling, from dry to botrytised, is the valley's calling card and Pegasus Bay's fame. Sauvignon Blanc and Pinot Gris are commercial crops on the gravels; Chardonnay is fine but smaller.","id":"r-vxea3da1","opts":["Sauvignon Blanc","Riesling","Pinot Gris","Chardonnay"],"q":"Which white grape is Waipara's signature?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Canterbury / Waipara","exp":"Pinot Noir on the limestone-bearing clay of the Omihi hills gives a savoury, structured red, firmer than Central Otago's. Merlot, Syrah and Cabernet Sauvignon need more heat than the valley's cool season gives.","id":"r-1xe7yof7","opts":["Merlot","Syrah","Cabernet Sauvignon","Pinot Noir"],"q":"Which red grape is the Waipara Valley's signature?"},{"accept":["nor'wester","norwester","north-wester","north westerly","northwesterly","foehn","föhn"],"ans":"nor'wester","cat":"Canterbury / Waipara","exp":"The nor'wester is a föhn wind that drops the Alps' rain on the west coast and arrives in Canterbury hot and dry, scorching leaves and stripping flowers.","id":"r-7808zpal","q":"Which hot, dry wind off the Alps is Canterbury's own vineyard hazard?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Canterbury / Waipara","exp":"Bell Hill and Pyramid Valley sit on chalky limestone in the inland Waikari basin. Schist is Central Otago's rock, greywacke gravel the Waipara floor's and basalt Banks Peninsula's.","id":"r-cqgeoppa","opts":["schist","greywacke gravel","limestone","volcanic basalt"],"q":"On which rock are Waikari's famous estates planted?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Canterbury / Waipara","exp":"Pegasus Bay's first vintage was 1991 and its Riesling and Pinot Noir carry the valley's name abroad. Bell Hill is the Waikari cult estate; Greystone and Black Estate are Omihi houses.","id":"r-93jh836w","opts":["Pegasus Bay","Greystone","Bell Hill","Black Estate"],"q":"Which Waipara house, planted in the mid 1980s, is the region's best-known name?"},{"accept":["Christchurch","christchurch city","otautahi","ōtautahi"],"ans":"Christchurch","cat":"Canterbury / Waipara","exp":"The Waipara Valley lies about an hour north of Christchurch; the modern industry began on the plains around the city and moved north for the warmth the Teviotdale Hills give.","id":"r-7i674ln7","q":"Which city lies about an hour's drive south of the Waipara Valley?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Canterbury / Waipara","exp":"North Canterbury, the northern part of the Canterbury GI, holds the Waipara Valley GI and is the name the trade now prefers. South Canterbury, the Canterbury Plains and Mid Canterbury are places, not wine GIs.","id":"r-8fm3s66g","opts":["North Canterbury","Canterbury Plains","Mid Canterbury","South Canterbury"],"q":"Which GI sits between Canterbury and Waipara Valley in the register's nesting?"}],"sheet":"new-zealand","slug":"canterbury-waipara","summary":"Canterbury is the long east coast of the South Island around Christchurch, and its wine lives mostly in the north, in the Waipara Valley an hour up the road, where the Teviotdale Hills keep out the cold sea breeze and the hot, dry nor'wester blows through. The modern industry began on the frosty, windy Canterbury Plains in 1978, and the industry moved to Waipara's warmer valley from the early 1980s. Riesling is the signature white, from dry to botrytised; Pinot Noir on the limestone-bearing clay of the Omihi hills is the signature red; Chardonnay, Pinot Gris and Sauvignon Blanc follow. Three GIs nest here: Canterbury, North Canterbury and Waipara Valley. Pegasus Bay is the house to know."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Inland southern South Island at 45°S, among the southernmost wine regions on earth; schist basins and gorges; New Zealand's only continental climate; Pinot Noir about three vines in four.","f":"Where is Central Otago?"},{"b":"Semi-arid, hot days, cold nights, frost. Behind the mountains the rain fails and the sea's moderation is gone, so the day-night swing ripens fruit and keeps acid.","f":"Why is Central Otago's climate continental?"},{"b":"Gibbston (coolest, highest), Bannockburn (warm, dry, most famous, GI 2022), Cromwell with Lowburn and Pisa (the centre), Bendigo (warmest east-bank terraces), Alexandra (driest, southernmost), Wanaka (lake-moderated, northernmost).","f":"Name the six sub-regions of Central Otago"},{"b":"1864 a French miner's vines at Clyde; 1981 Gibbston Valley and Black Ridge planted; 1987 first commercial vintage; 1997 Felton Road's first vintage; 2022 Bannockburn GI.","f":"Central Otago milestones: what happened in 1864, 1981, 1987, 1997 and 2022?"},{"b":"Dark cherry, plum, wild thyme and spice, ripe round tannin, generous body, a little more alcohol than Martinborough; whole bunch and wild ferment common, French oak a quarter to a third new.","f":"What is the style of Central Otago Pinot Noir?"},{"b":"Old gold sluicings on Lake Dunstan's south-western shore; warm, dry and the most famous sub-region; Felton Road, Mt Difficulty and Carrick; a registered GI since 1 February 2022.","f":"What is Bannockburn?"},{"b":"North-facing slopes to shed cold air, wind machines, water sprinklers, helicopters on the worst nights; frost possible in any month, a late April frost the nightmare.","f":"How do Central Otago growers fight frost?"},{"b":"Central Otago: schist, continental, the famous Pinot Noir. Waitaki Valley North Otago: limestone, cool, tiny, a separate GI to the north-east.","f":"How does Central Otago differ from the Waitaki Valley?"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Gris / Grigio","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"rising"},{"name":"Riesling","role":"minor"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Gewürztraminer","role":"minor"}],"index":6,"name":"Central Otago","questions":[{"a":1,"cat":"Central Otago","exp":"Bannockburn was registered on 1 February 2022, the first sub-regional GI inside Central Otago. Gibbston, Bendigo and Alexandra remain growers' names and addresses.","id":"r-bmms00kf","opts":["Alexandra","Bannockburn","Bendigo","Gibbston Valley"],"q":"Which Central Otago sub-region became a registered GI of its own in 2022?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Central Otago","exp":"Schist is the region's rock, and its loess, outwash and gravels are the soils. Limestone is the Waitaki's and Waikari's; basalt belongs to Banks Peninsula.","id":"r-ypdbdrsk","opts":["basalt","schist","granite","limestone"],"q":"Which bedrock underlies the vineyards of Central Otago?"},{"accept":["Pinot Noir","pinot nero","spätburgunder","spatburgunder","blauburgunder"],"ans":"Pinot Noir","cat":"Central Otago","exp":"Pinot Noir dominates, dark cherry and wild thyme with ripe tannin and generous body; Pinot Gris is a distant second, with Chardonnay and Riesling behind.","id":"r-5p4wu7x2","q":"Which grape accounts for about three vines in four in Central Otago?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Central Otago","exp":"Gibbston sits highest in the gorge west of Cromwell and picks last, giving lighter, floral Pinot Noir. Bannockburn and Bendigo are the warm sub-regions; Alexandra is the driest.","id":"r-0z97blhl","opts":["Gibbston","Bannockburn","Alexandra","Bendigo"],"q":"Which sub-region is the highest and coolest, in the Kawarau Gorge?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Central Otago","exp":"Central Otago lies around 45°S, among the southernmost vineyards on earth. Northland is near 35°S, Hawke's Bay near 39°S and Martinborough and Nelson near 41°S.","id":"r-ordaw648","opts":["35°S","39°S","41°S","45°S"],"q":"At about which latitude do Central Otago's vineyards lie?"},{"accept":["Felton Road","felton rd","felton road wines"],"ans":"Felton Road","cat":"Central Otago","exp":"Felton Road's biodynamic Bannockburn vineyards and its Block 3 and Block 5 Pinot Noirs are the region's benchmark. Mt Difficulty and Carrick are its Bannockburn neighbours.","id":"r-1jz6nns4","q":"Which Bannockburn house, planted in the early 1990s with a first vintage in 1997, is the region's most famous?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Central Otago","exp":"Cold air pools in the inland basins and frost is possible in any month, so growers plant north-facing slopes and stir or protect the vines on the worst nights. Drought is met with drip irrigation; botrytis is rare in the dry autumn.","id":"r-og10p4i8","opts":["drought","hail","frost","botrytis"],"q":"Which hazard do Central Otago growers fight with wind machines, sprinklers and helicopters?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Central Otago","exp":"Bendigo's north-facing terraces under the Dunstan Mountains are the warmest ground in the region, home of Quartz Reef and Prophet's Rock. Gibbston is the coolest and Wanaka lake-moderated.","id":"r-7ps94v7w","opts":["Bendigo","Alexandra","Gibbston","Wanaka"],"q":"Which sub-region sits on the east bank of Lake Dunstan with the region's warmest terraces?"}],"sheet":"new-zealand","slug":"central-otago","summary":"Central Otago is the inland south of the South Island, a country of schist mountains, gorges and dry basins at 45°S, among the southernmost wine regions on earth and the only one in New Zealand with a continental climate: hot dry summers, cold winters, fierce swings between day and night and frost possible in any month. A French miner planted vines at Clyde in 1864, but the modern region dates from the 1980s and its first commercial vintage from 1987. Pinot Noir is about three vines in four, dark cherry and thyme with ripe tannin, grown in six sub-regions: Gibbston, Bannockburn, Cromwell and Pisa, Bendigo, Alexandra and Wanaka. Bannockburn became a registered GI of its own in 2022."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"A hot, dry district of the Coastal Region north of Cape Town, wheat country whose old dry-farmed bush vines make Syrah blends and Chenin Blanc. Towns: Malmesbury, Riebeek-Kasteel.","f":"Swartland: where it is and what it is"},{"b":"Granite on the Paardeberg, perfumed and taut; Malmesbury shale and schist around Riebeek and Porseleinberg, deeper and savoury. Koffieklip gravel on both.","f":"Swartland's two soils and their landmarks"},{"b":"Sadie Family Wines, Malmesbury. Columella: Syrah-led Rhône-style red blend, first 2000. Palladius: Chenin-led old-vine white blend. The wines that made the Swartland.","f":"Columella and Palladius: what they are"},{"b":"Voluntary, from 2010: Swartland fruit only, native yeasts, no added acid or tannin, a limit on new oak. A seal on the bottle, no legal force.","f":"The Swartland Independent Producers charter"},{"b":"Malmesbury, Riebeekberg, Riebeeksrivier, Paardeberg, Paardeberg South, Porseleinberg, Piket-Bo-Berg (2022). Darling left in 2003 as its own district.","f":"Swartland's wards by name"},{"b":"Schist, Granite and Iron: three Syrahs from Riebeek-Kasteel, each from one soil type, used to teach the district's geology.","f":"Mullineux's single-soil Syrahs"},{"b":"Black land, after the renosterbos shrub that turns the hills dark after rain.","f":"What Swartland means"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chenin Blanc","role":"lead"},{"name":"Grenache / Garnacha","role":"blending"},{"name":"Cinsault","role":"blending"},{"name":"Mourvèdre / Monastrell","role":"blending"},{"name":"Carignan","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinotage","role":"minor"},{"name":"Viognier","role":"minor"},{"name":"Roussanne","role":"minor"}],"index":0,"name":"Swartland","questions":[{"a":2,"cat":"Swartland","exp":"The Swartland is a district of the Coastal Region beside Stellenbosch and Paarl. The Breede River Valley holds Robertson, the Cape South Coast holds Elgin and Walker Bay, and the Olifants River runs north of the Swartland.","id":"r-gj2jwixu","opts":["Cape South Coast","Breede River Valley","Coastal Region","Olifants River"],"q":"Which Wine of Origin region holds the Swartland district?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Swartland","exp":"Columella is Syrah led, with Mourvèdre, Grenache and other Rhône grapes from dry-farmed bush vines. Pinotage and Cabernet are the Stellenbosch story; Cinsaut plays a supporting part in the district's lighter reds.","id":"r-7bg29eko","opts":["Pinotage","Cinsaut","Cabernet Sauvignon","Syrah"],"q":"Which grape leads Columella, the Swartland's best-known red blend?"},{"accept":["Paardeberg","paardeberg","Perdeberg","the Paardeberg"],"ans":"Paardeberg","cat":"Swartland","exp":"The Paardeberg, horse mountain, is a dome of Cape granite whose Swartland face is the ward of Paardeberg, with Paardeberg South beside it; the Paarl face is Voor Paardeberg.","id":"r-0ws1h76f","q":"Which granite mountain on the Paarl border gives its name to a Swartland ward?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Swartland","exp":"Malmesbury shale and its schist weather to iron-rich clay loams that give deeper, more savoury Syrah; the granite is the Paardeberg's. There is no limestone of note here, and the Cape has no volcanic basalt.","id":"r-kbdh66as","opts":["Shale","Limestone","Basalt","Granite"],"q":"Which rock underlies the Riebeek hills and the land around Malmesbury?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Swartland","exp":"The old bush vines live on winter rain alone, their roots deep in the granite or shale, which keeps yields tiny and concentrates the fruit. Drip irrigation is common on newer trellised vines in warmer Cape districts; flood and sprinkler systems belong to bulk vineyards on the river flats.","id":"r-kjst2d2p","opts":["Dry farming","Overhead sprinklers","Flood irrigation","Drip irrigation"],"q":"Which watering regime is typical of the Swartland's old bush vines?"},{"accept":["Palladius","palladius"],"ans":"Palladius","cat":"Swartland","exp":"Palladius is the Chenin-led white blend of old bush vines that Sadie Family Wines at Malmesbury makes beside the Syrah-led Columella; the pair, from 2000 on, made the Swartland's name. Both are named for Roman writers on farming.","id":"r-920s3p27","q":"Which white blend from Sadie Family Wines is the partner of Columella?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Swartland","exp":"Darling, towards the Atlantic, became a separate district with Groenekloof as its ward, known for cool-climate Sauvignon Blanc. Malmesbury, Riebeekberg and Porseleinberg remain wards within the Swartland.","id":"r-jsrpq1yd","opts":["Riebeekberg","Darling","Malmesbury","Porseleinberg"],"q":"Which former Swartland ward became a district of its own in 2003?"}],"sheet":"south-africa","slug":"swartland","summary":"The Swartland is the hot, dry wheat country north of Cape Town, a district of the Coastal Region that was bulk wine land until a handful of growers showed, from about 2000, what its old dry-farmed bush vines could do. Its name means black land, after the renosterbos scrub that darkens the hills. Its wines are Syrah and Rhône-style red blends from the granite of the Paardeberg and the shale of the Riebeek hills, and Chenin Blanc from bush vines planted decades ago, savoury and textured. Malmesbury and Riebeek-Kasteel are the towns, Sadie Family Wines and Mullineux the houses the exam expects. Wine of Origin Swartland sets origin only; the growers' own charter of 2010 sets the style."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Cabernet Sauvignon and Bordeaux blends on granite foot slopes; Pinotage crossed here in 1925; False Bay breezes and the Cape Doctor keep a Mediterranean valley fresh.","f":"Stellenbosch: the three things to hold"},{"b":"Simonsberg-Stellenbosch, Jonkershoek Valley, Banghoek, Papegaaiberg, Devon Valley, Bottelary, Polkadraai Hills, Vlottenburg (2020). Helderberg is informal.","f":"Stellenbosch's eight wards"},{"b":"Paul Sauer: Kanonkop, Simonsberg, Cabernet-led Bordeaux blend, first 1981. Rubicon: Meerlust, near False Bay, Cabernet-led Bordeaux blend, first 1980.","f":"Paul Sauer and Rubicon: who makes them and what they are"},{"b":"1925 crossed at Stellenbosch; 1941 first wine at Elsenburg; 1959 the first commercial vintage, Lanzerac; 1961 released.","f":"Pinotage: the four dates"},{"b":"Decomposed granite on the Simonsberg and Helderberg foot slopes: Cabernet. Sandstone higher: whites. Malmesbury shale in the Bottelary: old Chenin, Pinotage, Shiraz.","f":"Stellenbosch soils and what grows where"},{"b":"Kaapse Vonkel, made by Simonsig on the Simonsberg in 1971, at first from Chenin Blanc; now Chardonnay and Pinot Noir.","f":"The first Cap Classique"},{"b":"False Bay, fifteen kilometres south, sends afternoon breezes; the Cape Doctor south-easter dries the canopy; the Simonsberg and Banghoek add altitude.","f":"What cools Stellenbosch"},{"b":"Grapes grown, wine made and bottled on one registered production unit. Many Stellenbosch farms qualify and print the term.","f":"Estate Wine on a Stellenbosch label"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"lead"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"blending"},{"name":"Cabernet Franc","role":"blending"},{"name":"Pinotage","role":"lead"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chenin Blanc","role":"lead"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"},{"name":"Cinsault","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"minor"}],"index":1,"name":"Stellenbosch","questions":[{"accept":["Cabernet Sauvignon","cabernet sauvignon","Cabernet","cabernet","Cab Sauv"],"ans":"Cabernet Sauvignon","cat":"Stellenbosch","exp":"Stellenbosch is the Cape's Cabernet district, bottled alone or leading Bordeaux blends such as Paul Sauer and Rubicon. Sauvignon Blanc takes the cooler upper slopes, Pinot Noir belongs to the Hemel-en-Aarde, and Chenin grows everywhere.","id":"r-8til2fyo","q":"Which grape is the signature of Stellenbosch's granite foot slopes?","sa":1},{"accept":["False Bay","false bay","Valsbaai"],"ans":"False Bay","cat":"Stellenbosch","exp":"False Bay lies fifteen kilometres south of the town and cools the Helderberg and the southern farms each afternoon; the Cape Doctor south-easter blows across it in summer.","id":"r-zuo2w074","q":"Which bay sends its afternoon breezes into the southern Stellenbosch vineyards?","sa":1},{"accept":["Simonsberg-Stellenbosch","simonsberg-stellenbosch","Simonsberg Stellenbosch","simonsberg stellenbosch","Simonsberg"],"ans":"Simonsberg-Stellenbosch","cat":"Stellenbosch","exp":"The south-facing foot slopes of the Simonsberg, north of the town, carry the district's densest cluster of famous estates on deep decomposed granite. The Paarl side of the same mountain is a separate ward, Simonsberg-Paarl, so the suffix matters.","id":"r-y2jsqlem","q":"Which Stellenbosch ward holds Kanonkop, Rustenberg and Warwick?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Stellenbosch","exp":"Lanzerac in the Jonkershoek valley sold the 1959 Pinotage in 1961. Kanonkop became the grape's standard-bearer later, Meerlust is known for Rubicon, and Simonsig for the first Cap Classique.","id":"r-z00hb4pv","opts":["Kanonkop","Lanzerac","Simonsig","Meerlust"],"q":"Which estate released the first commercial Pinotage, from the 1959 vintage?"},{"accept":["1925","nineteen twenty five","nineteen twenty-five"],"ans":"1925","cat":"Stellenbosch","exp":"The university's professor of viticulture crossed Pinot Noir with Cinsaut in 1925; the first wine was made at Elsenburg in 1941 and the first commercial bottling followed in 1961.","id":"r-yhccife9","q":"In which year was Pinotage crossed at Stellenbosch?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Stellenbosch","exp":"The Helderberg, the mountain above Somerset West, holds Vergelegen and Rust en Vrede but has never been gazetted as a ward, so its wines are labelled Stellenbosch. Banghoek, Bottelary and Papegaaiberg are three of the district's eight wards.","id":"r-js8oaggj","opts":["Helderberg","Papegaaiberg","Banghoek","Bottelary"],"q":"Which name on a Stellenbosch wine list is an informal sub-region rather than a registered Wine of Origin ward?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Stellenbosch","exp":"Simonsig's Kaapse Vonkel of 1971 began the category now called Cap Classique. Kanonkop is the Pinotage and Paul Sauer estate, Vergelegen and Rustenberg are known for Bordeaux-style reds.","id":"r-7j7ckk8o","opts":["Vergelegen","Rustenberg","Simonsig","Kanonkop"],"q":"Which Stellenbosch house made the Cape's first bottle-fermented sparkling wine in 1971?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Stellenbosch","exp":"Cape granite weathers to a deep, water-holding loam that carries Cabernet through the dry summer. The Cape has no limestone of note in Stellenbosch, no slate vineyards and no volcanic basalt.","id":"r-4du6n27d","opts":["Basalt","Slate","Limestone","Granite"],"q":"Which rock weathers into the reddish, clay-bearing soil of the Simonsberg foot slopes?"}],"sheet":"south-africa","slug":"stellenbosch","summary":"Stellenbosch is the Cape's fine wine capital: a university town founded in 1679, ringed by granite and sandstone mountains, fifteen kilometres from False Bay. It is a district of the Coastal Region with eight wards, and the exam wants three things from it. First, Cabernet Sauvignon and the Bordeaux blend, the district's signature on the mountain foot slopes. Second, Pinotage, crossed here in 1925 by the university's professor of viticulture and first sold by a Stellenbosch estate in 1961. Third, the cooling: the False Bay breeze and the Cape Doctor south-easter that keep a Mediterranean valley fresh. Kanonkop, Meerlust, Rustenberg, Vergelegen and Thelema are the houses to name, and the first Cap Classique was made here in 1971."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"A warm district of the Coastal Region north of Stellenbosch on the Berg River; Shiraz and Chenin country; home of the KWV (1918) and Nederburg (1791).","f":"Paarl: where, what and why it matters"},{"b":"Simonsberg-Paarl (shared with Stellenbosch as Simonsberg-Stellenbosch), Voor Paardeberg (shared with the Swartland's Paardeberg), Agter-Paarl behind the town.","f":"Paarl's wards and its two shared mountains"},{"b":"A Wine of Origin region name reserved for fortified wine from the Paarl and Tulbagh country; never on a table wine.","f":"Boberg: what the name covers"},{"b":"Growers' co-operative founded at Paarl in 1918; fixed minimum prices and, from 1957, planting quotas; built the Cape's fortified wine and brandy trade.","f":"The KWV in three facts"},{"b":"Edelkeur, the Cape's first botrytised Chenin Blanc dessert wine, 1969; the Nederburg Auction of rare Cape wines, held since 1975.","f":"Nederburg's two firsts"},{"b":"Franschhoek Valley, a district since 2010; Wellington, a district since 2012. Both were wards of Paarl before.","f":"Two districts carved from Paarl"},{"b":"It lies further from False Bay, so the sea breeze arrives weak, and the Du Toitskloof mountains hold the afternoon heat in the Berg River valley.","f":"Why Paarl is warmer than Stellenbosch"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chenin Blanc","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinotage","role":"blending"},{"name":"Viognier","role":"rising"},{"name":"Grenache / Garnacha","role":"rising"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"},{"name":"Muscat","role":"minor"}],"index":2,"name":"Paarl","questions":[{"accept":["KWV","kwv","the KWV","Ko-operatieve Wijnbouwers Vereniging","Kooperatieve Wijnbouwers Vereniging","Koöperatieve Wijnbouwers Vereniging"],"ans":"KWV","cat":"Paarl","exp":"The KWV, the co-operative winegrowers' association, fixed minimum prices and later planting quotas, and built the Cape's fortified wine and brandy trade from its Paarl cellars.","id":"r-1e5bxjsc","q":"Which growers' co-operative was founded at Paarl in 1918?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Paarl","exp":"Boberg is a region name that appears only on fortified wine. The Coastal Region is Paarl's region for table wine, the Breede River Valley holds Robertson, and the Klein Karoo holds Calitzdorp's port styles.","id":"r-yd7k5gij","opts":["Coastal Region","Breede River Valley","Boberg","Klein Karoo"],"q":"Which Wine of Origin name is reserved for fortified wine from the Paarl and Tulbagh country?"},{"accept":["Voor Paardeberg","voor paardeberg","Voor-Paardeberg","Voor Perdeberg","Voorpaardeberg"],"ans":"Voor Paardeberg","cat":"Paarl","exp":"Voor Paardeberg, in front of the Paardeberg, is Paarl's ward on the mountain it shares with the Swartland, whose own face carries the wards of Paardeberg and Paardeberg South; granite, old bush-vine Chenin and Pinotage on both sides.","id":"r-rbcy0elk","q":"Which Paarl ward lies on the Paarl face of the granite mountain that Paarl shares with the Swartland?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Paarl","exp":"The Simonsberg carries two wards, one on each side: Simonsberg-Paarl on the northern slopes and Simonsberg-Stellenbosch on the southern, so the suffix decides. Voor Paardeberg faces the Swartland's mountain and Agter-Paarl lies behind Paarl Mountain.","id":"r-w95z813m","opts":["Voor Paardeberg","Agter-Paarl","Simonsberg-Stellenbosch","Simonsberg-Paarl"],"q":"Which ward covers the Paarl slopes of the mountain that Paarl shares with Stellenbosch?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Paarl","exp":"Edelkeur is botrytised Chenin Blanc, a Noble Late Harvest. Riesling and Sémillon are the botrytis grapes of Germany and Sauternes, and Muscat of Alexandria, Hanepoot, makes the Cape's fortified sweet wines.","id":"r-9t8yjme6","opts":["Chenin Blanc","Muscat of Alexandria","Sémillon","Riesling"],"q":"Which grape made Nederburg's Edelkeur, the Cape's first botrytised dessert wine, in 1969?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Paarl","exp":"Wellington, the vine nursery town north of Paarl under the Groenberg, was a Paarl ward until 2012; Franschhoek had left in 2010. Tulbagh is a district beyond the mountains, Malmesbury a Swartland ward, and Darling a district since 2003.","id":"r-lkl3bs70","opts":["Wellington","Tulbagh","Malmesbury","Darling"],"q":"Which former ward of Paarl became a Wine of Origin district of its own in 2012?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Paarl","exp":"The Berg River rises in the Franschhoek mountains and flows through Paarl and Wellington to the sea. The Breede waters Robertson, the Eerste runs through Stellenbosch, and the Olifants runs north of the Swartland.","id":"r-q6tp3cog","opts":["Breede River","Berg River","Olifants River","Eerste River"],"q":"Which river runs north through the Paarl district towards the Atlantic?"}],"sheet":"south-africa","slug":"paarl","summary":"Paarl is the warm district north of Stellenbosch along the Berg River, named for the granite dome above the town that gleamed like a pearl after rain when Dutch explorers saw it in 1657. It was the seat of the KWV, the growers' co-operative founded here in 1918 that ran the industry for most of the twentieth century, and of Nederburg, the estate of 1791 known for its auction and botrytised Chenin. It is a Wine of Origin district of the Coastal Region, warmer and drier than Stellenbosch because the sea is further off; its grapes are Shiraz, Chenin Blanc, Cabernet, Pinotage and the Rhône whites. Franschhoek and Wellington were carved from it as districts in 2010 and 2012."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"French corner: the valley the Huguenot refugees settled from 1688, east of Stellenbosch and south-east of Paarl, with French farm names to this day.","f":"Franschhoek: name, settlers and date"},{"b":"The Franschhoek Valley district of the Coastal Region since 2010; before that a ward of Paarl. It has no wards of its own.","f":"Franschhoek in the Wine of Origin scheme"},{"b":"Sémillon, the old Groendruif, once nine tenths of the Cape's vines; the valley keeps bush vines from the early twentieth century on its sandy floor.","f":"Franschhoek's historic white grape"},{"b":"The valley's collective speciality, made by many of its cellars; first valley bottling in the mid 1980s; at least twelve months on lees.","f":"Franschhoek and Cap Classique"},{"b":"The valley's benchmark house, on a 1776 farm revived in the 1990s: Syrah, Cabernet, old-vine Sémillon, and the Swartland's Porseleinberg Syrah.","f":"Boekenhoutskloof: what to know"},{"b":"The Franschhoek, Wemmershoek and Groot Drakenstein mountains on three sides, above 1,500 metres; the open end faces Paarl, and the Berg River rises here.","f":"What closes the Franschhoek valley"},{"b":"Helshoogte Pass joins Stellenbosch to Franschhoek through Banghoek; the Franschhoek Pass climbs out of the valley's far end towards the Overberg.","f":"Two passes, two directions"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Sémillon","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"lead"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"lead"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"blending"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"blending"},{"name":"Chenin Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"minor"}],"index":3,"name":"Franschhoek","questions":[{"a":3,"cat":"Franschhoek","exp":"French Protestant Huguenots, fleeing after the revocation of the Edict of Nantes, were settled by the Dutch East India Company in the valley that became the French corner. The Moravians founded mission stations elsewhere in the Cape.","id":"r-ghaqj693","opts":["The Mennonites","The Moravians","The Puritans","The Huguenots"],"q":"Which refugees settled the Franschhoek valley from 1688 and gave it its name?"},{"accept":["2010","twenty ten","two thousand and ten"],"ans":"2010","cat":"Franschhoek","exp":"The Franschhoek Valley district was created in 2010 out of what had been a ward of Paarl; Wellington followed the same path in 2012.","id":"r-5djudi3f","q":"In which year did Franschhoek become a Wine of Origin district in its own right?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Franschhoek","exp":"Groendruif, green grape, is Sémillon, which was nine tenths of the Cape's vines in the 1820s; Franschhoek kept old bush vines of it on its sandy valley floor. Chenin is the Cape's most planted grape but not the valley's story.","id":"r-yfhhjy29","opts":["Sauvignon Blanc","Chardonnay","Sémillon","Chenin Blanc"],"q":"Which grape is Franschhoek's historic white, once known as Groendruif?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Franschhoek","exp":"Franschhoek was a ward within Paarl until it was made a district; Stellenbosch lies west across the Helshoogte Pass, Wellington was itself a Paarl ward until 2012, and Tulbagh is a separate district beyond the mountains.","id":"r-64ns68ok","opts":["Stellenbosch","Paarl","Tulbagh","Wellington"],"q":"Which Wine of Origin district was Franschhoek a ward of before 2010?"},{"accept":["Boekenhoutskloof","boekenhoutskloof","Boekenhoutkloof","Boekenhouts Kloof"],"ans":"Boekenhoutskloof","cat":"Franschhoek","exp":"Boekenhoutskloof, on a farm granted in 1776 and revived in the 1990s, makes Syrah, Cabernet and old-vine Sémillon in Franschhoek and bought the schist Porseleinberg farm, whose Syrah carries Wine of Origin Swartland, not Franschhoek.","id":"r-0hj8ql63","q":"Which Franschhoek house farms the Porseleinberg Syrah vineyard in the Swartland?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Franschhoek","exp":"The Berg River gathers in the Franschhoek mountains, crosses the valley and runs north through Paarl and Wellington to the Atlantic. The Eerste is Stellenbosch's river, the Breede waters Robertson, and the Palmiet drains Elgin.","id":"r-xpua0bbg","opts":["Berg River","Breede River","Eerste River","Palmiet River"],"q":"Which river rises in the mountains above Franschhoek and flows north through Paarl?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Franschhoek","exp":"The minimum rose from nine months to twelve, a rule the Cap Classique producers' association adopted first and the law then followed. Champagne asks twelve months on lees and fifteen months in all for a non-vintage wine, the figures candidates confuse it with.","id":"r-li4bsm29","opts":["Twelve months","Six months","Nine months","Fifteen months"],"q":"What is the current minimum time on lees for a Cap Classique?"}],"sheet":"south-africa","slug":"franschhoek","summary":"Franschhoek, the French corner, is the valley the Huguenot refugees settled from 1688, east of Stellenbosch and south-east of Paarl, closed on three sides by mountains. Its farms still carry the names of French villages, its restaurants made it the Cape's dining town, and its wine is varied rather than one thing: Sémillon from some of the Cape's oldest vines, Cabernet and Shiraz on the mountain slopes, Chardonnay and Sauvignon Blanc on the cooler ground, and a concentration of bottle-fermented sparkling houses. It was a ward of Paarl until 2010, when it became the Franschhoek Valley district of the Coastal Region. Boekenhoutskloof is the house the exam knows, and the Berg River rises in its mountains."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Klein Constantia's unfortified sweet wine from Muscat de Frontignan (Muscat Blanc à Petits Grains), late-picked and shrivelled on the vine, then matured in barrel. Revived with the 1986 vintage; sold in a 500 ml replica bottle.","f":"What is Vin de Constance?"},{"b":"Hendrik Cloete, who bought Groot Constantia in 1778; its sweet wine reached Europe's courts. Simon van der Stel had founded the estate in 1685, and it was divided after his death in 1712.","f":"Historic Constantia: who made it famous"},{"b":"Powdery mildew in the mid nineteenth century, a British tariff change in the early 1860s that took away the Cape's price advantage, and from 1886 phylloxera, which ruined the vineyards.","f":"What ended Constantia's first fame"},{"b":"A ward of the Cape Town district, created in 2017, in the Coastal Region, alongside Durbanville, Philadelphia and Hout Bay.","f":"Constantia's place in Wine of Origin"},{"b":"False Bay's south-easter up the valley, the cold Atlantic just over the ridge at Hout Bay, and east-facing slopes that lose the afternoon sun behind the Constantiaberg.","f":"What cools Constantia"},{"b":"Sauvignon Blanc: flinty, green fig, gooseberry, high acidity; often blended with Sémillon for age-worthy Bordeaux-style whites.","f":"Constantia's leading dry grape today"},{"b":"Groot Constantia's revived unfortified sweet wine from red and white Muscat de Frontignan.","f":"Grand Constance: whose, and from what"},{"b":"Napoleon had it shipped to St Helena; Jane Austen named it in Sense and Sensibility; Dickens and Baudelaire wrote of it too.","f":"Historic Constantia's famous admirers"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"lead"},{"name":"Sémillon","role":"blending"},{"name":"Muscat Blanc","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"blending"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"blending"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"minor"}],"index":4,"name":"Constantia","questions":[{"a":2,"cat":"Constantia","exp":"Vin de Constance is made from Muscat de Frontignan, the Cape name for Muscat Blanc à Petits Grains. Muscat of Alexandria is the Cape's Hanepoot, Chenin Blanc is the national white, and Furmint is Tokaji's grape.","id":"r-ksih5whv","opts":["Chenin Blanc","Furmint","Muscat de Frontignan","Muscat of Alexandria"],"q":"Which grape makes Vin de Constance?"},{"accept":["1685","sixteen eighty five","sixteen eighty-five"],"ans":"1685","cat":"Constantia","exp":"Simon van der Stel, then commander of the Cape, received the Constantia grant in 1685 and planted vines; the estate was divided after his death in 1712, giving Groot and Klein Constantia.","id":"r-zc847au6","q":"In which year was Simon van der Stel granted the land at Constantia?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Constantia","exp":"The Muscat is left to shrivel on the vine and the wine is never fortified, so no spirit is added. Noble rot is not the aim, and the Cape is far too warm for ice wine.","id":"r-csiphjgz","opts":["Noble rot on the bunches","Grapes frozen on the vine","Grape spirit added during fermentation","Berries shrivelled on the vine"],"q":"How does Vin de Constance reach its sweetness?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Constantia","exp":"Wine of Origin Cape Town was created in 2017 from four wards: Constantia, Durbanville, Philadelphia and Hout Bay. Stellenbosch, Paarl and Swartland are separate districts of the Coastal Region.","id":"r-5dpiomv5","opts":["Stellenbosch","Cape Town","Paarl","Swartland"],"q":"Which district has held the Constantia ward since 2017?"},{"accept":["Klein Constantia","klein constantia","Klein-Constantia"],"ans":"Klein Constantia","cat":"Constantia","exp":"Klein Constantia, the smaller part of van der Stel's estate, revived the wine in 1986. Groot Constantia, the original farm, makes its own sweet Muscat under the name Grand Constance.","id":"r-rswgddk7","q":"Which estate revived the sweet wine Vin de Constance with the 1986 vintage?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Constantia","exp":"Cool, flinty Sauvignon Blanc is the ward's dry signature. Chenin Blanc leads the Cape as a whole, Riesling is at its best in Elgin, and Muscat of Alexandria, the Cape's Hanepoot, grows in the warm inland valleys for sweet and fortified wine.","id":"r-w22o4czw","opts":["Sauvignon Blanc","Riesling","Muscat of Alexandria","Chenin Blanc"],"q":"Which white grape leads Constantia's dry wines today?"},{"accept":["False Bay","false bay","Valsbaai"],"ans":"False Bay","cat":"Constantia","exp":"False Bay lies a few kilometres to the south-east and cools the east-facing slopes each summer afternoon; the cold Atlantic at Hout Bay sits on the other side of the ridge.","id":"r-72cwe76x","q":"Which bay lies south-east of the Constantia valley and sends the summer south-easter over its vineyards?","sa":1},{"accept":["Sémillon","Semillon"],"ans":"Sémillon","cat":"Constantia","exp":"Sémillon brings wax, lemon and weight to Sauvignon Blanc's acidity, the classic Bordeaux pairing, and the blends gain toast and honey over a decade. A few Constantia farms also bottle Sémillon on its own.","id":"r-aqb9xfuo","q":"Which white grape is Sauvignon Blanc's usual partner in Constantia's age-worthy white blends?","sa":1}],"sheet":"south-africa","slug":"constantia","summary":"Constantia is the Cape's oldest fine wine address: a ward of the Cape Town district on the eastern slopes of the Constantiaberg, a short drive south of the city centre, with False Bay to the south-east and the Atlantic just over the ridge at Hout Bay. Simon van der Stel was granted the land in 1685, and by the late eighteenth century its sweet Muscat wine was poured at Europe's courts. Today the ward is cool, wet by Cape standards and best known for tense, flinty Sauvignon Blanc and Sémillon. The sweet legend returned with the 1986 vintage of Klein Constantia's Vin de Constance: unfortified Muscat de Frontignan, shrivelled on the vine."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Altitude (a basin around 300 metres up), the nearby sea, and summer cloud carried in by the south-easter; harvest comes weeks after Stellenbosch.","f":"Elgin: the three things that make it cool"},{"b":"A district of the Cape South Coast region, which it shares with Walker Bay, Overberg and Cape Agulhas; not part of the Coastal Region.","f":"Elgin's place in Wine of Origin"},{"b":"Apples and pears. Fruit farmers planted vines from the 1980s; the orchards still fill the basin floor.","f":"What Elgin grew before wine"},{"b":"Sauvignon Blanc, Chardonnay and Pinot Noir lead; Riesling and cool-climate Syrah are rising; some Merlot on warmer slopes.","f":"Elgin's grapes to name"},{"b":"Clay-rich Bokkeveld shale on the lower and middle slopes; sandier Table Mountain sandstone on the higher ground.","f":"The rocks under Elgin's vineyards"},{"b":"It carries the road from Somerset West over the mountains into the Elgin basin. The climb explains much of the change from Stellenbosch: more height, cooler days and summer cloud.","f":"Sir Lowry's Pass: why it matters to Elgin"},{"b":"Paul Cluver, the pioneering estate (Riesling, Chardonnay, Pinot Noir); Oak Valley; Iona; Shannon (Mount Bullet Merlot).","f":"Elgin houses to know"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"lead"},{"name":"Riesling","role":"rising"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"rising"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"minor"}],"index":5,"name":"Elgin","questions":[{"a":2,"cat":"Elgin","exp":"Elgin sits in the Cape South Coast region with Walker Bay, Overberg and Cape Agulhas. The Coastal Region holds Stellenbosch and Paarl, the Breede River Valley holds Robertson, and Olifants River lies far to the north.","id":"r-yhzaurea","opts":["Breede River Valley","Olifants River","Cape South Coast","Coastal Region"],"q":"Which Wine of Origin region holds the Elgin district?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Elgin","exp":"Elgin's cool, cloudy summers suit Pinot Noir, which rivals the Hemel-en-Aarde's for perfume and finesse. Pinotage and Cinsault are at their most typical in warmer districts, and late-ripening Cabernet Sauvignon struggles in Elgin's cool years.","id":"r-ab9qhxxb","opts":["Pinot Noir","Pinotage","Cabernet Sauvignon","Cinsault"],"q":"Which red grape is Elgin best known for?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Elgin","exp":"The south-easter carries moist sea air over the mountains and often lays cloud across the basin, cutting sunshine and slowing ripening. Elgin's rain falls in winter, and hail and berg winds are not its mark.","id":"r-h974uia4","opts":["Afternoon cloud cover","Frequent summer hail","Heavy summer rainfall","Hot berg winds"],"q":"Which summer weather feature most sets Elgin apart from Stellenbosch?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Elgin","exp":"Cape Riesling, also sold as South African or Paarl Riesling, is Crouchen Blanc, a French grape. True Riesling, once labelled Weisser or Rhine Riesling, now appears as plain Riesling, as on Elgin's labels.","id":"r-ztva32i7","opts":["Sauvignon Blanc","Chenin Blanc","Weisser Riesling","Crouchen Blanc"],"q":"Which grape does the term Cape Riesling mean on a South African label?"},{"accept":["Riesling","riesling","Rhine Riesling","Weisser Riesling","White Riesling"],"ans":"Riesling","cat":"Elgin","exp":"Elgin's cool, humid autumns favour noble rot on late-picked Riesling, and its Noble Late Harvest Riesling is among the Cape's best-known sweet wines. Elgin is the Cape's leading home for the true grape.","id":"r-qge9r8gi","q":"Which aromatic white grape gives Elgin's best-known Noble Late Harvest wines?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Elgin","exp":"Bokkeveld shale underlies Elgin and the Hemel-en-Aarde. Malmesbury shale is the Swartland's rock, lime-rich soils mark Robertson, and the Cape has no basalt vineyards of note.","id":"r-gdv2iktr","opts":["Basalt","Bokkeveld shale","Malmesbury shale","Limestone"],"q":"Which rock weathers into the clay-rich soils of Elgin's vineyard slopes?"},{"accept":["1980s","the 1980s","1980's","1980","eighties","the eighties"],"ans":"1980s","cat":"Elgin","exp":"Fruit farmers began planting vines in the 1980s, which makes Elgin one of the younger fine wine districts of the Cape.","id":"r-w9gy02sx","q":"In which decade did Elgin's fruit farmers begin planting wine grapes in earnest?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Elgin","exp":"South of the equator the sun tracks across the northern sky, so south-facing slopes receive the least direct sun. Elgin puts Sauvignon Blanc and Pinot Noir there and Syrah on warmer north-facing blocks.","id":"r-xougez85","opts":["North-facing","South-facing","East-facing","West-facing"],"q":"In Elgin, which slope aspect gives the coolest vineyard sites?"}],"sheet":"south-africa","slug":"elgin","summary":"Elgin is the Cape's best-known cool upland district: a high basin of apple and pear orchards over the mountains from Somerset West, reached by Sir Lowry's Pass and lying a short way inland from the sea. Fruit farmers began planting vines in the 1980s, and the district now makes some of the Cape's most nervy Sauvignon Blanc, fine Chardonnay and Pinot Noir, aromatic Riesling and peppery cool-climate Syrah. Altitude, winter rain and summer cloud brought by the south-easter keep the season long and slow, so harvest comes weeks after Stellenbosch. It is a district of the Cape South Coast region, and Paul Cluver, Oak Valley, Iona and Shannon are the names to know."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Hemel-en-Aarde Valley nearest the sea, then Upper Hemel-en-Aarde Valley, then Hemel-en-Aarde Ridge farthest inland; all in the Walker Bay district. The name is Afrikaans for heaven and earth.","f":"Hemel-en-Aarde's three wards, from the sea inland"},{"b":"Hemel-en-Aarde Valley, Upper Hemel-en-Aarde Valley, Hemel-en-Aarde Ridge, Bot River, Sunday's Glen, Stanford Foothills, Springfontein Rim.","f":"Walker Bay's seven wards"},{"b":"Founded in 1975 in the Hemel-en-Aarde Valley; bottles only Pinot Noir and Chardonnay under its name; the house that put the valley on wine lists.","f":"Hamilton Russell Vineyards: why it matters"},{"b":"Cold Walker Bay a few kilometres away, the summer south-easter, and the climb inland onto higher ground in the upper wards.","f":"What cools the Hemel-en-Aarde"},{"b":"Clay-rich Bokkeveld shale in the lower valley for structure; more decomposed granite farther inland for perfume; sandstone on the ridges above.","f":"The soils of the Hemel-en-Aarde"},{"b":"A district of the Cape South Coast region, around Hermanus, with Elgin, Overberg and Cape Agulhas as neighbours.","f":"Walker Bay in Wine of Origin"},{"b":"The south-easter at flowering disrupts fruit set, so yields are low and bunches small, concentrating the Pinot Noir and Chardonnay.","f":"Wind at flowering: why it matters here"},{"b":"A ward of the Cape Agulhas district near Africa's southern tip, east of Walker Bay; windswept Sauvignon Blanc, white blends and Shiraz.","f":"Elim: where, and what"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"lead"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"lead"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"lead"},{"name":"Pinotage","role":"minor"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chenin Blanc","role":"minor"}],"index":6,"name":"Walker Bay / Hemel-en-Aarde","questions":[{"accept":["Heaven and earth","heaven and earth","Heaven and Earth","heaven-and-earth","Heaven-and-Earth"],"ans":"Heaven and earth","cat":"Walker Bay / Hemel-en-Aarde","exp":"Hemel is heaven and aarde is earth; the name belongs to the valley behind Hermanus and to three of Walker Bay's wards, not to any brand.","id":"r-83oe24z9","q":"What does the Afrikaans name Hemel-en-Aarde mean in English?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Walker Bay / Hemel-en-Aarde","exp":"The cool maritime valley made its name with Pinot Noir and Chardonnay from 1975 onward. Chenin and Pinotage are the national grapes, Cabernet and Merlot lead Stellenbosch, and Syrah with Viognier is a Rhône pairing.","id":"r-n5ih5gvy","opts":["Pinot Noir and Chardonnay","Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot","Syrah and Viognier","Chenin Blanc and Pinotage"],"q":"Which two grapes made the Hemel-en-Aarde's reputation?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Walker Bay / Hemel-en-Aarde","exp":"The three Hemel-en-Aarde wards climb inland in order: the Valley nearest Hermanus, the Upper Valley in the middle and the Ridge on the high ground at the head of the valley. Bot River lies to the west, outside the valley.","id":"r-v4uv88vr","opts":["Hemel-en-Aarde Valley","Upper Hemel-en-Aarde Valley","Bot River","Hemel-en-Aarde Ridge"],"q":"Which Walker Bay ward sits at the inland head of the Hemel-en-Aarde valley?"},{"accept":["Hamilton Russell Vineyards","Hamilton Russell","Hamilton-Russell","HRV"],"ans":"Hamilton Russell Vineyards","cat":"Walker Bay / Hemel-en-Aarde","exp":"Hamilton Russell Vineyards, in the Hemel-en-Aarde Valley ward, bottles only Pinot Noir and Chardonnay under its own name and set the Burgundian course that Bouchard Finlayson, Newton Johnson and others followed.","id":"r-7sy77538","q":"Which house, founded in 1975, first proved that the Hemel-en-Aarde could make serious Pinot Noir and Chardonnay?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Walker Bay / Hemel-en-Aarde","exp":"Bokkeveld shale weathers to a stony clay that holds water and gives Pinot Noir structure. Lime-rich soils mark Robertson, the Cape has no volcanic vineyards of note, and alluvial gravel lines the river valleys.","id":"r-1sq5hvof","opts":["Bokkeveld shale","Alluvial gravel","Volcanic basalt","Limestone"],"q":"Which soil gives the lower Hemel-en-Aarde Valley its stony, clay-rich character?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Walker Bay / Hemel-en-Aarde","exp":"Walker Bay shares the Cape South Coast region with Elgin, Overberg and Cape Agulhas. The Coastal Region holds Stellenbosch and Constantia, the Breede River Valley holds Robertson, and the Klein Karoo lies inland.","id":"r-r5od7j3s","opts":["Coastal Region","Klein Karoo","Cape South Coast","Breede River Valley"],"q":"Which Wine of Origin region contains the Walker Bay district?"},{"accept":["Bot River","bot river","Botrivier","botrivier"],"ans":"Bot River","cat":"Walker Bay / Hemel-en-Aarde","exp":"Bot River sits beyond the Houw Hoek pass from Elgin, warmer than the Hemel-en-Aarde and known for old Chenin, Pinotage and Shiraz; it is a Walker Bay ward despite its nearness to Elgin.","id":"r-iuigadyz","q":"Which Walker Bay ward lies farthest west, on the road towards Elgin?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Walker Bay / Hemel-en-Aarde","exp":"Wind at flowering disrupts fruit set, leaving small, loose bunches and naturally low yields. Hail and spring frost are not the valley's problems, and the cool sea air limits sunburn.","id":"r-nv0s6q0f","opts":["Frost damage at budbreak","Poor fruit set at flowering","Hail damage at veraison","Sunburn on the bunches before harvest"],"q":"How does the strong south-easter of spring and summer most affect Hemel-en-Aarde yields?"}],"sheet":"south-africa","slug":"walker-bay","summary":"Walker Bay is the coastal district around Hermanus, the whale-watching town on the bay of that name south-east of Cape Town, and its heart is the Hemel-en-Aarde, the heaven-and-earth valley that climbs inland from the sea. It is the Cape's reference for Pinot Noir and Chardonnay: cool, windy and close to cold water, on clay-rich Bokkeveld shale and decomposed granite. Hamilton Russell Vineyards, founded in 1975, proved the case, and Bouchard Finlayson, Newton Johnson, Creation and Storm followed. The district holds seven wards, three of them carrying the Hemel-en-Aarde name, and sits in the Cape South Coast region."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Lime-rich soils rare in the Cape; Chardonnay and Cap Classique; a hot, dry, irrigated valley cooled by the afternoon south-easter.","f":"Robertson's three markers"},{"b":"A district of the Breede River Valley region, with Worcester and Breedekloof upstream; wards include McGregor, Bonnievale, Klaasvoogds and Eilandia.","f":"Robertson in Wine of Origin"},{"b":"Robertson's Cap Classique reference house; its non-vintage Brut was poured at Nelson Mandela's inauguration in 1994.","f":"Graham Beck: why a sommelier knows it"},{"b":"The Cape's fortified sweet wine from red or white Muscat de Frontignan, with grape spirit added before or early in fermentation; a Robertson and Klein Karoo speciality.","f":"What is Muscadel?"},{"b":"The Breede River and the Brandvlei Dam upstream near Worcester, by canal; rainfall is far too low for dry farming.","f":"Where Robertson's water comes from"},{"b":"The Langeberg to the north, the Riviersonderend range to the south; the Breede runs between them.","f":"The mountains on each side of Robertson"},{"b":"De Wetshof, the lime-soil pioneer; Graham Beck for Cap Classique; Springfield and Weltevrede for still wines.","f":"Robertson Chardonnay houses"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"lead"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"lead"},{"name":"Colombard","role":"blending"},{"name":"Muscat Blanc","role":"lead"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"rising"},{"name":"Chenin Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"minor"}],"index":7,"name":"Robertson","questions":[{"a":1,"cat":"Robertson","exp":"Robertson's lime-rich calcareous soils are scarce elsewhere in the Cape and underpin its Chardonnay. Decomposed granite marks much of Stellenbosch, Malmesbury shale the Swartland, and Table Mountain sandstone the high mountain slopes.","id":"r-nyk1m6ss","opts":["Decomposed granite","Calcareous soil","Table Mountain sandstone","Malmesbury shale"],"q":"Which soil type sets Robertson apart from most Cape districts?"},{"accept":["Breede River","breede river","Breede","breede","Breerivier","Breë River"],"ans":"Breede River","cat":"Robertson","exp":"Canals from the Breede and the Brandvlei Dam upstream water the valley; the river also channels the cooling south-easter inland from the south coast.","id":"r-7iylkkxd","q":"Which river runs through the Robertson district and supplies its irrigation?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Robertson","exp":"Robertson shares the Breede River Valley region with Worcester and Breedekloof. The Coastal Region holds Stellenbosch, the Cape South Coast holds Elgin and Walker Bay, and the Klein Karoo is a separate region farther east.","id":"r-frp5yeou","opts":["Coastal Region","Klein Karoo","Breede River Valley","Cape South Coast"],"q":"Which Wine of Origin region holds the Robertson district?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Robertson","exp":"Chardonnay, still and as Cap Classique base, is the district's calling card. Pinotage is Stellenbosch's crossing, Chenin is grown everywhere, and Cabernet leads the coastal districts.","id":"r-xhjejyrn","opts":["Chenin Blanc","Pinotage","Cabernet Sauvignon","Chardonnay"],"q":"Which grape is Robertson's signature on its lime-rich slopes?"},{"accept":["Graham Beck","graham beck","Graham Beck Wines"],"ans":"Graham Beck","cat":"Robertson","exp":"Graham Beck is Robertson's Cap Classique reference, making bottle-fermented sparkling wine from Chardonnay and Pinot Noir grown on the valley's lime-rich soils.","id":"r-fh1prv9h","q":"Which Robertson house's non-vintage Brut was poured at Nelson Mandela's 1994 inauguration?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Robertson","exp":"Grape spirit added to the sweet must stops or prevents fermentation, so the Muscat's sugar stays and the wine is very sweet. Straw drying is the passito method, botrytis makes Noble Late Harvest, and bottle fermentation makes Cap Classique.","id":"r-6z61kwsr","opts":["Grape spirit added to the must","Second fermentation in bottle","Grapes dried on straw mats","Botrytis-affected grapes fermented dry"],"q":"How is Robertson's Muscadel made?"},{"accept":["Cap Classique","Méthode Cap Classique","Methode Cap Classique","MCC"],"ans":"Cap Classique","cat":"Robertson","exp":"Cap Classique, also written Méthode Cap Classique, is the Cape's term for traditional-method sparkling wine, with its second fermentation in the bottle; Chardonnay and Pinot Noir from Robertson's lime soils make some of the best.","id":"r-cez945hp","q":"Which term do South African labels use for the bottle-fermented sparkling wine that is Robertson's best-known style abroad?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Robertson","exp":"The south-easter is drawn up the Breede valley from the south coast and tempers the afternoon heat. Berg winds bring heat, the north-wester brings winter rain, and the Atlantic breeze cools the west-coast districts, far beyond Robertson's mountains.","id":"r-6df09tb1","opts":["The south-easter up the valley","The north-wester before winter rain","The Atlantic breeze off the west coast","The berg wind from the interior"],"q":"Which wind cools Robertson's vineyards on summer afternoons?"}],"sheet":"south-africa","slug":"robertson","summary":"Robertson is the warm inland district of the Breede River Valley, east of Cape Town beyond Worcester, where vineyards line the river between the Langeberg and the Riviersonderend mountains. It is hot and dry, irrigated from the river, and cooled by an afternoon breeze drawn up the valley from the coast. Its distinction is lime: calcareous soils, rare in the Cape, that give Robertson its name for Chardonnay and Cap Classique, from De Wetshof and Graham Beck above all. The district also grows Sauvignon Blanc, Shiraz, Colombard for brandy and everyday whites, and Red and White Muscadel, its classic fortified wine."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"At least 120 g/L since the 2013 vintage. Five puttonyos is 120 g/L, six is 150 g/L; three and four are gone.","f":"Aszú: the modern sugar floor and the surviving puttonyos"},{"b":"136 litres. Puttonyos once counted the hods of aszú paste, 20 to 25 kg each, steeped in one gönci barrel of base wine.","f":"The gönci barrel: its size and its part in the puttonyos"},{"b":"Free run juice of aszú berries, at least 450 g/L of sugar, a few per cent of alcohol, served by the spoon; outside the puttonyos scale.","f":"Eszencia: what it is and its legal floor"},{"b":"Polish for as it comes: whole unsorted bunches pressed together. Száraz is dry and oxidative; édes is sweet, 45 g/L or more.","f":"Szamorodni: meaning, language and two styles"},{"b":"Furmint, Hárslevelű, Sárga Muskotály, Zéta, Kövérszőlő and Kabar. Furmint leads, Hárslevelű is the partner.","f":"Tokaj's six permitted grapes"},{"b":"Volcanic and loess. Rhyolite and andesite tuff weather to nyirok, a red clay; loess caps Tokaj Hill at Tarcal and Tokaj.","f":"Tokaj's soils in two words, and the clay's name"},{"b":"A royal decree of 1737 closed the Tokaj district. The cultural landscape was listed in 2002.","f":"Two dates: the closing decree and the UNESCO listing"},{"b":"Fordítás, a turning: must or wine steeped on aszú paste already pressed once. Máslás, a copy: wine fermented on the lees of an Aszú.","f":"Fordítás and Máslás: the difference"},{"b":"The Bodrog joins the Tisza at Tokaj town; the Zemplén hills rise behind. The rivers' mist breeds noble rot.","f":"The two rivers and the hills of Tokaj"},{"b":"Eighteen months in oak and at least 9% alcohol, under the rules in force since the 2013 vintage.","f":"Aszú's minimum oak time and minimum alcohol"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Furmint","role":"lead"},{"name":"Hárslevelű","role":"blending"},{"name":"Sárga Muskotály","role":"minor"},{"name":"Zéta","role":"minor"},{"name":"Kövérszőlő","role":"minor"},{"name":"Kabar","role":"minor"}],"index":0,"name":"Tokaj","questions":[{"a":3,"cat":"Tokaj","exp":"Furmint fills about two thirds of Tokaj and gives Aszú its acid spine. Hárslevelű is the perfumed partner, Sárga Muskotály a seasoning, and Zéta a botrytis prone cross used mostly for aszú berries.","id":"r-qfbmdaqs","opts":["Hárslevelű","Zéta","Sárga Muskotály","Furmint"],"q":"Which grape is the principal variety of Tokaji Aszú?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Tokaj","exp":"The reform set the floor at 120 g/L, the old five puttonyos level, and retired three (60 g/L) and four (90 g/L). Six puttonyos remains at 150 g/L.","id":"r-45r6ruz5","opts":["90 g/L","120 g/L","150 g/L","60 g/L"],"q":"Since the 2013 vintage, what is the minimum residual sugar of a Tokaji Aszú?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Tokaj","exp":"Szamorodni is Polish for as it comes: whole bunches pressed unsorted, botrytised and healthy berries together, so the wine may be dry or sweet. Aszú is the shrivelled berry and Fordítás the turning.","id":"r-3648li2q","opts":["As it comes","Turned over","Shrivelled berry","Noble sweet"],"q":"What does the word Szamorodni mean on a Tokaji label?"},{"accept":["puttony","puttonyos","putton","puttonyok"],"ans":"puttony","cat":"Tokaj","exp":"A puttony held 20 to 25 kilos of aszú berries, and the number added to a 136 litre gönci barrel of base wine gave the puttonyos; today the number marks a sugar band.","id":"r-d29aiwsf","q":"What was the name of the hod in which aszú berries were traditionally measured?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Tokaj","exp":"The Bodrog joins the Tisza at Tokaj, and the mist off their confluence brings noble rot. The Danube meets the Tisza far to the south in Serbia, and the Hernád and Drava are elsewhere.","id":"r-ul1ol6o2","opts":["Bodrog and Tisza","Danube and Drava","Bodrog and Hernád","Danube and Tisza"],"q":"Which two rivers meet at the town of Tokaj?"},{"accept":["Eszencia","Essencia","Tokaji Eszencia","Esszencia","Essence"],"ans":"Eszencia","cat":"Tokaj","exp":"Eszencia drains from the heaped aszú berries under their own weight, carries at least 450 g/L of sugar and reaches only a few per cent of alcohol; it stands outside the puttonyos scale.","id":"r-l8qxtl3n","q":"Which Tokaji wine is the free run juice of aszú berries, so sweet it barely ferments?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Tokaj","exp":"The 2013 reform set eighteen months in oak as the floor, replacing the old two years in barrel and one in bottle; the cellars are tunnels cut into tuff.","id":"r-64vkipuc","opts":["6 months","18 months","12 months","36 months"],"q":"What is the minimum period a Tokaji Aszú must spend in oak?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Tokaj","exp":"Both sit on the loess of Tokaj Hill at Tarcal and were ranked above the first class in the eighteenth century. Mád holds Szent Tamás and Király, Tolcsva Szentvér, and Tállya is the cool north west arm.","id":"r-wn2a2hqr","opts":["Tolcsva","Mád","Tarcal","Tállya"],"q":"In which village do the historic vineyards Mézes Mály and Szarvas lie?"}],"sheet":"hungary","slug":"tokaj","summary":"Tokaj is Hungary's great sweet wine district and the one the exam asks hardest. It lies in the north east where the Bodrog meets the Tisza under the Zemplén hills, on volcanic rock and loess, and autumn mists off the rivers breed noble rot on Furmint and Hárslevelű. Aszú berries are picked one by one, kneaded into a paste and steeped in base wine; the finished wine must hold at least 120 g/L of sugar and spend eighteen months in oak, and is labelled five or six puttonyos. Eszencia is the free run syrup of the berries, Szamorodni the unsorted bunch. Since the 1990s the same vineyards have made dry Furmint of real class."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"At least three varieties from a fixed list; Kékfrankos compulsory at 30 per cent or more; at least six months in oak; reserved to Eger and Szekszárd.","f":"Egri Bikavér: the blend rule in one breath"},{"b":"Bull's Blood. The 1552 siege of Eger: the Turks saw red wine in the defenders' beards and feared they drank bull's blood.","f":"Bikavér: what the name means and the legend's date"},{"b":"Classicus, Superior, Grand Superior. Each step raises ripeness, cuts yield and lengthens oak; Grand Superior is a single named dűlő.","f":"Eger's three tiers, in order"},{"b":"The Star of Eger, created 2010: dry white blend of at least four varieties, at least half Carpathian basin grapes, no variety above half.","f":"Egri Csillag: the year and the rule"},{"b":"The Bükk mountains; rhyolite tuff of the Bükkalja; the Szépasszony-völgy, Valley of the Beautiful Woman, with its cellar rows.","f":"The mountain, the rock and the cellar valley of Eger"},{"b":"A limestone hill above Eger, one of Hungary's highest vineyard hills, the leading Grand Superior site for Bikavér and Kékfrankos.","f":"Nagy-Eged: why it matters"},{"b":"The south western corner of the Eger district at the foot of the Mátra, moved over from the Mátra district after 1989; Debrői Hárslevelű, an off dry white.","f":"Debrő: where it is and what it makes"},{"b":"Kékoportó is Portugieser. Leányka, little girl, is Fetească Albă; Királyleányka is Fetească Regală.","f":"Kékoportó and Leányka: their other names"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Kékfrankos","role":"lead"},{"name":"Kadarka","role":"minor"},{"name":"Kékoportó","role":"minor"},{"name":"Zweigelt","role":"blending"},{"name":"Cabernet Franc","role":"blending"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"blending"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"blending"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"rising"},{"name":"Olaszrizling","role":"blending"},{"name":"Leányka","role":"minor"},{"name":"Hárslevelű","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"}],"index":1,"name":"Eger","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Eger","exp":"Kékfrankos must make up at least 30 per cent of the blend. Kadarka, Zweigelt and Cabernet Franc are on the permitted list but none is required.","id":"r-og9bygpd","opts":["Kékfrankos","Kadarka","Zweigelt","Cabernet Franc"],"q":"Which grape variety is compulsory in an Egri Bikavér blend?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Eger","exp":"The Eger rules set a floor of 30 per cent for Kékfrankos in every tier of Bikavér, with ceilings that stop any one variety from turning the blend into a varietal.","id":"r-4hr546ot","opts":["50 per cent","65 per cent","15 per cent","30 per cent"],"q":"What is the minimum share of Kékfrankos required in an Egri Bikavér?"},{"accept":["Bull's Blood","bulls blood","bull blood","blood of the bull","bull s blood"],"ans":"Bull's Blood","cat":"Eger","exp":"Bika is bull and vér is blood. The legend ties the name to the 1552 siege of Eger, when the Turks took the red wine on the defenders' beards for bull's blood.","id":"r-1uhepfrd","q":"What does the name Bikavér mean in English?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Eger","exp":"Egri Csillag, the Star of Eger, was created in 2010 as the white counterpart of Bikavér: at least four varieties, half of them Carpathian basin grapes. The others are varietal or historic names.","id":"r-rwsf07fv","opts":["Debrői Hárslevelű","Egri Olaszrizling","Egri Csillag","Egri Leányka"],"q":"Which white wine of Eger is a regulated blend created in 2010?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Eger","exp":"Eger's ladder runs Classicus, Superior, Grand Superior, the last from a single named dűlő. Prémium belongs to Villány's ladder, not Eger's.","id":"r-rg55l4oz","opts":["Grand Superior","Classicus","Prémium","Superior"],"q":"Which is the base tier of the Eger classification?"},{"accept":["6 months","six months","6","six","half a year"],"ans":"6 months","cat":"Eger","exp":"Classicus, the base tier of Egri Bikavér, must spend at least six months in oak; Superior and Grand Superior ask at least twelve months and further bottle age before release.","id":"r-yv6mfl3n","q":"What is the minimum period in oak for a Classicus Egri Bikavér?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Eger","exp":"Eger lies on the Bükkalja, the southern foothills of the Bükk. The Mátra is the district to the west, the Zemplén rises over Tokaj, and the Bakony stands north of Lake Balaton.","id":"r-iupia9re","opts":["Bükk","Bakony","Zemplén","Mátra"],"q":"Which mountain range shelters the Eger district from the north?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Eger","exp":"The Bükkalja is rhyolite tuff, a soft volcanic rock easily tunnelled and steady in temperature. Basalt belongs to Badacsony and Somló, Leitha limestone to Sopron's lakeside, and sand to the plain.","id":"r-82lu3onk","opts":["Pannonian sand","Rhyolite tuff","Leitha limestone","Basalt"],"q":"Into which rock are the cellars of Eger's Szépasszony-völgy cut?"}],"sheet":"hungary","slug":"eger","summary":"Eger is the red wine town of northern Hungary, on the southern foothills of the Bükk, south west of Tokaj. Its name belongs to Egri Bikavér, Bull's Blood, a blend of at least three varieties on a compulsory backbone of Kékfrankos, and since 2010 to Egri Csillag, the Star of Eger, its white counterpart. The soils are rhyolite tuff, clay and loess, the cellars are cut into the tuff, and the climate is cool for Hungarian red, which keeps the wines fresh, peppery and medium bodied rather than big. Pinot Noir does well here too. The ladder is Classicus, Superior and Grand Superior, rising in ripeness, oak and precision of site."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Southern foothills of the Mátra mountains around Gyöngyös, west of Eger; a white wine district of crisp, aromatic, early drinking wines.","f":"The Mátra: where, and what colour of wine"},{"b":"Pinot Gris, Müller-Thurgau, Gewürztraminer.","f":"Szürkebarát, Rizlingszilváni, Tramini: the international names"},{"b":"Olaszrizling is Welschriesling, unrelated to Riesling. Ottonel Muskotály is Muscat Ottonel, the district's aromatic signature.","f":"Olaszrizling and Ottonel Muskotály: what they are"},{"b":"Mátraalja, the foot of the Mátra. The Debrő corner, with its Hárslevelű, moved to the Eger district when the districts were redrawn after 1989.","f":"The Mátra's old name and the village corner it lost"},{"b":"Loess and brown forest soil over volcanic andesite; the mountain shelters the south facing foothills.","f":"Mátra soils in one line"},{"b":"Hungary's highest peak, in the Mátra range, just over a thousand metres; the vines stop far below it.","f":"Kékes: what and how high"},{"b":"A muscat scented Hungarian cross of 1930: light, very aromatic, low in alcohol, drunk young and cold.","f":"Irsai Olivér: what kind of wine"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Olaszrizling","role":"lead"},{"name":"Rizlingszilváni","role":"blending"},{"name":"Szürkebarát","role":"lead"},{"name":"Ottonel Muskotály","role":"minor"},{"name":"Irsai Olivér","role":"minor"},{"name":"Gewürztraminer","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"blending"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"rising"},{"name":"Kékfrankos","role":"minor"},{"name":"Zweigelt","role":"minor"},{"name":"Kadarka","role":"minor"}],"index":2,"name":"Mátra","questions":[{"a":2,"cat":"Mátra","exp":"Szürkebarát means grey monk and is Pinot Gris; it makes a light dry white in the Mátra and a fuller one on the Balaton. Pinot Blanc, Chardonnay and Sylvaner are separate varieties.","id":"r-zbh34c1n","opts":["Chardonnay","Sylvaner","Pinot Gris","Pinot Blanc"],"q":"Szürkebarát, a grape of the Mátra, is the Hungarian name for which variety?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Mátra","exp":"Rizlingszilváni is Müller-Thurgau, the old Riesling and Sylvaner cross. Welschriesling is Olaszrizling, and true Riesling is Rajnai Rizling in Hungary.","id":"r-sh6zt3zx","opts":["Müller-Thurgau","Riesling","Welschriesling","Sylvaner"],"q":"Rizlingszilváni, widely planted in the Mátra, is which grape?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Mátra","exp":"Gyöngyös sits at the foot of the Mátra range and holds the district's largest wineries. Eger is the neighbouring district to the east, Miskolc the city of the Bükk, Kecskemét the market town of the Kunság plain.","id":"r-ki0favup","opts":["Gyöngyös","Miskolc","Kecskemét","Eger"],"q":"Which town is the centre of the Mátra wine district?"},{"accept":["Mátraalja","Matraalja","Mátraalji","Matraalji"],"ans":"Mátraalja","cat":"Mátra","exp":"Mátraalja means the foot of the Mátra. The shortening came in the same reorganisation that moved the Debrő corner from the Mátra to Eger.","id":"r-jrqd23z6","q":"What was the Mátra wine district called before its name was shortened?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Mátra","exp":"The Mátra is a white wine district built on Olaszrizling, Szürkebarát, Rizlingszilváni and the Muscats; Kékfrankos and Zweigelt are a minority, often as rosé.","id":"r-cmpwyvry","opts":["Sparkling","White","Rosé","Red"],"q":"Which colour of wine makes up most of the Mátra's production?"},{"accept":["Felső-Magyarország","Felso-Magyarorszag","Felso Magyarorszag","Upper Hungary","Felső-Magyarországi","Felso-Magyarorszagi"],"ans":"Felső-Magyarország","cat":"Mátra","exp":"Felső-Magyarország, Upper Hungary, groups the Mátra with Eger and Bükk, the three districts of the northern hills; Sopron belongs to Felső-Pannon and Badacsony to the Balaton region.","id":"r-hwnv37q8","q":"To which of Hungary's six larger wine regions does the Mátra district belong?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Mátra","exp":"Irsai Olivér was bred in 1930 and is drunk young and cold for its muscat scent. Cserszegi Fűszeres is a later cross from Irsai Olivér, Zenit a Hungarian white cross, and Zéta a Tokaj cross of Furmint and Bouvier.","id":"r-xjstyzsk","opts":["Zéta","Zenit","Cserszegi Fűszeres","Irsai Olivér"],"q":"Which Hungarian cross, planted in the Mátra for a light aromatic white, dates from 1930?"}],"sheet":"hungary","slug":"matra","summary":"The Mátra is the white wine district on the southern foothills of the Mátra mountains, west of Eger and north east of Budapest, around the town of Gyöngyös. It is one of Hungary's largest districts by area and was long called Mátraalja. The vines stand on loess and brown forest soil over volcanic andesite, sheltered by the mountains, and give crisp, aromatic whites: Olaszrizling, Rizlingszilváni, Szürkebarát, Ottonel Muskotály, Irsai Olivér, Tramini, Chardonnay and Sauvignon Blanc, with Kékfrankos and Zweigelt the reds. The exam wants its synonyms and its place on the map more than any single wine."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Far western Hungary at the foot of the Alps, on Lake Fertő, the Neusiedlersee; Burgenland lies on the far shore.","f":"Sopron: where, and across which lake from which Austrian region"},{"b":"Kékfrankos: Blaufränkisch in Austria, Lemberger in Germany. Sopron is called the Kékfrankos capital.","f":"Sopron's signature grape and its two other names"},{"b":"Gneiss and mica schist in the Sopron Hills, the Alps' last spur; loess, clay and Leitha limestone towards the lake.","f":"Sopron's soils: hills and lakeside"},{"b":"German speaking vintners who planted beans between the vines, from Bohnenzüchter, bean grower; their cellars line the old town.","f":"The Poncichter of Sopron"},{"b":"1921, the plebiscite that kept Sopron in Hungary. 2001, the UNESCO listing of the Fertő and Neusiedlersee landscape.","f":"Two dates for Sopron: the vote and the lake"},{"b":"Cool, wet and windy, the Alps' edge open to Atlantic weather, the shallow lake softening frost and holding autumn warmth.","f":"Sopron's climate in one line"},{"b":"Felső-Pannon, Upper Pannon, with Neszmély, Etyek-Buda, Mór and Pannonhalma.","f":"Sopron's wine region grouping and its companions"},{"b":"An Austrian cross of 1922, Blaufränkisch and St. Laurent; soft, plummy, a partner to Kékfrankos at Sopron.","f":"Zweigelt: the parentage"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Kékfrankos","role":"lead"},{"name":"Zweigelt","role":"blending"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"rising"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"blending"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"minor"},{"name":"Cabernet Franc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"minor"},{"name":"Grüner Veltliner","role":"blending"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Gewürztraminer","role":"minor"},{"name":"Leányka","role":"minor"}],"index":3,"name":"Sopron","questions":[{"a":2,"cat":"Sopron","exp":"Sopron is called the capital of Kékfrankos, the grape Austria grows as Blaufränkisch across Lake Fertő. Zweigelt and Pinot Noir are secondary here and Kadarka belongs to the south.","id":"r-lr60u93u","opts":["Pinot Noir","Kadarka","Kékfrankos","Zweigelt"],"q":"Which grape variety is the signature of the Sopron wine district?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Sopron","exp":"Fertő and Neusiedlersee are one shallow steppe lake shared by Hungary and Austria, with Sopron on the Hungarian shore and Burgenland on the other. The other three are Alpine lakes in Carinthia.","id":"r-py09efth","opts":["Ossiachersee","Neusiedlersee","Millstätter See","Wörthersee"],"q":"By which name is Lake Fertő known in Austria?"},{"accept":["Grüner Veltliner","Gruner Veltliner","Gruener Veltliner","Grüner","Gruner"],"ans":"Grüner Veltliner","cat":"Sopron","exp":"Zöld is green and Veltelini is Veltliner: the grape Austria grows as Grüner Veltliner across the lake, crisp and white peppered on the Hungarian shore too. Roter Veltliner is a different grape.","id":"r-o8m95r62","q":"Zöld Veltelini, a white grape of Sopron, is the Hungarian name for which variety?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Sopron","exp":"Sopron sits against the Austrian border, with Burgenland across the lake and over the hills; Slovakia lies to the north, Slovenia and Croatia to the south.","id":"r-pgzs27cs","opts":["Slovakia","Croatia","Slovenia","Austria"],"q":"Which country lies directly across the border from the Sopron wine district?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Sopron","exp":"The Sopron Hills are the easternmost spur of the Alps, crystalline gneiss and mica schist. Basalt belongs to Badacsony and Somló, loess and sand to the plain, limestone and red sandstone to Csopak on the Balaton; Leitha limestone lies only by the lake at Fertőrákos.","id":"r-06wib73a","opts":["Limestone and red sandstone","Basalt and tuff","Gneiss and mica schist","Loess and sand"],"q":"Which rocks make up the Sopron Hills behind the town?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Sopron","exp":"Kőszeg, under the Kőszeg Hills on the Austrian border, belongs to the Sopron district though it lies fifty kilometres south. Szombathely, Mosonmagyaróvár and Győr are towns of western Hungary outside the district.","id":"r-dhbq1r8z","opts":["Mosonmagyaróvár","Kőszeg","Győr","Szombathely"],"q":"Which historic walled town fifty kilometres south of Sopron is an outlying part of the Sopron wine district?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Sopron","exp":"Felső-Pannon, Upper Pannon, groups Sopron with Neszmély, Etyek-Buda, Mór and Pannonhalma. Felső-Magyarország is Eger, Mátra and Bükk; Pannon is Villány, Szekszárd, Pécs and Tolna.","id":"r-csubeaq9","opts":["Felső-Pannon","Balaton","Pannon","Felső-Magyarország"],"q":"To which of Hungary's larger wine regions does the Sopron district belong?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Sopron","exp":"Zweigelt was bred in Austria in 1922 from Blaufränkisch and St. Laurent, which is why it sits so naturally beside Kékfrankos on both shores of the lake.","id":"r-bss6rrz6","opts":["Blaufränkisch and St. Laurent","Portugieser and St. Laurent","Blaufränkisch and Pinot Noir","Blaufränkisch and Cabernet Sauvignon"],"q":"Zweigelt, Sopron's second red grape, is a cross of which two varieties?"}],"sheet":"hungary","slug":"sopron","summary":"Sopron is Hungary's far western red wine district, a historic town between the last hills of the Alps and the shallow steppe lake the Hungarians call Fertő and the Austrians Neusiedlersee, with Burgenland on the far shore. It is the capital of Kékfrankos: the same grape Austria grows as Blaufränkisch across the water, here peppery, bright and medium bodied in one of the coolest and rainiest climates in the country. Zweigelt, Pinot Noir and the Bordeaux varieties follow, with crisp Zöld Veltelini and Chardonnay among the whites. The soils are gneiss and mica schist in the hills, loess and Leitha limestone towards the lake. The exam wants the lake, the grape and its synonym, and the border."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Northern shore of Lake Balaton, a flat topped basalt capped witness hill of 437 metres over Pannonian sand and clay.","f":"Badacsony: where, and what kind of hill"},{"b":"Blue stalk. Functionally female, so it sets fruit only beside a pollinator, traditionally Budai Zöld. Lean, mineral, high acid, revived since the 1990s.","f":"Kéknyelű: meaning, flowering and its pollinator"},{"b":"Olaszrizling is Welschriesling; Rizlingszilváni is Müller-Thurgau; Rajnai Rizling is true Riesling.","f":"The three Rieslings of the Balaton that are not one grape"},{"b":"Pinot Gris. The historic Badacsonyi Szürkebarát was full, honeyed and off dry; the modern wine is usually dry.","f":"Szürkebarát on Badacsony: the grape and the old style"},{"b":"Badacsony, Balatonfüred-Csopak, Balaton-felvidék, Balatonboglár, Zala and Nagy-Somló.","f":"The six districts of the Balaton wine region"},{"b":"It reflects sunlight onto the south facing slopes, holds warmth into autumn, buffers frost and breathes humidity that can bring noble rot.","f":"Why the lake matters to the northern shore"},{"b":"Olaszrizling on red sandstone and limestone east along the shore, under a growers' charter stricter than the district rules.","f":"Csopak: the grape and the charter"},{"b":"The south shore district, loess and sand, warmer; the base of much of Hungary's sparkling wine.","f":"Balatonboglár: shore and speciality"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Olaszrizling","role":"lead"},{"name":"Szürkebarát","role":"lead"},{"name":"Kéknyelű","role":"minor"},{"name":"Riesling","role":"minor"},{"name":"Furmint","role":"minor"},{"name":"Budai Zöld","role":"minor"},{"name":"Rózsakő","role":"minor"},{"name":"Gewürztraminer","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Kékfrankos","role":"minor"}],"index":4,"name":"Badacsony (Balaton)","questions":[{"a":2,"cat":"Badacsony (Balaton)","exp":"Kéknyelű, blue stalk, is Badacsony's own grape, functionally female and nearly lost in the state era. Juhfark belongs to Somló, Ezerjó to Mór, and Királyleányka is a Transylvanian white grown at Eger.","id":"r-3ybrcx22","opts":["Királyleányka","Ezerjó","Kéknyelű","Juhfark"],"q":"Which rare native white grape is the pride of Badacsony?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Badacsony (Balaton)","exp":"Badacsony is a basalt capped volcanic stump over soft Pannonian sand and clay, with columns of basalt on its flanks. Rhyolite tuff belongs to Tokaj and Eger, schist to Sopron.","id":"r-j09qsfdu","opts":["Basalt","Rhyolite","Granite","Schist"],"q":"Which rock caps Badacsony hill and the other witness hills of the Tapolca basin?"},{"accept":["blue stalk","blue-stalk","bluestalk","blue stem","blue stalked"],"ans":"blue stalk","cat":"Badacsony (Balaton)","exp":"Kék is blue and nyél is stalk or handle, after the blue tinge of the bunch stem; the grape's flowers are functionally female and need a pollinator.","id":"r-z9bwm5ie","q":"What does the grape name Kéknyelű mean in English?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Badacsony (Balaton)","exp":"Kéknyelű has functionally female flowers and sets fruit only with a pollinator; Budai Zöld, green of Buda, is the traditional partner, and the cross Rózsakő comes from the pair.","id":"r-3ec7m8x4","opts":["Furmint","Budai Zöld","Olaszrizling","Szürkebarát"],"q":"Which grape is traditionally planted among Kéknyelű to pollinate it?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Badacsony (Balaton)","exp":"Badacsony, Balatonfüred-Csopak and Balaton-felvidék line the northern shore; Balatonboglár is the southern shore and Zala lies to the west.","id":"r-9lux4qvc","opts":["Eastern","Western","Southern","Northern"],"q":"On which shore of Lake Balaton does the Badacsony district lie?"},{"accept":["Csopak","Csopaki","Csopak village"],"ans":"Csopak","cat":"Badacsony (Balaton)","exp":"Csopak lies in the Balatonfüred-Csopak district east along the shore, on red Permian sandstone and limestone rather than Badacsony's basalt; its growers' Csopaki Kódex holds yield, site and style tighter than the district rules.","id":"r-31k8yk61","q":"Which village east of Badacsony on the Balaton's northern shore holds its Olaszrizling to a growers' charter stricter than the district rules?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Badacsony (Balaton)","exp":"Rajnai Rizling is Rhine Riesling, the true grape. Olaszrizling is Welschriesling and Rizlingszilváni is Müller-Thurgau, the two false friends the exam likes to set beside it.","id":"r-a2oricmy","opts":["Müller-Thurgau","Riesling","Welschriesling","Sylvaner"],"q":"Rajnai Rizling, grown on Badacsony, is the Hungarian name for which grape?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Badacsony (Balaton)","exp":"Six: Badacsony, Balatonfüred-Csopak, Balaton-felvidék, Balatonboglár, Zala and Nagy-Somló, the last a separate hill to the north west of the lake.","id":"r-kpclxbv3","opts":["Six","Five","Eight","Four"],"q":"How many wine districts make up the Balaton wine region?"}],"sheet":"hungary","slug":"badacsony","summary":"Badacsony is a flat topped basalt hill on the northern shore of Lake Balaton, the largest lake in Central Europe, and the name of the white wine district around it. Basalt and basalt tuff cap Pannonian sand and clay, the lake throws light and warmth back onto the south facing slopes, and the wines are fuller and rounder than Hungary's other volcanic whites: Olaszrizling for everyday, Szürkebarát as the historic rich style, Rajnai Rizling and Furmint for structure, and Kéknyelű, the rare native blue stalk, as the district's own treasure. Badacsony is one of six districts in the Balaton region, with Csopak's Olaszrizling to the east and the south shore's Balatonboglár across the water."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"A single extinct volcano, a basalt-capped butte, standing alone in western Hungary north-west of Lake Balaton; the country's smallest wine district.","f":"Somló: what kind of landform, and where?"},{"b":"Juhfark, sheep's tail, for its long tight bunch. A white grape: high acid, smoky, saline, slow to open.","f":"Somló's signature grape, and what its name means"},{"b":"Juhfark, Furmint, Hárslevelű and Olaszrizling.","f":"The four whites of the traditional Somló blend"},{"b":"Basalt cap, basalt tuff and ash, then Pannonian sand and clay; loess at the foot.","f":"Somló's soils, top to bottom"},{"b":"Balaton. The district's administrative name is Nagy-Somló; labels say Somlói.","f":"The wine region (borrégió) that holds Somló"},{"b":"The Habsburg court drank it and called it the wine of the wedding night, said to favour a male heir.","f":"The Somló legend worth one sentence"},{"b":"Dry, unbotrytised, flinty and smoky; also the base of the hill's traditional-method sparkling wine.","f":"Furmint on Somló: how does it differ from Tokaj?"},{"b":"Ság hill near Celldömölk and Kissomlyó, both small basalt volcanoes to the north-west.","f":"Other hills inside the Nagy-Somló district"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Juhfark","role":"lead"},{"name":"Furmint","role":"lead"},{"name":"Hárslevelű","role":"blending"},{"name":"Olaszrizling","role":"blending"},{"name":"Tramini","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"}],"index":5,"name":"Somló","questions":[{"a":2,"cat":"Somló","exp":"Juhfark means sheep's tail, after its long tight bunch. Kéknyelű belongs to Badacsony, Ezerjó to Mór, and Leányka is an Eger and Transylvanian white.","id":"r-1qp5r4q6","opts":["Kéknyelű","Ezerjó","Juhfark","Leányka"],"q":"A white grape grown almost nowhere else takes its name from the shape of its bunch and gives the austere, smoky wine of this hill. Which grape?"},{"accept":["Nagy-Somló","nagy somlo","nagy-somlo","nagy somloi","nagysomlo","nagy somlo district"],"ans":"Nagy-Somló","cat":"Somló","exp":"Nagy means great, so the district is Great Somló and takes in the smaller hills of Ság and Kissomlyó as well as the main butte.","id":"r-snt4orw8","q":"The hill's wines are labelled Somlói, but the district carries a longer administrative name. Give it.","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Somló","exp":"Somló is an eroded volcano with a basalt cap over tuff and ash; the growers credit it with the smoke and flint in the wines. Limestone is Villány's rock, granite breaks through at Mórágy near Szekszárd.","id":"r-91aorsq4","opts":["Basalt","Limestone","Granite","Schist"],"q":"What rock forms the cap of the hill and the stony soils of its slopes?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Somló","exp":"Somló sits with Badacsony and the lake districts in the Balaton region, though the hill is well inland of the water. Pannon holds Villány and Szekszárd; Duna holds Kunság.","id":"r-aawvugh9","opts":["Pannon","Balaton","Duna","Felső-Magyarország"],"q":"Hungary groups its districts into six wine regions. Which one holds Somló?"},{"accept":["Welschriesling","welschriesling","welsch riesling","welschrisling","welsch rizling"],"ans":"Welschriesling","cat":"Somló","exp":"Olasz means Italian, so the Hungarian name says Italian Riesling, and Austria's Welschriesling says foreign Riesling for the same grape. It is no relation of true Riesling: a neutral, fresh, bitter-almond white for early drinking.","id":"r-uco7y4rj","q":"Olaszrizling, the blending white of Somló's old field blends, is grown in Austria under which name?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Somló","exp":"Száraz is dry; félszáraz is off-dry, félédes medium sweet and édes sweet. Somló makes its Furmint dry and without botrytis, so száraz is the word a guest sees most often on the hill's labels.","id":"r-r5j1brco","opts":["Dry","Medium sweet","Sweet","Off-dry"],"q":"What does the word száraz mean on the label of a Somlói Furmint?"},{"accept":["Furmint","furmint grape","somloi furmint","somló furmint"],"ans":"Furmint","cat":"Somló","exp":"Furmint's high acid and modest fruit make a natural sparkling base, and on Somló it is grown dry with no botrytis. Juhfark is the still-wine signature; the bubbles lean on Furmint.","id":"r-qok7k0lx","q":"Which grape forms the base of the traditional-method sparkling wine that modern Somló estates have made a speciality?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Somló","exp":"Juhfark is neutral and hard in youth, all acid and volcanic smoke, and only with years does honey and beeswax arrive. Lychee is Gewürztraminer's mark, blackcurrant Cabernet's, petrol aged Riesling's.","id":"r-20spmetz","opts":["Petrol, lime zest and honey","Lychee, rose petal and ginger","Blackcurrant, cedar and tobacco","Smoke, flint and high acid"],"q":"A guest orders a young Somlói Juhfark. What is the wine most likely to show?"}],"sheet":"hungary","slug":"somlo","summary":"Somló is a single extinct volcano standing alone on the flat country of western Hungary, north-west of Lake Balaton, and it is the smallest wine district in the country. The hill is white wine almost entirely: Juhfark, the grape named for its sheep's-tail bunch, with Furmint, Hárslevelű and Olaszrizling beside it. The wines are lean and high in acid, smoky and saline rather than fruity, and they open slowly over years. The fame is old: the Habsburg court drank the hill's wine, and legend made it the wine of the wedding night. The protected name on a label is Somlói; the district's administrative name is Nagy-Somló."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"At least four varieties; Kékfrankos at least 45 per cent; Kadarka at least 5 per cent and compulsory; the rest from the permitted list; at least a year in wood.","f":"Szekszárdi Bikavér: the blend rule in one breath"},{"b":"Deep wind-blown loess over red clay: warm, well drained, soft tannins and full body. Granite breaks through only at Mórágy.","f":"Szekszárd's soil and what it gives"},{"b":"Eger and Szekszárd. Eger's is volcanic and cooler; Szekszárd's is loess and warmer, with Kadarka compulsory.","f":"The two Hungarian districts allowed to label Bikavér"},{"b":"The old native red: pale, light-bodied, peppery and raspberry-scented, thin-skinned and rot-prone, so hard to grow and nearly lost.","f":"Describe Kadarka in a sentence"},{"b":"Szekszárd's siller: a short-macerated light red between rosé and red, named for its fox colour, drunk young and cool.","f":"What is fuxli?"},{"b":"Tolna county, on loess hills west of the Danube and the Sárköz plain; Pannon wine region with Villány, Pécs and Tolna.","f":"Szekszárd: county, river, wine region"},{"b":"Iván-völgy, Görögszó, Bati-kereszt, Baranya-völgy and Porkoláb-völgy: named sites above the town, the district's crus in all but law.","f":"Szekszárd's famous valley vineyards"},{"b":"Szekszárd: loess, warmer, rounder, Kadarka required. Eger: volcanic tuff, cooler, higher acid, Kékfrankos minimum lower, Kadarka optional.","f":"Szekszárd against Eger in two lines"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Kékfrankos","role":"lead"},{"name":"Kadarka","role":"rising"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"blending"},{"name":"Cabernet Franc","role":"blending"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"blending"},{"name":"Zweigelt","role":"minor"},{"name":"Olaszrizling","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"minor"}],"index":6,"name":"Szekszárd","questions":[{"accept":["Kadarka","kadarka grape","kadarka variety"],"ans":"Kadarka","cat":"Szekszárd","exp":"Szekszárd wrote its native red into the rule at a minimum of five per cent, where Eger lists it among the options. Kékfrankos is the frame in both.","id":"r-ypmu5jb1","q":"One grape is compulsory in Szekszárdi Bikavér but only permitted in Egri Bikavér. Name it.","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Szekszárd","exp":"Four is the floor, with Kékfrankos the largest share and Kadarka compulsory. Eger fixes its own numbers for Egri Bikavér, so learn the two rules apart rather than as one.","id":"r-8k2id5pm","opts":["Four","Five","Six","Three"],"q":"How many grape varieties, at the least, must go into a Szekszárdi Bikavér?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Szekszárd","exp":"Wind-blown loess lies tens of metres deep over red clay. Basalt is Somló's and Badacsony's rock, limestone underlies Villány, and granite shows only at Mórágy in the district's south.","id":"r-b7dp73xt","opts":["Loess","Granite","Basalt","Limestone"],"q":"What soil covers the Szekszárd hills and gives the reds their soft, full frame?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Szekszárd","exp":"Pannon takes in Szekszárd, Villány, Pécs and Tolna in the south of Transdanubia. Duna is the sandy plain; Felső-Magyarország is Eger, Mátra and Bükk.","id":"r-kqcrwhe5","opts":["Balaton","Duna","Pannon","Felső-Magyarország"],"q":"Hungary groups its wine districts into six regions. Which one holds Szekszárd?"},{"accept":["Fuxli","fuxli","fuchsli","siller","szekszardi fuxli"],"ans":"Fuxli","cat":"Szekszárd","exp":"Fuxli, named for its fox colour, is the Szekszárd word for a siller, the Central European light red made with a few hours on the skins and drunk young and cool.","id":"r-ki47ukde","q":"Szekszárd's cellars pour a pale, short-macerated red that sits between rosé and red. What is it called locally?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Szekszárd","exp":"Szekszárd stands where the loess hills meet the Danube's plain; the Sió, draining Balaton, joins the river just to the north. The Tisza bounds Kunság on the far side of the plain, the Drava is the southern border, and the Hernád comes down from Slovakia to the Tisza in the north-east.","id":"r-ojmbs5ec","opts":["The Drava","The Danube","The Tisza","The Hernád"],"q":"The Sárköz plain east of the town is the old flood plain of which river?"},{"accept":["Kékfrankos","kekfrankos","kek frankos","blaufrankisch","blaufränkisch","lemberger"],"ans":"Kékfrankos","cat":"Szekszárd","exp":"Kékfrankos is Blaufränkisch under its Hungarian name and the frame of both Bikavérs; Szekszárd sets the higher floor. Kadarka supplies the spice at a minimum of five per cent.","id":"r-g70a9ifd","q":"Which grape must make up the largest share of a Szekszárdi Bikavér, with a minimum of 45 per cent?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Szekszárd","exp":"Kadarka is thin-skinned and gives little colour or tannin, with raspberry and white pepper on lively acid. Deep blackcurrant describes Cabernet; chocolate and vanilla a heavily oaked Merlot.","id":"r-c2zk8jcg","opts":["Deep colour, firm tannin and blackcurrant","Orange tint, high alcohol, nutty","Opaque, soft, chocolate and vanilla","Pale colour, light tannin, peppery spice"],"q":"A guest asks what a varietal Szekszárdi Kadarka will be like. Which description fits?"}],"sheet":"hungary","slug":"szekszard","summary":"Szekszárd is the red-wine town of southern Transdanubia, on loess hills that rise from the west bank of the Danube's flood plain in Tolna county. Kékfrankos leads, Kadarka is the old native red the district has revived, and Merlot and the two Cabernets fill out the blends. Its Bikavér is the only one outside Eger allowed the name, and its rules are stricter on the native grapes. The wines are warmer, rounder and spicier than Eger's, with a southern softness, and rosé and the pale red called fuxli are local habits. It belongs to the Pannon wine region with Villány, Pécs and Tolna."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Hungary's warmest district, far south on a limestone ridge near Croatia, famous for Cabernet Franc and Bordeaux-style reds; Pannon wine region.","f":"Villány in one sentence"},{"b":"Classicus: everyday, highest yields. Prémium: sixty hectolitres per hectare, a year in wood. Super Prémium: thirty-five, at least two years with a year in wood.","f":"Villány's three tiers and their numbers"},{"b":"The district's own designation, first granted for the 2015 harvest: Cabernet Franc alone, made to the Prémium or Super Prémium rules, never blended.","f":"What is Villányi Franc?"},{"b":"Triassic and Jurassic limestone and dolomite under a mantle of loess with pockets of red clay; warmth from the loess, freshness from the rock.","f":"Villány's soils, top to bottom"},{"b":"Portugieser. The old Hungarian name was retired from labels under European naming rules; the grape makes Villány's light, juicy traditional red.","f":"Kékoportó: what is it now?"},{"b":"2006, the first formal quality classification inside a Hungarian district, under the DHC mark; Super Prémium joined with a later revision.","f":"The year Villány adopted its tiers"},{"b":"The Villány-Siklós wine road, opened in 1994, along the south face of the ridge from the castle town to the cellar villages.","f":"Hungary's first wine road"},{"b":"Szársomlyó, over four hundred metres, above Nagyharsány; its south face holds the district's warmest vineyards.","f":"The ridge's highest point and its village"},{"b":"Villánykövesd and Palkonya: rows of Swabian press-houses cut into loess, eighteenth century, still used for ageing.","f":"Villány's cellar villages"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Cabernet Franc","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"lead"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"blending"},{"name":"Kékfrankos","role":"blending"},{"name":"Portugieser","role":"minor"},{"name":"Zweigelt","role":"minor"},{"name":"Kadarka","role":"minor"},{"name":"Olaszrizling","role":"minor"}],"index":7,"name":"Villány","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Villány","exp":"Villányi Franc is a varietal rule, Cabernet Franc alone with no blending, and only at Prémium or Super Prémium level. The lower figures belong to other countries' varietal thresholds.","id":"r-phqd9ml4","opts":["One hundred per cent","Fifty-one per cent","Eighty-five per cent","Seventy-five per cent"],"q":"A bottle is labelled Villányi Franc. What share of the wine must be Cabernet Franc?"},{"accept":["Croatia","croatia","republic of croatia","hrvatska"],"ans":"Croatia","cat":"Villány","exp":"The ridge lies in Baranya county in Hungary's far south, a few kilometres from the Croatian border, with the Drava on the plain beyond. Warm air arriving from that side is why Villány ripens Cabernet Sauvignon as no other Hungarian district can.","id":"r-ocrbq3qj","q":"Villány's limestone ridge runs a few kilometres from Hungary's border with which country?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Villány","exp":"Super Prémium is capped at thirty-five hectolitres per hectare with at least two years' ageing and a year of it in wood; Prémium allows sixty. The highest figure is far above either capped tier.","id":"r-2z8hc7jc","opts":["35 hectolitres per hectare","100 hectolitres per hectare","60 hectolitres per hectare","45 hectolitres per hectare"],"q":"What is the maximum yield for a Villány wine at Super Prémium level?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Villány","exp":"Kék means blue, and Kékoportó was the Hungarian name for Portugieser until European naming rules retired it. Kékfrankos is Blaufränkisch, Kéknyelű a white of Badacsony, Kadarka the pale native red.","id":"r-tk4i7gpg","opts":["Kadarka","Kékfrankos","Kéknyelű","Kékoportó"],"q":"Villány's traditional light red is now labelled Portugieser. Under which older Hungarian name was it sold?"},{"accept":["2006","in 2006","the 2006 vintage"],"ans":"2006","cat":"Villány","exp":"The growers' community fixed yields and ageing for Classicus and Prémium from 2006; Super Prémium joined with a later revision and Villányi Franc followed for the 2015 harvest.","id":"r-kwl987nm","q":"In which year did Villány adopt its tiered quality system under the DHC mark, the first such system inside a Hungarian district?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Villány","exp":"Szársomlyó rises over four hundred metres with bare limestone on its south face and the district's warmest vineyards below. Badacsony, Szent György-hegy and Csobánc are basalt hills of the Balaton region.","id":"r-hgkx7b1a","opts":["Badacsony","Szársomlyó","Csobánc","Szent György-hegy"],"q":"Which hill above Nagyharsány is the highest point of the Villány ridge?"},{"accept":["Siklós","siklos","siklos castle","siklós castle town"],"ans":"Siklós","cat":"Villány","exp":"Siklós anchors the western end of the ridge, where the vineyards once grew more white than red; the Villány-Siklós wine road links its castle to the cellar villages in the east.","id":"r-lblozpe6","q":"Hungary's first wine road, opened in 1994, runs along the ridge from Villány to which castle town?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Villány","exp":"The ridge is Triassic and Jurassic limestone and dolomite; the loess mantle gives body and the rock below gives freshness. Basalt is the Balaton hills' rock, granite shows at Mórágy, and red sandstone is the Permian ground of the Balaton uplands around Csopak.","id":"r-omzloclq","opts":["Basalt","Granite","Limestone","Red sandstone"],"q":"What bedrock lies beneath the loess on the Villány hills?"}],"sheet":"hungary","slug":"villany","summary":"Villány is Hungary's warmest wine district, in the far south on a limestone ridge that runs east to west a few kilometres from the Croatian border. It is red country: Cabernet Franc is its flagship and the district's own designation, Villányi Franc, belongs to it; Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Kékfrankos and Portugieser, the light red once called Kékoportó, make up the rest. In 2006 the district adopted Hungary's first district quality tiers, Classicus and Prémium, with yield and ageing fixed for each; Super Prémium joined later. The wines are ripe, full, oak-aged and the most Bordeaux-like in the country. Cellar rows at Villánykövesd and Palkonya and the Villány-Siklós wine road are its visiting face."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"The sandy plain between the Danube and the Tisza, Hungary's largest district; quartz sand keeps phylloxera out, so vines grow on their own roots.","f":"Kunság: where, and what makes it unusual"},{"b":"The most continental in Hungary: hottest summers, coldest winters, most sunshine, least rain, and frost on the flat land as the constant hazard.","f":"Kunság's climate in a line"},{"b":"Cserszegi Fűszeres, Irsai Olivér and Olaszrizling; the first two are crossings bred for the sand, the third is Welschriesling.","f":"The plain's three leading whites"},{"b":"Irsai Olivér crossed with Traminer, bred at Cserszegtomaj in 1960; spicy, Muscat-scented, early-drinking.","f":"Cserszegi Fűszeres: parents and year"},{"b":"Arany Sárfehér, the neutral high-acid white that bases Hungarian sparkling wine; Izsáki Arany Sárfehér is a protected name of its own.","f":"Izsák's claim to fame"},{"b":"Duna, with Hajós-Baja and Csongrád; Duna-Tisza közi is the PGI the plain's volume wine carries.","f":"The wine region that holds Kunság, and its neighbours"},{"b":"Kékfrankos, Kadarka and Zweigelt, light and soft on sand, mostly for young reds and rosé; old-vine Kadarka is the exception worth seeking.","f":"Kunság's reds in brief"},{"b":"Phylloxera ruined the hill vineyards from the 1880s; the louse cannot tunnel through quartz sand, so the plain was planted ungrafted and in quantity.","f":"Why vineyards moved to the plain"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Cserszegi Fűszeres","role":"lead"},{"name":"Irsai Olivér","role":"lead"},{"name":"Olaszrizling","role":"lead"},{"name":"Arany Sárfehér","role":"blending"},{"name":"Ezerjó","role":"minor"},{"name":"Kövidinka","role":"minor"},{"name":"Chardonnay","role":"blending"},{"name":"Kékfrankos","role":"lead"},{"name":"Kadarka","role":"minor"},{"name":"Zweigelt","role":"blending"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"minor"}],"index":8,"name":"Kunság","questions":[{"a":1,"cat":"Kunság","exp":"Phylloxera needs a soil it can move through, and sand with a high quartz content defeats it. The louse survives cold winters elsewhere, and European vines are not resistant by nature.","id":"r-p94nnemu","opts":["Spring floods drown the louse","Quartz sand keeps phylloxera out","Winter cold kills the louse each year","The vines are a resistant native species"],"q":"Why can vines on the plain between the Danube and the Tisza be planted on their own roots?"},{"accept":["Duna","duna wine region","danube","duna borregio","duna borrégió"],"ans":"Duna","cat":"Kunság","exp":"Duna, the Danube region, takes in Kunság with Hajós-Baja and Csongrád. Pannon is the southern Transdanubian hill country, Balaton the lake districts and Somló.","id":"r-gx5fxjkb","q":"Hungary groups its districts into six wine regions. Which one holds Kunság?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Kunság","exp":"Duna-Tisza közi, between the Danube and the Tisza, is the OFJ of the plain, with more varieties and higher yields than the Kunsági OEM. Dunántúli covers Transdanubia, Balatonmelléki the lake country and Felső-Magyarországi the north-east.","id":"r-j9fw1u0q","opts":["Balatonmelléki","Felső-Magyarországi","Dunántúli","Duna-Tisza közi"],"q":"Under which protected geographical indication does most of the plain's volume wine leave Kunság?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Kunság","exp":"The district fills the sandy ridge between the Danube on the west and the Tisza on the east, above both rivers' flood plains. The Drava is the southern border with Croatia, the Hernád joins the Tisza from the north-east, and the Rába and Marcal flow in the west near Somló.","id":"r-vtu0enoi","opts":["The Danube and the Tisza","The Tisza and the Hernád","The Rába and the Marcal","The Danube and the Drava"],"q":"Which two rivers bound the Kunság district on its western and eastern sides?"},{"accept":["Cserszegi Fűszeres","cserszegi fuszeres","cserszegi","cserszegi fuszeres grape"],"ans":"Cserszegi Fűszeres","cat":"Kunság","exp":"Bred at Cserszegtomaj near Keszthely for frost resistance and aroma, it smells of Muscat and spice and is the sand's best-known glass. Irsai Olivér is itself a crossing.","id":"r-rlvfmq2c","q":"One aromatic crossing of Irsai Olivér and Traminer, bred in 1960, leads the plain's whites. Name it.","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Kunság","exp":"Wind-blown quartz sand over river alluvium, poor and fast-draining, is the plain's ground; loess is Szekszárd's, basalt Somló's and Badacsony's, limestone Villány's.","id":"r-cw2tfx01","opts":["Basalt","Limestone","Sand","Loess"],"q":"What soil dominates the vineyards of Kunság?"},{"accept":["Sparkling wine","sparkling","fizz","sparkling wines","bubbly"],"ans":"Sparkling wine","cat":"Kunság","exp":"Pezsgő is the Hungarian word for sparkling wine of any method; most of the plain's is tank-method from the neutral, high-acid Arany Sárfehér grown around Izsák.","id":"r-lwfalhs7","q":"A Hungarian label on a bottle of Izsák fizz reads pezsgő. What does the word mean?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Kunság","exp":"Arany Sárfehér's neutrality and acid make it the sparkling base, and Izsák's protected name rests on it. Cserszegi and Irsai are too aromatic; Kövidinka is a soft old variety for still wine.","id":"r-54850rmn","opts":["Arany Sárfehér","Kövidinka","Cserszegi Fűszeres","Irsai Olivér"],"q":"Which grape, neutral and high in acid, is the traditional base for the sparkling wines of the plain?"}],"sheet":"hungary","slug":"kunsag","summary":"Kunság is the great sandy plain between the Danube and the Tisza, Hungary's largest wine district by far and the engine of its everyday wine. Vines went there after phylloxera, because the louse cannot live in quartz sand, and ungrafted vines still stand. The climate is extreme: among the hottest summers and coldest winters in the country, with abundant sunshine, little rain and frost as the constant hazard. The wines are light, fresh and simple: aromatic whites from Cserszegi Fűszeres, Irsai Olivér and Olaszrizling, sparkling base from Arany Sárfehér around Izsák, and soft reds from Kékfrankos, Kadarka and Zweigelt. Kiskőrös, Kecskemét, Izsák and Soltvadkert are its towns, and it sits in the Duna wine region."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Xinomavro alone, 100%, dry red only. Rosé, sparkling and blends from the same slopes go out as PGI Imathia or PGI Macedonia.","f":"Naoussa: the grape rule and the colour of wine allowed"},{"b":"Imathia, Central Macedonia, on the eastern and south-eastern foothills of Mount Vermio, west of Thessaloniki, at about 150 to 350 metres.","f":"Where is Naoussa? Region, mountain, nearest city"},{"b":"Acid black. Pale garnet to brick, high acid, high tannin, sun-dried tomato, olive, dried rose, spice; the Nebbiolo decoy.","f":"Xinomavro: translate the name and give the blind-tasting markers"},{"b":"Four years at least before release, divided between oak and bottle, under the national rule for PDO reds; Reserve is the shorter term. Both are national words, not Naoussa's own.","f":"Naoussa Grande Reserve: the ageing floor"},{"b":"1971, among the first Greek appellation decrees, under the national appellation law of 1969; a PDO today.","f":"Naoussa's appellation date and the law behind it"},{"b":"Naoussa: alone, red. Amyndeon: alone, red, rosé and sparkling. Goumenissa: with at least 20% Negoska. Rapsani, in Thessaly, blends it with Krassato and Stavroto.","f":"The four Xinomavro appellations and how each uses the grape"},{"b":"Traditional: long maceration, years in old cask, Reserve and Grande Reserve. Modern: whole bunch, short maceration, older oak, drunk young with red fruit in front.","f":"Naoussa's two schools of winemaking"},{"b":"16 to 18 degrees, decant a young bottle; roast lamb, game birds, mushrooms, tomato braises, aged cheese. Young-vine style a touch cooler.","f":"Serving Naoussa: temperature, decant, dishes"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Xinomavro","role":"lead"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"minor"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"minor"}],"index":0,"name":"Naoussa","questions":[{"a":1,"cat":"Naoussa","exp":"Naoussa is Xinomavro alone. Agiorgitiko is Nemea's grape in the Peloponnese, Negoska is the blending partner at Goumenissa, and Limnio is an ancient red of Lemnos and the Macedonian coast.","id":"r-61raak8q","opts":["Negoska","Xinomavro","Limnio","Agiorgitiko"],"q":"Which grape variety is permitted in a wine labelled Naoussa?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Naoussa","exp":"Naoussa sits on the eastern slopes of Mount Vermio in Imathia. Olympus is Rapsani's mountain, Paiko stands above Goumenissa, and Kyllini rises west of Nemea.","id":"r-5aywpn1x","opts":["Vermio","Kyllini","Paiko","Olympus"],"q":"On the foothills of which mountain do the vineyards of Naoussa lie?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Naoussa","exp":"Xinomavro and Nebbiolo share pale colour, high acid, high tannin, dried rose and tar, and a long life. Sangiovese is brighter and less tannic, Aglianico darker, and Nerello Mascalese is Etna's decoy for Pinot Noir.","id":"r-jrtybs4s","opts":["Nerello Mascalese","Nebbiolo","Aglianico","Sangiovese"],"q":"With which Italian grape is Xinomavro most often paired as a blind-tasting decoy?"},{"accept":["Macedonia","Central Macedonia","Makedonia","Greek Macedonia","Imathia"],"ans":"Macedonia","cat":"Naoussa","exp":"Naoussa lies in Macedonia, the northern Greek region around Thessaloniki, not the country over the border; its regional unit is Imathia. Amyndeon in Florina and Goumenissa in Kilkis are the other Macedonian Xinomavro districts, and Nemea is in the Peloponnese.","id":"r-1tkrkpov","q":"In which region of Greece does the Naoussa appellation lie?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Naoussa","exp":"Boutari was founded at Naoussa in 1879 and made the appellation's name; Kir-Yianni, founded in 1997 at Yianakohori by a member of the same family, is a separate house. Tsantali is the Macedonian firm that revived Rapsani, and Gaia Wines works Nemea and Santorini.","id":"r-4xww6w8x","opts":["Boutari","Gaia Wines","Tsantali","Kir-Yianni"],"q":"Which house, founded at Naoussa in 1879, made the appellation's name across Greece?"},{"accept":["acid black","sour black","acid-black","sour-black","black acid"],"ans":"acid black","cat":"Naoussa","exp":"Xino is sour or acid and mavro is black: the name describes the grape's high acidity and dark skin, though the wine it makes is pale.","id":"r-wohb2xbt","q":"What does the grape name Xinomavro mean in English?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Naoussa","exp":"Naoussa is a dry red appellation only. Rosé and sparkling Xinomavro are permitted at Amyndeon, and sweet red is a Nemea tradition, not a Naoussa one.","id":"r-u7j9ufz0","opts":["Sweet red","Dry rosé","Sparkling rosé","Dry red"],"q":"Which style of wine may carry the Naoussa appellation?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Naoussa","exp":"Mature Xinomavro smells of sun-dried tomato, black olive, dried rose and spice. Gooseberry and grass say Sauvignon Blanc, blackcurrant and cedar say Cabernet, and lychee says Gewürztraminer.","id":"r-1et91pai","opts":["Gooseberry and cut grass","Lychee and rose petal","Sun-dried tomato and olive","Blackcurrant and cedar"],"q":"Which pair of aromas is the classic signature of a mature Naoussa?"}],"sheet":"greece","slug":"naoussa","summary":"Naoussa is the reference appellation for Xinomavro and the red the exam treats as Greece's answer to Nebbiolo. It lies in Imathia in Central Macedonia, on the eastern and south-eastern foothills of Mount Vermio west of Thessaloniki, and has carried its own appellation since the first Greek decrees of 1971. The wine is dry red from Xinomavro alone: pale in colour, high in acid, firmly tannic, smelling of sun-dried tomato, olive, dried rose and spice, and built to age for decades. A continental climate softened by the mountain, soils that shift from clay and limestone to sand and schist, and a split between the traditional long-aged houses and a gentler young school give a server plenty to say."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Xinomavro alone. Dry red, dry still rosé, and sparkling rosé, dry or semi-dry. It is the Greek appellation to name for rosé and for sparkling Xinomavro.","f":"Amyndeon: the grape and the three styles the appellation allows"},{"b":"Florina, Western Macedonia, a plateau at about 570 to 750 metres between four lakes: the coldest appellation in Greece and among the highest, harvest into October.","f":"Amyndeon: where, how high, how cold"},{"b":"Sandy soils over limestone and old lake deposits; phylloxera cannot move through sand, so Xinomavro survives ungrafted, and Kali Riza, good root, names a vineyard of such vines.","f":"Why are many Amyndeon vines own-rooted?"},{"b":"Vegoritida, the largest; Petron beside it; Zazari and Cheimaditida to the west. They moderate frost and heat on the plateau.","f":"Name the four lakes of Amyndeon"},{"b":"Same grape, two faces of Vermio. Amyndeon: paler, raspberry and rose, lighter tannin, earlier drinking. Naoussa: firmer, tomato and olive, longer-lived.","f":"Amyndeon against Naoussa in the glass"},{"b":"1972, a year after Naoussa. Alpha Estate, founded in 1997 at Agios Panteleimon on Lake Vegoritida, built the plateau's reputation abroad on old-vine, single-block Xinomavro.","f":"Amyndeon: appellation year and the house that made its name abroad"},{"b":"Rosé cold, 8 to 10 degrees, with meze, grilled fish and octopus. Sparkling rosé as the aperitif of a Greek dinner: strawberry, rose, dry savoury finish.","f":"Serving Amyndeon rosé and sparkling rosé"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Xinomavro","role":"lead"},{"name":"Roditis","role":"minor"},{"name":"Sauvignon Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Malagousia","role":"minor"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"minor"},{"name":"Pinot Noir","role":"minor"}],"index":1,"name":"Amyndeon","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Amyndeon","exp":"Amyndeon covers red, still rosé and sparkling rosé from Xinomavro. Naoussa and Goumenissa are dry red only, and Rapsani is a dry red blend of three grapes.","id":"r-1f1pntor","opts":["Amyndeon","Rapsani","Naoussa","Goumenissa"],"q":"Which Greek appellation permits Xinomavro to be made as a sparkling rosé?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Amyndeon","exp":"Amyndeon is in Florina in Western Macedonia. Imathia holds Naoussa, Kilkis holds Goumenissa and Larissa holds Rapsani in Thessaly.","id":"r-ee38x4uy","opts":["Imathia","Larissa","Florina","Kilkis"],"q":"In which regional unit of Greece does the Amyndeon appellation lie?"},{"accept":["Xinomavro","Xynomavro","Xinomauro","Ksinomavro"],"ans":"Xinomavro","cat":"Amyndeon","exp":"Amyndeon, like Naoussa, is Xinomavro alone; the difference is that Amyndeon may make rosé and sparkling wine as well as red from it.","id":"r-fyt9np9x","q":"Which grape variety is permitted in the wines of Amyndeon?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Amyndeon","exp":"Phylloxera cannot tunnel through loose sand, so Amyndeon's sandy soils, like Santorini's ash, keep ungrafted Xinomavro alive. Altitude, hybrids and the appellation rules have nothing to do with it.","id":"r-k18u4ji0","opts":["The soils are sandy and phylloxera cannot spread through sand","The vines are a resistant hybrid of Xinomavro","Grafting is forbidden by the appellation's rules","The altitude is too high for the louse to survive the winter"],"q":"Why do old vines on the Amyndeon plateau often grow on their own roots?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Amyndeon","exp":"The Amyndeon plateau sits at about 570 to 750 metres, among the highest appellation vineyards in Greece and the reason for its late harvest and bright acidity.","id":"r-y88um21u","opts":["100 to 250 metres","570 to 750 metres","300 to 450 metres","900 to 1,100 metres"],"q":"Roughly how high do the vineyards of Amyndeon lie?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Amyndeon","exp":"Amyndeon sits on the colder, higher north-western face of Vermio on sand, so its Xinomavro is paler, higher-toned and readier than Naoussa's firmer, longer-lived wine. No Xinomavro appellation is sweet or fortified, and the plateau uses oak more gently than Naoussa's old school, not more.","id":"r-grp0bsas","opts":["Oakier and higher in alcohol","Deeper and more tannic","Sweeter and fortified","Paler and earlier-drinking"],"q":"How does a red Amyndeon usually compare with a Naoussa in the glass?"},{"accept":["1972","nineteen seventy-two","nineteen seventy two"],"ans":"1972","cat":"Amyndeon","exp":"Amyndeon followed Naoussa by a year: Naoussa, Nemea and Rapsani were among the first decrees of 1971, Amyndeon came in 1972 and Goumenissa in 1979.","id":"r-gepa0eec","q":"In which year was the Amyndeon appellation established?","sa":1}],"sheet":"greece","slug":"amyndeon","summary":"Amyndeon, also written Amynteo and Amyntaio, is the coldest appellation in Greece and among the highest: a plateau at roughly 570 to 750 metres in Florina, Western Macedonia, ringed by mountains and set between four lakes. Its grape is Xinomavro alone, as at Naoussa, but here the appellation covers dry red, still rosé and sparkling rosé, so Amyndeon is the name a sommelier reaches for when Greek rosé or sparkling rosé comes up. Sandy soils let old vines stand on their own roots, winters bring snow and spring frost, and the red is paler, higher-toned and earlier-drinking than Naoussa's. The appellation dates from 1972, a year after its neighbour over the mountain."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Xinomavro blended with Negoska, Negoska at least 20%; dry red only. The one Macedonian Xinomavro appellation that must blend.","f":"Goumenissa: the grape rule"},{"b":"From Negush, the old Slavic name of Naoussa. Deep colour, lower acid, higher alcohol, soft tannin: the flesh Xinomavro lacks. Grown almost only at Goumenissa.","f":"Negoska: origin of the name and what it brings"},{"b":"Kilkis regional unit, Central Macedonia, north-west of Thessaloniki, on the south-eastern foothills of Mount Paiko above the Axios plain; lower and warmer than Naoussa.","f":"Where is Goumenissa?"},{"b":"1979. Medium ruby, deeper than Naoussa: sour cherry, plum, tomato leaf, spice, fresh acid, rounder tannin; drinks within a few years.","f":"Goumenissa: appellation date and style in the glass"},{"b":"Naoussa: alone. Amyndeon: alone, with rosé and sparkling. Goumenissa: Negoska at least 20%. Rapsani, Thessaly: Krassato and Stavroto in equal parts.","f":"The four Xinomavro appellations and their partners"},{"b":"The Vardaris, the cold north wind down the Axios valley: dries the vineyards after rain, checks rot, and can chill the spring.","f":"The wind of Goumenissa and what it does"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Xinomavro","role":"lead"},{"name":"Negoska","role":"blending"},{"name":"Roditis","role":"minor"},{"name":"Assyrtiko","role":"minor"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"minor"}],"index":2,"name":"Goumenissa","questions":[{"a":1,"cat":"Goumenissa","exp":"Goumenissa is Xinomavro blended with at least 20% Negoska. Krassato and Stavroto are Rapsani's partners for Xinomavro, and Limnio is an ancient red of Lemnos and Chalkidiki.","id":"r-6j0wqjy1","opts":["Limnio","Negoska","Krassato","Stavroto"],"q":"Which grape must make up at least 20% of a wine labelled Goumenissa?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Goumenissa","exp":"Goumenissa sits on Mount Paiko in Kilkis. Vermio holds Naoussa and Amyndeon, Olympus holds Rapsani, and Athos is the monastic peninsula of Chalkidiki.","id":"r-xfo2pwej","opts":["Paiko","Vermio","Olympus","Athos"],"q":"On the foothills of which mountain do the vineyards of Goumenissa lie?"},{"accept":["1979","nineteen seventy-nine","nineteen seventy nine"],"ans":"1979","cat":"Goumenissa","exp":"Goumenissa came in 1979, eight years after Naoussa and seven after Amyndeon, the last of the three Macedonian Xinomavro appellations.","id":"r-jiylqyna","q":"In which year was Goumenissa established as an appellation?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Goumenissa","exp":"Negoska is deep-coloured, lower in acid, higher in alcohol and soft in tannin, filling out what Xinomavro lacks; acidity and tannin are Xinomavro's own contribution.","id":"r-k4mez3dd","opts":["Aromatic lift and low alcohol","Colour, alcohol and softness","Acidity and tannin","Botrytis and sweetness"],"q":"What does Negoska chiefly contribute to the Goumenissa blend?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Goumenissa","exp":"Goumenissa is in Kilkis, north-west of Thessaloniki. Imathia holds Naoussa, Florina holds Amyndeon, and Pella lies between them around Edessa.","id":"r-fy1rcnww","opts":["Pella","Imathia","Florina","Kilkis"],"q":"In which regional unit of Greece does Goumenissa lie?"},{"accept":["Naoussa","Naousa","Negush","Niaousta"],"ans":"Naoussa","cat":"Goumenissa","exp":"Negush was the old Slavic name of Naoussa, so Negoska means the grape of Naoussa; the irony is that Naoussa's appellation does not permit it and Goumenissa requires it.","id":"r-0m5mdza0","q":"From the old Slavic name of which Greek wine town does the grape Negoska take its name?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Goumenissa","exp":"Goumenissa, like Naoussa and Rapsani, is dry red only. Of the Xinomavro appellations only Amyndeon admits rosé and sparkling wine.","id":"r-4sh5acc6","opts":["Dry red","Dry white","Dry rosé","Sparkling rosé"],"q":"Which style of wine may carry the Goumenissa name?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Goumenissa","exp":"Negoska must be at least 20%, so Xinomavro may reach 80% at most; a 100% Xinomavro from Macedonia is Naoussa or Amyndeon.","id":"r-zvw26rng","opts":["100%","90%","80%","70%"],"q":"What is the largest share of the blend Xinomavro may take in a Goumenissa?"}],"sheet":"greece","slug":"goumenissa","summary":"Goumenissa is the third Xinomavro appellation of Greek Macedonia and the one that blends. It lies in the Kilkis regional unit north-west of Thessaloniki, on the south-eastern foothills of Mount Paiko above the plain of the Axios river, and has been an appellation since 1979. The rule is Xinomavro with at least 20% Negoska, a local grape that brings colour, flesh and alcohol to Xinomavro's acid and tannin, so the wine is rounder and readier than Naoussa and darker in the glass. Lower, warmer and smaller than its two cousins, it is a district of a few family estates and one large house, and the blending rule is the fact a Certified candidate must hold."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Xinomavro, Krassato and Stavroto in equal parts, a third each. Dry red only.","f":"Rapsani: the three grapes and their shares"},{"b":"Thessaly, Larissa regional unit, on the eastern and south-eastern lower slopes of Mount Olympus above the Vale of Tempe, a few kilometres from the Aegean.","f":"Rapsani: where it lies"},{"b":"Xinomavro: acid, tannin, dried rose and tomato. Krassato: alcohol, body, roundness. Stavroto: colour and a softer tannin.","f":"What each grape brings to Rapsani"},{"b":"Iron-rich schist: thin, stony, quick-draining, low vigour; gives the wine its earthy, mineral stamp and its warmer, fuller frame beside Naoussa.","f":"Rapsani's soil in two words, and its effect"},{"b":"Appellation in 1971; terraces abandoned as the village emptied; revived in the 1990s by a large Macedonian house; a few estates since, with Reserve and Grande Reserve as the calling cards.","f":"Rapsani's history in one line"},{"b":"Rapsani: deeper colour, fuller, warmer, rounder tannin, red fruit, thyme, earth. Naoussa: paler, higher acid, firmer tannin, tomato and olive, longer-lived.","f":"Rapsani against Naoussa in the glass"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Xinomavro","role":"lead"},{"name":"Krassato","role":"blending"},{"name":"Stavroto","role":"blending"}],"index":3,"name":"Rapsani","questions":[{"a":1,"cat":"Rapsani","exp":"Rapsani is Xinomavro with the two local grapes Krassato and Stavroto in equal parts. Negoska belongs to Goumenissa, Agiorgitiko to Nemea, and blends with Merlot or Syrah are regional PGI wines.","id":"r-xbkws34l","opts":["Xinomavro, Merlot and Syrah","Xinomavro, Krassato and Stavroto","Xinomavro, Negoska and Limnio","Agiorgitiko, Mavroudi and Mavrotragano"],"q":"Which three grapes make up a wine labelled Rapsani?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Rapsani","exp":"Rapsani lies on the eastern foot of Mount Olympus in Thessaly. Vermio holds Naoussa and Amyndeon, Paiko holds Goumenissa, and Pelion is the peninsula south-east of Volos.","id":"r-tt59n8pg","opts":["Olympus","Vermio","Paiko","Pelion"],"q":"On the slopes of which mountain is Rapsani grown?"},{"accept":["Thessaly","Thessalia","Thessalía","Larissa"],"ans":"Thessaly","cat":"Rapsani","exp":"Rapsani is the Xinomavro appellation of Thessaly, in the Larissa regional unit; the other three Xinomavro appellations are all in Macedonia.","id":"r-j1wdof7v","q":"In which region of Greece does the Rapsani appellation lie?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Rapsani","exp":"The decree fixes Xinomavro, Krassato and Stavroto in equal shares, a third each. A 20% floor is Goumenissa's rule for Negoska, and Naoussa is Xinomavro alone.","id":"r-g4zf4w2k","opts":["Winemaker's choice","Equal parts","At least 20% each","At least half Xinomavro"],"q":"What share of a Rapsani blend does the appellation set for each of its three grapes?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Rapsani","exp":"Rapsani's terraces are weathered, iron-stained schist, thin and poor. Volcanic ash and pumice is Santorini, red clay is Nemea's valley floor, and deep alluvial silt is plain-country soil, not a terrace on Olympus.","id":"r-pa0ebtrt","opts":["Red clay","Deep alluvial silt","Iron-rich schist","Volcanic ash and pumice"],"q":"Which soil is the signature of Rapsani's terraces?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Rapsani","exp":"Tsantali took up the abandoned Olympus terraces in the 1990s and made Rapsani Reserve and Grande Reserve the appellation's calling cards for a generation. Boutari is Naoussa's old house, Skouras is a Peloponnese estate near Argos, and Alpha Estate belongs to the Amyndeon plateau.","id":"r-1d0r1dlf","opts":["Alpha Estate","Skouras","Boutari","Tsantali"],"q":"Which house replanted Rapsani's abandoned terraces on Olympus and revived the appellation?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Rapsani","exp":"A large Macedonian house replanted the Olympus terraces and relaunched Rapsani in the 1990s; the appellation itself dates from 1971, but the vineyards had largely been abandoned.","id":"r-57r6gsq9","opts":["The 1990s","The 1980s","The 2000s","The 1970s"],"q":"In which decade was Rapsani revived after years of neglect?"}],"sheet":"greece","slug":"rapsani","summary":"Rapsani is the Xinomavro appellation of Thessaly and the odd one out: a blend of three grapes, Xinomavro, Krassato and Stavroto, in equal shares, grown on the south-eastern lower slopes of Mount Olympus above the Vale of Tempe and within sight of the Aegean. An appellation since the first decrees of 1971, it fell near to abandonment before a large Macedonian house revived it in the 1990s, and a few estates have since followed. Iron-rich schist, terraces climbing the mountain and the sea's breath give a red fuller and warmer than Naoussa, with red fruit, spice and earth over Xinomavro's acid and a tannin rounded by its two partners. The exam asks the three grapes and the mountain."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Agiorgitiko alone, 100%. Red only: dry, with rare semi-sweet and sweet reds; rosé and blends are PGI. Appellation since 1971, the largest red PDO in Greece.","f":"Nemea: the grape, the colours and the date"},{"b":"St George's grape, Greece's most planted red. Deep purple, plum, sour cherry, sweet spice, medium acid, soft velvety tannin; from rosé to Grande Reserve and sweet wine.","f":"Agiorgitiko: the name and the glass"},{"b":"Floor, 250 to 400 m, red clay, plush reds and sweet wine. Slopes, 400 to 650 m, clay-limestone, Koutsi, the serious dry wine. Plateau, 650 to 900 m, Asprokambos, freshest. Convention, not law.","f":"Nemea's three altitude bands"},{"b":"Corinthia regional unit, north-eastern Peloponnese, south-west of Corinth, in the hills between the Corinthian coast and the plain of Argos, with Mount Kyllini to the west.","f":"Where is Nemea?"},{"b":"Hercules's first labour: the Nemean lion. Ancient Nemea held the Nemean Games, one of the four Panhellenic games. The old sweet red was called blood of Hercules.","f":"The story on the Nemea label"},{"b":"Nemea: deep colour, plum and spice, soft tannin, medium acid, Peloponnese. Naoussa: pale, tomato and olive, high acid and tannin, Macedonia.","f":"Nemea against Naoussa in the glass"},{"b":"Agiorgitiko rosé, whites, and blends with Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot or Syrah: all PGI Peloponnese or PGI Corinth, however famous the bottle.","f":"What leaves the Nemea appellation and where it goes"},{"b":"Young Nemea 15 to 17 degrees with lamb, souvlaki, moussaka, pastitsio, tomato braises. Reserve with roast meat and aged cheese. Sweet Nemea cool with dried fruit or chocolate.","f":"Serving Nemea at the table"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Agiorgitiko","role":"lead"},{"name":"Cabernet Sauvignon","role":"minor"},{"name":"Merlot","role":"minor"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"minor"},{"name":"Roditis","role":"minor"},{"name":"Malagousia","role":"minor"}],"index":4,"name":"Nemea","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Nemea","exp":"Nemea is Agiorgitiko alone. Xinomavro is Naoussa's grape, Mavrodaphne makes the fortified sweet wine of Patras, and Limnio is an ancient red of Lemnos.","id":"r-natiymaj","opts":["Agiorgitiko","Xinomavro","Mavrodaphne","Limnio"],"q":"Which grape variety makes the wines of Nemea?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Nemea","exp":"Nemea is in Corinthia in the north-eastern Peloponnese. Macedonia holds Naoussa, Amyndeon and Goumenissa, Thessaly holds Rapsani, and Crete has Peza, Archanes, Dafnes and Sitia.","id":"r-ltloenbq","opts":["Macedonia","Thessaly","Crete","Peloponnese"],"q":"In which part of Greece does the Nemea appellation lie?"},{"accept":["St George's grape","Saint George's grape","St George's","Saint George's","grape of St George","grape of Saint George","of Saint George"],"ans":"St George's grape","cat":"Nemea","exp":"Agios Georgios is Saint George, so Agiorgitiko is St George's grape, after a chapel or feast day in the district; it is also Greece's most planted red variety.","id":"r-hudahr9a","q":"What does the name Agiorgitiko mean in English?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Nemea","exp":"Koutsi, south-west of the town at about 500 to 600 metres on clay-limestone, is the slope the leading estates name. Asprokambos is the high plateau, Archaia Nemea the valley floor, and Malandreni a village towards Argos.","id":"r-t14g9tb2","opts":["Malandreni","Archaia Nemea","Koutsi","Asprokambos"],"q":"Which village stands on the most famous hillside of Nemea's middle zone?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Nemea","exp":"Nemea is red only: dry, semi-sweet and sweet, the sweet from dried grapes or fortified. Rosé, white and sparkling wine from the same vineyards are PGI Peloponnese or PGI Corinth.","id":"r-bznbxki0","opts":["Dry white","Sweet red","Dry rosé","Sparkling rosé"],"q":"Which style from the Nemea district may carry the appellation name?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Nemea","exp":"Agiorgitiko carries colour and fruit to spare with a soft, velvety tannin, which is why Nemea is the Greek red poured most. Pale garnet with firm tannin is Xinomavro at Naoussa, pale pink with sharp acidity is an Amyndeon rosé, and tawny and sweet is an old Mavrodaphne of Patras.","id":"r-ddmr3rtm","opts":["Deep purple with soft tannin","Pale garnet with firm tannin","Pale pink with sharp acidity","Tawny with a sweet finish"],"q":"Which description fits a young Nemea in the glass?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Nemea","exp":"Asprokambos is the high band of Nemea at about 650 to 900 metres, the coolest and latest; the floor is 250 to 400 and the slopes 400 to 650.","id":"r-wy3osum9","opts":["250 to 400 metres","100 to 250 metres","650 to 900 metres","400 to 650 metres"],"q":"Roughly how high does the plateau of Asprokambos lie?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Nemea","exp":"Nemea is red only, so the rosé goes out as PGI Peloponnese or PGI Corinth. Mantinia is Moschofilero's white appellation and Macedonia is the northern mainland.","id":"r-fz29go4p","opts":["PDO Nemea","PGI Peloponnese","PGI Macedonia","PDO Mantinia"],"q":"Under which designation is an Agiorgitiko rosé from Nemea's vineyards sold?"}],"sheet":"greece","slug":"nemea","summary":"Nemea, in Corinthia in the north-eastern Peloponnese south-west of Corinth, is Greece's largest red appellation and the home of Agiorgitiko, St George's grape and the country's most planted red. An appellation since 1971, it is red from Agiorgitiko alone, dry above all, with rare semi-sweet and sweet reds allowed; rosé and blends are regional wine. The zone climbs from a warm valley floor on red clay through the hillside villages, Koutsi above all, to a high plateau at Asprokambos, and altitude decides the wine: plush below, structured and fresh above. Agiorgitiko is deep in colour, soft in tannin and friendly young, so Nemea is the Greek red a server pours most; the lion Hercules killed here is the label's story."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Moschofilero, at least 85%; the rest Asproudes, the old local white grapes. Dry white above all, with a sparkling white provided for. An appellation since 1971.","f":"Mantinia: the grape and its minimum"},{"b":"The plateau of Tripoli in Arcadia, central Peloponnese, at roughly 600 to 700 metres, ringed by mountains with Mount Mainalo to the west.","f":"Where is Mantinia, and how high?"},{"b":"Pale with a faint pink glint; rose, orange blossom, lemon, white pepper; light, around 12 per cent alcohol, crisp acid. Drink it young and cold.","f":"Moschofilero in the glass"},{"b":"No. The name and the perfume suggest one, but it is a distinct pink-skinned Greek variety; the Muscats of Greece are Muscat Blanc and Muscat of Alexandria.","f":"Is Moschofilero a Muscat?"},{"b":"Late, often well into October, one of the last in Greece. Autumn rain and rot are the danger at picking; spring frost on the basin floor is the other.","f":"Mantinia's harvest and its risks"},{"b":"PGI Arcadia or PGI Peloponnese: Mantinia is a white appellation, so the pink wine from the same grape leaves it.","f":"Where does a Moschofilero rosé go?"},{"b":"At 7 to 9 degrees, young, as an aperitif or with oysters, crab, shrimp, raw fish and gently spiced dishes; a fine answer for a guest who likes dry Riesling or Albariño.","f":"Serving Mantinia at the table"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Moschofilero","role":"lead"},{"name":"Asproudes","role":"blending"},{"name":"Gewurztraminer","role":"minor"}],"index":5,"name":"Mantinia","questions":[{"a":3,"cat":"Mantinia","exp":"Mantinia must be at least 85% Moschofilero, the remainder from the old local white grapes called Asproudes; it is not a single-variety rule like Nemea's Agiorgitiko.","id":"r-ntgbiyrh","opts":["51%","75%","100%","85%"],"q":"What minimum share of its principal grape must a Mantinia PDO white contain?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Mantinia","exp":"Mantinia is the plateau of Tripoli in Arcadia, central Peloponnese. Corinthia holds Nemea, Achaea holds Patras with its Muscat and Mavrodaphne, and Messinia lies to the south-west.","id":"r-dfouabk1","opts":["Arcadia","Achaea","Messinia","Corinthia"],"q":"In which regional unit of the Peloponnese does the Mantinia plateau lie?"},{"a":2,"cat":"Mantinia","exp":"Moschofilero is pink to grey-skinned, like Pinot Gris, so it is pressed quickly for a pale white; with skin contact it makes a rosé sold as regional wine.","id":"r-28cz73is","opts":["Blue-black","Golden yellow","Pink to grey","Pale green"],"q":"What colour is the skin of a ripe Moschofilero grape?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Mantinia","exp":"The plateau of Tripoli lies at roughly 600 to 700 metres, with vineyards higher on its edges; the altitude and the inland position explain the cold nights and the late harvest.","id":"r-nzyvvypw","opts":["1,200 to 1,400 metres","600 to 700 metres","300 to 400 metres","100 to 200 metres"],"q":"Roughly how high does the Mantinia plateau lie?"},{"accept":["Moschofilero","Moscofilero","Moschophilero","Mosxofilero","Moschofilero grape"],"ans":"Moschofilero","cat":"Mantinia","exp":"Mantinia is at least 85% Moschofilero, an aromatic grape pressed into a pale, floral, crisp dry white; despite its name and perfume it is not a member of the Muscat family.","id":"r-kvpg7f26","q":"Which grape is the principal variety of the Mantinia PDO?","sa":1},{"accept":["Asproudes","Asproudhes","Asproudes grapes"],"ans":"Asproudes","cat":"Mantinia","exp":"Asproudes is the plateau's collective name for its old white grapes, which may make up the 15% of a Mantinia that is not Moschofilero.","id":"r-84j2cjrc","q":"Which local term covers the old white varieties allowed up to 15% in a Mantinia?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Mantinia","exp":"The cold, high plateau ripens Moschofilero late, usually in October, one of the last harvests in Greece; the islands pick in August, and July and December are out of the question for a dry white.","id":"r-r6bhazij","opts":["October","December","August","July"],"q":"In which month is the Mantinia harvest usually brought in?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Mantinia","exp":"Young Mantinia is pale, with rose, citrus and spice and crisp acid at low alcohol. Deep gold and honeyed suggests a sweet wine, pale ruby a red, and amber a long-aged sweet wine.","id":"r-p691zrx6","opts":["Deep gold and honeyed with low acidity","Pale and floral with crisp acidity","Amber and nutty from long cask ageing","Pale ruby with firm tannin"],"q":"Which description fits a young Mantinia in the glass?"}],"sheet":"greece","slug":"mantinia","summary":"Mantinia is the high plateau of Arcadia in the central Peloponnese, around the town of Tripoli, and the home of Moschofilero, a pink-skinned, aromatic grape pressed pale into one of the freshest dry whites in Greece. An appellation since 1971, it requires at least 85% Moschofilero, the rest from the old local white grapes, and it is a white appellation above all. At 600 to 700 metres and more, with cold winters and one of the latest harvests in the country, it gives wine of low alcohol, bright acid and a perfume of rose, citrus and spice. Pour it as an aperitif or with shellfish and gentle spice; it smells like a Muscat but it is not one."},
{"asked":["intro","certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"At least 85% Assyrtiko, the remainder Athiri and Aidani. Older guides give 75%, the rule before an amendment of recent years.","f":"Dry Santorini: the grape rule"},{"b":"The volcanic ash and pumice hold no clay, so the louse cannot survive. The vines grow on their own roots, some root systems centuries old.","f":"Why is there no phylloxera on Santorini?"},{"b":"A vine whose canes are woven into a low basket on the ground, the grapes inside, sheltered from the meltemi wind, blown sand and the sun.","f":"The kouloura: what it is and why"},{"b":"Hardly any summer rain: night humidity and dew off the sea, soaked up by the porous pumice, and the winter rain held in the ground.","f":"How do Santorini's vines get water?"},{"b":"Of the night, from the old night picking and pressing. Today a riper dry Santorini with at least 13.5% alcohol and at least three months in oak.","f":"Nykteri: the name and the rule"},{"b":"At least 51% Assyrtiko with Aidani, sun-dried, fermented to very high sugar, aged at least two years in cask; never fortified. One word, unlike Tuscany's Vin Santo.","f":"Vinsanto: grapes, method, ageing"},{"b":"Pale lemon; lemon, lime, flint, smoke, sea salt; bone dry, searing acid, real body and alcohol. Ages ten years and more towards honey and toast.","f":"Santorini in the glass"},{"b":"A Late Bronze Age eruption: the volcano's centre collapsed into the sea, leaving a caldera ringed by Santorini, Thirasia and Aspronisi, and the ash buried the town of Akrotiri.","f":"What made the landscape of Santorini?"},{"b":"At 8 to 10 degrees with oysters, crab, shrimp, grilled fish, lemon and olive oil; decant an old one. Vinsanto cool with blue cheese or nut desserts.","f":"Serving Santorini at the table"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Assyrtiko","role":"lead"},{"name":"Athiri","role":"blending"},{"name":"Aidani","role":"blending"},{"name":"Mavrotragano","role":"rising"},{"name":"Mandilaria","role":"minor"}],"index":6,"name":"Santorini","questions":[{"a":1,"cat":"Santorini","exp":"Dry Santorini needs at least 85% Assyrtiko, the rest Athiri and Aidani. 51% is the Vinsanto minimum, and 75% is the older rule some guides still print.","id":"r-hhvrfs34","opts":["51%","85%","75%","100%"],"q":"What minimum share of Assyrtiko does a dry PDO Santorini require?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Santorini","exp":"Phylloxera needs clay to live and move, and Santorini's ash and pumice hold none, so the vines grow on their own roots. They are not grafted, the winters are mild, and nothing is flooded on this dry island.","id":"r-3cjg9bmt","opts":["The island is too cold in winter","The volcanic soil holds no clay","The vineyards are flooded each winter","The vines are grafted on resistant rootstock"],"q":"Why can phylloxera not survive in Santorini's vineyards?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Santorini","exp":"The kouloura is a vine whose canes are woven into a low basket on the ground to shelter the grapes from the meltemi, blown sand and the sun; a cave winery is a kanava.","id":"r-q94uguzi","opts":["A basket-trained vine","A clay amphora","A cave winery","A drying terrace"],"q":"What is a kouloura on Santorini?"},{"accept":["Nykteri","Nichteri","Nyhteri","Nikteri","Nychteri"],"ans":"Nykteri","cat":"Santorini","exp":"Nykteri means of the night, from the old night harvest and pressing; today it requires riper grapes, at least 13.5% alcohol and at least three months in oak.","id":"r-2vmqikao","q":"What name is given to the riper, oak-aged style of dry Santorini, once pressed at night?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Santorini","exp":"Vinsanto's grapes are laid in the sun to shrivel, then fermented and aged at least two years in cask; it is never fortified. Spirit makes Samos Vin Doux, noble rot makes Tokaji, and frozen grapes make ice wine.","id":"r-4asjizmj","opts":["Sun-drying the grapes","Noble rot","Adding grape spirit","Freezing on the vine"],"q":"How is the sweetness of Vinsanto achieved?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Santorini","exp":"The meltemi, the strong summer northerly of the Aegean, is why vines are woven into low kouloura baskets. The sirocco blows hot from Africa, the mistral down the Rhône valley, and the bora on the Adriatic.","id":"r-irgzz4x6","opts":["Bora","Mistral","Sirocco","Meltemi"],"q":"Which wind shapes the training of Santorini's vines?"},{"accept":["Aidani","Aedani","Aidhani","Aithani"],"ans":"Aidani","cat":"Santorini","exp":"Vinsanto is at least 51% Assyrtiko, with Aidani, an aromatic white of flowers and herbs, as the main partner; Athiri more often softens the dry wine.","id":"r-fat2q1lj","q":"Which white grape is Assyrtiko's main partner in Vinsanto?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Santorini","exp":"The Santorini appellation covers white wines only, so Mavrotragano and Mandilaria reds go out as PGI Cyclades. Paros is its own appellation, and PGI Crete belongs to the larger island to the south.","id":"r-74zckhpc","opts":["PDO Santorini","PDO Paros","PGI Cyclades","PGI Crete"],"q":"Under which designation is a dry red Mavrotragano from Santorini sold?"}],"sheet":"greece","slug":"santorini","summary":"Santorini is a ring of volcanic islands in the southern Cyclades, north of Crete, and the home of Assyrtiko at its most intense: bone dry, saline, high in both acid and alcohol, and able to age for a decade or more. The vines grow on their own roots in ash and pumice where phylloxera has never survived, many of them woven into low kouloura baskets against the wind, and they drink the night's sea moisture rather than rain. The appellation, from 1971, covers the dry white, at least 85% Assyrtiko with Athiri and Aidani, its riper, oak-aged cousin Nykteri, and Vinsanto, the sun-dried sweet wine. It is the Greek white every guest has heard of."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Heraklion: Peza, Archanes, Dafnes, Handakas-Candia, Malvasia Handakas-Candia. Lasithi: Sitia, Malvasia Sitia.","f":"Crete's seven appellations, by district"},{"b":"Kotsifali: alcohol, softness, spice, little colour. Mandilaria: deep colour and tannin, low alcohol. Blended at Peza and Archanes, Kotsifali the larger share.","f":"Kotsifali and Mandilaria: who brings what?"},{"b":"White from Vilana alone; red from Kotsifali with Mandilaria. South-east of Heraklion, a few hundred metres up.","f":"Peza: the two wines"},{"b":"Pale, soft, early-ripening red. Leads Dafnes and Sitia red, with a little Mandilaria at Sitia; sun-dried for the island's sweet red.","f":"Liatiko: where and how"},{"b":"Crete's rising white, saved from near extinction: apricot, peach, herbs, chalky texture, good acid. Mostly sold as PGI Crete; ages well with lees or oak.","f":"Vidiano in one breath"},{"b":"Minoan press at Vathypetro in the Bronze Age; the Venetian Malmsey trade of Candia; phylloxera only in the 1970s, then replanting and the rescue of old grapes.","f":"Crete's wine history in three marks"},{"b":"Altitude, several hundred metres up; north-facing slopes cooled by Aegean breezes; the mountain spine sheltering them from hot winds off Africa.","f":"What tempers the heat on Crete?"},{"b":"Vilana with meze and grilled fish; Vidiano with lobster or roast chicken; Kotsifali reds slightly cool with lamb; sweet Liatiko with nuts and cheese.","f":"Serving Cretan wine at the table"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Vilana","role":"lead"},{"name":"Vidiano","role":"rising"},{"name":"Kotsifali","role":"lead"},{"name":"Mandilaria","role":"blending"},{"name":"Liatiko","role":"lead"},{"name":"Thrapsathiri","role":"minor"},{"name":"Dafni","role":"minor"},{"name":"Plyto","role":"minor"},{"name":"Malvasia di Candia Aromatica","role":"minor"},{"name":"Syrah / Shiraz","role":"minor"}],"index":7,"name":"Crete","questions":[{"a":2,"cat":"Crete","exp":"Mandilaria is deep in colour and tannic and is blended with Kotsifali, which brings alcohol and spice but little colour. Liatiko leads Dafnes and Sitia, and Romeiko is the red of Chania.","id":"r-t605nfns","opts":["Romeiko","Kotsifali","Mandilaria","Liatiko"],"q":"Which grape gives the red wine of Peza most of its colour and tannin?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Crete","exp":"Peza white is Vilana alone. Vidiano is the rising white usually sold as PGI Crete, Thrapsathiri partners Vilana in Sitia, and Assyrtiko belongs to Santorini.","id":"r-0oiizwb7","opts":["Thrapsathiri","Assyrtiko","Vidiano","Vilana"],"q":"Which grape alone makes the white wine of Peza?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Crete","exp":"Dafnes, west of Heraklion, is a Liatiko appellation for dry and sweet red. Kotsifali and Mandilaria make Peza and Archanes, and Agiorgitiko belongs to Nemea.","id":"r-88qxzlas","opts":["Liatiko","Mandilaria","Agiorgitiko","Kotsifali"],"q":"Which grape leads the red wines of Dafnes?"},{"accept":["Vidiano","Vidianó","Vithiano","Vidiano grape"],"ans":"Vidiano","cat":"Crete","exp":"Vidiano, saved over recent decades, gives apricot, herbs and a chalky texture with good acid and now leads the island's white revival, mostly as PGI Crete.","id":"r-fmh79rxj","q":"Which white grape, rescued from near extinction, has become Crete's flagship white?","sa":1},{"a":1,"cat":"Crete","exp":"Sitia and Malvasia Sitia are in Lasithi, the dry east. The other five Cretan appellations are in Heraklion, while Chania and Rethymno have regional wines only.","id":"r-m1s652ru","opts":["Chania","Lasithi","Heraklion","Rethymno"],"q":"In which regional unit do Sitia and Malvasia Sitia lie?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Crete","exp":"Crete has seven: Peza, Archanes, Dafnes, Handakas-Candia and Malvasia Handakas-Candia in Heraklion, and Sitia and Malvasia Sitia in Lasithi.","id":"r-mwxs54iw","opts":["Seven","Four","Nine","Five"],"q":"How many PDO appellations does Crete hold?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Crete","exp":"Phylloxera arrived on Crete only in the 1970s, about eighty years after it reached the mainland, and the replanting swept away many old varieties; Santorini's ash has never let it in.","id":"r-o7co0ogm","opts":["1890s","1970s","1920s","2000s"],"q":"In which decade did phylloxera reach Crete?"},{"accept":["Archanes","Arhanes","Arkhanes","Archanes PDO","PDO Archanes"],"ans":"Archanes","cat":"Crete","exp":"Archanes, around the town south of Heraklion, is red only, Kotsifali with Mandilaria. Peza uses the same two grapes for its red but also makes a white from Vilana, and Dafnes is a Liatiko appellation.","id":"r-njognl8h","q":"Which Cretan appellation makes red wine only, from Kotsifali with Mandilaria?","sa":1}],"sheet":"greece","slug":"crete","summary":"Crete, the largest Greek island and the most southerly wine region in the country, keeps its own grapes and seven appellations. The whites are Vilana, the old staple of Peza, and Vidiano, the rising star of apricot and herbs, with Thrapsathiri, Dafni, Plyto and the aromatic Malvasia beside them. The reds are soft, spicy Kotsifali, dark, tannic Mandilaria and pale, early Liatiko, which also makes the island's sweet red. Five appellations lie in Heraklion, Peza, Archanes, Dafnes, Handakas-Candia and Malvasia Handakas-Candia, and two in Lasithi, Sitia and Malvasia Sitia. Mountains, altitude and north-facing slopes make fresh wine possible this far south."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Robola of Kefalonia, dry white; Mavrodaphne of Kefalonia, fortified sweet red; Muscat of Kefalonia, sweet white.","f":"Kefalonia's three appellations"},{"b":"Pale lemon; lemon, lime, white flowers, flint and smoke; lean, high acid, a stony, saline finish. Drink cold within a few years, with fish and shellfish.","f":"Robola in the glass"},{"b":"The Omala valley and the slopes of Mount Ainos, on bare limestone scree and karst, mostly low bush vines, some still on their own roots.","f":"Where Robola grows best"},{"b":"Different grapes. The names look alike and older references linked them through the Venetians; Robola is Kefalonia's, Ribolla Gialla belongs to Friuli and Slovenia.","f":"Are Robola and Ribolla Gialla the same grape?"},{"b":"The largest Ionian island, off the west coast of mainland Greece, north of Zakynthos; Venetian until 1797, British from 1815 to 1864.","f":"Where is Kefalonia?"},{"b":"Vostilidi, golden and aromatic; Zakynthino, soft and herbal; Tsaousi, soft and low in acid, also a table grape. Revived by a few estates as dry regional wine.","f":"Kefalonia's rare local whites"},{"b":"A fortified sweet red aged in cask: raisin, prune, coffee, chocolate. Serve cool with chocolate desserts, dried fruit or nuts, as one would a tawny port.","f":"Mavrodaphne of Kefalonia at the table"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Robola","role":"lead"},{"name":"Mavrodaphne","role":"lead"},{"name":"Muscat Blanc","role":"minor"},{"name":"Vostilidi","role":"rising"},{"name":"Zakynthino","role":"minor"},{"name":"Tsaousi","role":"minor"}],"index":8,"name":"Kefalonia","questions":[{"a":0,"cat":"Kefalonia","exp":"Robola of Kefalonia is Robola alone. Assyrtiko is Santorini's grape, Moschofilero is Mantinia's, and Vidiano is the rising white of Crete.","id":"r-5zveiwt0","opts":["Robola","Moschofilero","Assyrtiko","Vidiano"],"q":"Which grape alone makes the dry white appellation wine of Kefalonia?"},{"a":3,"cat":"Kefalonia","exp":"Kefalonia is the largest of the Ionian islands, west of mainland Greece. Santorini and Samos are in the Aegean, Crete's south coast faces the Libyan Sea, and the Adriatic lies to the north beyond Corfu.","id":"r-1ov62mmb","opts":["Libyan","Adriatic","Aegean","Ionian"],"q":"In which sea does the island of Kefalonia lie?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Kefalonia","exp":"Robola grows on bare limestone scree and karst. Volcanic ash is Santorini's soil, schist marks the terraces of Samos, and granite plays no part in Kefalonia's vineyards.","id":"r-4r28p5si","opts":["Volcanic ash","Limestone","Granite","Schist"],"q":"What is the main soil of Kefalonia's Robola vineyards?"},{"accept":["Mount Ainos","Ainos","Mt Ainos","Enos","Mount Enos","Aenos"],"ans":"Mount Ainos","cat":"Kefalonia","exp":"Mount Ainos, over 1,600 metres and a national park of Kefalonian fir, rises above the Omala valley, the heart of Robola.","id":"r-1oeeud09","q":"Which mountain rises above the Robola vineyards of Kefalonia?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Kefalonia","exp":"Mavrodaphne of Kefalonia is a fortified sweet red aged in cask, like its Patras cousin. The dry white is Robola, and a sun-dried sweet white would be Vinsanto or Samos Nectar.","id":"r-wk9aai2m","opts":["Fortified sweet red","Traditional method sparkling","Unfortified dry white","Sun-dried sweet white"],"q":"Which style is Mavrodaphne of Kefalonia?"},{"accept":["Muscat","Muscat Blanc","Muscat Blanc a Petits Grains","Muscat of Kefalonia","Moschato","Moscato"],"ans":"Muscat","cat":"Kefalonia","exp":"Muscat of Kefalonia is the island's sweet white PDO, from Muscat Blanc. The other two appellations are Robola of Kefalonia, the dry white, and Mavrodaphne of Kefalonia, a fortified sweet red.","id":"r-f21d4wg4","q":"Which grape gives its name to the sweet white appellation of Kefalonia?","sa":1},{"a":2,"cat":"Kefalonia","exp":"The high Omala valley below Mount Ainos, home of the growers' cooperative, is Robola's heartland. Paliki and the Lixouri coast in the west are lower and warmer, and Argostoli is the capital on its bay.","id":"r-fe6uzogv","opts":["The Paliki peninsula","The bay of Argostoli","The Omala valley","The Lixouri coast"],"q":"Which area of Kefalonia is the heart of Robola production?"}],"sheet":"greece","slug":"kefalonia","summary":"Kefalonia, the largest of the Ionian islands, off the west coast of mainland Greece, is the home of Robola, a white of lemon, flint and smoke grown on bare limestone up the slopes of Mount Ainos, some of it still on its own roots. The island holds three appellations: Robola of Kefalonia for the dry white, and the sweet Mavrodaphne of Kefalonia and Muscat of Kefalonia. Its Venetian and British past, its earthquakes and its rare local grapes, Vostilidi, Zakynthino and Tsaousi among them, make it a small region with a long memory. Robola is the answer the exam wants, and it is not the Ribolla Gialla of Friuli."},
{"asked":["certified","advanced","master"],"cards":[{"b":"Muscat Blanc à Petits Grains, locally Moschato Aspro, the only grape of the appellation.","f":"Samos: the grape"},{"b":"Grape spirit added to the must at or near the start of fermentation, to about 15% alcohol, keeping nearly all the sugar: fresh, very sweet and golden.","f":"Samos Vin Doux: how it is made"},{"b":"A Vin Doux Naturel, fortified after a partial fermentation, from selected fruit of the best, higher vineyards; it names no classified site.","f":"Samos Grand Cru: what the term means"},{"b":"Unfortified: Muscat dried in the sun until it shrivels, fermented slowly to a natural stop and aged several years in oak; amber, dried apricot, caramel.","f":"Samos Nectar: the method"},{"b":"To about 800 metres, on stone terraces up Mount Ampelos, whose name means vine; the harvest runs for weeks, from the coast in late summer to the highest terraces in autumn.","f":"How high do the vineyards of Samos climb?"},{"b":"The Union of Winemaking Cooperatives of Samos, founded in 1934, which gathers the growers' grapes and makes the Vin Doux, Grand Cru, Nectar and Anthemis.","f":"Who makes most Samos wine?"},{"b":"Fortified: Vin Doux, Vin Doux Naturel, Grand Cru, Anthemis. Unfortified: Nectar, from sun-dried grapes.","f":"Fortified or not? The sweet wines of Samos"},{"b":"Chilled. Vin Doux with fruit tarts and baklava, Grand Cru with blue cheese or foie gras, Nectar with caramel, bread pudding, nuts or chocolate.","f":"Samos at the table"}],"grapes":[{"name":"Muscat Blanc","role":"lead"}],"index":9,"name":"Samos","questions":[{"a":2,"cat":"Samos","exp":"Samos is Muscat Blanc à Petits Grains, Moschato Aspro, alone. Muscat of Alexandria is the Muscat of Lemnos, Moschofilero is Mantinia's unrelated grape, and Malvasia leads the sweet whites of Crete.","id":"r-ibp8und5","opts":["Malvasia Aromatica","Muscat of Alexandria","Muscat Blanc","Moschofilero"],"q":"Which grape makes the wines of the Samos appellation?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Samos","exp":"Nectar is the unfortified Samos, from sun-dried Muscat aged in oak. Vin Doux, the Grand Cru and the long-aged Anthemis are all fortified with grape spirit.","id":"r-o4gjvppb","opts":["Grand Cru","Nectar","Vin Doux","Anthemis"],"q":"Which Samos wine is made from sun-dried grapes without fortification?"},{"a":0,"cat":"Samos","exp":"The terraces climb Mount Ampelos, whose name means vine, to about 800 metres. Kerkis is the higher western peak of Samos, Ainos is Kefalonia's mountain, and Ida is Crete's Psiloritis.","id":"r-n3bc2hhd","opts":["Mount Ampelos","Mount Kerkis","Mount Ida","Mount Ainos"],"q":"On which mountain do most of the vineyard terraces of Samos lie?"},{"accept":["Moschato Aspro","Moschato","Moschato Samou","Moscato Aspro","White Muscat"],"ans":"Moschato Aspro","cat":"Samos","exp":"Moschato Aspro, white Muscat, is the Greek name for Muscat Blanc à Petits Grains, the only grape of the Samos appellation.","id":"r-mn8nvd80","q":"What is the local Greek name for the Muscat grape of Samos?","sa":1},{"a":3,"cat":"Samos","exp":"Samos lies in the eastern Aegean, separated from the Turkish coast by the narrow Mycale strait; it is not in the Cyclades with Santorini.","id":"r-mj3dfalp","opts":["Albania","Cyprus","Bulgaria","Turkey"],"q":"Which country's coast lies closest to the island of Samos?"},{"accept":["Vin Doux Naturel","VDN","Vin Doux Naturel Grand Cru","Vins Doux Naturels"],"ans":"Vin Doux Naturel","cat":"Samos","exp":"Vin Doux Naturel, with its Grand Cru from the higher vineyards, ferments partway before grape spirit stops it. Plain Vin Doux is fortified at the start, and Vin Naturellement Doux, sold as Nectar, is not fortified at all.","id":"r-8yr6s9av","q":"Which French term on a Samos label means the Muscat was fortified partway through its fermentation?","sa":1},{"a":0,"cat":"Samos","exp":"Samos Grand Cru is a Vin Doux Naturel from selected fruit of the best, higher vineyards, fortified after a partial fermentation; it names no classified site, and noble rot plays no part.","id":"r-9osugm1b","opts":["Fruit from selected higher vineyards","At least ten years of ageing in oak casks","Grapes shrivelled by noble rot","A single vineyard classified by law"],"q":"What does Grand Cru mean on a bottle of Samos?"},{"a":1,"cat":"Samos","exp":"Vin Doux is fortified at or near the start of fermentation, so almost all the sugar stays and the alcohol reaches about 15 per cent; the Vin Doux Naturel ferments further before its spirit.","id":"r-w1tjynox","opts":["Once fermentation has fully finished","Near the start of fermentation","Halfway through drying the grapes","Just before bottling after cask ageing"],"q":"At what point is grape spirit added to Samos Vin Doux?"}],"sheet":"greece","slug":"samos","summary":"Samos, an island in the eastern Aegean barely two kilometres off the Turkish coast, is the great Greek name for sweet Muscat. Its one grape is Muscat Blanc à Petits Grains, locally Moschato Aspro, grown on stone terraces that climb Mount Ampelos to about 800 metres. One of Greece's oldest appellations, it makes sweet wine in several ways: Vin Doux, fortified early with spirit; Vin Doux Naturel and its Grand Cru, fortified after a partial fermentation; and Nectar, unfortified, from sun-dried grapes. A growers' union founded in 1934 makes most of it. Lord Byron called for Samian wine, and servers still pour it with dessert."}
